export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** you are on call and something is broken right now.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** for each of the four incidents Forge\nactually produces, a confirming signal, a stop-the-bleeding action, and the\nconfiguration change that prevents a repeat. &#x2A;*Time:** read once now, so you do\nnot read it for the first time at 03:00."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A deployment other people depend on."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "These are incident procedures. They deliberately do not explain how Forge\nworks (Architecture does that) and they do not\ntune anything. When you have stopped the bleeding and want to make the system\nfaster rather than merely alive, go to Tuning."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "If you are not yet sure *which* incident you are having, start at\nTroubleshooting, which indexes symptoms to causes.\nThis page assumes you already know."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The scheduler's `INFO` log. Most of Forge's diagnostic output is at\n`INFO`, and the shipped Compose example defaults to `warn`. If you are at\n`warn` you will see a hung system and nothing else. `RUST_LOG=info` is the first move in\nalmost every procedure below."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A metrics pipeline. Several of these incidents are only visible as a rate\nchange. Observability brings one up in about ten\nminutes; doing it during an incident is a bad trade, so do it beforehand."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worker-is-oom-killed",
    "content": "The most common production incident, and the one with the most misleading\nfirst symptom: your developers report a *build* failure, not an\n*infrastructure* failure."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "Two distinct failures land here and they need different responses. Work\nout which one you have before doing anything."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "**The action's child process was killed.** The worker survived. The action\nreports **exit code 9**, and the worker logs:"
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "That exit code is a fixed sentinel: every signal collapses to 9, so you cannot\ntell SIGKILL from SIGTERM from the code alone. The `warn!` line above is the\nonly thing that tells you it was a signal at all. From Bazel's side this looks\nlike an ordinary action failure with a non-zero exit status, so **Bazel will\nnot treat it as an infrastructure error and will not retry it**. On the\nscheduler it lands in\n`forge_execution_completed_count_total{execution_result=\"failure\"}` like\nany other non-zero exit; there is no exported metric that singles out\nsignal deaths, so the log line is what you alert on."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "**The worker process itself was killed.** The scheduler notices after\n`worker_timeout_s` (default 10 seconds, checked once a second), evicts it, and\nre-queues its in-flight actions. A lost worker does not spend the action's\n`max_job_retries`; it has its own budget, `max_worker_loss_retries` (default\n10\\), so after that many losses the client finally sees, as a\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION` that Bazel does not resubmit:"
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "That string is the single best diagnostic for a memory-pressured worker fleet.\nIf you see it, you are in this case, not the first one."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it",
    "content": "Forge samples per-action peak RSS only on Linux. On macOS workers\n`resource_usage` is `None`; no memory data is reported at all, so the only\nevidence you will have is the exit code and the log line."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding",
    "content": "Drain the affected worker rather than killing it, so its in-flight actions\nfinish instead of being destroyed:"
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding",
    "content": "The path segment after `scheduler/` is the scheduler's `name` from the\n`schedulers` list, not a REAPI instance name. The endpoint only exists on a\nlistener with `admin: {}` in its services (the shipped Kubernetes config has\nnone); put it on the worker-API listener. It has no authentication; reach it\nover a private path only. See\nSecurity hardening."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding",
    "content": "Undrain with a trailing `0` when the host is healthy again. A drained worker\nappears in the scheduler's matching diagnostics as:"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Change"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Where"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Effect"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Lower `max_inflight_tasks`"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "worker config"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Caps concurrent actions per worker. Defaults to `0`, which means **infinite**; one worker will happily run every action you send it. This is the single most effective knob."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Raise the container memory limit"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Kubernetes `resources.limits.memory`, or the host"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Named explicitly by the worker's own warning."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Reduce per-action concurrency"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Your build's toolchain flags"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "A single `-j` in a wrapped compiler multiplies with `max_inflight_tasks`."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-it-stop-happening",
    "content": "Forge itself has **no memory configuration**. No `max_memory`, no\nper-action memory cap, nothing. Memory is bounded only indirectly, by store\neviction policies and by whatever the container runtime enforces. Do not go\nlooking for a config field; it does not exist."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-1",
    "content": "Writes start failing with an OS-level message wrapped in a Forge err\\_tip:\n`Failed to write data into filesystem store`, `Failed to flush in filesystem\nstore`, `Failed to sync in filesystem store`."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-1",
    "content": "The gRPC code will be `Unknown`, **not** anything storage-shaped. `ENOSPC`\narrives as `ErrorKind::StorageFull`, which Forge's `io::ErrorKind` to\ngRPC-code map does not name, so it falls to the catch-all arm. No\nENOSPC-specific handling exists anywhere in the tree."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-1",
    "content": "That has an unpleasant consequence: `Unknown` is **retryable**. A full disk\ngets retried through the entire retry budget before it fails, so the first\nthing you notice is usually latency, not errors."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-1",
    "content": "The filesystem store issues an explicit flush before renaming a file into the\ncontent path, precisely because tokio defers write errors to the next write\nor flush. Without it, a truncated file would be renamed in and served as a\nvalid blob, so a disk-full event produces errors, not silent corruption."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-look-for-first",
    "content": "If your eviction snapshot says &#x2A;*\"of unlimited\"**, no size-based eviction is\nconfigured, and this incident was inevitable. `eviction_policy` is an\n`Option` on the filesystem store, and every field in it defaults to `0`, which\nmeans *never evict*. The config's own doc comment is blunt about it: failure to\nset this value causes items to never be removed."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-1",
    "content": "Set `max_bytes` and restart the process. Eviction is only ever invoked on map\nmutation (on insert and remove), so it runs as soon as traffic resumes."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-1",
    "content": "`evict_bytes` creates a low watermark: once `max_bytes` is reached, eviction\ncontinues down to `max_bytes - evict_bytes` rather than evicting one entry per\ninsert forever. Set it to roughly 10% of `max_bytes`."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-1",
    "content": "Eviction compares the store's *own accounting* against `max_bytes`. It never\nlooks at the filesystem's actual free space, and a failed write does not\ntrigger eviction. If you set `max_bytes` larger than the volume, or leave it\nunset, the store will fill the disk and then keep erroring indefinitely."
  }, {
    "heading": "size-the-volume-for-twice-max_bytes",
    "content": "The filesystem store keeps an executable-variant directory\n(`{content_path}.exec`) alongside the content path, and &#x2A;*that directory is\ninvisible to `max_bytes`**. A variant is deleted when its primary entry is\nevicted, which bounds the total at roughly `2 * max_bytes` in the worst case\n(every blob also held as an executable). The variants are only ever created on\na worker, for executable inputs hardlinked out of its fast store, so this\napplies to worker fast tiers rather than to a CAS server's store."
  }, {
    "heading": "size-the-volume-for-twice-max_bytes",
    "content": "Provision accordingly: if you want a 50 GB worker cache, give it a 100 GB\nvolume."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-layout-footguns",
    "content": "`temp_path` must be on the **same block device** as `content_path`. If it is\nnot, the atomic rename becomes a copy: slower, and it doubles peak space\nduring every write."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-layout-footguns",
    "content": "Everything in `temp_path` is deleted at startup, and the content path is\nrescanned and re-accounted. A crash mid-write leaves a partial temp file that\nis reclaimed on the next boot, but only on the next boot. A process that\ncrashes repeatedly without a clean restart accumulates."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-actually-happens",
    "content": "Both Sentinel and Cluster modes are supported; the default is `standard`. On a\nSentinel failover the demoted master answers `-READONLY`, which Forge\nclassifies as retryable alongside dropped connections, refusals and IO errors."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-actually-happens",
    "content": "Each retryable failure reconnects and sleeps 100 ms, up to 5 attempts.\nReconnects are serialised behind a dedicated mutex so a failover storm does not\nhave every in-flight request stampeding the new master."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-actually-happens",
    "content": "**Net behaviour: about 500 ms of retrying, then the operation fails with the\nunderlying Redis error. The process does not exit.**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-actually-happens",
    "content": "Forge serves errors during a Redis outage rather than crashing. Process\nliveness is unchanged and the port still accepts connections. The health\nendpoint only notices if the Redis store is itself a registered indicator (a\nleaf at the top of a named store), in which case its `PING` check fails and\n`/status` returns `503`; wrapped under `fast_slow` or `existence_cache` it\nregisters nothing. Alert on the error rate, not on restarts."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "Scheduler backend"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "Impact of a Redis outage"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "`memory` (the default when unset)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "Nothing in the scheduler. Only Redis-backed *stores* fail, serving `Unavailable` (or `DeadlineExceeded` on a timed-out command) after roughly 500 ms of retries."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "`redis`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-breaks-depends-on-your-backend",
    "content": "The awaited-action database is unavailable: no new actions can be tracked and existing subscriptions stop delivering. The process stays up and keeps accepting connections."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-2",
    "content": "If you are on the `memory` backend, this is a cache-availability incident, not\na scheduler incident. Builds degrade to local execution and cache misses. Fix\nRedis at your leisure."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-2",
    "content": "If you are on the `redis` backend, builds hang. Failing over Redis is the fix;\nthere is no Forge-side mitigation."
  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
