  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
    "content": "A configured `0` is coerced to `1`."
  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
    "content": "`response_timeout_s` and `connection_timeout_s` are **deprecated**;\n`response_timeout_s` is ignored entirely. Both log a warning at startup. Setting\nboth `connection_timeout_s` and `connection_timeout_ms` is a startup error."
  }, {
    "heading": "configuration-worth-checking-now",
    "content": "The Redis scheduler backend downcasts to a concrete standard-connection type.\nPointing `experimental_backend: redis` at a Cluster- or Sentinel-mode store\ncan fail at startup with `Could not downcast to redis store in\n  RedisAwaitedActionDb::new`. Use a separate standard-mode store for the\nscheduler if you run Redis in cluster mode for your CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-queue-backpressures-and-never-recovers",
    "content": "The subtlest incident of the four, because nothing errors and nothing restarts.\nActions re-queue forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism",
    "content": "`ResourceExhausted` is Forge's backpressure code. When a worker returns\nit for an action, two things happen: the action goes back to `Queued` and\n(this is the important part) **its attempt counter is not incremented**. If\nthe worker still has other actions in flight it is also marked `is_paused`,\nand that flag clears as soon as any of those actions completes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism",
    "content": "That is deliberate. Backpressure is not the action's fault, so it should not\nburn a retry. But it means a worker that returns `ResourceExhausted`\n*permanently* produces an invisible infinite loop: the scheduler hands it the\naction, it rejects it, the action requeues without counting, and the next\nmatching pass hands it over again. `max_job_retries` does not protect you,\nbecause no attempt is ever counted, and an idle worker is never paused, so\nnothing takes it out of the candidate set."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism",
    "content": "The usual cause is `experimental_precondition_script`, the intended hook for\n\"do not send me work, my disk is filling up\". If the script's condition never\nclears, the loop never ends."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-2",
    "content": "then look at the scheduler's periodic pass: the action keeps appearing in\n`Oldest actions in state` as `Queued`, its age grows, and it never reaches a\nretry limit. The worker itself is quiet; the script's exit status is logged\nonly at `TRACE` (`Preconditions script returned`), so `RUST_LOG=info` on the\nworker shows nothing. If the worker still holds other work you will also see,\nin the scheduler's matching diagnostics:"
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-it-2",
    "content": "`is_paused=true` is only ever set while `inflight` is non-zero; a worker with\nnothing in flight that is still refusing work does not appear here at all,\nbecause it is, as far as the scheduler can tell, a perfectly available\nworker."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-3",
    "content": "Fix or remove the precondition script and restart the worker. Nothing can\nclear `is_paused` from the outside; it is set and cleared by the scheduler\nin response to what the worker returns."
  }, {
    "heading": "stop-the-bleeding-3",
    "content": "If the precondition script is doing its job correctly, and the host really is\nout of disk, then this is the disk-full incident wearing a different mask. Go\nto The disk fills up."
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Several Forge limits queue instead of erroring, which is why \"hung\" is a\nmore common symptom than \"failed\":"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Limit"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Behaviour at the limit"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Open file descriptors"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "24576 (`max_open_files`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "**Blocks on a semaphore.** Never produces `EMFILE`. Permits are 80% of the *achieved* `nofile` limit (19660 by default), which in a container with a hard cap may be far below what you configured."
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "`max_concurrent_requests` (gRPC scheduler)"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "unlimited"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Queues, does not reject."
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "`max_client_permits` (Redis)"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "500"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Blocks."
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "`max_concurrent_writes` (filesystem)"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "unlimited"
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "Blocks when set."
  }, {
    "heading": "related-limits-that-block-rather-than-reject",
    "content": "The FD limit is the one that catches people. If the kernel's hard limit caps\nthe raise below what you asked for, the semaphore silently shrinks to 80% of\nwhat it actually got, and the only evidence is a startup warning (if the\nraise call fails outright, the semaphore stays at 80% of 24576 regardless of\nwhat the kernel allows):"
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "Clients sit on `WaitExecution` for hours, the scheduler log is quiet apart\nfrom `Could not find AwaitedAction for the given operation id`, idle workers\ntake nothing, and the provisioner reads demand as zero. The scheduler's\nrecords are being evicted from Redis."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "`maxmemory` and `maxmemory-policy` are per Redis server, not per database.\nA deployment that puts the scheduler's records in the same instance as the\nCAS's small blobs and the BEP event stream, under `allkeys-lru`, has agreed\nthat growth in any of them may evict any of them. An evicted CAS blob is a\ncache miss. An evicted scheduler record is a client waiting on an operation\nnothing will ever finish, and a queue entry that, until v1.7.3, stopped the\nabandoned sweep at the first record it could not read, so the corpses behind\nit were never retired either."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "What to check, in order:"
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "`redis-cli info memory`: `used_memory` against `maxmemory`, and\n`evicted_keys` in `info stats`. Any eviction at all on the scheduler's\ninstance is this incident in progress."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "What is growing. A stalled BEP consumer is the usual cause: count keys by\nprefix. Postgres disk full stops the consumer, so check that too."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "The scheduler's own counters: `scheduler.awaited_action.orphans` (queue\nentries whose record was gone on read), `scheduler.queue.depth`,\n`scheduler.sweep.failures`, and `worker.connected.count`. Depth above\nzero with no connected worker for minutes is the stall itself."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "What to change:"
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "Give the scheduler its own Redis with `maxmemory-policy noeviction`. A\nfull instance then refuses writes, which is an error the scheduler reports\nand a client sees, instead of a silent loss. The Helm chart deploys one as\n`scheduler-redis`."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "Bound the BEP stream with `key_ttl_s` sized for the longest consumer\noutage you mean to survive, not for convenience."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "Alert on Redis memory and evictions and on the counters above before the\nqueue does the alerting for you."
  }, {
    "heading": "every-build-stalls-and-nothing-errors",
    "content": "From v1.7.3 the matching pass and the abandoned sweep skip a listed\noperation whose record is gone, count it, and carry on, so an eviction no\nlonger makes the pile permanent. It still makes every affected client wait\nout its timeout."
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler-redis-keeps-completed-actions",
    "content": "**Symptom.** With the Redis scheduler backend, `DBSIZE` on the scheduler's\ndatabase grows by one `aa_*` hash per action ever run and never falls; the\nrecords all read `completed` and `TTL` says `-1`. Once the search index holds\nmore than one cursor page, listings over it (the admin read the worker\nprovisioner makes, the scheduler admin endpoints) can fail with\n`Failed to get operations list! Parse: Non map item`."
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler-redis-keeps-completed-actions",
    "content": "**Cause.** Before v1.7.3 the Redis backend received `retain_completed_for_s`\nas configured, without the default applied: left unset it arrived as 0, and the versioned update treats a\nzero TTL as no expiry, so completed records were written to live forever.\nThe memory backend applied the 60 second default; only Redis leaked. The\nlisting failure is a second bug in the same release: a cursor page arrived\nin the RESP3 shape the first page's parser understood but the cursor reader\ndid not."
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler-redis-keeps-completed-actions",
    "content": "**Fix.** Upgrade to v1.7.3 or later; new completions expire after\n`retain_completed_for_s`. Records written before the upgrade keep their\nmissing expiry, so give them one once, on the scheduler's Redis database\n(0 in the chart's layout):"
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler-redis-keeps-completed-actions",
    "content": "A queued or executing record is left alone; only completed ones get the\nsixty seconds a fresh completion would."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Not an incident so much as a procedure that behaves worse than you expect."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Worker reconnect is a **flat 0.5-second retry with no exponential backoff, no\njitter, no cap and no configuration field**. A scheduler outage therefore\nproduces a 2 Hz reconnect storm from every worker, for the whole outage."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Worse, on reconnect each worker calls `kill_all()` and destroys every action it\nhad in flight, to avoid resource-locking itself against the new work the\nscheduler will send. A scheduler restart is disruptive to in-flight builds by\ndesign."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Before restarting a scheduler with running work, drain each worker (see\nabove) and wait for `inflight=0`."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Log lines to grep for during a restart:"
  }, {
    "heading": "restarting-the-scheduler",
    "content": "The third means the 10-second drain window expired with work still in transit."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-next",
    "content": "The symptom-to-cause index, for when you do not yet know which incident you\nare in."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-next",
    "content": "Once it is alive again: the levers that make it fast, and the ones that do\nnothing."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-next",
    "content": "Set up the metrics pipeline before the next incident, not during it."
  }],
