import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Worker execution",
  "description": "How an action becomes a running process: input materialization, what isolation you actually get, and what the worker throws away."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running workers, debugging an action that behaves\ndifferently on a worker than locally, or deciding how much to trust the\nactions they run. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** the lifecycle, the\nfilesystem layout, the exact isolation boundary, and the list of things the\nworker deliberately discards. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty-five minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The worker is where the abstractions stop. Everything upstream is digests and\nstate machines; here there is a directory, a process, and an exit code. Most\nof the surprising behaviour in a Forge cluster originates in this crate,\nbecause this is the only place where the operating system gets a vote."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-connection-is-one-stream",
    "content": "A worker opens exactly one bidirectional gRPC stream to the scheduler\n(`ConnectWorker`, the sole RPC on Forge's own `WorkerApi` service), and\neverything flows over it. It has no polling loop and no second connection."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-connection-is-one-stream",
    "content": "The worker supplies only an ID *prefix*; the scheduler mints the actual\nworker ID by appending a UUIDv6, so IDs are unique and time-ordered. Which\nworker gets an action is decided separately, by the LRU-ordered\nmatching engine."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-connection-is-one-stream",
    "content": "The worker's heartbeat period is derived as **half** its configured\n`worker_api_endpoint.timeout`, 2.5 seconds by default. Eviction is\ngoverned by the *scheduler's* `worker_timeout_s`, 10 seconds by default\n(four heartbeats). Any message from the worker refreshes the clock, not\nonly the heartbeat. Nothing reconciles the two knobs. Raise the worker's\ntimeout without raising the scheduler's and you get workers evicted\nmid-heartbeat and reconnecting in a loop."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "On startup the worker &#x2A;*purges and recreates its `work_directory`**. Anything\nleft there by a previous run is gone. This is intentional (a worker that\ninherits state is a worker whose actions are not hermetic), but it means the\nwork directory must not be somewhere you also keep things."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "Each action gets a tree:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "Input materialization's hot path is a **hardlink**: no copy, no `chmod`, no\nbyte movement at all. The worker links the file straight out of the\nfilesystem CAS into the action's input tree."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "This one decision produces four consequences that account for a large share\nof worker bug reports."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "**Inputs are read-only.** A hardlink shares the inode, and CAS blobs are\nstored `0o444` (or `0o555` when executable). An action that modifies an input\nfile in place gets `EACCES`. Actions that work locally and fail remotely are\nvery often doing exactly this."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "**Executables cost a second inode.** You cannot `chmod` a hardlink without\n`chmod`-ing the shared CAS blob, so an executable input needs its own copy,\none per digest, not one per action. This also sidesteps `ETXTBSY`, where\nwriting to a file that is currently executing fails."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "**`work_directory` must be on the same filesystem as the CAS content path.**\nHardlinks cannot cross filesystem boundaries. This is the single most common\nworker misconfiguration, and its symptom (everything works but is\ninexplicably slow, or fails outright) does not visibly point at a mount\ntable."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "**The fast store must be a `FilesystemStore`.** The worker downcasts to the\nconcrete type to find the file to link, and **refuses to start** if it is\ngiven anything else. It does not degrade to copying."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-filesystem-and-the-hardlink-that-everything-depends-on",
    "content": "Parents of `output_files` and `output_paths` are pre-created. Parents of\n`output_directories` are **not**. An action that expects its output\ndirectory's parent to exist has to create it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "This section is deliberately blunt, because overclaiming here is dangerous."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "**With the default configuration, a Forge worker provides no\nOS-level isolation whatsoever.** `use_namespaces` defaults to `false`. The\naction runs as an ordinary child process of the worker, with the worker's\nprivileges, on the worker's network, with access to whatever the worker can\nreach."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Turning namespaces on improves this, but far less than the word \"sandbox\"\nsuggests. Here is the complete list, in both directions:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Provided when enabled"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "**Not** provided, even when enabled"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "PID namespace"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Network namespace; the action has the host's network"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "IPC namespace"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "seccomp filtering; every syscall is available"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "UTS namespace"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "cgroups; no CPU or memory limit is applied"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "User namespace (identity map only)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "`chroot` / `pivot_root`; the host filesystem is visible"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "An init-stub reaper, via double fork"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Any privilege drop; the uid map is identity, range 1"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Optional mount namespace that tmpfs-masks sibling action directories"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "Anything at all on macOS or Windows"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "The user namespace maps one uid to itself. It does not sandbox the action's\nidentity; it exists so the other namespaces can be created without\nprivileges. Namespaces are **Linux-only** and hard-error elsewhere; this is\nnot a silent no-op you can leave enabled in a mixed fleet."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-isolation-you-actually-get",
    "content": "If you run untrusted actions, the isolation boundary has to come from\nsomewhere else: the `entrypoint` wrapper, which can invoke whatever sandbox\nyou choose, or the container the worker itself runs in. Forge's\nnamespace support is a hygiene measure (it stops actions tripping over\neach other's processes and mounts), not a security boundary. Note also that\ninside Docker, creating namespaces requires a privileged container, which\ntrades away more than it buys back."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-environment-is-built-from-nothing",
    "content": "The worker calls `env_clear()` first. Nothing is inherited. Variables are\nthen applied in this order:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-environment-is-built-from-nothing",
    "content": "`additional_environment` from the worker's config"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-environment-is-built-from-nothing",
    "content": "On Windows only, `SystemRoot` and `PATH`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-environment-is-built-from-nothing",
    "content": "The action's own `Command.environment_variables`, **last**"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-environment-is-built-from-nothing",
    "content": "Two things follow. **Action-declared variables override worker config**, not\nthe other way round; worker config is a default, not a policy. And on Linux\nand macOS **no `PATH`, `HOME` or `TMPDIR` is injected at all**. An action that\ndoes not declare them does not have them, which is correct for hermeticity\nand is why toolchains that assume a working `PATH` fail here. See\ntoolchains and hermeticity."
  }, {
    "heading": "timeouts-signals-and-the-output-you-lose",
    "content": "A requested timeout above `max_action_timeout_s` (20 minutes if unset) is\n**rejected with `InvalidArgument`, not clamped**. The client is told its\nrequest was unacceptable rather than silently receiving a shorter deadline\nthan it asked for. That is the right choice, and one worth knowing before you\nset a low ceiling on a cluster whose clients ask for long ones. An action\nthat requests no timeout at all runs under `max_action_timeout_s`."
  }, {
    "heading": "timeouts-signals-and-the-output-you-lose",
    "content": "Three things about failure output are worth internalising:"
  }, {
    "heading": "timeouts-signals-and-the-output-you-lose",
    "content": "**A timed-out or killed action returns empty stdout and stderr.** The\ncaptured bytes are explicitly discarded, not truncated. The single most\nuseful diagnostic for \"why did this hang\" is the one you do not get."
  }, {
    "heading": "timeouts-signals-and-the-output-you-lose",
    "content": "**Signal death is reported as exit code 9**, indistinguishable from a genuine\n`exit(9)`. An OOM kill and a program that chose to exit 9 look identical."
  }, {
    "heading": "timeouts-signals-and-the-output-you-lose",
    "content": "**stdout and stderr are buffered entirely in worker memory**, with no cap and\nno streaming. An action that writes a gigabyte to stdout costs a gigabyte of\nworker RAM, and no part of it is visible until the action ends."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "The ordering here is subtle and matters for anyone reasoning about\nconsistency. When the command exits, the worker sends `ExecuteComplete` to\nthe scheduler (the process is done, so a new action can be assigned) and\n*then* uploads outputs, deletes the action directory, and writes the action\ncache entry. The final result update, which is what a client is waiting on,\ncomes after all of that, so a client that observes completion and\nimmediately queries the cache finds the result; but the scheduler knows the\ncommand finished slightly before the bytes exist."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "The first reply is the worker's admission decision. A dispatch is a\nproposal until the worker answers it: `ExecuteAccepted` confirms the ledger\ncharge the scheduler made on the send, `ExecuteDeclined` hands it back. The\nworker declines when it already holds `max_inflight_tasks` actions, when the\naction's memory reservation is more than the worker currently has free, or\nwhen it is shutting down. A declined action returns to the queue without\ncounting as an attempt, and the worker is paused until its next keepalive,\nor, for a decline for memory, until a keepalive reports that much free. The\nscheduler's channel to each worker is bounded at its concurrency plus a\nmargin; a worker that has stopped reading has its dispatch requeued the same\nway rather than piling up work it will never see. A worker only sends these\nreplies to a scheduler that announced support in its `ConnectionResult`, so\nan older scheduler sees the old behaviour: the worker runs whatever it is\nsent. `dispatch_ack_timeout_s` on the scheduler adds the converse: a dispatch\nno worker acknowledged within the timeout is taken back, which only makes\nsense once every connected worker acknowledges."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "Four behaviours in the upload path:"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "**A declared-but-missing output is not an error.** An action that promises\n`output_files` and does not produce them completes successfully with those\noutputs absent. Bazel will notice; a hand-rolled client may not."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "**Absolute symlinks are dereferenced and uploaded as content.** Only\n*relative* output symlinks are preserved as symlinks. An action that emits an\nabsolute symlink gets a copy of the target instead, which is usually what\nyou want across machines, and occasionally very much not."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "**`node_properties` is `None`.** Output file modes and mtimes are dropped. A\nfile that was executable in the action's output tree is not necessarily\nexecutable when a client materialises it."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploading-outputs-and-what-is-dropped",
    "content": "**`server_logs` is not implemented.** The field exists in the protocol; the\nworker never populates it."
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "The entire action directory is deleted after every action, with a `Drop`\nbackstop for the panicking case and a path-canonicalization check so that a\nmalicious or malformed operation ID cannot direct the delete outside the work\ndirectory."
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Three separate mechanisms get called \"eviction\" in worker discussions, and\nconflating them makes capacity planning impossible:"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Domain"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Governed by"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Behaviour"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "The action directory"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "nothing"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Deleted unconditionally after every action"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "The filesystem CAS"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "`eviction_policy.max_bytes`"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "LRU over stored blobs"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "The directory cache (only if `directory_cache` is configured)"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "`max_entries` (1000), `max_size_bytes` (10 GB)"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "LRU over materialized input trees"
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "Only the second is your cache size. The third is a distinct, much smaller\ncache of materialized trees, off by default, and its defaults are not derived\nfrom the second."
  }, {
    "heading": "cleanup-and-three-things-called-eviction",
    "content": "A persistent worker process is spawned once (with the worker's own\nenvironment, and **without** namespaces or `env_clear()`) and reused across\nactions. Each action still gets its own materialized input directory, which\nis handed to the process as the request's `sandbox_dir`, but the process\nitself carries whatever state it accumulates from one request to the next.\nThat is the point (it is what makes them fast), but it also means a\npersistent worker is a shared mutable environment across the actions it\nserves. See persistent workers."
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "Every worker field and default is in the generated\nworker configuration reference. The\nexecution code itself is"
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "and\n."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "It is almost certainly modifying an input file in place. Inputs are\nhardlinks to read-only CAS blobs. Copy the file into the output tree\nfirst, or declare it as an output rather than an input."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not on the strength of Forge's own isolation. With defaults there is\nnone; with namespaces enabled there is still no network isolation, no\nseccomp, no cgroups, no filesystem root change and no privilege drop. Put\nthe boundary in the `entrypoint` wrapper or in the container the worker\nruns in, and size that boundary for genuinely hostile input."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Because output from a timed-out or killed action is discarded rather than\nreturned. To get diagnostics out of a hanging action, have it write to a\ndeclared output file rather than to stdout, or lower the action's own\ninternal timeout so it exits normally before the worker kills it."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No. Materialization is hardlink-based and hardlinks cannot cross\nfilesystems. Both must live on the same filesystem, which is also the\nthing to check first when a worker is unexpectedly slow."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "stdout and stderr are buffered entirely in memory with no cap. An action\nthat is chatty on stdout costs RAM proportional to everything it has\nprinted, held until it exits."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Which of these guarantees you get by default, which you have to configure,\nand which the protocol never offered in the first place."
  }],
