    "heading": "one-file-six-keys",
    "content": "Event publishing, still experimental"
  }, {
    "heading": "one-file-six-keys",
    "content": "`schedulers` and `workers` are optional because a cache-only deployment has\nneither. That's Getting started: a config with just `stores` and `servers` is a\ncomplete, valid Forge cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-a-request-traverses-them",
    "content": "Read that diagram as: &#x2A;*the `servers` array is the only entry point, and\neverything else is reached from it by name.** A service doesn't contain a\nstore, it names one. A worker doesn't contain a scheduler, it dials one over\nthe worker API. The names are the wiring."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-a-request-traverses-them",
    "content": "This is why the config can describe both a single process and a fleet without\nchanging shape. Split the same file into three (one with `stores` and a CAS\nserver, one with `schedulers` and an execution server, one with `workers`)\nand the names become network addresses instead of in-process references.\nNothing else changes."
  }, {
    "heading": "everything-is-a-named-array",
    "content": "Every top-level collection is an **array of objects**, not a map keyed by\nname. Stores and schedulers carry their `name` beside the type key; workers\ncarry it inside the `local` block; servers have an optional `name`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "everything-is-a-named-array",
    "content": "The `name` sits beside the type key rather than wrapping it, because the\ntype-specific fields are flattened into the same object."
  }, {
    "heading": "everything-is-a-named-array",
    "content": "You'll find configs written with the name as a key wrapping the body:\n`schedulers` as an object mapping `MAIN_SCHEDULER` to its scheduler spec,\nrather than as an array. That shape does not parse. If you copied one and\ngot an error mentioning `invalid type: map`, this is why."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-typo-is-a-startup-error-not-a-silent-default",
    "content": "Nearly every struct in the config crate is `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`.\nMisspell a key and the process refuses to start and tells you which key it\ndidn't recognise, rather than ignoring it and behaving strangely three hours\nlater."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-typo-is-a-startup-error-not-a-silent-default",
    "content": "This is worth knowing because it changes how you should edit configs: the\nfastest way to check a change is to start the binary. It parses the whole file\nbefore it binds a single socket."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "**Learn the mechanics**"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "The config file: JSON5, environment\nsubstitution, how the binary finds the file, and how to check one before\nyou trust it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "**Learn the four sections**"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "Stores: declaring and naming stores, and the one\nidea that unlocks the rest of the system: stores compose."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "Servers and services: listeners,\nthe eleven services, and the port split that keeps workers off the public\ninterface."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "Schedulers and workers: the\nminimum of each, and the properties contract between them."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "**Put it together**"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pages-in-order",
    "content": "Your first full config: an empty\nfile to a running cache-and-execution cluster, one block at a time."
  }, {
    "heading": "start-here-or-skip-ahead",
    "content": "**Start here** if you have a cluster running from a copied example and want to\nunderstand what you copied."
  }, {
    "heading": "start-here-or-skip-ahead",
    "content": "**Go back a section** if nothing is running yet.\nGetting started gets you a cache, and\nRemote execution gets you workers; both hand you a\nworking config you can then read here."
  }, {
    "heading": "start-here-or-skip-ahead",
