import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Scaling workers",
  "description": "How much work one worker should take, how many workers you need, which signal tells you, and how to let something else make the decision, including the parts Forge does not enforce for you."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** you have a worker pool serving real builds and the queue is\neither backing up or sitting idle. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a sized\nworker, a scaling signal you trust, and either a manual playbook or an\nautoscaler wired to it. &#x2A;*Time:** an afternoon for the manual version, longer if\nyou're building the autoscaling path."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A worker pool serving real builds."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Run multiple workers is how you get from one\nworker to several. This page is about the questions that come next: how much\nwork each one should take, how many you need, and who decides."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-axes-and-only-one-of-them-is-a-forge-knob",
    "content": "Capacity has a vertical axis (how many actions one worker runs at once) and a\nhorizontal axis (how many workers exist). Forge has a field for the first.\nIt has nothing at all for the second, by design: a worker is a process that\ndials the scheduler and announces itself, so \"more workers\" means \"start more\nprocesses,\" and whatever starts processes for you is the thing that scales."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-axes-and-only-one-of-them-is-a-forge-knob",
    "content": "Start with the vertical axis, because it changes what the horizontal one costs."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "The knob is `max_inflight_tasks` on the worker's config."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "Three things about it are not what most people assume, and each has bitten\nsomebody."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "**The default is `0`, and `0` means unlimited.** An unconfigured worker will\naccept as many concurrent actions as the scheduler sends it. That is fine on a\nsmall pool and a genuinely bad time on a large one, because the failure mode is\nnot a queue; it's memory exhaustion or an out-of-file-descriptors error partway\nthrough a build. Set it explicitly."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "**It is enforced on the scheduler, not on the worker.** The worker sends the\nnumber when it registers; the scheduler tracks how many actions each worker is\nholding and stops assigning past the limit. The worker itself has no admission\ncheck and will run whatever it is handed. In normal operation this distinction\nis invisible. It matters if you ever run two schedulers against one worker, or\nif you're reasoning about what protects the host: the answer is the scheduler's\nbookkeeping, and nothing else."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "**`cpu_count` does not limit anything on the worker.** This is the one that\nsurprises people most. The `cpu_count` platform property, usually populated\nwith `query_cmd: \"nproc\"` in the shipped examples, is *advertising*. It tells\nthe scheduler what to match against. It does not throttle execution, and the\nworker derives no concurrency from the host's CPU count at all. Declare\n`cpu_count: 64` on a four-core box and the scheduler will happily route\n64-core actions to it."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "One is what the scheduler matches on; the other is what the scheduler counts.\nIf your actions declare `cpu_count` as a `minimum` requirement, that number is\nconsumable (the scheduler subtracts it on assignment and restores it on\ncompletion), so it does bound concurrency indirectly. But it does so only for\nactions that declare it. `max_inflight_tasks` is the bound that applies to\neverything."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-one-worker",
    "content": "A reasonable starting point is `max_inflight_tasks` at roughly the host's core\ncount for compile-shaped work, then adjusted by observation: if per-action\ndurations climb as the number in flight rises, you're oversubscribed; if the\nhost sits at half its CPU with a deep queue, you're undersubscribed."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "A worker sized generously runs into limits that are not on the worker config,\nand they're worth knowing before you go hunting."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**File descriptors.** Forge holds a process-wide semaphore over open files,\ndefaulting to **24,576**. Every action materializing inputs and writing outputs\ndraws from it. Raise it with `max_open_files` under `global` (the shipped\nKubernetes worker config sets 524,288) and raise the OS limit to match, or the\nsemaphore will let through more than the kernel allows."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**gRPC connections to the CAS.** The `grpc` store's `connections_per_endpoint`\ndefaults to **1**. One HTTP/2 connection multiplexes streams well up to a point\nand then becomes the bottleneck, and on a worker running dozens of actions\nagainst a remote CAS that point arrives early. This is the first knob to reach\nfor when a worker's CPU is idle and its actions are slow."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**Store-level concurrency caps.** `max_concurrent_writes`,\n`max_concurrent_requests`, and Redis's `connection_pool_size` (default 3) all\nsit between the worker and its bytes. A worker that scales up without these\nscaling with it just queues internally."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**One `work_directory` per worker process, always.** The worker **purges its\nwork directory on startup**. Two workers pointed at the same path means the\nsecond one to start deletes the first one's in-flight state. On a shared host,\ngive each worker its own path; in containers this is free."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "Not CPU. Worker CPU tells you whether a machine is busy; it cannot tell you\nwhether there is work waiting that nobody is doing, and that's the only question\nan autoscaler needs answered."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "The signal is **queue depth**: actions in the `queued` stage rather than\n`executing`. Forge emits `execution.active.count` as an up-down counter\nattributed by `execution.stage`, which reaches Prometheus as\n`forge_execution_active_count` after the OTLP collector's Prometheus\nexporter adds its `forge` namespace."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "The shipped recording rule is:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "That's the series to scale on. Three caveats before you copy it into production."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "**Queue depth is a derived series, not a metric the binary emits.** No\nmetric name exists that an HPA can target directly. You scale on the recording rule, read\nthrough an external-metrics adapter."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "**The example alert's window is wrong for autoscaling.** The shipped\n`NativeLinkQueueBacklog` alert fires on a queue over 100 sustained for 15\nminutes. That is a correct alert (\"you are chronically undersized\") and a\nterrible autoscaling trigger. An HPA wants a window in the tens of seconds. Use\nthe alert for paging and a much shorter window for scaling."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "**The repository disagrees with itself about worker utilization.**\n`forge:worker_utilization` is defined one way in\n`deployment-examples/metrics/prometheus-recording-rules.yml` and differently in\nthe inline rules inside `prometheus-config.yaml`, and the Kubernetes alert uses\na third expression. `forge:actions_per_worker` is also misnamed; it\nrecords a per-worker sum, not an average. Scale on queue depth, which is\nunambiguous, and treat the utilization series as a dashboard aid rather than a\ncontrol input."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-to-scale-on",
    "content": "`execution.active.count` comes from the in-memory awaited-action store only.\nIf you have moved the scheduler to `experimental_backend` with Redis, the\nsignal this whole page depends on is not being emitted. Confirm which backend\nyou're on before you design an autoscaler around it."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Set `max_inflight_tasks` explicitly on every worker** and confirm it took\neffect by watching per-worker action counts during a busy build. Leaving it at\nthe default means the horizontal axis is being tuned against an unbounded\nvertical one, and nothing you measure will be stable."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Get `forge:queue_depth` onto a dashboard** and watch it through a normal\nday. You are looking for its shape: how deep it goes at peak, how long it stays\nthere, and how quickly it drains. That shape is what sizing decisions are made\nagainst."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Find your per-worker throughput.** With a stable pool size, divide actions\ncompleted by workers by time. This is the number that converts \"the queue is 400\ndeep\" into \"I need N more workers,\" and it is workload-specific enough that\nnobody else's figure helps."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Scale manually first, on a written rule.** Something as plain as \"if queue\ndepth exceeds 200 for two minutes, add four workers; if it's under 20 for ten\nminutes, remove two\" is enough. Run it by hand for a week. You will learn more\nabout your build's shape than from any amount of upfront design, and you'll have\nthe thresholds an autoscaler needs."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Then automate that rule.** On Kubernetes, an HPA on the external metric backed\nby `forge:queue_depth`, plus a cluster autoscaler or Karpenter underneath\nso pods have somewhere to land. Elsewhere, whatever your platform's equivalent\nis. The rule you validated by hand becomes the policy.\nThe reference deployment is that assembled end\nto end, including the parts the shipped manifests are missing."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Move the fleet to spot capacity once retries are proven.** A worker that\ndisappears mid-action has its work requeued with its affinity cleared, so\ninterruption is a cost rather than an incident. Verify that in a staging pool by\nkilling a worker mid-build before you rely on it in production."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "**Wire the drain endpoint to your interruption notice.** For shutdowns you get\nwarning about (spot termination, node upgrades), call the scheduler's admin\nendpoint so the worker finishes what it holds and takes nothing new:"
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "The route's second segment is the scheduler's `name` from the `schedulers`\nlist (`MAIN_SCHEDULER` in the shipped configs), not the REAPI instance name,\nand the `admin` service has to be enabled on a listener for the route to\nexist at all. Nothing in the repository wires this up for you. It is a short\nscript, and it's the difference between a clean scale-in and a burst of\nretried actions."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Two read-only listings sit next to it, for the same scripts and for an\nautoscaler that wants the scheduler's own view rather than its store:"
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "`workers` returns every connected worker with what it registered, what it\nhas left after its running actions' reservations, its running count, its\npause and drain flags and the free memory it last reported. `demand`\nreturns every queued action with its operation id, when it was queued, its\npriority and its platform properties, reservations included. A worker's id\nstarts with the name it registered under, which on Kubernetes is the pod\nname, so a drain script can find the ids to drain from the pod."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Queue depth rises under load and returns to near zero afterwards, rather than\nsitting at a floor; a floor means you are permanently undersized."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Adding workers visibly shortens the queue's drain time. If it doesn't, the\nbottleneck is the CAS or the network, not worker count."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Per-action durations stay flat as the pool grows. Durations that climb with\npool size mean the workers are contending for something shared."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Killing a worker mid-build produces retried actions and a successful build,\nnot a failed one."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-out",
    "content": "Scale-in does not coincide with a spike in retried actions. If it does, the\ndrain hook isn't being called."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "Being explicit, because the gap is larger than the docs elsewhere imply."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**`deployment-examples/docker-compose/docker-compose-multi-worker.yml`** defines\nthree worker services. They are copy-pasted and identical apart from a\n`WORKER_NAME` and a private data volume each: same config file, same shared\nCAS volume, two CPUs and 2 GB each. It has no `deploy.replicas` and no\nheterogeneity. A fourth worker means pasting a fourth block."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**`deployment-examples/docker-compose/test-multi-worker-simple.json5`** is a\ngenuinely different pattern worth knowing about: **three workers inside one\nprocess**, as three entries in the `workers` array of a single config. One\nbinary, one config file, three registrations with the scheduler. It's a useful\nshape for a machine you want to subdivide, and it is not the same thing as three\ncontainers."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**`kubernetes/components/worker/worker.yaml`** sets `replicas: 3`. The `lre-cc`\nand `lre-rs` overlays patch it to 1. The `siso-chromium` overlay does not patch\nit, so it inherits 3, which looks unintentional."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**No HorizontalPodAutoscaler, KEDA `ScaledObject`, or VPA exists anywhere in\nthe repository for Forge.** The single HPA in the tree scales the\nOpenTelemetry collector. No heterogeneous-pool example exists in\n`forge-config/examples/` either; every shipped worker example is a single\nhomogeneous pool. The autoscaling path on this page is one you assemble from\nstandard Kubernetes parts; the recording rules are the raw material, not a\nfinished artifact."
  }, {
    "heading": "backpressure-when-a-worker-needs-to-stop",
    "content": "One mechanism lets a worker refuse work without being removed:\n`experimental_precondition_script`. It runs before every action, and a non-zero\nexit produces a `ResourceExhausted` error. The scheduler requeues the action\nwithout counting it as an attempt, and marks the worker `is_paused` **only if\nthat worker still has other actions in flight**; the pause clears as soon as\none of them completes. An idle worker whose script fails is never paused: it\nkeeps its registration and is handed the action again on the next\nmatching pass, and rejects it again."
  }, {
    "heading": "backpressure-when-a-worker-needs-to-stop",
    "content": "This is the hook for host-level conditions the scheduler cannot see: disk\nfilling, a shared license pool exhausted, a GPU in a bad state. It costs a\nprocess spawn per action, so keep the script trivial. And note the\n`experimental_` prefix: it is not covered by stability guarantees."
  }, {
    "heading": "heterogeneous-pools",
    "content": "One scheduler can route to pools of different shapes; the mechanism is platform\nproperties, and it is covered in full on\nplatform properties. The scaling point\nis narrower: **each pool is a separate scaling unit with its own queue-depth\nseries**, because `forge:queue_depth` groups by instance and priority, not\nby which pool could serve the work."
  }, {
    "heading": "heterogeneous-pools",
    "content": "If you scale a mixed fleet on one aggregate number, a backlog of GPU actions\nwill scale up your compile pool and change nothing. Either split the recording\nrule by the property that distinguishes the pools, or run a scheduler instance\nper pool. The second is blunter and easier to reason about."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Actions are queued that no worker matches. This is nearly always a platform\nproperty mismatch: an action declaring something no worker advertises, or a\nscheduler key configured as `priority` (which constrains nothing) when it should\nbe `exact` or `minimum`. Adding workers will not help. Check the scheduler's\nworker-match logging, and see\nplatform properties.\nTroubleshooting\nhas the log lines that distinguish \"nothing matches\" from \"everything is busy\",\nand why a bad property *value* hangs where a bad property *name* errors."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The bottleneck is downstream. Workers pull inputs from and push outputs to the\nCAS, so a saturated CAS or a saturated network means more workers just wait in a\ndifferent place. Check CAS latency as you scale, and check\n`connections_per_endpoint` on the workers' `grpc` store; the default of 1 makes\na single connection the ceiling."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`worker_timeout_s` defaults to **10 seconds**, checked once a second. That is a\nshort leash and it is deliberate (fast detection means fast requeue), but a\nworker whose host is starved for longer than that, or whose network hiccups,\nwill be evicted. If you see churn without actual failures, raise it. The cost\nof raising it is a longer stall before a genuinely dead worker's actions are\nretried. Releases up to 1.7.2 defaulted to 5 seconds and only counted\nheartbeats, so a worker busy reporting results could still be evicted."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "A configured `0` is read as unset and falls back to the default of **3**. The\nfield cannot express \"never retry\" through that field. If an action must not be\nretried, that has to be handled above Forge."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`max_action_timeout_s` on the worker defaults to **20 minutes**, and a client\nrequesting a longer timeout is **rejected outright** rather than clamped down to\nthe maximum. Long link steps and EDA runs need this raised on the worker before\nthe client asks for it. (The field also accepts the older spelling\n`max_action_timeout`.)"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The process-wide open-file semaphore defaults to 24,576, which a worker running\nmany concurrent actions over a filesystem store will reach. Raise `max_open_files`\nunder `global`, and raise the OS `nofile` limit to at least match; the\nsemaphore does not know what the kernel will allow."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Workers are being killed with work in flight. The drain endpoint exists for\nexactly this and nothing calls it automatically. Wire it to your platform's\npre-shutdown hook, and give the pod a termination grace period long enough for a\ntypical action to finish."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check they do not share a `work_directory`. The worker purges that directory at\nstartup, so a restart of one worker destroys the other's state. Each worker\nprocess needs its own."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The other half of capacity: composing the store graph, sizing eviction, and\nwhy a second CAS process is harder than a second worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The full symptom-to-cause index, including the failures that produce no error\nstring at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Getting `execution.active.count` out of the binary and into something an\nautoscaler can query, which is the prerequisite for everything above."
  }],
