export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Silicon and large builds",
  "description": "EDA flows, large C++ and LLVM trees, and ML graphs are the workloads remote execution was invented for: thousands of actions, hardware that differs from action to action, and a long tail that decides the wall clock."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone whose build is measured in thousands of actions and\ntens of minutes, on hardware that isn't uniform. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:**\nan understanding of why these builds are the hard case, and how Forge routes\nactions to the machines that suit them. &#x2A;*Time:** about fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-a-build-large-in-the-way-that-matters",
    "content": "Size in gigabytes is not the interesting axis. Three properties are, and a build\nthat has all three is the shape remote execution was designed around."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-a-build-large-in-the-way-that-matters",
    "content": "**Action count.** An EDA flow (synthesis, place-and-route, timing, DRC, LVS)\nor a full LLVM tree is tens of thousands of actions. At that count the graph is\nwide enough that the number of cores you can throw at it is the only thing\nsetting the wall clock. A laptop has sixteen. A worker pool has however many you\nask for."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-a-build-large-in-the-way-that-matters",
    "content": "**Heterogeneity.** The actions are not interchangeable. A link step wants a\nmachine with a great deal of memory and will use one core doing it. A thousand\ncompiles want a thousand cores and very little memory each. A simulation wants a\nGPU. A vendor tool wants a specific OS, sometimes a specific kernel, and a\nlicense seat. Sizing one machine for all of that means sizing it for the worst\ncase and wasting it on every other action."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-a-build-large-in-the-way-that-matters",
    "content": "**The long tail.** Large graphs have a critical path, and near the end of the\nbuild most of the fleet is idle while a handful of long actions finish. The\ntotal is set by the longest chain, not by the sum of the work. That's why \"add\nmore cores\" stops helping at a point, and why *which* machine runs the long\nactions matters more than how many machines you have."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-caching-alone-runs-out",
    "content": "On a small build, the cache is most of the story. On a build like this it is\nstill enormously valuable (it is still the thing you deploy first, and Getting\nstarted is still where to start), but it stops being sufficient, for a specific reason."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-caching-alone-runs-out",
    "content": "A cache helps with work somebody has already done. A large build has a long\nlist of actions that nobody has done, because a real change in a low-level\nheader invalidates a large subtree, and because verification runs are new by\nconstruction. Those actions have to execute. The question stops being \"can we\navoid this work\" and becomes \"where does this work run, and how much of it runs\nat once.\""
  }, {
    "heading": "why-caching-alone-runs-out",
    "content": "That is the transition from Getting started to Remote execution, and this class of build is the\nclearest case for making it."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "The mechanism is **platform properties**: key-value pairs an action declares as\nrequirements, matched against properties a worker declares about itself. The\nscheduler will not assign an action to a worker that does not satisfy them."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "A worker declares its properties in its configuration, either as literal values\nor as a command run at startup whose output becomes the value, which is how a\nworker discovers, say, its own core count rather than having it hard-coded per\nhost."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "The scheduler declares, separately, which property keys exist and how each one\nis matched. Four kinds exist and the differences are load-bearing:"
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "**`minimum`** compares numerically: the worker's value must be greater than or\nequal to the action's. It is also **consumable**: when an action requesting\n`cpu_count: 8` is assigned, eight is subtracted from that worker's available\ncount and restored when the action completes. This is how a 64-core worker takes\neight 8-core actions and not sixty-four of them."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "**`exact`** requires string equality. This is the one for OS, ISA, container\nimage, or a GPU model, anything where \"more\" is meaningless."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "**`priority`** does not restrict anything by value. It requires only that the\nkey be present. The behaviour its name suggests, preferring some workers over\nothers, is an unimplemented TODO in the scheduler."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "**`ignore`** matches everything, and is the way to accept a property that\nclients send without letting it affect placement."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "A key configured as `priority` will match a worker regardless of the value on\neither side. If you use it for something that must actually be satisfied, the\naction will happily land on a worker that cannot satisfy it. Use `minimum` for\nquantities and `exact` for identities; reach for `priority` only when you mean\n\"record this, don't route on it.\""
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "An unknown property key is refused rather than silently matched: the scheduler\nlogs `Unknown platform property` and the action stays queued, so a typo in a\nrequirement stalls visibly instead of quietly sending a GPU action to a CPU\nworker."
  }, {
    "heading": "routing-actions-to-the-hardware-that-fits",
    "content": "Platform properties is the reference\nfor the mechanism."
  }, {
    "heading": "special-hardware-and-what-forge-actually-knows-about-it",
    "content": "Forge has no GPU support, and that phrasing is deliberate rather\nthan a shortcoming. No GPU-aware code exists anywhere in the codebase: no\nCUDA integration, no device discovery, no special-cased scheduling. GPUs are\nrouted the same way a specific kernel version or a licensed tool is: as plain\nplatform properties that you name and the scheduler matches."
  }, {
    "heading": "special-hardware-and-what-forge-actually-knows-about-it",
    "content": "The convention across the shipped example configurations is a pair:"
  }, {
    "heading": "special-hardware-and-what-forge-actually-knows-about-it",
    "content": "`gpu_count` as `minimum` so it is both a threshold and consumable: a worker\nwith four GPUs can hold four single-GPU actions. `gpu_model` as `exact` so an\naction needing a particular device does not land on a different one."
  }, {
    "heading": "special-hardware-and-what-forge-actually-knows-about-it",
    "content": "The Kubernetes example config in `kubernetes/forge/forge-config.json5`\ndeclares both `gpu_count` and `gpu_model` as `priority`, which, per the\nsection above, enforces nothing. An action requesting four GPUs will schedule\nonto a worker with none. If you started from that file, change both."
  }, {
    "heading": "special-hardware-and-what-forge-actually-knows-about-it",
    "content": "The same pattern covers everything else that varies. An `os` and an `arch` key\nas `exact` give you cross-platform pools from one scheduler. A `license_seats`\nkey as `minimum` makes a vendor tool's seat count a scheduling constraint rather\nthan a race, because `minimum` is consumable: the seats are held for the duration\nof the action and released when it finishes. That last one is a genuinely useful\ntrick for EDA flows and is not apparent from the config reference."
  }, {
    "heading": "running-a-heterogeneous-fleet",
    "content": "The pool is not one shape of machine. It's several, each a worker process with\nits own configuration, all registering with the same scheduler."
  }, {
    "heading": "running-a-heterogeneous-fleet",
    "content": "Nothing about this requires more than one scheduler, or a separate deployment\nper pool. Each worker announces what it is; the scheduler routes on that.\nRun multiple workers is the mechanics, and\ntuning covers sizing each pool once you're running them."
  }, {
    "heading": "running-a-heterogeneous-fleet",
    "content": "The practical advice is to start with one pool and split it only when you can\nname the reason. Two pools with different properties is a real operational cost,\nand it pays for itself when a specific class of action is either starved of the\nhardware it needs or hogging hardware it doesn't."
  }, {
    "heading": "toolchains-are-the-hard-part-not-the-scheduling",
    "content": "For this class of build the routing is the straightforward half. The half that takes the\ntime is making the actions hermetic enough to run somewhere else at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "toolchains-are-the-hard-part-not-the-scheduling",
    "content": "Large old codebases accumulate assumptions: a compiler at an absolute path, a\ntool installed by the platform team years ago, an environment variable set in a\nshell profile, a vendor toolchain with a license daemon. All of that works\nlocally and none of it exists on a worker. Getting a large build onto remote\nexecution is mostly the work of finding those assumptions and declaring them."
  }, {
    "heading": "toolchains-are-the-hard-part-not-the-scheduling",
    "content": "Forge's answer is **Local Remote Execution**: building the toolchain\nitself as a declared, reproducible artifact so that the environment a worker\nruns in is the same one you get locally, by construction rather than by\nconvention. Local Remote Execution is the explanation, and\nToolchains and hermeticity is\nthe practical path."
  }, {
    "heading": "toolchains-are-the-hard-part-not-the-scheduling",
    "content": "This is also the reason to be honest about sequencing. Getting started (caching) costs\nyou a URL in a config file. Remote execution costs you a toolchain project. The payoff on\na build of this size justifies it, but it is a project, and planning it as a\nconfig change is how these migrations stall."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-build-like-this-looks-like-when-it-works",
    "content": "One published account is worth reading rather than paraphrasing:\nReid Kleckner's write-up\ndescribes building LLVM with `recc` executing remotely against Forge and\ntaking a build from roughly seventeen minutes to roughly four. It is one\nperson's measurement on their own hardware, with caveats the post itself spells\nout (precompiled headers disabled, a mixed home fleet, a wireless link), so\ntreat it as an illustration of the shape rather than a number to plan against,\nbut the shape is right. A wide graph of independent compiles is exactly the case\nwhere adding remote cores collapses the wall clock."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-build-like-this-looks-like-when-it-works",
    "content": "The honest general statement is that speedups on this class of build vary by an\norder of magnitude between workloads, because they depend on how wide the graph\nis, how long the critical path is, and how much of the build was already\ncacheable. The way to find out is to measure your own."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-build-like-this-looks-like-when-it-works",
    "content": "CMake with recc is the path\nfor CMake-based C++ trees, which is what most large non-Bazel C++ projects are."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Partly. `recc` puts a CMake-based build on the remote cache by intercepting\ncompiler invocations, and it's the documented path for large C++ trees. The\nsetup in these docs is cache-only (`RECC_CACHE_ONLY=1`, so misses compile\nlocally); recc can also forward compiles to remote workers once you have them,\none compiler invocation at a time. Remote execution of the whole graph needs a\nbuild tool that describes every action's complete input set, which is what\nBazel, Buck2, Siso and BuildStream do and what Make does not."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Give them different properties and let `minimum` do the accounting. A link\naction declaring a large `memory_kb` only matches workers that have it, and\nbecause `minimum` is consumable, one such action occupies that worker's memory\nbudget until it finishes rather than letting three more land on top of it. If\nthey are still colliding, that's the signal to split the pool."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes: a worker is a process, and pools are distinguished only by the properties\nthey declare. Declare `os` as `exact` on the scheduler, have each worker declare\nits own, and one scheduler routes to all of them. The constraint is not\nForge's; it's that the action's toolchain has to exist for the target\nplatform."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The tool still talks to the license server as it always did, so the worker\nneeds network access to it. What changes is that seat exhaustion becomes a\nscheduling problem you can express: a `license_seats` key matched as `minimum`\nholds seats for the duration of an action and releases them after, which turns\na race into a queue."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Usually yes, because \"most actions are unique\" is rarely true across a whole\ntree: the third-party dependencies, the toolchain, and everything below the\nchange you made are shared. It stops being true for workloads that are\ngenuinely new every run, and some ML training and fuzzing jobs are exactly that.\nFor those, remote execution is the part that helps and the cache is close to\nfree anyway."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The reference for the routing mechanism: how to declare properties on workers,\nhow to match them on the scheduler, and how each matching kind behaves."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The toolchain half of the problem: making the environment a worker builds in\nreproducible rather than assumed."
  }],
