export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who wants the cache to survive a restart without\npaying for object storage, and anyone building the fast tier of a larger\ncomposition. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a `filesystem` store sized to\nyour disk, with eviction that actually runs. &#x2A;*Time:** fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you can edit. See Configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "Create the parent directory and start Forge. The store scans\n`content_path` on boot and adopts every file whose name parses as a store key\nand generation (`<hash>-<size>-<generation>` under `d2/`, or\n`<key>-<generation>` under `s2/`); a file whose\nname does not parse is deleted. The scan reads metadata, not content, so it does not check\nthat bytes still match their digest. That scan is what makes the cache survive\nrestarts, and it is also why boot time grows with the number of cached\nobjects."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "The generation gives each upload its own file. Eviction of an older upload\ncan finish after a replacement arrives without moving the replacement's file.\nIf several generations survive a shutdown, startup keeps the newest generation\nfor each key, subject to the eviction policy."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "Startup migrates the earlier `d/` and `s/` layout, and the older flat digest\nlayout, into the new directories. A file that cannot be moved remains readable\nfrom its original location, and migration is retried on the next startup.\nEarlier Forge versions cannot read the new layout; use a separate cache\ndirectory when rolling back to an earlier version."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-paths",
    "content": "`content_path` holds the data. `temp_path` holds objects mid-upload and\nmid-delete, while their contents cannot yet be trusted."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-paths",
    "content": "Completing an upload is an atomic rename from the temp path into the content\npath. Across a device boundary a rename becomes a copy, which is neither\natomic nor free. Put both under one parent directory and the problem cannot\narise."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-paths",
    "content": "Everything in `temp_path` is deleted on every startup. Nothing you care about\nshould ever be written there by hand."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "`eviction_policy` is optional in the schema and mandatory in practice. Without\nit, nothing is ever removed and the store grows until the disk is full."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Eviction is LRU: touching an entry refreshes its timestamp, and inserts run the\npolicy until the store fits again. Four independent limits are available, all\ndefaulting to `0`, which means \"don't evict on this axis\":"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Meaning"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "`max_bytes`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Total size on disk before eviction starts"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "`evict_bytes`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Keep evicting until `max_bytes - evict_bytes` is reached"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "`max_seconds`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Age since last access before an entry is evicted"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "`max_count`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Number of entries before eviction starts"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Set `evict_bytes` to something like 10% of `max_bytes`. Without it, the store\nsits exactly at the limit and every single insert triggers an eviction;\na low watermark turns that thrash into one bulk pass per 10%."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eviction-policy-you-cannot-skip",
    "content": "Size accounting uses `block_size` (default 4 KiB), not the byte length of the\nfile, because a one-byte object still consumes a whole block. If your\nfilesystem uses a different block size, set it, or your `max_bytes` will be a\nsignificant underestimate of real disk use."
  }, {
    "heading": "tuning-that-matters-on-real-hardware",
    "content": "**`max_concurrent_writes`** (default `0`, unlimited). Every write streams into\na temp file and calls `sync_all()`. Enough concurrent writes will saturate disk\nI/O and start blocking the async runtime. On a busy shared CAS, set this to\nsomething bounded (a few dozen) rather than leaving it open."
  }, {
    "heading": "tuning-that-matters-on-real-hardware",
    "content": "**`read_buffer_size`** (default 32 KiB). Leave it alone unless you are\nbenchmarking."
  }, {
    "heading": "tuning-that-matters-on-real-hardware",
    "content": "**`evict_page_cache`** (default `false`). When true, the store advises the\nkernel to drop the page cache for each blob after it is read or written."
  }, {
    "heading": "tuning-that-matters-on-real-hardware",
    "content": "On a real filesystem this takes a globally serialized, all-CPU kernel path\nthat stalls on many-core hosts, and it throws away the page cache that\nmakes a fast tier fast in the first place. It exists for deployments that\nspecifically need this store's I/O kept out of the page cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "if-this-store-feeds-a-worker",
    "content": "A worker's `cas_fast_slow_store` must have a `filesystem` store as its `fast`\nhalf. The worker builds each action's input tree by hard-linking files out of\nthat store, and hard links require real files on a real filesystem; a memory\nor object store cannot supply them."
  }, {
    "heading": "if-this-store-feeds-a-worker",
    "content": "The worker's `work_directory` must be on the **same filesystem** as that\nstore's `content_path`, for the same reason. See\nSchedulers and workers."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Create the directories** and make sure the process user owns them:\n`mkdir -p /var/lib/forge/cas/content /var/lib/forge/cas/tmp`."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the store** with both paths under one parent and an `eviction_policy`\nwhose `max_bytes` leaves headroom on the device."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Start Forge** and run a build against it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Restart Forge** and run the same build again. The second run should be\na cache hit."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Files appear under `content_path/d2/` named `<hash>-<size>-<generation>` while a build\nruns."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "`temp_path` is empty when no upload is in flight, and empty after a restart."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "`du -sh` on `content_path` stabilises near `max_bytes` rather than growing\npast it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A build re-run after a restart of Forge is a cache hit, not a rebuild."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "An upload that encounters a missing file during duplicate comparison removes\nthe stale index entry and stores the uploaded content. A read removes only the\nentry whose file was missing. In a `fast_slow` composition, that read can fall\nthrough to the slow store and refill the fast tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Repeated missing-file warnings after recovery suggest that files are still\nbeing removed outside the store. Check for external cleanup of `content_path`."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The store has no `eviction_policy`, or has one with `max_bytes: 0`. Both mean\n\"never evict\". Add a real limit and restart; the boot scan will bring the\nstore back under the limit."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "If the limit is set and the disk still filled, check `block_size`: on a\nfilesystem with 16 KiB blocks, an accounting `block_size` of 4 KiB\nunderestimates real usage by up to 4x for small objects."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`temp_path` and `content_path` are on different filesystems. Check with\n`df /var/lib/forge/cas/content /var/lib/forge/cas/tmp`. If the\ndevice column differs, move one of them."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "That's the boot scan reading the metadata of every file in `content_path`\n(name, size, access time). It's proportional to entry count. A tighter `max_count` or\n`max_seconds` keeps the store smaller; a `fast_slow` pair with a small fast\ntier over a durable slow tier keeps boot cheap without giving up capacity."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check `max_concurrent_writes`. Unlimited means the store will happily issue\nmore concurrent `sync_all()` calls than the device can retire, at which point\ndisk I/O becomes the bottleneck for every request including reads."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The boot scan only deletes files whose names it cannot parse as a store key;\neverything else is re-added, and the eviction policy then runs against the\nwhole set, so a store over `max_bytes` or `max_count` is trimmed at startup.\nSeparately, switching the digest function between `sha256` and `blake3`\n(`default_digest_hash_function` on the server, `--digest_function` on the\nclient) does not delete anything, but every lookup misses because clients now\ncompute different digests. See Stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Put this filesystem store in front of a durable one, or behind\ndeduplication and compression."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The usual slow tier: durability you don't have to operate."
  }],
