export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Azure Blob Storage",
  "description": "Back the cache with an Azure Blob container: account and container naming, the endpoint override for Azurite, and when a SAS URL replaces everything else."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running Forge on Azure who wants the durable\ntier to be a Blob Storage container. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a\ncontainer-backed CAS behind a local fast tier. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes, most\nof it access configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you can edit (see Configuration), and an Azure storage account you can write to."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Azure Blob is one `provider` value of the same cloud object store used by\nS3 and compatible,\nGCS and\nOCI Object Storage. The composition around\nit is identical; only the provider block changes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "`account_name` and `container` are the pair you have to set. From them the store\nbuilds the endpoint `https://{account_name}.blob.core.windows.net/{container}`.\nThe container is a blob container inside the storage account. Create it before\nstarting Forge; the store does not create it for you."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-ways-to-point-at-a-container",
    "content": "**Account plus container.** The default above. The endpoint is derived, and\nthe store authenticates with Entra ID Workload Identity and nothing else: it\nreads `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` and `AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE`\n(the variables AKS workload identity injects into a pod) and fails at startup\nif they are missing. A VM managed identity, a storage account key or an\n`az login` session are not tried. Nothing secret is in the config."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-ways-to-point-at-a-container",
    "content": "**An endpoint override.** `endpoint` replaces the host portion only; the\ncontainer is still appended to whatever you give. This is how you point the\nstore at an emulator such as Azurite:"
  }, {
    "heading": "three-ways-to-point-at-a-container",
    "content": "That address is Azurite's default. Plain `http://` is accepted as-is; the\nAzure store does not read `insecure_allow_http`. Workload Identity is still\nthe credential in this mode, so the three environment variables must be\npresent; if the emulator cannot accept such a token, use `sas_url` instead."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-ways-to-point-at-a-container",
    "content": "**A SAS URL.** `sas_url` takes precedence over all of the above: when it is\nset, `account_name`, `container` and `endpoint` are ignored for URL\nconstruction and no credential is attached, because the signature in the URL\n*is* the credential."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-ways-to-point-at-a-container",
    "content": "Anyone holding the URL has whatever the signature grants, so keep it in the\nenvironment rather than the config file, and note that the store will start\nfailing the moment the signature expires, with an authentication error.\nPrefer Workload Identity where you have the choice."
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "What it decides"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`key_prefix`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "none"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Namespacing inside one container; give the CAS `cas/` and the AC `ac/`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`endpoint`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "derived"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Host override; ignored when `sas_url` is set"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`sas_url`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "none"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Full pre-signed URL; overrides the derived endpoint and the credential"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`retry`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Exponential backoff with jitter, shared with every other provider"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`consider_expired_after_s`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`0` (never)"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "How old a blob may be and still count as present"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`max_retry_buffer_per_request`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "5 MiB"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "How much of a single-request upload is buffered so it can be retried"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "`multipart_max_concurrent_uploads`"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "10"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Concurrent staged-block uploads within one large blob"
  }, {
    "heading": "options-worth-setting",
    "content": "Blobs under 5 MiB with a known size are uploaded in one request; anything\nlarger, or of unknown size, is staged as blocks of at least 5 MiB and\ncommitted with a block list. `retry` and `consider_expired_after_s` behave as\ndescribed in the shared options section;\n`insecure_allow_http` and `disable_http2` are accepted but not read by the\nAzure store."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Create the storage account and container** in the region your workers run\nin. A standard general-purpose v2 account on hot tier is the right default;\ncool and archive tiers price retrieval in a way a build cache cannot afford."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Grant access.** Assign the workload identity the Storage Blob Data\nContributor role scoped to the container, or mint a SAS with read and write.\nThe store never lists or deletes blobs."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Set the Workload Identity variables** (`AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_CLIENT_ID`,\n`AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE`) in the process environment, unless you use\n`sas_url`. Uncommitted blocks left by a process killed mid-upload are discarded\nby Azure after a week on their own."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the store** as the `slow` half of a `fast_slow` pair whose `fast` half is\na local filesystem store."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Run a build twice**: once cold, once after emptying the local fast tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Blobs appear under `key_prefix` in the container during the first build."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The second build is a cache hit even after `content_path` is emptied, which\nproves reads fall through to the container."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Container request counts are far lower than blob counts, which proves the\nfast tier is absorbing repeat reads."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "No uncommitted blocks accumulate between builds."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Either the workload identity lacks Storage Blob Data Contributor on the\ncontainer, or a `sas_url` has expired. A SAS expiry surfaces as an\nauthentication failure, so check the signature's window before assuming the\nrole assignment is wrong. If the store did not start at all with a message\nabout the Workload Identity credential, the `AZURE_*` variables are missing."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`sas_url` wins over everything. If it is set, `account_name`, `container` and\n`endpoint` play no part in building the URL. Unset it to go back to the derived\nendpoint."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The store does not create containers. Create it in the storage account first,\nand check that `container` matches exactly; container names are lowercase."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`endpoint` must include the account path segment:\n`http://127.0.0.1:10000/devstoreaccount1`, not just the host and port. The\ncontainer is appended to whatever you give. Without `sas_url` the store still\nneeds the Workload Identity variables to start, and the emulator has to accept\nthe resulting token; a `sas_url` minted for the emulator avoids both."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check `consider_expired_after_s`. A non-zero value makes blobs older than that\nwindow invisible on purpose, so they pair with a matching lifecycle rule. If\nyou didn't intend that, set it back to `0`."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Deduplication, compression and existence caching in front of the container,\nthe layers that decide what it actually costs."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The same store type with a different provider, and the shared options in\nfull."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "the-recipe",
    "content": "The recipe"
