import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Schedulers and workers",
  "description": "The minimum config for each, the properties contract between them, and the fields that decide what happens when something goes wrong."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone adding execution to a config that currently only\ncaches. &#x2A;*What you'll know at the end:** the minimum scheduler and worker\nblocks, how the two are wired to each other, and which of the many optional\nfields actually change behaviour you'll notice. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config whose `stores` and `servers` you understand. See Stores and Servers and services."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "A scheduler is one entry in the `schedulers` array, with a name and a type:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`simple` is the scheduler you want. The other four types (`grpc`,\n`cache_lookup`, `property_modifier`, and `historical_resource`) either\nforward to another scheduler or wrap one to change its behaviour; none of them\nis a starting point."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`supported_platform_properties` is the whole contract. It declares which\nproperty keys this scheduler understands and how each is matched:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Value"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Meaning"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`minimum`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Parsed as a number; a worker must advertise at least this much"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`exact`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Parsed as a string; must match the worker's value exactly"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`priority`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Not used for filtering; passed to the worker as information"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "`ignore`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "Actions may request the key; workers need not advertise it"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-scheduler",
    "content": "A property key an action requests that is **not** in this map is an error. A\nkey that is in the map but that no worker advertises means the action never\nmatches anything and queues silently. That failure mode gets its own page,\nPlatform properties, because it's\nthe first wall almost everyone hits."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "A worker is one entry in the `workers` array. `local` is currently the only\ntype; it means the worker runs actions in this process, on this machine:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "Four of those fields are load-bearing."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "**`worker_api_endpoint`** is the address of the *scheduler's* `worker_api`\nservice, the private port from\nServers and services, not the public\none. The worker dials out; the scheduler never dials in. That direction is why\na worker fleet can sit behind NAT with no inbound rules."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "**`cas_fast_slow_store`** must name a `fast_slow` store whose `fast` half is a\n`filesystem` store. The worker builds each action's input tree by hard-linking\nfiles out of that fast store into the work directory, which requires real\nfiles on a real filesystem. The `slow` half must eventually resolve to the\nsame CAS the client and scheduler use, or be a `noop`, when the fast tier is\nthe only storage."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "**`work_directory`** must be on the **same filesystem** as the fast store's\n`content_path`, for the same hard-linking reason. It is fully managed by the\nworker and purged on startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "**`platform_properties`** is the worker's half of the contract. Each key is\neither a static list of values or a `query_cmd` that is run (as a command, not\nthrough a shell) each time the worker connects to the scheduler, with its\noutput split on newlines:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "Every key the scheduler declares as `minimum` or `exact` must appear here, or\nthis worker will never be matched for any action requesting it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-minimum-worker",
    "content": "The correct shape is `upload_action_result: { ac_store: \"AC_MAIN_STORE\" }`.\nConfigs showing `upload_action_result: { upload_action_result: { ... } }`\nare wrong and will be rejected; `ac_store` is a direct field. Omitting the\nblock entirely is also valid, and means the worker runs actions but never\ncaches their results, which looks exactly like a broken cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "Nothing in the scheduler names a worker. Workers are not declared to the\nscheduler at all; they connect to it, announce their platform properties, and\nare added to the pool. Removing a worker means stopping it."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "That is what the three pieces of wiring add up to:"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "the `execution` service names the scheduler, so clients can submit actions;"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "the `worker_api` service names the same scheduler, on a different port, so\nworkers can join;"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "each worker names the `worker_api` address, so it knows where to join."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-they-find-each-other",
    "content": "In a single-process config all three are the same process and the endpoint is\n`127.0.0.1`. In a fleet, the scheduler is its own deployment and that endpoint\nis a service address. The config shape does not change."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "`SimpleSpec` has ten optional fields. Most can stay at their defaults\nforever. These four are the ones worth setting deliberately:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`worker_timeout_s`** (default 10 s): how long a silent worker stays in the\npool. Any message from the worker counts, not only the heartbeat. Keep it at\nleast twice the worker's `worker_api_endpoint.timeout`; a worker evicted\nmid-action has every action it held re-queued, each with an attempt spent."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`max_action_executing_timeout_s`** (default 0, disabled): caps how long an\naction can sit in `Executing` with no update, even from a worker that is\notherwise healthy. This is the setting that catches a worker stuck on one\nspecific action rather than dead. Without it, such an action hangs until a\nclient gives up."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`max_job_retries`** (default 3): how many times an action that fails with\nan internal error is retried before the last error is returned to the client.\nIt exists to stop one poisonous action from cycling through and destabilising\nyour whole fleet. A lost worker and a memory escalation do not spend it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`max_worker_loss_retries`** (default 10): how many times an action whose\nworker was lost (disconnected, timed out, evicted, or OOM-killed as a whole)\nis queued again before the client gets a `FAILED_PRECONDITION`. Losing a\nworker is not the action's failure, so this budget is separate and larger;\nit only stops an action that takes a worker down every time it runs."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`retain_completed_for_s`** (default 60 s): how long a completed action's\nresult stays available for a late `WaitExecution` call. Clients that\ndisconnect and reconnect need this window to be longer than their reconnect\ntime."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "On the worker side, &#x2A;*`max_inflight_tasks`** (default 0, meaning unlimited) is\nthe one to set on any real machine. Unlimited means the worker accepts every\naction the scheduler offers and lets the OS sort out the contention."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`drain_on_shutdown`** (off by default) makes a worker announce that it is\ndraining the moment it gets `SIGTERM`, so the scheduler stops dispatching to\nit while it finishes what it holds. It needs a scheduler that understands the\ndrain flag; an older one removes the worker on the spot and requeues its\nactions."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "Three bounds keep one action from taking the worker with it.\n&#x2A;*`max_captured_output_bytes`*&#x2A; (default 0, unbounded) caps how much of an\naction's stdout or stderr the worker holds in memory; past it the output\nspills to a file under the action directory and is uploaded from disk, so a\ntool that prints gigabytes costs disk, not RAM. &#x2A;*`precondition_timeout_ms`*&#x2A;\n(default 30000) ends a hanging precondition script and refuses the action as\nbackpressure. &#x2A;*`orphan_sweep_interval_s`** (default 0, off) removes action\ndirectories under `work_directory` that no running action owns and that a\nfailed cleanup left behind; the directory is always purged at startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "When the worker ends an action, for a timeout or a scheduler cancel, the\nprocess group gets SIGTERM first and &#x2A;*`kill_grace_ms`*&#x2A; (default 5000) to\nwrite its own cleanup before SIGKILL follows; the memory reservation kill\nskips the grace. A killed action keeps the stdout and stderr it produced.\n&#x2A;*`set_tmpdir`** (default true) gives every action its own `TMPDIR` under its\naction directory, so concurrent actions writing the same temporary file name\nno longer collide; a `TMPDIR` in the action's own environment still wins, and\ncode that hard-codes `/tmp` is unaffected. A timeout above `max_action_timeout_s`\nis clamped to it rather than rejected."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "When the action's own process has exited, anything it left running in its\nprocess group (a background job, a helper that daemonized) is killed, since\nit would otherwise hold the action's output pipes open and the action would\nsit there until its timeout; the pipes then get five seconds to drain, and\nwhat was read by then is kept. The worker also makes itself the parent of\nevery process an action leaves behind and reaps them on a timer, so a build\nwhose actions fork and do not wait no longer leaves hundreds of zombies on\nthe worker for the life of the pod. Under `use_namespaces` the stub does\nthe reaping inside the action's PID namespace."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "Every executed action reports its resource usage to the scheduler with an\n`outcome`: `COMPLETED`, `KILLED_MEMORY` (the worker's reservation kill, or a\nSIGKILL from the kernel with the last memory sample within 10% of the limit),\n`KILLED_TIMEOUT` or `KILLED_EXTERNAL`, plus wall time and the reservation the\naction ran under. The scheduler logs every kill outcome with its mnemonic."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`resource_enforcement`** (off by default, Linux only) makes the worker hold an\naction to the memory reservation the scheduler placed it by. With\n`memory: soft` the worker kills the action's process group once two\nconsecutive samples exceed `memory_kb` plus `memory_headroom_percent`\n(default 20), or one sample exceeds twice that, and fails it with\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION` naming the reservation and the observed peak. Samples\nare 250 ms apart, so an action that allocates faster than the pod's whole\nlimit in half a second can still reach the cgroup limit first; the\nreservations admitted onto a worker must also sum to less than its limit\nwith the headroom included. Without it the kill comes from the pod's cgroup limit,\nand on cgroup v2 that kill takes the worker and every action on it, not just\nthe one that grew. The sample is the group's proportional set size, so a\nparent's pages shared with its forked children count once. Actions run\nthrough a persistent worker are not covered: that process outlives the action\nand serves others, so only the pod's limit bounds it. With enforcement on, the\nworker reads an action's properties from the scheduler's dispatch rather than\nthe client's request, so a reservation the scheduler placed the action by is\nthe one enforced and exported to the environment."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "`disk: guard` adds a check before any input is fetched: an action whose\n`disk_kb` reservation exceeds the free space under the work directory, less\nwhat the actions already admitted reserved, is refused with `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, which the scheduler requeues without\ncounting an attempt and holds off this worker until its next keepalive,\ninstead of running the fetch into a full disk.\n`disk: soft` is the guard plus a kill: every ten seconds the worker adds up\nthe files the action has written under its own directory (those modified\nsince its command started; inputs were materialized before that and are\nnot counted), and an action past its `disk_kb` reservation by\n`disk_headroom_percent` is killed and fails with `FAILED_PRECONDITION`, its\noutcome `KILLED_DISK`. The walk runs on the blocking pool as its own task,\nso memory sampling carries on meanwhile. With either disk mode the action's\nusage report carries `peak_disk_kb`, what it wrote, from one walk of the\naction directory at completion, which is the only walk `guard` pays. The\nfigure is reported and exported for now; the scheduler's sizing consumer\ncomes with the size ladder."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "**`capacity`** makes the worker advertise CPU and memory from its own cgroup\nlimits instead of from `platform_properties`: it reads `cpu.max` and\n`memory.max` at its cgroup v2 root, keeps back `overhead_cpu_millicores`\n(thousandths of a core) and `overhead_memory_kb` for itself, divides the\nmemory by the enforcement headroom (`memory_headroom_percent`, which follows\n`resource_enforcement` unless set), and sets the two properties to the\nresult at registration. The limit is the number the kernel enforces, so the\nnumber the scheduler packs against is derived from it rather than typed next\nto it. Where the cgroup cannot be read, hand-typed `cpu_count` and\n`memory_kb` stand with a warning; with neither typed the worker refuses to\nstart, since it could take no action that asks for either. `cpu_unit` is the scale of the CPU\nproperty: `cores` by default, rounded down, the scale `cpu_count: {\nquery_cmd: \"nproc\" }` and an action's `cpu_count=1` use; a fleet that\nadvertises and requests thousandths of a core sets `millicores`. The\noverhead is always in thousandths of a core."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
    "content": "`experimental_backend` defaults to memory, so restarting the scheduler loses\nthe queue. A Redis backend exists for shared state across scheduler\nreplicas. Both are covered in\nProduction configuration; for a single\nscheduler, the default is correct."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "Adding execution to a cache-only config takes four edits, all of which you've\nnow seen:"
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "**Add a `schedulers` array** with one `simple` entry, naming the platform\nproperties you intend to match on."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "**Add `execution` to the public server's services**, naming the CAS store and\nthe scheduler."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "**Add a second server on a private port** with `worker_api` naming the same\nscheduler, and nothing else client-facing on that port."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "**Add a `workers` array** with one `local` entry pointing at that private\nport, plus the `fast_slow` store it needs."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "The worker logs `Worker registered with scheduler` with its assigned worker\nid."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "A build submitted with matching platform properties reports actions\nexecuting remotely rather than queuing."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "Every key the scheduler declares `minimum` or `exact` appears in the\nworker's `platform_properties`."
  }, {
    "heading": "putting-the-pieces-together",
    "content": "Your first full config does exactly\nthis, from an empty file, one block at a time."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and that's what `forge-config/examples/basic_cas.json5` is: one\nprocess with stores, a scheduler, a worker, a public server on `:50051` and a\nprivate one on `:50061`. It's the right shape for a laptop and for testing.\nSplitting into separate processes is a deployment change, not a config\nrewrite; the same blocks move into separate files."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Run more worker processes. The `workers` array can hold several entries in one\nprocess, but they share the machine, so the useful form of scaling is more\nmachines each running a worker config that dials the same scheduler. Nothing\non the scheduler side changes."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "It wraps every action. The configured command is prepended to the action's own\ncommand, so an `entrypoint` of `run.sh` and an action of `sleep 5` executes\n`run.sh sleep 5`. It's how you put actions inside a sandbox, a container, or a\ntimeout wrapper without the client knowing."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "`max_action_timeout_s` on the worker defaults to 20 minutes. An action that\nrequests no timeout gets that limit; an action that requests a *longer* one is\nrejected outright with `InvalidArgument: Action timeout of N seconds is\ngreater than the maximum allowed timeout`. Either way, an action legitimately\nneeding longer needs this raised on the worker, not just on the client."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "It runs before every action, and a non-zero exit pauses the worker; it stops\naccepting new work until the script succeeds. The intended use is resource\ngating: don't take another action while disk is nearly full or a shared\nresource is saturated."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Empty file to a running cache-and-execution cluster, one block at a time,\nwith a check after each."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The matching contract in depth, and how to diagnose a queue that never\ndrains."
  }],
  "headings": [{
