          children: "upload_action_result: { upload_action_result: { ... } }"
        }), "\nare wrong and will be rejected; ", _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "ac_store"
        }), " is a direct field. Omitting the\nblock entirely is also valid, and means the worker runs actions but never\ncaches their results, which looks exactly like a broken cache."]
      })
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.h2, {
      id: "how-they-find-each-other",
      children: "How they find each other"
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "Nothing in the scheduler names a worker. Workers are not declared to the\nscheduler at all; they connect to it, announce their platform properties, and\nare added to the pool. Removing a worker means stopping it."
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "That is what the three pieces of wiring add up to:"
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.ul, {
      children: ["\n", _jsxs(_components.li, {
        children: ["the ", _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "execution"
        }), " service names the scheduler, so clients can submit actions;"]
      }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.li, {
        children: ["the ", _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "worker_api"
        }), " service names the same scheduler, on a different port, so\nworkers can join;"]
      }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.li, {
        children: ["each worker names the ", _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "worker_api"
        }), " address, so it knows where to join."]
      }), "\n"]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: ["In a single-process config all three are the same process and the endpoint is\n", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "127.0.0.1"
      }), ". In a fleet, the scheduler is its own deployment and that endpoint\nis a service address. The config shape does not change."]
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.h2, {
      id: "the-fields-that-change-what-youll-notice",
      children: "The fields that change what you'll notice"
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "SimpleSpec"
      }), " has ten optional fields. Most can stay at their defaults\nforever. These four are the ones worth setting deliberately:"]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "worker_timeout_s"
        })
      }), " (default 10 s): how long a silent worker stays in the\npool. Any message from the worker counts, not only the heartbeat. Keep it at\nleast twice the worker's ", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "worker_api_endpoint.timeout"
      }), "; a worker evicted\nmid-action has every action it held re-queued, each with an attempt spent."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "max_action_executing_timeout_s"
        })
      }), " (default 0, disabled): caps how long an\naction can sit in ", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "Executing"
      }), " with no update, even from a worker that is\notherwise healthy. This is the setting that catches a worker stuck on one\nspecific action rather than dead. Without it, such an action hangs until a\nclient gives up."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "max_job_retries"
        })
      }), " (default 3): how many times an action that fails with\nan internal error is retried before the last error is returned to the client.\nIt exists to stop one poisonous action from cycling through and destabilising\nyour whole fleet. A lost worker and a memory escalation do not spend it."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "max_worker_loss_retries"
        })
      }), " (default 10): how many times an action whose\nworker was lost (disconnected, timed out, evicted, or OOM-killed as a whole)\nis queued again before the client gets a ", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "FAILED_PRECONDITION"
      }), ". Losing a\nworker is not the action's failure, so this budget is separate and larger;\nit only stops an action that takes a worker down every time it runs."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "retain_completed_for_s"
        })
      }), " (default 60 s): how long a completed action's\nresult stays available for a late ", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "WaitExecution"
      }), " call. Clients that\ndisconnect and reconnect need this window to be longer than their reconnect\ntime."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: ["On the worker side, ", _jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "max_inflight_tasks"
        })
      }), " (default 0, meaning unlimited) is\nthe one to set on any real machine. Unlimited means the worker accepts every\naction the scheduler offers and lets the OS sort out the contention."]
    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: [_jsx(_components.strong, {
        children: _jsx(_components.code, {
          children: "drain_on_shutdown"
        })
      }), " (off by default) makes a worker announce that it is\ndraining the moment it gets ", _jsx(_components.code, {
        children: "SIGTERM"
