export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who wants several Forge processes to share one\nlow-latency store, or who needs the scheduler's pub/sub subscriptions.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a Redis-backed store with concurrency and\ntimeouts set so it survives a burst. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you can edit (see Configuration), and a reachable Redis instance."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Redis sits between the filesystem and object storage: it is shared across\nmachines like a bucket, and roughly a millisecond away like a local disk. What\nit is not is cheap per byte. Use it for small, hot, shared objects (the Action\nCache, scheduler state, the index side of a `dedup` store) and put the bulk\nCAS somewhere that prices storage by the gigabyte."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "`addresses` is the only required field. The URL carries everything Redis needs:\nscheme, optional credentials, host, port and database index.\n`redis://username:password@redis-server-url:6380/99` selects port 6380 and\ndatabase 99. Keep credentials out of the file by writing\n`redis://${REDIS_USER}:${REDIS_PASSWORD}@redis:6379/` and exporting the\nvariables; see The config file."
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "`mode` takes `standard` (the default), `sentinel` or `cluster`."
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "Mode"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "What goes in `addresses`"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "`standard`"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "Exactly one server URL"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "`sentinel`"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "Exactly one sentinel URL, not a data node; the master name comes from a `?sentinelServiceName=` query parameter (default `master`), and a `redis+sentinel://` scheme is accepted"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "`cluster`"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "Any subset of the cluster's nodes; the client discovers the rest"
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "The store connects to the sentinel, asks it for the current master, and\nre-resolves the master when a write hits a demoted replica or the connection\ndrops. Passing more than one address in `standard` or `sentinel` mode is\nrejected at startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "standalone-sentinel-or-cluster",
    "content": "`key_prefix` namespaces one Redis instance across several stores, and matters\nmore here than with object storage: a Redis instance is much more likely to be\nshared with something else you run. Give the CAS, the AC and the scheduler\ndistinct prefixes."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Redis is single-threaded per node, so the settings that matter are the ones\nbounding how much work you can have in flight."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "What it decides"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`max_client_permits`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "500"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Ceiling on concurrent actions against the store"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`connection_pool_size`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "3"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Accepted but not used by the current client, which holds one multiplexed connection"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`read_chunk_size`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "64 KiB"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Bytes pulled per chunk when streaming a blob out"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`max_chunk_uploads_per_update`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "10"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Concurrent chunk uploads within one write"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`command_timeout_ms`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "10000"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Per-command ceiling"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`connection_timeout_ms`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "3000"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Ceiling on establishing a connection"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`health_check_timeout_ms`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "4000"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Ceiling on the health-check `PING`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`scan_count`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "10000"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`COUNT` hint per `SCAN` iteration"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`key_ttl_s`"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "0 (never)"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Expiry set on every key this store writes; must exceed the longest upload"
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`max_client_permits` is the most useful lever. It exists precisely to stop\ntimeouts caused by many, many inflight actions: when the ceiling is reached,\nwork queues instead of piling onto Redis and blowing through\n`command_timeout_ms`. Lower it if you see timeouts under load; that is almost\nalways the right first move."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "Peak memory in the client process is roughly\n`read_chunk_size × max_chunk_uploads_per_update × concurrent uploads`. Working\nbackwards from available memory, divide by about ten to leave room for\neverything else the process is doing."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "A larger chunk means a single command moves more data, so a chunk that is too\nlarge will exceed `command_timeout_ms` on a busy or distant Redis and fail the\nwhole operation. If large blobs are timing out, lower the chunk size or raise\nthe timeout, don't raise the chunk size."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`max_count_per_cursor` (default 1500) bounds how many entries one cursor\nreturns; it exists to reduce thundering-herd effects when many workers hit the\nprovisioner at once."
  }, {
    "heading": "sizing-it-so-it-doesnt-time-out",
    "content": "`response_timeout_s` is superseded by `command_timeout_ms` and is ignored\nwith a warning; `connection_timeout_s` is superseded by\n`connection_timeout_ms` and is still honoured (converted to milliseconds)\nif the millisecond field is unset, but setting both is an error.\n`broadcast_channel_capacity` is unused by the current client.\n`experimental_pub_sub_channel` remains, and is what scheduler\nsubscriptions ride on."
  }, {
    "heading": "where-redis-actually-earns-its-place",
    "content": "Rather than as a bulk CAS, Redis is usually the fast or index half of a\ncomposition:"
  }, {
    "heading": "where-redis-actually-earns-its-place",
    "content": "The index is small, hot and shared by every process; the content is large, cold\nand cheap per byte. Compose stores covers the\nrest of these shapes."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Provision Redis** with enough memory for what you intend to keep, and decide\nits eviction policy on the Redis side, because Forge does not manage it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the store** with `addresses`, the right `mode`, and a `key_prefix` that\nwon't collide with anything else using that instance."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Reference credentials through the environment** rather than writing them into\nthe URL in the config file."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Run a build**, then run it again from a second machine pointed at the same\nRedis, to confirm the store really is shared."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Load-test it** and watch for `command_timeout_ms` errors. If you see them,\nlower `max_client_permits` before touching anything else."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Keys appear under `key_prefix`: `SCAN` with a `MATCH` on the prefix returns\nentries while a build runs."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A second machine pointed at the same Redis gets cache hits for objects the\nfirst one uploaded."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "No command timeouts in the logs under your normal peak load."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Redis memory use plateaus where its own eviction policy says it should,\nrather than climbing to the instance limit."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Too much in flight. Lower `max_client_permits` so work queues in Forge\ninstead of piling onto a single-threaded Redis. Only after that, consider\nraising `command_timeout_ms`."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`read_chunk_size` is too large for the round-trip time to this Redis: one\ncommand is moving more data than fits in `command_timeout_ms`. Lower the chunk\nsize."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Forge does not evict from Redis; that's the instance's `maxmemory` and\n`maxmemory-policy`. The one knob on this side is `key_ttl_s`, which puts an\nexpiry on every key the store writes (meant for stores whose consumer can\nstop, not for scheduler state). Set the Redis-side limits, and reconsider\nwhether the bulk CAS should live here at all. For large blobs, a filesystem or object store tier is both\ncheaper and better suited."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check `mode`. For `sentinel`, `addresses` must hold exactly one sentinel URL,\nnot a data node, and the master name must match your sentinel configuration\n(`?sentinelServiceName=mymaster`; the default is `master`); pointing at a\nreplica directly gives read-only errors on write. For `cluster`, any subset of\nnodes works and the client discovers the rest."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "They share a `key_prefix`, or neither sets one. Give each deployment its own,\nor its own database index in the URL."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Where a Redis store belongs inside a larger composition, usually as the\nindex or fast half, not the bulk store."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Redis also backs scheduler state when more than one scheduler shares a\nqueue."
  }],
