export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Upgrade to a newer version",
  "description": "Read the changelog for the things that actually break, upgrade the three process types in the right order, and know what happens to the cache you already have."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running a pinned version who wants a newer one.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a rehearsed upgrade, a rollback that works, and\nyour existing cache intact. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes for the rehearsal, minutes\nfor the upgrade itself."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running deployment with a pinned version."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge releases every week or two. Most releases change nothing you depend on,\nwhich makes it tempting to upgrade casually and equally tempting to never\nupgrade at all. Both go wrong in the same way: the thing that breaks an upgrade\nis almost never the code, it is the config."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Nearly every block of the config is parsed with `deny_unknown_fields`. A field\nthat was renamed or removed in the new version is not ignored: it is a startup\nfailure with a message naming the field (renamed fields sometimes keep the old\nname as a serde alias, in which case both spellings parse). That is the\nbehaviour you want, because the alternative is a silently ignored setting and a\ndeployment that is quietly not doing what the file says. But it means an\nupgrade is a config compatibility exercise first and a binary swap second."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-stable-and-what-isnt",
    "content": "The **client-facing API** is the Remote Execution API. It is a published,\nversioned protocol, and Bazel talking to Forge over `:50051` and `:50052`\nis the most stable surface here. You do not generally need to upgrade clients in\nstep with the server."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-stable-and-what-isnt",
    "content": "The **config schema** is the surface that moves. Fields get added constantly,\nwhich is harmless; occasionally one is renamed or removed, which is not."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-stable-and-what-isnt",
    "content": "The **worker API** between a worker and the scheduler is internal. Nothing\npromises that a worker from one release talks to a scheduler from another, so\ntreat scheduler and workers as a unit that upgrades together."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-stable-and-what-isnt",
    "content": "Stored **data** is content-addressed, which does most of the work for you: a\nblob's key is its digest, so bytes written by an old version are found by a new\none. What is not automatically portable is the on-disk or in-Redis *layout* a\nstore uses to organize those bytes. Layout changes are rare (the filesystem\nstore still migrates its old single-folder layout in place at startup), and they\nare one of the things to look for when you read the changelog."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "`CHANGELOG.md` is generated per release, grouped into features, bug fixes,\ndocumentation, testing and CI, miscellaneous, and bumps and version updates,\nwith a `compare/v1.6.4..v1.6.5` link in each release heading that shows every\ncommit between the two."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "Skimming the whole thing is not the point. Read it for:"
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "Renamed, removed or newly-required **config fields**: these are the ones that\nstop your process from starting."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "Changes to **default values**, which do not fail loudly and are the reason to\nset explicitly anything you actually care about."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "**Store layout or serialization** changes, which determine whether existing\ncached data is still reachable."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "Anything about the **worker protocol**, which decides whether you can upgrade\nworkers and scheduler independently."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "Skip several versions and you must read every intervening release's entries, not\njust the newest, because the breaking change may be three releases back."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-changelog-for-four-things",
    "content": "The configuration reference is published per\nrelease. Open the page for the version you are moving to and diff it against\nthe one you are on. It is the fastest way to see field-level changes without\nreading commits."
  }, {
    "heading": "rehearse-the-config-before-you-touch-production",
    "content": "No `--validate` flag exists. The binary takes one positional argument, the\nconfig file (plus the usual `--help` and `--version`), and parses it at\nstartup, which is exactly what you want, because the check is just \"does the\nnew binary accept this file\":"
  }, {
    "heading": "rehearse-the-config-before-you-touch-production",
    "content": "A config error fails within a second, before any port is bound, with a message\nnaming the offending field. If instead it starts up and begins listening, the\nconfig parsed and you can stop the container."
  }, {
    "heading": "rehearse-the-config-before-you-touch-production",
    "content": "Run this for every config file you have (the CAS, the scheduler, and each\ndistinct worker) against the version you are moving to. A parse failure here is\na five-minute fix. The same failure during a rolling restart is an outage."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "**Pin, don't float.** If anything in your deployment says `:latest`, fix that\nfirst. You cannot control an upgrade you did not choose, and you cannot roll\nback to a tag that means something different now."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "**Rehearse every config file** against the new image as above, and fix whatever\nfails."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "**Upgrade the CAS servers first.** They hold the data, they serve the most\ntraffic, and they are the piece other processes depend on. A filesystem store\nrescans its `content_path` at startup to rebuild its index, so a restart with a\nlarge cache is not instant. Expect a pause proportional to the number of\nobjects, and do not interpret it as a hang."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "**Upgrade the scheduler and the workers together.** The worker API between them\nis internal. With a `simple` scheduler the queue is in memory, so restarting it\ndrops queued actions. Clients retry, but do it when a retry storm is cheap."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "**Run a real build**, then run it again. The first proves the new version works;\nthe second proves it can still read what the old version wrote, which is the\nquestion that actually matters."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "Every process is running the intended tag, and you can prove it rather than\nassume it."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "A build run before the upgrade gets cache hits after it. This is the\ndata-compatibility check."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "Remote execution actions complete on upgraded workers."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "Nothing in any process log mentions an unknown or deprecated config field."
  }, {
    "heading": "upgrade-in-the-right-order",
    "content": "The previous image tag plus the previous config file are still somewhere you\ncan reach in under a minute."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-back",
    "content": "Rollback is the same operation in reverse, with one asymmetry worth internalizing."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-back",
    "content": "Config is not backward compatible in the direction you need it to be. If you\nadded a field that only exists in the new version, the old binary rejects that\nfile, because `deny_unknown_fields` cuts both ways. &#x2A;*Roll back the config and the\nimage together, as a pair.** Keeping the two versions of each config file next\nto each other for a release or two costs nothing and turns a rollback into a\nsingle decision."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-back",
    "content": "Data written by the newer version stays readable by the older one in the normal\ncase, because content addressing does not change. If the changelog mentioned a\nstore layout change, that is the case where it does not, and it is also the case\nwhere rolling back means accepting a cold cache. Reading for that in advance is\nwhat makes the difference between a rollback and an incident."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Exactly the failure this design intends. The message names the field. Find it in\nthe new version's config reference. Usually it was renamed, occasionally it\nmoved under a different parent, rarely it is gone because the feature it\ncontrolled is. Fix the file, do not try to make the parser lenient."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The new version is not finding the old data. Check first that `content_path`,\nthe bucket, or the Redis instance is genuinely the same one. A changed volume\nmount or a changed key prefix looks exactly like a format incompatibility. If\nthe paths are identical, re-read the changelog for the intervening releases with\nstore layout in mind."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Version skew across the worker API. Bring the workers to the same version. This\nis why the two upgrade together rather than independently."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "A filesystem store walks `content_path` at startup to rebuild its in-memory\nindex, and that scales with object count. It is not stuck. If the pause is long\nenough to matter operationally, that is a signal about cache size rather than\nabout the upgrade, and Tuning has the storage levers."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Being several releases behind is not itself dangerous. The risk is that the\nupgrade becomes several config migrations at once with no way to tell which one\nbroke. If you are many versions back, step through in two or three hops and run\na build at each, rather than making one jump you cannot bisect."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Usually no. Bazel and Forge talk the Remote Execution API, which is stable\nacross these releases. Client upgrades matter when you want a client-side\nfeature, not because the server moved."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The per-version field reference, which is what you diff when a release note is\nambiguous."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The settings worth pinning explicitly so a changed default never surprises you."
  }],
  "headings": [{
