import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Persistent workers",
  "description": "Keep a JVM or Node compiler process warm across actions instead of paying its startup cost every time, and understand exactly what Forge gives up to do it."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone whose remote builds are dominated by JVM or\nTypeScript compile actions. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** compile actions\nreusing a warm process, and a clear-eyed view of the isolation you trade for it.\n&#x2A;*Time:** an hour, most of it in your build rules rather than in Forge."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Remote execution working end to end. See Your first remote action."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A `javac` invocation spends a large fraction of its wall time before it compiles\nanything: start a JVM, load the compiler, warm the JIT. Locally you amortize\nthat across a build because Bazel keeps the process alive. Under\nremote execution the default is one process per action, so\nevery single compile pays the full tax, and a thousand-target Java build pays it\na thousand times."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Persistent workers remove that. The tool is started once, told it is a\npersistent worker, and then fed one request per action over stdin while staying\nalive between them."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-to-know-before-you-start",
    "content": "**Little needs configuring in Forge.** The pool is on by default and\nthe protocol is chosen by two platform properties on each action. The\nworker's `persistent_workers` block (v1.7.3 and later) sizes it: how many\nprocesses per key, how long an idle one lives, how many requests one serves,\nhow long an action at the cap waits for a process to come back, and\n`enabled: false` to run every such action one-shot.\nThe one thing Forge does need from you is on the scheduler: those two\nkeys must be listed in `supported_platform_properties`, because a scheduler\nolder than v1.6.6 rejects an action that carries a key it does not know."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-to-know-before-you-start",
    "content": "**The module is licensed separately.** Most of the repository is\n`FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0`. Every file under `forge-worker/src/persistent_worker/`\ninstead carries a Business Source License 1.1 header stating that use of the\nmodule requires an enterprise license agreement. That is a different obligation\nfrom the rest of the binary, and nothing in the binary enforces it; the module\nis compiled in and activates from the action, not from a flag you set. Read\nOpen source and Enterprise\nfor what that means in practice, and\nthe license against your intended use, before\nyou build on this."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "The worker looks at two of the action's platform properties (the REAPI\n`Platform` on the `Action`): `supports-workers` must be exactly `1`, and\n`requires-worker-protocol` picks the wire format. Nothing on the worker's own\n`platform_properties` is involved."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "Because the scheduler fails to match any action carrying a platform property\nkey it has not been told about (`Unknown platform property`), declare both keys\non the scheduler. `ignore` lets actions request a key without any worker having\nto advertise it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "Then put the two keys on the action. The rule sketches in\n`deployment-examples/persistent-workers/` (Java, Kotlin and TypeScript) show\nthem as `execution_requirements`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "Kotlin is identical in shape. TypeScript wrappers conventionally use the JSON\nframing instead:"
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "The worker reads the `Platform` proto, not Bazel's execution info. Bazel\nbuilds the remote `Platform` from `exec_properties` (on the target or the\nexecution platform) and `--remote_default_exec_properties`; it does not copy\n`execution_requirements` into it. If your actions never take the persistent\npath, set the same two keys as `exec_properties` on the target, or pass\n`--remote_default_exec_properties=supports-workers=1` for a whole build, and\ncheck the worker log for `Spawned new persistent worker`."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "Both wire formats are fully implemented. `proto` is length-delimited protobuf;\n`json` is newline-delimited JSON with camelCase field names, where a request's\ninput digests are base64. `proto` is the default when the property is absent.\nAny other value is logged and the action falls back to ordinary one-shot\nexecution."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-an-action-in",
    "content": "The decision is made on the worker, per action, at the moment it is about to\nexecute. The scheduler only needs to know the keys exist; it does not treat\nthese actions differently."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "Actions are pooled by a key derived from the command line:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "The **executable**: the first argument."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "The **startup arguments**: every argument after the executable &#x2A;up to the\nfirst one beginning with `@`*, which is Bazel's response-file convention.\nEverything from that `@` onward is per-request payload, sent in the\n`WorkRequest` rather than used for pooling."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "The **wire format** from `requires-worker-protocol`."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "Two actions with the same three share a process; anything else gets its own.\nThis is why the argfile convention matters more than it looks: an action with\n**no** `@argfile` puts its entire command line into the key, so every action\nwith different arguments becomes its own worker and you get no reuse at all\nwhile everything still appears to work."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-two-actions-share-a-process",
    "content": "If the worker has an `entrypoint` configured, it is prepended to the command\nbefore the key is derived, so the executable in the key is the entrypoint,\nnot your tool, and the tool name becomes a startup argument. It still pools\ncorrectly, but the key is not what you would predict from the rule."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "This is the part to read twice. A persistent worker process outlives the\nactions it serves, so some of the isolation an ordinary action gets cannot\napply to it, and some of it did not apply until v1.7.3."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "**Process namespaces, but not the mount namespace.** With `use_namespaces`\nthe worker process gets its own PID, user, IPC and UTS namespaces, as an\nordinary action does, so its children are reaped with it. It never gets a\nmount namespace or the private `/tmp` (`isolate_tmp`): both are per action,\nand the process is not. Persistent workers see the host's `/tmp` and the\nwhole of the worker's filesystem, whatever `use_mount_namespace` says."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "**The action's environment, and only that.** The process is started with a\ncleared environment plus the command's `environment_variables`, the way an\nordinary action is, and that environment is part of the worker's key, so two\nactions that differ in it never share a process. `additional_environment` on\nthe config, the side-channel sources and the per-action `TMPDIR` are not\napplied: they are per action, and the process is not."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "**A shared, long-lived working directory.** The process's own working directory\nis the worker's root action directory, not the per-action one; it has to be,\nbecause per-action directories are deleted when their action finishes. The\nper-action directory is instead handed to the tool as `sandbox_dir` on each\nrequest, and **it is the tool's job to honor it**. A worker that writes relative\nto its own cwd will scribble into a directory shared with every other action it\never serves."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "**No input digests.** The request carries an empty input list, so a tool cannot\nuse it to validate or invalidate its own cached state. Anything the tool caches\nacross requests, it caches on trust."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "**Measured, not enforced.** What the process and its children use while they\nserve a request is sampled and reported as the action's resource usage, so the\nsizing loop learns what the tool needs. `resource_enforcement` never kills a\npersistent worker for one request's reservation: the process is shared, and a\nkill would take every action queued behind it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-isolation-you-give-up",
    "content": "The consequence is a real correctness requirement, not a footnote: &#x2A;*a\npersistent worker must be hermetic by construction, because nothing is enforcing\nit.** State that leaks between requests produces wrong outputs that are then\ncached as if they were right, which is the worst failure mode a build system\nhas. Only enable this for tools written for the worker protocol and tested under\nit."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "The pool is configured under `persistent_workers` on the worker; every value\nbelow is its default."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**Four workers per key.** A fifth concurrent action for the same key waits\nup to `acquire_timeout_s` for one of the four to come back, as Bazel's own\n`worker_max_instances` queue does; only if none does in that time does it\nfall back to normal one-shot execution, and it logs that it did. Your build\nstays correct and gets slower by a tool startup per fallback; the worker's\n`persistent_worker_fallbacks` counter says how often."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**Two hundred requests per worker.** After that the process is retired and the\nnext action starts a fresh one. This is deliberate recycling of accumulated JVM\nstate, and it means a long build will restart workers periodically by design."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**Five minutes idle.** A sweeper runs on a timer and shuts down a worker\nthat has served nothing for `idle_timeout_s`, so a pool of JVMs left over from\none build does not hold the worker's memory through the next."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**Dead workers are noticed on the next acquire**, not proactively. A JVM that\nOOMs while idle is discovered when the next action asks for it, and that action\nstarts a replacement."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**Shutdown is not graceful.** Stdin is closed, giving the tool `shutdown_grace_ms`\nto exit on EOF, and then it is killed outright. No `SIGTERM` is sent."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**One request at a time per process.** Multiplexed workers are not supported; a\nrequest carrying a non-zero request ID is rejected."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pools-actual-behaviour",
    "content": "**`enabled: false` turns it off.** Every action then runs one-shot, whatever it\nasks for."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Confirm the tool is actually a worker.** It must accept `--persistent_worker`,\nread framed `WorkRequest` messages from stdin, and write framed `WorkResponse`\nmessages to stdout. Most JVM compilers have a worker wrapper already; a plain\ncompiler binary does not become one by being declared as one."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Declare the execution requirements** on the action, with an `@argfile` holding\neverything that varies per action so that the pooling key stays stable."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Audit the tool for cross-request state.** Anything cached in memory between\nrequests must be keyed on inputs the request actually carries. Anything written\nto disk must go under `sandbox_dir`."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Run a build and read the worker's log.** You are looking for one process\nstart serving many actions, not one start per action."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Compare compile durations** against the same build with the execution\nrequirements removed. The first action of each key should be unchanged; the\nrest should drop sharply."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The action-duration distribution for that mnemonic becomes bimodal: a few slow\nfirst-of-key actions, the rest much faster."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Process count on the worker host stays flat during a build instead of churning\nonce per action."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Building the same targets twice in a row, cache disabled, is faster the second\ntime within a single build: the pool is warm."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Nothing in the Forge worker log says it fell back to one-shot execution."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Outputs are byte-identical to the same build without persistent workers. This\nis the one that matters."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Either the properties are not reaching the action's `Platform`, or every action\nis getting its own key. Check `supports-workers` is exactly `\"1\"` and is sent as\na platform property (see the callout above), and that the scheduler declares\nboth keys; an undeclared key leaves the action queued forever. Then check the\ncommand line has an `@argfile`: without one, every distinct command line is a\ndistinct key, and you get a warm process per action, which is no process reuse\nat all."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Stop and disable persistent workers for that mnemonic. This is the signature of\nstate leaking between requests: a cache in the tool keyed on something the\nrequest no longer determines, or output written relative to the process's cwd\ninstead of `sandbox_dir`. Wrong outputs get cached, so also consider what needs\ninvalidating."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "More than `max_workers_per_key` concurrent actions for one key (four by\ndefault) wait up to `acquire_timeout_s` for a process to come back and then\nfall back to one-shot execution, and one-shot execution of a worker-wrapped\ntool can be slower than the plain tool. Raise the cap, or reduce concurrency\nfor that mnemonic. The CPU time reported for a pooled action is what the\nprocess spent on it; its peak memory is the whole process, including what\nearlier requests left resident."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "It is resolving paths relative to its own working directory, which is the\nworker's root directory rather than the action's. Every path must be resolved\nagainst the `sandbox_dir` the request carries. Tools written for local Bazel\nworkers usually get this right; wrappers written in-house usually do not, at\nfirst."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The property must be exactly `proto` or `json`. Anything else is rejected and\nthe action falls back to one-shot execution."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "A persistent worker's stderr is discarded, because it belongs to the process\nrather than to any one action and cannot be attributed safely. What surfaces as\nthe action's stderr is the `output` field of the `WorkResponse`, and the\naction's stdout is always empty. If a tool reports errors on its real stderr\ninstead of in the response, those errors are invisible; that is a bug in the\nworker wrapper."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Warm JVMs are the point and they are not free: up to four live processes per\ndistinct key, each holding a heap. The 200-request cap recycles them eventually,\nbut the steady state is higher than one-shot execution. Size worker hosts for\nthe pool, not for one compiler."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Worker-host sizing and the levers that matter once a pool of warm processes is\npart of your steady state."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Why hermeticity is the property everything else rests on, and what it means\nthat persistent workers rely on the tool to preserve it."
  }],
