import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Production configuration",
  "description": "The shape a real Forge cluster runs in: three processes, a split port surface, and what to turn on before other people depend on it."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** you have Forge working and now other people are going\nto depend on it. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a three-process deployment\nwhose ports, state, and failure behaviour you can explain to someone else.\n&#x2A;*Time:** an afternoon, plus however long your change process takes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A working deployment that only you depend on."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A production configuration is not the quickstart configuration with bigger\nnumbers. It differs in one structural way: &#x2A;*the CAS, the scheduler, and the\nworkers become three separate processes.** Everything else on this page (the\nport split, the store composition, the hardening checklist) follows from that\none decision."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Every config on this page is adapted (paths and hostnames changed) from\n,\nwhich CI builds and runs end to end on every pull request: `run_integration_tests.sh`\nbrings the three processes up with `docker compose` and then drives real Bazel\nbuilds through them."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape",
    "content": "Three facts about that picture are the whole design:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape",
    "content": "**The scheduler owns no storage.** It reaches the CAS through `grpc` stores, the\nsame way a client would. That is why you can restart it without losing blobs, and\nwhy it needs no volume."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape",
    "content": "**The worker also owns no durable storage.** Its local disk is a read cache in\nfront of the CAS, not a source of truth. A worker can be deleted mid-action; the\nscheduler retries the action elsewhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape",
    "content": "**Only the CAS process is stateful.** It is the one thing you back up, size for\ngrowth, and think carefully about before restarting."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-1--the-cas-and-action-cache",
    "content": "This is the process that holds bytes. Two stores, one for content and one for\naction results, each with its own eviction budget:"
  }, {
    "heading": "process-1--the-cas-and-action-cache",
    "content": "`stores` is an **array of named stores**, not an object map; every store carries\nits own `name`, and other parts of the config refer to it by that name.\n`filesystem` requires both `content_path` and `temp_path`; the config parser uses\n`deny_unknown_fields`, so a typo or an invented key is a startup failure rather\nthan a silently ignored line."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-1--the-cas-and-action-cache",
    "content": "Each entry in `cas`, `ac`, `bytestream`, and `capabilities` carries an optional\n`instance_name` (default: the empty string, which is also what Bazel sends by\ndefault). If you set a non-empty one, every client has to pass it too\n(`--remote_instance_name=` for Bazel). An empty `capabilities: []` still answers\n`GetCapabilities` with cache capabilities for any instance name; an entry with\n`remote_execution` is only needed to advertise remote execution."
  }, {
    "heading": "moving-off-local-disk",
    "content": "`filesystem` is the right terminal store for a single CAS node with a real disk.\nFor a CAS you intend to replicate, put an object store underneath instead and keep\nthe local disk as the fast tier:"
  }, {
    "heading": "moving-off-local-disk",
    "content": "The store key is `experimental_cloud_object_store` with a `provider` discriminator:\n`aws`, `gcs`, `azure`, `ontap`, `r2`, or `oci`. No `s3_store` key exists."
  }, {
    "heading": "moving-off-local-disk",
    "content": "`fast_slow` never verifies that a blob present in `fast` also exists in `slow`.\nIf the fast tier is wiped, anything that only ever landed there is gone. Size\nthe fast tier as a cache of the working set and treat the slow tier as the\nthing you can lose data from."
  }, {
    "heading": "moving-off-local-disk",
    "content": "The full backend list and the tradeoffs between them are in\nStorage backends and\nStore overview."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-2--the-scheduler",
    "content": "The scheduler holds the queue and matches actions to workers. It reaches the CAS\nover gRPC rather than owning a store:"
  }, {
    "heading": "process-2--the-scheduler",
    "content": "`schedulers`, like `stores`, is an **array of named entries**."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-2--the-scheduler",
    "content": "`supported_platform_properties` is the matching contract between this scheduler\nand its workers. Each value is a match rule (`exact`, `minimum`, `priority`, or\n`ignore`). A worker that advertises a property the scheduler does not list is\nrejected at registration (`Unknown platform property`), and an action that\nrequests one is rejected the same way. Getting this out of sync with the\nworkers is the single most common reason actions sit in the queue forever; see\nplatform properties for how the two\nhalves line up."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-2--the-scheduler",
    "content": "The default scheduler keeps its queue in process memory. Restarting it loses the\nin-flight queue; clients retry, but work in progress is repeated. No\nmechanism reconstructs the queue from the Action Cache. If you need the\nqueue to survive a restart, the scheduler has an experimental Redis backend\n(`experimental_backend: { redis: { redis_store: \"...\" } }`). The only example\nof it in this repository is\n`forge-config/examples/worker_with_redis_scheduler.json5`; there is no\nCompose or Kubernetes deployment of it, so treat it as something to validate\nyourself rather than something to copy."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-3--the-workers",
    "content": "Workers are the fleet you scale. Note `servers: []`: a worker serves nothing and\nneeds no inbound port at all. It dials out to the scheduler and the CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-3--the-workers",
    "content": "`worker_api_endpoint`, `cas_fast_slow_store`, `work_directory`, and\n`platform_properties` are all required. `upload_action_result` takes `ac_store`\ndirectly; it is not nested twice."
  }, {
    "heading": "process-3--the-workers",
    "content": "`work_directory` is where actions are staged and executed. It sees every input\nfile and every output file of every action, so put it on the fastest local disk\nthe machine has, and size it for the largest action tree you run rather than the\naverage one."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-ports-are-public",
    "content": "Port"
  }, {
