    "heading": "which-ports-are-public",
    "content": "`worker_api`, `health`"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-ports-are-public",
    "content": "**Workers only**"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-ports-are-public",
    "content": "`worker_api` is how a process claims to be a worker and receives commands to\nrun. It is on its own listener specifically so that it can be on its own\nnetwork policy. Anything that can reach `:50061` can register as a worker,\nreceive your actions, and return whatever outputs it likes, which then land in\nyour Action Cache and get served to everyone. Keep it inside the cluster.\nSecurity hardening\ncovers the full consequence and what to do about it."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-ports-are-public",
    "content": "`health` is served alongside `worker_api` in the reference config. It is the only\nreadiness signal the binary exposes; there is no metrics port to probe."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Thing"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Lose it and…"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "So"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "CAS content store"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Builds recompile from source until it warms back up"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Back it up or accept the warm-up; this is the one stateful component"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Action Cache"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Same, but cheaper; results re-derive from the CAS"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Size it small; it is result metadata, not outputs"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Scheduler queue"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "In-flight actions are re-run by clients"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Restart freely, off-peak"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Worker local disk"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Worker re-fetches inputs from the CAS"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Delete workers at will; this is the property that makes autoscaling safe"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "`work_directory`"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Nothing; it is scratch space"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "Never put it on network storage"
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-durable-and-whats-disposable",
    "content": "The rule this table encodes: &#x2A;*exactly one component is stateful.** If you find\nyourself needing to protect a scheduler's disk or a worker's disk, something has\ndrifted from this shape."
  }, {
    "heading": "raise-the-file-descriptor-limit",
    "content": "Every open blob is a file descriptor. The binary gates file opens on a semaphore\nsized to 80% of the limit it actually achieved, so a CAS at the default limit\ndoes not error under load; it waits."
  }, {
    "heading": "raise-the-file-descriptor-limit",
    "content": "`global` accepts exactly three keys: `max_open_files` (required whenever the\n`global` block is present), `default_digest_hash_function`, and\n`default_digest_size_health_check`. It has no `metrics` or `tracing` section;\ntelemetry is configured by environment variable, below."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-namespaces-on-linux-workers",
    "content": "Bazel in particular can leave zombie processes behind on a worker. Two opt-in\nflags contain that:"
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-namespaces-on-linux-workers",
    "content": "`use_namespaces` puts each action in its own process namespace;\n`use_mount_namespace` additionally isolates the worker root in a new mount\nnamespace and gives each action a private `/tmp` (its own temporary directory,\nbound over `/tmp` and removed with the action), and only works when\n`use_namespaces` is on. Both are off by default and both are recommended in\nproduction. Set `isolate_tmp: false` if actions must see the host's `/tmp`,\nfor example because a tool they run lives there. The container\nrunning the worker needs the privileges to create namespaces; the reference\nCompose stack marks the executor service `privileged: true` for exactly this."
  }, {
    "heading": "put-tls-on-the-client-facing-listeners",
    "content": "Setting `client_ca_file` makes the listener require a client certificate signed by\nthat CA, and `client_crl_file` lets you revoke one. That is the whole\nauthorization model on the listener: &#x2A;*any client with a valid, unrevoked\ncertificate gets full access to that listener's services.** No per-identity\nallow-list exists and no mapping from certificate subject to permissions. If you need\nfiner-grained access than \"trusted or not,\" put a proxy in front."
  }, {
    "heading": "put-tls-on-the-client-facing-listeners",
    "content": "The certificates checked into `deployment-examples/` are self-signed and named\n`do-not-use-in-prod` for a reason. Generate your own."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-telemetry-somewhere",
    "content": "The config file has no metrics section, and the binary has no Prometheus scrape endpoint\nin the binary. Telemetry is OTLP over gRPC, configured by environment:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-telemetry-somewhere",
    "content": "Prometheus sees Forge through an OpenTelemetry Collector that exports to it,\nor through Prometheus' own OTLP receiver. Observability\nhas the collector config and the Grafana dashboard."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-in-to-cache-metrics-if-you-want-them",
    "content": "Execution metrics are emitted unconditionally. **Cache metrics are not**: a store\nonly reports hits, misses, and errors on its reads, writes, and deletes if you\nwrap it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-in-to-cache-metrics-if-you-want-them",
    "content": "Without the wrapper there are no `forge_cache_*` series at all, and any\ndashboard panel or alert built on them stays empty forever. The wrapper is opt-in\nbecause it costs hot-path timing on every operation."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-in-to-cache-metrics-if-you-want-them",
    "content": "Each process starts and stays up: `forge /etc/forge/cas.json5` exits\nnon-zero immediately on a config error rather than starting degraded, so a\nprocess that is still running has a config the parser fully accepted."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-in-to-cache-metrics-if-you-want-them",
    "content": "A build against `grpc://cas.internal:50051` gets cache hits on a second run, and\na build against `grpc://scheduler.internal:50052` reports remote execution rather\nthan falling back to local."
  }, {
    "heading": "opt-in-to-cache-metrics-if-you-want-them",
    "content": "`:50061` is refused from outside the cluster."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "Four signals cover most of what goes wrong. All four are recording rules in\n:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "`forge:queue_depth`: actions waiting, by priority. Rising and staying\nrisen means you need workers; spiking and draining is normal."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "`forge:worker_utilization`: the fraction of workers actually executing.\nLow utilization with a deep queue means a matching problem, not a capacity\nproblem."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "`forge:execution_queue_time_p95`: how long an action waits before a\nworker picks it up. This is the number your users experience as \"the build farm\nis slow.\""
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "`forge:cache_hit_rate`: requires the `cache_metrics` wrapper above."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-watch",
    "content": "Observability covers the pipeline that produces these."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-tune",
    "content": "Once the shape is right, Tuning is the table of levers: the\nsignal you observe, the knob that moves it, and what it costs you."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-do-when-it-breaks",
    "content": "Troubleshooting indexes symptoms to causes, and\nRunbooks has the procedure for each of the four incidents\nthis shape actually produces."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "They are lost from the scheduler's point of view. The default scheduler holds\nits queue in process memory and nothing reconstructs it on startup. Clients\nre-submit, so no build fails, but work in progress is repeated. Restart\nschedulers when the queue is shallow."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Advertise the property on the workers that have the hardware\n(`gpu: { values: [\"nvidia-a100\"] }`) and declare it as `\"exact\"` in the\nscheduler's `supported_platform_properties`. An action requesting that value\nis then only ever dispatched to a matching worker. Both halves are required:\na worker advertising a property the scheduler does not list is rejected at\nregistration."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. It never verifies that an object in `fast` also exists in `slow`. For\nartifacts that must survive a fast-tier wipe (remote execution outputs\nespecially), make sure the write path covers both tiers deliberately. More\nstore gotchas:\nStore overview."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and for a single-machine deployment you should: put the stores,\nschedulers, workers, and servers in one file and run one binary. The split\nexists so the three roles can be scaled and firewalled independently. If you\nhave one machine, you have neither problem."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The lever table: which knob moves which signal, in which direction, and what it\ncosts."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No service authenticates inbound callers. Plan the network before\nanyone else can reach it."
  }],
