  "title": "Toolchains and hermeticity",
  "description": "Your action ran on a worker and failed on a missing compiler. Here is why that happens, and how to pin the toolchain so local and remote builds hash identically."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone whose actions execute remotely but fail on the\nworker for reasons that never happen locally. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:**\na Nix-pinned toolchain that produces the same action digests on your machine\nand on a worker. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes for the walkthrough."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "An action is a closed description of some work: input digests, a command\nline, an environment, and platform properties. If the compiler your command\ninvokes isn't in that description, the action is a lie: it works on your\nmachine because your machine happens to have the compiler, and it fails on a\nworker that doesn't."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "This is not a Forge behaviour. It is what remote execution *is*: the\nprotocol will faithfully reproduce whatever you described, and no more.\nCaching lets you get away with an under-described action because the only\nmachine that ever ran it was yours. Execution doesn't."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "Two ways out exist."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "**Make the environment part of the image.** Bake the toolchain into the\nworker's container, and route actions to it with the `container-image`\nproperty. Minimal, and it's what most fleets do first.\nContainers and images covers\nthis path."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "**Make the toolchain part of the action.** Pin every tool at a\ncontent-addressed path so the toolchain itself is an input the CAS knows\nabout. This is Local Remote Execution, and it's the rest of this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "The second is strictly stronger: it gets you identical action digests\nlocally and remotely, which means a local build and a remote build share\ncache entries instead of each maintaining their own.\nWhat LRE is and why is the conceptual write-up; this\npage is the mechanism."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem",
    "content": "A worker image with your toolchain in it is a legitimate answer, and it's\nthe right one if you don't already use Nix. Read the problem statement\nabove, then decide which of the two paths you're on."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-local-remote-execution",
    "content": "Three toolchain families, and they do not behave the same way once you leave\n`x86_64-linux`."
  }, {
