    "heading": "what-ships-in-local-remote-execution",
    "content": "`x86_64-linux` only (container `forge-worker-lre-java`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-local-remote-execution",
    "content": "The flake module always emits the Rust platform and toolchain flags, and adds\nthe C++ ones only on Linux, where the generated config is `x86_64` and\ncarries a `container-image` property. On an `x86_64-linux` host that means\nC++ actions can run locally out of the same `/nix/store` paths the worker\nimage has, or remotely on that image, with the same digest either way. On\nmacOS there is no local C++ or Java toolchain at all, so those actions can\nonly execute on a remote worker running the matching image, and even a\nNix-pinned toolchain costs a network round-trip: &#x2A;*LRE gives you\nhermeticity, not offline-ness.**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-local-remote-execution",
    "content": "Reading \"local remote execution\" as \"everything runs locally\" leads to a\nconfusing first hour on macOS. What is local is the *toolchain definition*.\nWhether the action executes locally depends on which family it uses."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix-with-flakes-enabled",
    "content": "The next-gen installer\nis the shortest path. If you already have Nix, make sure\n`experimental-features = nix-command flakes` is in your `nix.conf`."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix-with-flakes-enabled",
    "content": "The pinning *is* the guarantee here: every tool lives at a\ncontent-addressed `/nix/store` path, and that path is what makes action\nhashes match across machines. No non-Nix version of this mechanism exists."
  }, {
    "heading": "pull-the-template",
    "content": "This writes a `flake.nix` importing `forge.flakeModules.lre` and\npinning `lre = { inherit (pkgs.lre.lre-cc.meta) Env; }`, plus a\n`hello-world.cpp` example, its `BUILD.bazel`, and `platforms/BUILD.bazel`."
  }, {
    "heading": "pull-the-template",
    "content": "`bazel` is the only template the flake ships;\nExamples and templates has the\nfull list and what to do if you want a different starting point."
  }, {
    "heading": "initialise-git-before-you-enter-the-shell",
    "content": "This step is not optional and it fails quietly."
  }, {
    "heading": "initialise-git-before-you-enter-the-shell",
    "content": "The flake module's install script checks for a `.git` directory before\ngenerating anything. In a plain non-git folder, `nix develop` prints this\nand carries on without writing `lre.bazelrc` (no error, no toolchain):"
  }, {
    "heading": "enter-the-dev-shell",
    "content": "This fetches the Nix-pinned toolchain and writes `lre.bazelrc` (a symlink\ninto the Nix store). On `aarch64-darwin` the generated file looks like this,\nabbreviated; your store paths will differ:"
  }, {
    "heading": "enter-the-dev-shell",
    "content": "Note that it registered only the **Rust** platform and toolchains even\nthough the flake asked for the `lre-cc` `Env`: the module emits the Rust\nlines on every system and adds the `generated-cc` platform and\n`cc-toolchain` lines only on Linux. `Env` only decides which store paths\nare listed (and therefore fetched) in the commented header. The template's\nown `.bazelrc` separately adds\n`--extra_execution_platforms=@//platforms:lre-cc` for the C++ example, which\nis the container-based platform from the table above."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-userbazelrc-at-your-cluster",
    "content": "`nix flake init` writes a `user.bazelrc` with the literal string `TODO` as\nevery value. That is what lands on disk, not a placeholder left in this page:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-userbazelrc-at-your-cluster",
    "content": "Filled in against a plain local cluster: no TLS, so `grpc://`, and no BES\nendpoint to point at:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-userbazelrc-at-your-cluster",
    "content": "A hosted or self-managed cluster is the same shape with `grpcs://` and real\nhostnames, plus a `bes_backend` line if you want build results streamed."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-userbazelrc-at-your-cluster",
    "content": "If you build before editing this file, Bazel fails on the literal hostname\n`TODO`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-userbazelrc-at-your-cluster",
    "content": "Everything before that DNS failure worked: the Nix toolchain, the platform\nwiring, and the `lre-cc` execution platform selection. `0s remote,\nremote-cache` means Bazel had already committed to executing remotely."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "With a real endpoint and a worker running the `forge-worker-lre-cc`\nimage, this compiles `src/hello-world.cpp` on that worker and returns the\nbinary through the same CAS your local Bazel reads from. Run it again and\nit's a cache hit."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "`lre.bazelrc` exists in the workspace root and contains\n`--extra_toolchains` lines."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "`bazel build` reports the action as `remote` (for `lre-cc`), or, run\nwithout `--remote_executor`, builds locally with the pinned toolchain\n(Rust on any of the four systems, C++ on `x86_64-linux`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "The **same target built on two different machines** produces a cache hit\non the second. This is the actual test of hermeticity; same-machine\nre-runs prove nothing about it."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "You can still validate the cache half.\nLocal testing starts a Forge\ninstance on `localhost`, which is enough to exercise `--remote_cache`\nagainst real credentials before you also need a worker matching\n`lre-cc`'s platform requirements."
