  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "You can get *reproducibility across a fleet* by baking the toolchain into\none worker image and routing every action to it. That's path 1 at the top\nof this page, and for many teams it's enough. What you don't get is matching\naction digests between a developer's laptop and the fleet, because the\nlaptop isn't running that image. Nix pinning is what closes that gap."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Note this is a client-side requirement only. The Forge server, the\nscheduler, and the workers run fine with no Nix anywhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Local testing validates the *protocol*\nwith whatever toolchain is on your `PATH`; it deliberately has no toolchain\nopinion, which is what makes it a good first step. This page pins the\ntoolchain so local and remote actions hash identically. Do that one first if\nyou just want to see `Execute` work."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The first `nix develop` is slow; it's fetching a full toolchain closure,\nand 15 to 20 GB of free disk is a reasonable expectation. After that it's a\nno-op. At build time, pinned toolchains are marginally *faster* to cache\nagainst, because the digest stability means fewer spurious misses."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "They compose: a workspace can register Rust, C++, and Java toolchains at\nonce, and Bazel resolves per target. The practical constraint is that only\nRust has native non-Linux configs, so on macOS your C++ and Java actions\nneed a reachable worker while your Rust actions can still run locally."
  }, {
