export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "AI coding agents",
  "description": "An agent's edit-build-test loop runs far more often than a human's, so build time sets both its wall clock and a large part of its token bill, and a shared cache turns most of those iterations into downloads."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running coding agents against a codebase big enough\nthat building it is not instant. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a clear model\nof why build infrastructure is agent infrastructure, and what specifically to\nput in place. &#x2A;*Time:** about ten minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-loop-is-the-unit-of-work",
    "content": "A coding agent does not write a patch and stop. It edits, builds, reads the\nerror, edits again, builds again, and repeats until the tests pass. That loop is\nthe agent's entire mechanism for finding out whether it was right."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-loop-is-the-unit-of-work",
    "content": "A human runs that loop a few times an hour and spends the gaps thinking. An\nagent runs it as fast as the tooling allows, in a tight cycle, and frequently\nruns several in parallel across candidate approaches. The economics invert: for\na person, build time is an interruption; for an agent, build time *is* the task\nduration."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-loop-is-the-unit-of-work",
    "content": "The arithmetic is unforgiving. A four-minute build inside a thirty-iteration\nsession is two hours of wall clock in which nothing is being decided. Cut the\nbuild to forty seconds and the same session is twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "Here is the part that is rarely noticed: agents are unusually good cache clients,\nand the reason is that most of what they do is repetitive in exactly the way\ncontent-addressing rewards."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "**They revisit states they've already been in.** An agent that tries an\napproach, fails a test, and reverts has returned to a tree it already built. To\na content-addressed cache that is not a similar state, it is *the same* state:\nsame input hashes, same keys, every action a hit. The build after a revert can\nbe nearly free."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "**They change one thing at a time.** A well-behaved agent makes a small,\ntargeted edit. Small edits invalidate a small part of the action graph, and\neverything downstream of the untouched parts is still keyed the same. The\nrebuild is proportional to the edit, not to the repository."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "**They run in parallel over shared ancestry.** Three agents exploring three\napproaches to the same bug share almost all of their build graph. The first to\nbuild the common ancestry pays for it; the other two get hits. Same for an agent\nand the human reviewing its work."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "**They repeat each other's work across sessions.** A fresh session on the same\ncommit rebuilds what yesterday's session already built. With a shared cache that\nis a download."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "The general form: &#x2A;*cache hit rate rises with the number of participants who\nshare ancestry, and agents generate a great deal of shared ancestry.** A cache\nthat is a modest optimization for one developer becomes the dominant factor when\na fleet of agents is hammering the same tree."
  }, {
    "heading": "determinism-is-the-other-half",
    "content": "Content-addressing gives agents something beyond speed, and it may matter more."
  }, {
    "heading": "determinism-is-the-other-half",
    "content": "An agent debugging a failure needs the failure to be real. If a test fails\nbecause of a stale artifact, a leaked environment variable, or a machine that\ndiffers from the one that built the dependency, the agent will chase a\nphantom, and it will chase it with great energy, writing plausible fixes for a\nproblem that does not exist. Nondeterminism doesn't just slow an agent down; it\nsends it confidently in the wrong direction and burns tokens doing so."
  }, {
    "heading": "determinism-is-the-other-half",
    "content": "Hermetic, content-addressed builds make \"same inputs, same outputs\" a property\nof the system rather than a hope. When an agent reruns a build and the result\nchanges, that change is now information."
  }, {
    "heading": "determinism-is-the-other-half",
    "content": "Remote execution requires hermetic actions, and a build that quietly depends\non the machine it runs on will fail remotely while working locally. That's\nreal work, and it's worth doing regardless: a build an agent can trust is a\nbuild a person can too. See\nToolchains and hermeticity."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-token-argument",
    "content": "Wall clock is the visible cost. The token bill is the hidden one, and for\nteams running agents at scale it is frequently the larger."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-token-argument",
    "content": "Every iteration of the loop spends tokens: the agent reads build output,\nreasons about it, and produces the next edit. Slow builds don't just make the\nloop longer, they make it *wider*: a long feedback delay pushes an agent toward\nbatching more speculative changes per iteration, which produces bigger diffs,\nnoisier failures, and more context to re-read when something breaks."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-token-argument",
    "content": "Fast, trustworthy builds let an agent work in small verified increments. Small\nincrements mean less context re-read per iteration and fewer wasted iterations\nchasing failures that were never real."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "In rough order of return:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "**A shared cache both agents and humans use.** Not one per agent, not one for CI\nand another for developers. The value is in the sharing, and splitting it is the\nmost common way teams leave the win on the table.\nShared cache is that deployment."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "**Agents pointed at it from the start.** An agent that builds locally with no\nremote cache is generating exactly the redundant work this is designed to\nabsorb. This is one flag in the build configuration the agent already uses."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "**Hermeticity treated as a bug class.** Every action that depends on unnamed\nstate is a source of failures your agent cannot debug and will misdiagnose."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "**Remote execution once caching is steady.** Remote execution adds parallelism across a\nworker pool for the actions that genuinely have to run. It matters most when\nagents work in parallel, because that's when local cores become the ceiling."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-actually-put-in-place",
    "content": "**Enough cache retention to cover how agents actually work.** Eviction policy is\nusually sized for a linear history of commits. Agents branch, revert and\nrevisit far more than that, and a cache that has already evicted the tree an\nagent is returning to gives back the biggest single win on this page. Size for\nthe breadth of states in play, not just the depth of history."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No: that removes the main benefit. Cache entries are keyed by the hash of their\ninputs, so two agents cannot corrupt each other's results; the worst case is\nthat they store separate entries for genuinely different work. Isolation you\nmight want for other reasons is a network and access-control question, not a\ncache-partitioning one."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not through ordinary use. An entry is only reachable by the hash of the inputs\nthat produced it, so a bad edit produces a differently-keyed entry that nobody\nelse asks for. What *can* cause a bad shared entry is a non-hermetic action\nwhose output depends on something outside its declared inputs, which is the\nsame hazard for humans, and another reason hermeticity is the foundation. Write\naccess to the cache is a trust boundary; see\nTLS and authentication."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Indirectly, and importantly. It doesn't fix a genuinely flaky test, but it\nremoves the large class of failures caused by stale or inconsistent build state,\nso the flakes that remain are real ones. For an agent, that distinction is the\ndifference between a debuggable signal and noise."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The benefit tracks shared ancestry, so it's weaker across unrelated repos and\nstill real within each one plus their common dependencies. If the repos share a\ntoolchain or a base image, those actions are shared cache entries even when the\nsource trees aren't."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "You don't configure the agent, you configure the build tool the agent invokes,\nand the agent picks it up because it runs the same commands you do. A remote\ncache flag in the checked-in build configuration is inherited by every agent and\nevery human automatically. Quickstart sets that\nup."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The deployment this page argues for: one cache, shared by everyone and\neverything that builds the tree."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The other direction: making these docs and this codebase legible to an agent\nthat's reading them."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "the-loop-is-the-unit-of-work",
    "content": "The loop is the unit of work"
  }, {
    "id": "why-agents-hit-caches-more-than-people-do",
    "content": "Why agents hit caches more than people do"
  }, {
    "id": "determinism-is-the-other-half",
