    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "A generic blob store that will store files on the cloud\nprovider. This configuration will never delete files, so you are\nresponsible for purging old files in other ways.\nIt supports the following backends:"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Amazon S3:**\nS3 store will use Amazon's S3 service as a backend to store\nthe files. This configuration can be used to share files\nacross multiple instances. Uses system certificates for TLS\nverification via `rustls-platform-verifier`."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Google Cloud Storage:**\nGCS store uses Google's GCS service as a backend to store\nthe files. This configuration can be used to share files\nacross multiple instances."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Azure Blob Store:**\nAzure Blob store will use Microsoft's Azure Blob service as a\nbackend to store the files. This configuration can be used to\nshare files across multiple instances."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**NetApp ONTAP S3:**\nNetApp ONTAP S3 store will use ONTAP's S3-compatible storage as a backend\nto store files. This store is specifically configured for ONTAP's S3 requirements\nincluding custom TLS configuration, credentials management, and proper vserver\nconfiguration."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "This store uses AWS environment variables for credentials:"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "`AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Cloudflare R2:**\nR2 store uses Cloudflare's R2 service as a backend. R2 speaks the\nS3 API, so this is a thin wrapper that derives the account-scoped\nendpoint (`https://{account_id}.r2.cloudflarestorage.com`) for you."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI) Object Storage:**\nOCI store uses Oracle Cloud Infrastructure's S3-compatible Object\nStorage API. The path-style endpoint is derived from your Object\nStorage `namespace` and `region` as\n`https://{namespace}.compat.objectstorage.{region}.oci.customer-oci.com`.\nAuthenticate with a Customer Secret Key (Access Key/Secret Key pair\ncreated under User Settings -> Customer secret keys in the OCI\nconsole); the secret cannot be retrieved after generation, so read\nit from an env var via shellexpand."
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "experimental_cloud_object_store",
    "content": "**Type:** ExperimentalCloudObjectSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "ontap_s3_existence_cache",
    "content": "ONTAP S3 Existence Cache provides a caching layer on top of the ONTAP S3 store\nto optimize repeated existence checks. It maintains an in-memory cache of object\ndigests and periodically syncs this cache to disk for persistence."
  }, {
    "heading": "ontap_s3_existence_cache",
    "content": "The cache helps reduce latency for repeated calls to check object existence,\nwhile still ensuring eventual consistency with the underlying ONTAP S3 store."
  }, {
    "heading": "ontap_s3_existence_cache",
    "content": "Example JSON5 config:"
  }, {
    "heading": "ontap_s3_existence_cache",
    "content": "**Type:** OntapS3ExistenceCacheSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "verify",
    "content": "Verify store is used to apply verifications to an underlying\nstore implementation. It is strongly encouraged to validate\nas much data as you can before accepting data from a client,\nfailing to do so may cause the data in the store to be\npopulated with invalid data causing all kinds of problems."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify",
    "content": "The suggested configuration is to have the CAS validate the\nhash and size and the AC validate nothing."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "verify",
    "content": "**Type:** VerifySpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "completeness_checking",
    "content": "Completeness checking store verifies if the\noutput files & folders exist in the CAS before forwarding\nthe request to the underlying store.\nNote: This store should only be used on AC stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "completeness_checking",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "completeness_checking",
    "content": "**Type:** CompletenessCheckingSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "compression",
    "content": "A compression store that will compress the data inbound and\noutbound. There will be a non-trivial cost to compress and\ndecompress the data, but in many cases if the final store is\na store that requires network transport and/or storage space\nis a concern it is often faster and more efficient to use this\nstore before those stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "compression",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "compression",
    "content": "**Type:** CompressionSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "A dedup store will take the inputs and run a rolling hash\nalgorithm on them to slice the input into smaller parts then\nrun a sha256 algorithm on the slice and if the object doesn't\nalready exist, upload the slice to the `content_store` using\na new digest of just the slice. Once all parts exist, an\nAction-Cache-like digest will be built and uploaded to the\n`index_store` which will contain a reference to each\nchunk/digest of the uploaded file. Downloading a request will\nfirst grab the index from the `index_store`, and forward the\ndownload content of each chunk as if it were one file."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "This store is exceptionally good when the following conditions\nare met:"
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "Content is mostly the same (inserts, updates, deletes are ok)"
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "Content is not compressed or encrypted"
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "Uploading or downloading from `content_store` is the bottleneck."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "Note: This store pairs well when used with `CompressionSpec` as\nthe `content_store`, but never put `DedupSpec` as the backend of\n`CompressionSpec` as it will negate all the gains."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "Note: When running `.has()` on this store, it will only check\nto see if the entry exists in the `index_store` and not check\nif the individual chunks exist in the `content_store`."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup",
    "content": "**Type:** DedupSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "existence_cache",
    "content": "Existence store will wrap around another store and cache calls\nto has so that subsequent `has_with_results` calls will be\nfaster. This is useful for cases when you have a store that\nis slow to respond to has calls.\nNote: This store should only be used on CAS stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "existence_cache",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "existence_cache",
    "content": "**Type:** ExistenceCacheSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow",
    "content": "`FastSlow` store will first try to fetch the data from the `fast`\nstore and then if it does not exist try the `slow` store.\nWhen the object does exist in the `slow` store, it will copy\nthe data to the `fast` store while returning the data.\nThis store should be thought of as a store that \"buffers\"\nthe data to the `fast` store.\nOn uploads it will mirror data to both `fast` and `slow` stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow",
    "content": "WARNING: If you need data to always exist in the `slow` store\nfor something like remote execution, be careful because this\nstore will never check to see if the objects exist in the\n`slow` store if it exists in the `fast` store (i.e. it assumes\nthat if an object exists in the `fast` store it will exist in\nthe `slow` store)."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow",
    "content": "***Example JSON5 config:***"
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow",
    "content": "**Type:** FastSlowSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "shard",
    "content": "Shards the data to multiple stores. This is useful for cases\nwhen you want to distribute the load across multiple stores.\nThe digest hash is used to determine which store to send the\ndata to."
  }, {
    "heading": "shard",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "shard",
    "content": "**Type:** ShardSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "filesystem",
    "content": "Stores the data on the filesystem. This store is designed for\nlocal persistent storage. Restarts of this program should restore\nthe previous state, meaning anything uploaded will be persistent\nas long as the filesystem integrity holds."
  }, {
    "heading": "filesystem",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "filesystem",
    "content": "**Type:** FilesystemSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store",
    "content": "Store used to reference a store in the root store manager.\nThis is useful for cases when you want to share a store in different\nnested stores. Example, you may want to share the same memory store\nused for the action cache, but use a `FastSlowSpec` and have the fast\nstore also share the memory store for efficiency."
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store",
    "content": "**Type:** RefSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "size_partitioning",
    "content": "Uses the size field of the digest to separate which store to send the\ndata. This is useful for cases when you'd like to put small objects\nin one store and large objects in another store. This should only be\nused if the size field is the real size of the content, in other\nwords, don't use on AC (Action Cache) stores. Any store where you can\nsafely use `VerifySpec.verify_size = true`, this store should be safe\nto use (i.e. CAS stores)."
  }, {
    "heading": "size_partitioning",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "size_partitioning",
    "content": "**Type:** SizePartitioningSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "grpc",
    "content": "This store will pass-through calls to another gRPC store. This store\nis not designed to be used as a sub-store of another store, but it\ndoes satisfy the interface and will likely work."
  }, {
    "heading": "grpc",
    "content": "One major GOTCHA is that some stores use a special function on this\nstore to get the size of the underlying object, which is only reliable\nwhen this store is serving the a CAS store, not an AC store. If using\nthis store directly without being a child of any store there are no\nside effects and is the most efficient way to use it."
  }, {
    "heading": "grpc",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "grpc",
    "content": "**Type:** GrpcSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "redis_store",
    "content": "Stores data in any stores compatible with Redis APIs."
  }, {
    "heading": "redis_store",
    "content": "Pairs well with `SizePartitioning` and/or `FastSlow` stores.\nIdeal for accepting small object sizes as most Redis store\nservices have a max file upload of between 256Mb-512Mb."
  }, {
    "heading": "redis_store",
    "content": "If you are using Redis together with any stores above it\ne.g. existence cache, you will need to configure `notify-keyspace-events`\nto `KA` as per https\\://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/pubsub/keyspace-notifications/#configuration\nin order for us to get eviction events. Failing to do so will get you\nlog messages complaining about it, as well as errors like https\\://github.com/toxicwind/forge/issues/2436"
  }, {
    "heading": "redis_store",
    "content": "**Example JSON5 config:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "redis_store",
    "content": "**Type:** RedisSpec"
  }, {
    "heading": "noop",
    "content": "Noop store is a store that sends streams into the void and all data\nretrieval will return 404 (`NotFound`). This can be useful for cases\nwhere you may need to partition your data and part of your data needs\nto be discarded."
