  }, {
    "heading": "resourceenforcementconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "resourceenforcementconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "resourceenforcementconfig",
    "content": "20"
  }, {
    "heading": "resourceenforcementconfig",
    "content": "Percent above its disk reservation an action's own files may reach before it is killed, with `disk: soft`."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "The pool of persistent worker processes (Bazel `supports-workers`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Description"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`enabled`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "boolean"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "true"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Run an action whose platform properties carry `supports-workers=1` in a pooled worker process that outlives it and serves the next action with the same executable, startup arguments and environment. Off, every such action runs as a one-shot process like any other."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`max_workers_per_key`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint)"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "4"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Most worker processes kept per key (executable, startup arguments, environment, protocol). An action whose key is at the cap and has no idle process waits `acquire_timeout_s` for one to come back, then runs one-shot. 0 takes the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`idle_timeout_s`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "300"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Seconds an idle worker process is kept before the sweeper shuts it down. 0 takes the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`max_requests_per_worker`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "200"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Requests a worker process serves before it is retired and replaced, which bounds what a long-lived process accumulates. 0 takes the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`shutdown_grace_ms`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "5000"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Milliseconds a worker process being shut down gets to exit on its own (its stdin is closed) before SIGKILL. 0 takes the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "`acquire_timeout_s`"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "30"
  }, {
    "heading": "persistentworkersconfig",
    "content": "Seconds an action waits for a worker process to come back when its key is at `max_workers_per_key` with none idle, before it runs one-shot instead. 0 takes the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Description"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`ac_store`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "\\{No uploading is done}"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Underlying AC store that the worker will use to publish execution results into. Objects placed in this store should be reachable from the scheduler/client-cas after they have finished updating."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`upload_ac_results_strategy`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "UploadCacheResultsStrategy"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`SuccessOnly`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "In which situations should the results be published to the `ac_store`, if set to `SuccessOnly` then only results with an exit code of 0 will be uploaded, if set to Everything all completed results will be uploaded."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`historical_results_store`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "\\{CAS store of parent}"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Store to upload historical results to. This should be a CAS store if set."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`upload_historical_results_strategy`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "UploadCacheResultsStrategy"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`FailuresOnly`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "In which situations should the results be published to the historical CAS. The historical CAS is where failures are published. These messages conform to the CAS key-value lookup format and are always a `HistoricalExecuteResponse` serialized message."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`success_message_template`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "\"\" (no message)"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Template to use for the `ExecuteResponse.message` property. This message is attached to the response before it is sent to the client. The following special variables are supported:; `digest_function`: Digest function used to calculate the action digest: `action_digest_hash`: Action digest hash: `action_digest_size`: Action digest size: `historical_results_hash`: `HistoricalExecuteResponse` digest hash: `historical_results_size`: `HistoricalExecuteResponse` digest size."
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "`failure_message_template`"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "\"\" (no message)"
  }, {
    "heading": "uploadactionresultconfig",
    "content": "Same as `success_message_template` but for failure case."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerproperty",
    "content": "Plus exactly one of the following variants (the key selects the variant):"
  }, {
    "heading": "values",
    "content": "List of static values.\nNote: Generally there should only ever be 1 value, but if the platform\nproperty key is `PropertyType::Priority` it may have more than one value."
  }, {
    "heading": "values",
    "content": "**Type:** array of string"
  }, {
    "heading": "query_cmd",
    "content": "A dynamic configuration. The string will be executed as a command\n(not shell) and will be split by \"\\n\" (new line character)."
  }, {
    "heading": "query_cmd",
    "content": "**Type:** string"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Advertise CPU and memory from what the worker can actually see. The\nworker reads `cpu.max` and `memory.max` from its own cgroup v2 directory\n(found through `/proc/self/cgroup`, so a privileged container that sees\nthe host's tree still finds its own) up to the nearest limited ancestor,\nthe pod's limits on Kubernetes; with no limit at any level the configured\nproperties stand. It takes off what it needs for itself, divides\nthe memory by the enforcement headroom, and sets the two properties to\nthe result at registration. What the scheduler packs against is then\nderived from the one number that is enforced, instead of typed in twice."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Description"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`cpu_property_name`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`cpu_count`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Property that carries CPU, on the scale `cpu_unit` names."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`memory_property_name`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`memory_kb`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Property that carries memory, in KiB."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`cpu_unit`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "CpuUnit"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "cores"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "The scale the CPU property is advertised on. Whole cores by default, the scale every other example in this configuration uses; a fleet that advertises and requests thousandths of a core sets `millicores`. Getting this wrong is a thousandfold error in how many actions the scheduler packs onto the worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`overhead_cpu_millicores`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "1000"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "CPU the worker keeps for itself, in thousandths of a core whatever `cpu_unit` says."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`overhead_memory_kb`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "2097152 (2 GiB)"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Memory the worker keeps for itself (the process, its directory cache, output buffers), in KiB."
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "`memory_headroom_percent`"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "unset"
  }, {
    "heading": "capacityconfig",
    "content": "Percent the advertised memory is reduced by, so that actions at their enforcement ceiling (reservation plus headroom) still fit the limit. Unset, it follows `resource_enforcement.memory_headroom_percent` while memory enforcement is on, and is 0 otherwise, so the two cannot drift apart by being typed twice."
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "One of:"
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`property`: string — The name of the platform property in the action to get the value from."
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`value`: string — The raw value to set."
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`\"from_environment\"` — Take the value from the local environment corresponding to the name key"
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`\"timeout_millis\"` — The max amount of time in milliseconds the command is allowed to run (requested by the client)."
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`\"side_channel_file\"` — A special file path will be provided that can be used to communicate with the parent process about out-of-band information. This file will be read after the command has finished executing. Based on the contents of the file, the behavior of the result may be modified."
  }, {
    "heading": "environmentsource",
    "content": "`\"action_directory\"` — A \"root\" directory for the action. This directory can be used to store temporary files that are not needed after the action has completed. This directory will be purged after the action has completed."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Description"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`max_entries`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint)"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "1000"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Maximum number of cached directories."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`max_size_bytes`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint64)"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "10737418240 (10 GB)"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Maximum total size in bytes for all cached directories (0 = unlimited)."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`cache_root`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`{work_directory}/../directory_cache`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Base directory for cache storage. This directory will be managed by the worker and should be on the same filesystem as `work_directory`."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`experimental_subtree_caching`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "boolean"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "false (only root directories are cached; existing behavior)"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Optional and experimental: additionally cache every subdirectory by its own `Directory` digest, not just the root directory. REAPI Merkle nodes are content-addressed, so a subtree that is byte-identical between two different roots has the same digest and can be materialized with a single hardlink pass instead of being rebuilt from the CAS. This makes the common \"one input file changed out of thousands\" case reuse every unchanged subtree."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`max_concurrent_fetches`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "integer (uint)"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "64"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "Maximum number of concurrent slow-store fetches across ALL directory constructions of this cache. This bound protects backing stores from RPC storms: per-level construction concurrency compounds multiplicatively across tree levels and concurrent actions."
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "`experimental_get_tree_prefetch`"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "boolean"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "—"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "false"
  }, {
    "heading": "directorycacheconfig",
    "content": "On a directory-cache miss, prefetch every `Directory` proto of the tree with one logical `GetTree` traversal instead of fetching protos level by level (which costs one round trip per tree DEPTH). The \\[REAPI request contract] permits servers to impose their own limit even when no page size is specified, and the \\[REAPI response contract] requires clients to continue with the returned page token. A paginated traversal may therefore use multiple RPCs. Only takes effect when the slow tier is a `grpc` store; any prefetch failure falls back to the per-level path. Worthwhile when worker-to-CAS latency is non-trivial and trees are deep; measured 5-34x on the proto phase at 5-25ms RTT. Note the serving CAS pays the tree walk against its own backend, so its directory protos should be served from a fast tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "When the scheduler matches tasks to workers that are capable of running\nthe task, this value will be used to determine how the property is treated."
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "One of:"
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "string"
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "`\"minimum\"` — Requires the platform property to be a u64 and when the scheduler looks for appropriate worker nodes that are capable of executing the task, the task will not run on a node that has less than this value."
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "`\"exact\"` — Requires the platform property to be a string and when the scheduler looks for appropriate worker nodes that are capable of executing the task, the task will not run on a node that does not have this property set to the value with exact string match."
  }, {
    "heading": "propertytype",
    "content": "`\"priority\"` — Does not restrict on this value and instead will be passed to the worker as an informational piece. In the future this will be used by the scheduler and worker to cause the scheduler to prefer certain workers over others, but not restrict them based on these values."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "When a worker is being searched for to run a job, this will be used\non how to choose which worker should run the job when multiple\nworkers are able to run the task."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Value"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Description"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "`\"least_recently_used\"`"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Prefer workers that have been least recently used to run a job."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "`\"most_recently_used\"`"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Prefer workers that have been most recently used to run a job."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "`\"least_loaded\"`"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Prefer the worker running the fewest actions, least recently used among equals. A worker that just joined has nothing running, so it takes the next action instead of waiting for every older worker to be used once more, and a burst spreads across the fleet instead of filling one worker to its concurrency cap."
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "`\"best_fit\"`"
  }, {
    "heading": "workerallocationstrategy",
    "content": "Prefer the worker the action fits most tightly: among the workers that can take it, the one whose remaining `minimum` properties (memory, CPU) would be smallest afterwards, as a share of what it advertises. Big actions are kept for the workers with big room and small ones fill the gaps, so a fleet of mixed sizes wastes the least. A `priority` property on the action that a worker carries with the same value prefers that worker before the fit is judged, which is what such a property is for (a zone, a cache locality). Ties go to the fewest running actions, then least recently used."
  }, {
    "heading": "memoryescalationspec",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "memoryescalationspec",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "memoryescalationspec",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
