import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "The store model",
  "description": "What a store actually is, how composition behaves, and where a digest stops being a claim and becomes a fact."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone composing stores in a config file and wanting to\npredict the result, and anyone writing a new store backend. &#x2A;*What you'll\nhave at the end:** the trait, the key space, the composition rules, and the\none place verification actually happens. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A Forge store is not a database binding. It is one small trait\n(eight required methods), and everything you configure as a \"store\" is either\nan implementation of it or a wrapper around other implementations of it. The\n`stores` block in your config is a graph, and its edges have semantics that\nthe JSON5 does not show you."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-names-one-file",
    "content": "`Store`, `StoreLike` and `StoreDriver` all live in\n,\nand the split between them matters when you read the source."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-names-one-file",
    "content": "`StoreDriver` is the trait a backend implements. `Store` is a\n`#[repr(transparent)]` newtype around `Arc<dyn StoreDriver>`, the handle\neverything else holds. `StoreLike` is the ergonomic layer callers use: its\nmethods (`get`, `has`, `update_oneshot` and the rest) accept anything that\nconverts into a `StoreKey` and forward to the pinned driver, so callers never\ndeal with `Pin<&dyn StoreDriver>` directly."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-names-one-file",
    "content": "A driver must supply eight methods: `post_init`, `has_with_results`,\n`update`, `get_part`, `inner_store`, `as_any`, `as_any_arc`, and\n`register_remove_callback`. Only the middle three are the ones you would\nguess. The other five are what makes composition work, and `inner_store` in\nparticular is where composition either succeeds or silently stops."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-key-space-is-not-just-digests",
    "content": "`StoreKey` has two variants: `Digest(DigestInfo)` and `Str(Cow<str>)`. Most\nof Forge deals in the first. The second exists because two subsystems\nneed to store things that are not content-addressed at all: the Build Event\nProtocol service, and the scheduler's awaited-action database."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-key-space-is-not-just-digests",
    "content": "This has a consequence people hit and misread. `StoreKey::into_digest()`\nconverts a `Str` key to a digest by BLAKE3-hashing the string, with the hash\nfunction hardcoded. That conversion is lossy and irreversible: a store that\nonly understands digests will accept a `Str` key and file it somewhere\ndeterministic, but the original string is gone. The filesystem store handles\nthis more honestly by keeping the two in separate namespaces on disk (`s/`\nfor string keys, `d/` for digests) so the two can never collide."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-key-space-is-not-just-digests",
    "content": "Elsewhere, a hardcoded hash function would be a correctness bug, because\nthe client picks the digest function per request. Here it is not: these\nkeys never cross the REAPI wire and are never compared against a\nclient-supplied digest. The hash is an internal placement function, not an\nidentity."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "This is the single most important thing on this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "**Forge does not derive a blob's digest from its bytes on upload.** The\nclient declares the digest in the ByteStream resource name, and the server\nstores the bytes under the declared key. See\n.\n(The one exception is a zstd-compressed ByteStream upload, where the decoder\nalready has to read every byte and rejects a decoded stream whose size or\nhash does not match the digest.)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "For plain uploads, the only thing that turns a claimed digest into a verified\none is a `verify` store somewhere in the graph, and both of its checks\ndefault to **off**:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "What it costs when on"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "`verify_size`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "`false`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "Nearly nothing; a running byte count"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "`verify_hash`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "`false`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "One hash pass over every uploaded byte"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement",
    "content": "A cache with no `verify` store in the write path is a cache that will store,\nand later serve, whatever a buggy or hostile client claims. That is not a\nForge-specific hazard (it is how the REAPI is specified), but the\ndefault being `false` means it is a decision you are making whether or not\nyou know it. Correctness and hermeticity\ncovers what to do about it."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "The implementation in"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "is worth understanding, because three of its properties are not apparent from the config."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "**Size is checked eagerly, before a byte is read.** If the declared size is\nalready wrong at the point the request arrives, the upload is rejected\nwithout transferring anything."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "**The hash function is read per-request from the ambient context**, not from\nconfig. This is what lets one store serve both SHA-256 and BLAKE3 clients\nwithout duplicating the graph."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "**Verification runs concurrently with the write, not before it.** The inner\nstore's `update` and the checker are driven together, so verification adds no\nserialised pass. The cost of that design is that a failing upload has already\nbeen partially written to the inner store when the failure is detected."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "Size is enforced along three separate paths (overshoot, an exact hit that\nturns out not to be EOF, and undershoot at EOF), and the hash is compared at\nEOF. Every failure is `InvalidArgument`, which is the correct code: the\nclient sent something that did not match what the client said it was sending."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-verify-store-actually-works",
    "content": "`VerifyStore::get_part` is a bare pass-through. Verification is a\n**write-side** property only. A blob that entered the store before you added\nthe verify wrapper, or through a path that bypassed it, will be served\nwithout complaint forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "composition-and-the-barriers-in-it",
    "content": "`inner_store` is how the system looks *through* a wrapper to find an\noptimisation opportunity, for example, to discover that the real backing\nstore is a filesystem store and a hardlink is possible."
  }, {
    "heading": "composition-and-the-barriers-in-it",
    "content": "Most wrappers return `self` from `inner_store`. That is not laziness; it is a\nstatement that looking through them would be wrong, because they transform\nthe bytes or the key. A `compression` store cannot let a caller hardlink its\ninner file: the inner file is not the blob, so most wrappers are\n**optimisation barriers**, and only three forward: `size_partitioning`,\n`shard`, and `ref_store`, the three that route without transforming."
  }, {
    "heading": "composition-and-the-barriers-in-it",
    "content": "The practical rule when composing: **put transforming wrappers as close to the\nbackend as you can**. A `verify` in front of a `fast_slow` is a barrier in\nfront of the fast path."
  }, {
    "heading": "composition-and-the-barriers-in-it",
    "content": "A caller reaching through the routing wrappers can discover the filesystem\nstore and hardlink from it. A caller reaching through a transforming wrapper\ncannot, because what is on disk underneath it is not the blob."
  }, {
    "heading": "composition-and-the-barriers-in-it",
    "content": "`StoreOptimizations` is the vocabulary for the other half of this:\n`FileUpdates`, `NoopUpdates`, `NoopDownloads`, `LazyExistenceOnSync`,\n`SubscribesToUpdateOneshot`, capabilities a store advertises so callers can\nskip work rather than discovering at runtime that it was unnecessary."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast-slow-writes-through-and-deliberately-re-uploads",
    "content": "With the default `fast_direction` and `slow_direction`, writes go to both\ntiers concurrently and **both must succeed**. It has no write-behind and no\neventual consistency window: an upload that returns OK is durable in the slow\ntier."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast-slow-writes-through-and-deliberately-re-uploads",
    "content": "The surprising part is existence. `fast_slow`'s `has_with_results`\n**intentionally never consults the fast store** (unless the slow tier is a\n`noop` store, in which case only the fast tier is checked). It asks the slow\ntier, then counts any slow-tier writes still in flight. A blob that is\npresent only in the fast tier is reported absent, which causes the client to\nupload it again, and that upload is what gets it into the slow tier. The\napparent inefficiency is the mechanism that stops the fast tier from becoming\na place where data can be lost."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast-slow-writes-through-and-deliberately-re-uploads",
    "content": "Reads use a leader/follower scheme so that a hundred simultaneous misses for\nthe same blob produce one fetch from the slow tier rather than a hundred.\nFollowers wait up to one minute for the leader before giving up and fetching\nfor themselves; that timeout is what stops a stuck leader from wedging every\nreader behind it."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup-is-a-chunking-index-and-it-has-opinions",
    "content": "A `dedup` store splits blobs with content-defined chunking (FastCDC), stores\nthe chunks, and writes an index under the **original** key. Two large blobs\nthat share most of their content share most of their chunks."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup-is-a-chunking-index-and-it-has-opinions",
    "content": "Two things to know before enabling it. First, chunk hashing is **hardcoded to\nBLAKE3** regardless of the digest function the request used, which is\ncorrect for the same reason as `StoreKey::into_digest`, since chunk hashes\nare placement, not identity, but it does mean a `dedup` store's internal\nlayout is unrelated to your clients' digest function."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup-is-a-chunking-index-and-it-has-opinions",
    "content": "Second, a corrupted index reports itself inconsistently: `has()` returns\n`NotFound` while `get_part` returns `Internal`. If you are debugging a store\nwhere existence checks and reads disagree, a damaged dedup index is the\nfirst thing to suspect."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-that-are-not-what-their-filenames-say",
    "content": "and"
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-that-are-not-what-their-filenames-say",
    "content": "are not store implementations. Both are thin constructors that return an\n`S3Store`. Cloudflare R2 and OCI object storage are S3-compatible, and\nForge treats them as exactly that. If you are chasing a bug in R2\nbehaviour, the code you want is in `s3_store.rs`."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-that-are-not-what-their-filenames-say",
    "content": "`RefStore` is the other oddity: it resolves its target by name at\n`post_init`, holding a weak reference to the store manager, using\n`UnsafeCell` with a hand-written `Sync` impl. It is the mechanism that lets\none store be named by several others without duplicating it, and it is why\nstore construction happens in two phases."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-things-that-are-not-what-their-filenames-say",
    "content": "When `FilesystemStore::has_with_results` is asked about the empty blob (the\ndigest of zero bytes) and that entry is not on disk yet, it creates the\nzero-byte file as a side effect of what reads, from its name, like a pure\nquery. Anything counting inodes or watching the content directory should\nexpect an entry that no upload produced."
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "Every store type, every field and every default is in the generated\nstore configuration reference. This\npage deliberately does not restate field names, because the reference is\ngenerated from the Rust types and this page is not."
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "For choosing a backend rather than understanding the model, start at\nStore backends."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "On the write path of anything shared between people, yes. The cost is one\nhash pass over uploaded bytes, concurrent with the write rather than\nserialised before it. The thing it buys is that a digest in your cache\nmeans what it says. On a purely local single-developer cache the\ncalculation is different, because you are the only client and you are not\ndefending against yourself."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "That is `fast_slow` working as designed: existence checks skip the fast\ntier, so a blob present only there is reported missing and re-uploaded,\nwhich is what lands it in the slow tier. The alternative would be a cache\nthat reports a blob present when the only durable copy of it does not\nexist."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes, and it must. Hardlinking works by handing the worker the store's own\nfile; a compression store's file is not the blob. That is why it returns\nitself from `inner_store`; the barrier is the correct answer, not a\nmissing optimisation."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No, and the config parser rejects it before anything starts. They hold\ndifferent value types under keys from the same space, so sharing a store\ncorrupts both."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "How an action that missed the cache becomes an action running on a worker."
  }],
