export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Architecture",
  "description": "The four roles, who actually writes to the action cache, and which parts of the system hold state you can lose."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone deciding how to deploy Forge, or trying to\npredict which component's failure costs them what. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the\nend:** the four roles, the path an action takes on a miss and on a hit, and\nan accurate answer to \"what do I have to back up\". &#x2A;*Time:** fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge implements the\nRemote Execution API, and that\nAPI describes four roles: a content-addressable store, an action cache, a\nscheduler, and workers. It is worth being precise about what \"four roles\"\nmeans here, because it is the source of the most common deployment mistake."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Four roles is the shape of the protocol, not the shape of the program.**\nOne `forge` binary exists. Which roles it plays is decided by the\nconfig file you hand it; there are no per-role images and no build features.\nA single-developer setup runs all four in one process; a large cluster runs\nseveral deployments of the same image with different configs. See\nthe architecture deep dive for what\nthat binary actually does at startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "cas",
    "content": "Every byte Forge touches lives in the CAS, keyed by a digest of its\ncontent. Source files, object files, binaries, stdout, all of it. Two\nidentical files anywhere in your organisation collapse into one stored blob,\nbecause they hash to the same key."
  }, {
    "heading": "cas",
    "content": "The digest function is **SHA-256 or BLAKE3**, negotiated per request rather\nthan fixed at build time. Blobs stored under different digest functions do\nnot share a namespace: switching the function is equivalent to starting with\nan empty cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "cas",
    "content": "The CAS is the only role holding data you would miss. Lose it and every\ncached artifact is gone. It is also not one thing: a production CAS is a\n*stack* of stores composed in config. The store model\nis the page for that."
  }, {
    "heading": "ac",
    "content": "The action cache maps `action digest → ActionResult`. The action digest\ncovers the command line, every input file's digest, the environment\nvariables, and the platform properties, so an AC hit means *this exact\ncomputation has been performed before, and here is where its outputs are*."
  }, {
    "heading": "ac",
    "content": "The AC does not hold the outputs. It holds digests pointing into the CAS,\nwhich is why an AC entry whose CAS blobs have been evicted is a real failure\nmode rather than a theoretical one; see\ncorrectness and hermeticity."
  }, {
    "heading": "ac",
    "content": "Losing the AC is survivable: clients re-execute, workers refill it."
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler",
    "content": "The scheduler receives `Execute` RPCs, decides which queued action goes next,\nfinds a worker whose platform properties satisfy the action's, and assigns\nthe work. It also merges duplicate requests, so two developers building the\nsame commit wait on one execution rather than two."
  }, {
    "heading": "scheduler",
    "content": "In configuration \"the scheduler\" can be a stack rather than a single\ncomponent: a `cache_lookup` scheduler can be placed in front of the real\nscheduler to answer `Execute` from the action cache, so an action that hits\nthe cache never appears in queue depth. None of the shipped example configs\ndo this, because Bazel already calls `GetActionResult` before it calls\n`Execute`. Scheduler internals covers\nthe merge rule, the ordering rule and the retry rule."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "A worker fetches the action's inputs from the CAS, materialises them into a\ndirectory, runs the command, uploads the outputs, and reports back."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "Workers hold no durable state. A worker that dies mid-action costs you that\naction, not your cache. What they do hold is a local filesystem that is\ndeliberately not clean between actions;\nworker execution explains why, and what it\nmeans for hermeticity."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "The development configuration adds an opt-in\n`single_use` worker lifecycle.\nA worker accepts one action, uploads its results, and exits. Run it as the only\nworker in its process and have the launcher replace the whole container with\nfresh writable volumes. This removes local files, processes, and caches left\nby previous actions. Restarting a process inside an existing container does\nnot provide that isolation. The shared CAS and action cache remain available."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "Development builds also offer\n`experimental_buck2_file_capture`\nfor dedicated Linux execution containers. Forge starts an operator-supplied\ncollector only for Buck2 assignments with invocation metadata, then waits for\nbounded final capture before deleting action files. The collector owns storage\nand access control. Capture failure does not change the action's execution result.\nThis option requires the `single_use` lifecycle and does not add a file server to\nForge."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "To keep overlapping Buck2 builds on separate workers, also enable\n`experimental_buck2_invocation_isolation`\non the Execution services for that instance. Identical in-flight actions from\ndifferent invocations then schedule separately. Retries in the same invocation\ncan still join their existing action. Completed action-cache hits remain shared\nand launch neither a worker nor a collector. Upgrade every scheduler serving the\ninstance before enabling this option; it does not change other build tools."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "It is natural to assume the scheduler records the result, since the\nscheduler is what answered the client. It does not. The worker writes the\n`ActionResult` into its configured `upload_action_result.ac_store` itself\n(as an `UpdateActionResult` call when that store is a gRPC proxy), from\n."
  }, {
    "heading": "workers",
    "content": "The operational consequence: &#x2A;*the worker's `ac_store` is what decides\nwhether anything gets cached.** A worker with no `ac_store` refuses to\nstart unless `upload_ac_results_strategy` is `never`; a worker with\n`never` runs every action and caches nothing, with builds still\nsucceeding and no errors anywhere in the logs. The symptom is a cache hit\nrate pinned near zero."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "The concrete version. A developer runs `bazel build //app:server`, which\nexpands to roughly two thousand actions. Take one of them: compiling\n`main.cc`."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**Cold, nothing in the cache:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**Bazel builds the action digest.** The compiler's own digest, the\ncommand line, `main.cc` and every header it includes, the platform\nproperties. All of it hashes into one digest."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**`GetActionResult(digest)` → `NOT_FOUND`.** A single round trip, a few\nmilliseconds."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**Bazel uploads what's missing.** `FindMissingBlobs` first, then\n`BatchUpdateBlobs` for the ones the CAS does not have. On a cold cache\nthis is most of the wall time."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**`Execute(action)` to the scheduler.** The scheduler either merges this\ninto an identical in-flight action or queues it."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**A worker is assigned.** The action's platform properties\n(`OSFamily: Linux`, `container-image: ...`) have to be satisfiable by a\nworker's declared properties, or the action queues indefinitely rather\nthan failing."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**The worker runs it.** Fetch inputs, materialise the input root, execute\nthe command under a timeout, capture stdout, stderr and the declared\noutput paths."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**The worker uploads.** Output blobs to the CAS, then the `ActionResult`\nto the AC."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**Bazel downloads what it needs.** With `--remote_download_minimal` it may\ndownload nothing at all and keep only the digests."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "**Warm, the same build on a colleague's machine:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "Step 2 returns an `ActionResult` instead of `NOT_FOUND`. Steps 3 through 7\ndo not happen. The scheduler is never contacted; no worker is involved; no\ncompiler runs. The cost of that action is one gRPC round trip plus whatever\noutput bytes the client actually wants."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "That gap, one round trip against a compile, is the whole value\nproposition, and it is why the cache hit rate is the number worth watching\nrather than worker count."
  }, {
    "heading": "one-build-twice",
    "content": "A high hit rate with disappointing wall time usually means the client is\ndownloading outputs it does not need. `--remote_download_minimal` (Bazel)\nkeeps intermediate artifacts remote. The other common cause is a\nmismatched `instance_name` between client and server, which produces a\npermanently empty cache with no error."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Deployment planning comes down to this table, and one widespread claim about\nit is wrong."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Role"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Durable state"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "If you lose it"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "CAS"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Yes, the blobs"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Every cached artifact. Rebuild from scratch."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "AC"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Yes, but cheap"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Clients re-execute and refill it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Scheduler"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "**Yes, by default**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "In-flight actions. See below."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Worker"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "No"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "The action it was running, which is retried."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "The scheduler is frequently described as stateless. It is not, by default:\nthe awaited-action state (what is queued, what is executing, who is waiting\non it) lives **in memory in that process**. Two scheduler replicas behind a\nload balancer with the default configuration are two independent schedulers\nthat cannot merge each other's actions or answer each other's clients."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-holds-state",
    "content": "Making the scheduler horizontally scalable means moving that state out, which\nis what the Redis-backed state manager is for.\nScheduler internals covers what that\nchanges and what it costs."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-rust-why-this-shape",
    "content": "Three properties drove the design, and all three are operational rather than\naesthetic."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-rust-why-this-shape",
    "content": "**No garbage collector.** A build's tail latency is very visible: a p99 stall\nin the scheduler shows up as developers watching a progress bar. Removing the\nGC removes an entire category of latency spike that is difficult to tune away\nin a managed runtime."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-rust-why-this-shape",
    "content": "**Memory safety without a runtime.** A cache serving a wrong artifact is a\nworse incident than a cache serving no artifact, because it is silent and it\npropagates. Rust removes the memory-corruption class of that bug at compile\ntime while keeping predictable resource use."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-rust-why-this-shape",
    "content": "**Content addressing all the way down.** No cache invalidation exists\nlogic, because there is nothing to invalidate; a blob's key is its content.\nEviction is a size-and-age policy rather than a correctness mechanism. Most\nof the hard bugs in a build cache come from invalidation, and this design\ndoes not have the concept."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The CAS, and only the CAS. The AC is a lookup table that refills itself.\nWorkers hold nothing durable. The scheduler holds in-flight state that is\nworth nothing after a restart anyway; the clients will retry."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes. The same executable serves any subset, selected by config. Start\nwith one process running everything; split the services out when one of\nthem becomes the bottleneck, not before."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "A namespace within one cluster. Digests from different instance names\nnever collide, so one deployment can serve isolated teams. Every client\nand server must agree on it; a mismatch looks exactly like a\npermanently empty cache, with no error on either side. More vocabulary\nin the glossary."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No, and most deployments start without it. A shared cache alone\n(CAS and AC, no scheduler, no workers) is the majority of the benefit\nfor the minority of the operational cost. The\nRemote execution section is the path from one to\nthe other when you want it."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Its platform properties could not be satisfied by any connected worker.\nA key the scheduler does not know is not rejected at submission either:\nthe matching pass logs an `Unknown platform property` error and the\naction stays queued. Scheduler internals covers the matching rule in\ndetail."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The shape of the program rather than the protocol: the crate graph, the\nstartup ordering, and where an action's path forks inside the binary."
  }],
