export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "History and licensing",
  "description": "What Forge was built to replace, the three properties that drove the rewrite, and exactly how the source is licensed."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone evaluating Forge against an existing build\nfarm, and anyone who needs to answer a licensing question precisely. &#x2A;*What\nyou'll have at the end:** the design pressures that produced the current\nsystem, and a file-by-file account of what license applies. &#x2A;*Time:** ten\nminutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Remote build execution is not a new idea. Google shipped an internal version\nof it long before the\nRemote Execution API was\npublished, and by the early 2020s there were several open implementations of\nthe published protocol. Forge started in December 2020 as a Rust\nimplementation of the CAS half of that protocol (first called Rust CAS, then\nTurbo Cache from April 2022), was renamed to Forge in December 2023\n(the `cas` executable became `forge` at the same time), and cut its\nfirst tagged release, 0.1.0, on 2023-12-20. The\nargument for writing a new implementation rather than running an existing\none is the one below: the protocol is narrow enough that a rewrite is\ntractable, and what was expensive in the existing options was the substrate."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Family"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Strength"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "The recurring problem"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "JVM-based servers"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Mature, complete, well understood"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Tail latency under GC pressure, and a memory footprint that makes horizontal scaling the only answer"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Go services"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Fast on the happy path, straightforward to operate"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Contention on hot artifacts and on control-plane locks, which shows up as a cluster-wide stall rather than a slow request"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Proprietary clouds"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Fast, someone else operates it"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "No source access, and pricing that scales with the thing you are trying to do more of"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
    "content": "Two of those three problems are runtime problems rather than design problems.\nThat observation is the whole argument for the rewrite: the protocol was\nfine, the storage model was fine, and what was expensive was the substrate."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-properties",
    "content": "**Predictable latency.** In a build system, the p99 is the number developers\nexperience: a single stalled action blocks everything downstream of it in\nthe graph. Removing the garbage collector removes a class of latency spike\nthat is very hard to tune away rather than eliminate."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-properties",
    "content": "**Memory safety without a runtime.** A build cache that serves a *wrong*\nartifact is a far worse incident than one that serves *no* artifact: it is\nsilent, and it propagates to everyone who pulls from the cache. Rust removes\nthe memory-corruption path to that outcome at compile time, without adding a\nmanaged runtime back in."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-properties",
    "content": "**One binary, many roles.** CAS, action cache, scheduler and worker are the\nsame executable with different configuration. It makes the small deployment\ntrivial and the large deployment a scaling exercise rather than a\nre-architecture. Architecture covers what that\nmeans in practice, and\nthe deep dive covers what the binary\ndoes with the config it is handed."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-properties",
    "content": "A fourth property arrived with the project rather than driving it: because\nthe toolchain definitions could be shared between the local machine and the\nremote workers, the same hermetic build could run in both places. That became\nLocal Remote Execution, and it is the part of Forge\nwith no real equivalent elsewhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "This question gets answered vaguely often enough to be worth stating exactly."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "The repository's root"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "is the **Functional Source License 1.1 with an Apache 2.0 future grant**\n(`FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0`). In practice that means two things. You may use, read,\nmodify and deploy the software for any purpose *except* offering it to others\nas a competing commercial product or service. And each release converts to\nplain Apache 2.0 on the second anniversary of its publication; the grant is\nirrevocable and written into the license itself, not a promise made\nelsewhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "Five files are **Business Source License** instead, and they are worth\nknowing by name because they are the ones an internal license review will\nflag:"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "File or module"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "What it is"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "`forge-util/src/metrics.rs`"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "The metrics collection layer"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "`forge-worker/src/persistent_worker/`: `mod.rs`, `pool.rs`, `live_worker.rs`, `protocol.rs`"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "Remote persistent workers"
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "When you add a file, copy the license header from the file next to it\nrather than from memory. A new file under `persistent_worker/` needs the\nBUSL header; a new file in the same crate's `src/` does not."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "Nothing in the repository requires a contributor license agreement or a\nDeveloper Certificate of Origin sign-off. What it does require is\ncryptographically signed commits; see\nthe contribution guidelines."
  }, {
    "heading": "versioning",
    "content": "Forge versions look like SemVer and are not."
  }, {
    "heading": "versioning",
    "content": "declares `compatibility_level = 0`, which is Bazel's way of saying no\ncompatibility promise is being made across versions. The config schema and\nthe metrics surface have both changed in minor releases; the changelog marks\nsuch entries as breaking."
  }, {
    "heading": "versioning",
    "content": "The wire protocol is the exception, and it is the one that matters for most\ndeployments: Forge implements the Remote Execution API, which is\nversioned upstream and changes slowly. A client that worked against one\nForge release will keep working against the next.\nRelease and versioning is the page for\nthe details."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-these-docs-were-rewritten",
    "content": "The previous documentation accumulated during the period when the project\nwas changing shape weekly. It described commands that no longer existed,\ncomponents that had been renamed, and performance numbers with no source.\nThis rewrite holds every page to the same contract: every command is run or\nschema-checked before it ships, every claim about the code links to the code,\nand pages that cannot meet that bar say what they do not know."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-these-docs-were-rewritten",
    "content": "The source lives in"
  }, {
    "heading": "why-these-docs-were-rewritten",
    "content": "and corrections are the most welcome kind of pull request.\nWorking on documentation is the how."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "It is source-available today and open source on a timer. The FSL 1.1\ngrant permits everything except offering Forge itself as a\ncompeting commercial service, and each release converts to Apache 2.0\ntwo years after publication. If your concern is \"can I read and modify\nthe code that runs my builds\", the answer is yes, without qualification."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes. Running it to build your own software is a permitted purpose,\ncommercial or not. The restriction is on reselling Forge as a\ncompeting product or service. The two BUSL modules are the ones to\ncheck with counsel if you plan to redistribute."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Predictable tail latency without a garbage collector, and memory safety\nwithout a managed runtime. The cost is compile time and a smaller pool\nof contributors, a real trade, made deliberately, for a component whose\nworst failure mode is silently serving the wrong bytes."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Less than it looks like. `compatibility_level = 0` in `MODULE.bazel`\nmeans no cross-version compatibility guarantee. Read the changelog\nbefore upgrading, and pin the version in your deployment."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The idea that came out of the rewrite rather than driving it: the same\nhermetic toolchain on your laptop and on the remote workers."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "what-the-existing-options-traded-away",
