  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** you have VMs or physical machines and no interest in\nrunning a cluster orchestrator to get a build cache. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the\nend:** three systemd units, a disk layout that will not surprise you, host\nsizing you can defend, and a restart procedure. &#x2A;*Time:** an afternoon."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config you wrote (see Your first full config), and hosts you can install a systemd unit on."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "No systemd unit exists in the Forge repository. That is the honest\nstarting point for this page: unlike Compose and Kubernetes, where you can\npoint at shipped files, here you write everything. The good news is that there\nis very little to write: the binary takes one positional argument and reads\nnothing else from its command line."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "If what you actually want is a cache for a handful of people rather than a\nproduction deployment, Shared cache is the\nshorter road and ends somewhere useful."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "Three units, one per process. They are near-identical; the differences are the\nconfig file and, for the worker, the security posture."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-service-account-and-directories",
    "content": "Put the configuration files in `/etc/forge/{cas,scheduler,worker}.json5`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-scheduler-unit",
    "content": "Identical apart from the config file and the fact that it needs no writable\nstate at all when the scheduler backend is the default in-memory one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-unit",
    "content": "This one is different, and the differences matter."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-unit",
    "content": "If your worker configuration sets `use_namespaces: true`, the process calls\n`unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER | CLONE_NEWPID | ...)`, which `NoNewPrivileges=true`\ncan block depending on kernel and distribution policy. If you are **not**\nusing namespaces, add `NoNewPrivileges=true` back; it costs nothing."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-unit",
    "content": "Either way, understand what you are relying on: Forge's namespace\nsupport is a hermeticity and process-reaping mechanism, not a sandbox.\nActions run as the worker's own uid, see the whole filesystem the unit can\nsee, and can reach every Forge port on the network.\nSecurity hardening\nis the full picture, and it is the reason `MemoryMax` and `ProtectSystem`\nare in this unit rather than left to chance."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-unit",
    "content": "Then:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Setting"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Where"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Why you'd change it"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`LimitNOFILE`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "unit"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Forge raises its own soft limit at startup toward `max_open_files` (default 24,576) but cannot exceed the hard limit systemd grants. Set the unit's limit **above** the config value, or the config value is capped (the startup log line `set_open_file_limit() assigns new open file limit N` shows what it got)."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`max_open_files`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`global` in the config"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "The binary reserves 80% of the *achieved* limit as a concurrency semaphore. Exhausting it does not error; it blocks."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`MemoryMax`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "worker unit"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Forge's configuration has no memory limit anywhere. The cgroup is the only bound that exists."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`RestartSec`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "worker unit"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "The worker's own scheduler-reconnect retry is a hard-coded 0.5 s with no backoff. A restart loop plus that retry is a lot of connection attempts; keep `RestartSec` at seconds, not milliseconds."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`TimeoutStopSec`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "worker unit"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "On `SIGTERM` the worker rejects new actions, waits for in-flight ones to finish, then tells the scheduler it is going away and exits. If this timer fires first, systemd `SIGKILL`s it and every in-flight action fails and re-queues."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`NL_LOG`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "environment"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`json` if anything is ingesting these logs. Any unrecognised value silently falls back to `pretty`; see CLI and environment."
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "The CAS host is the one where placement matters."
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Path"
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Requirement"
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "`content_path`"
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "The bulk of the data. On the CAS host, provision `max_bytes` plus headroom. On a worker host, provision **twice** the fast tier's `max_bytes`: the worker creates an executable-variant directory next to `content_path` that is invisible to the eviction accounting, so that store can reach roughly `2 × max_bytes` in the worst case."
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "`temp_path`"
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Must be on the **same block device** as `content_path`. Otherwise the atomic rename that completes every write degrades to a copy: slower, and it doubles peak space during writes. Everything here is deleted on every startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Worker `work_directory`"
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Scratch. Purged at worker startup, which means &#x2A;*two workers must never share one.** A second worker starting will delete the first worker's in-flight action trees."
  }, {
    "heading": "disk-layout",
    "content": "Forge evicts on the size it has accounted for, not on the size the\nfilesystem reports, and it evicts only when the store is mutated, never in\nresponse to a failed write. An `eviction_policy` is optional and its\n`max_bytes` defaults to zero, which means *never evict*. Leaving it unset on\na `filesystem` store is the single most common way to fill a disk.\nRunbooks has the recovery."
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "No sizing numbers are published for Forge; measure your own workload\nbefore buying anything. What the code does fix:"
