    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Notes"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "CAS storage"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "The `max_bytes` you set, plus headroom"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "It is a cache of the working set, not an archive; the eviction policy is the only bound."
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Worker vCPU"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "1 per concurrent action"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Set `max_inflight_tasks` to match; it defaults to **0, meaning unlimited**."
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Worker memory"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "The sum of your largest concurrent actions"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "The configuration has no per-action memory limit; `MemoryMax` on the unit is the only bound."
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Scheduler"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "Small"
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "It holds queue state in memory and owns no storage."
  }, {
    "heading": "host-sizing",
    "content": "CAS storage and I/O dominate the bill; the scheduler is the cheapest process in\nthe system. If you are choosing where to spend, spend on CAS I/O."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "The three processes have genuinely different restart characteristics, and\ntreating them the same is how a routine upgrade turns into a stack of failed\nbuilds."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "**The scheduler** is the disruptive one. When it goes away, every worker\nnotices within seconds and reconnects at a flat 0.5-second interval with no\nbackoff and no jitter; a fleet of 200 workers produces up to 400 connection\nattempts per second at the socket for the whole outage. Worse, each worker's\nreconnect path calls `kill_all()` first, destroying every action it was\nrunning. Restart the scheduler when the queue is quiet, and restart it once."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "**Workers** drain themselves on `SIGTERM` (and on a first `SIGINT`; a second\n`SIGINT` exits at once): with `drain_on_shutdown: true`\nthe process tells the scheduler at once that it is draining (a `GoingAway`\nwith the drain flag), so nothing new is dispatched to it, finishes the\nactions it is running, and exits; the scheduler removes it when the stream\ncloses. Without the flag the worker finishes first and announces itself\nlast, and until then the scheduler keeps offering it work it refuses. The\nwait is bounded by `max_action_timeout_s`. `systemctl stop` is therefore\nsafe as long as `TimeoutStopSec` outlasts your longest action. To take a worker out of\nrotation without stopping it, and if your scheduler configuration enables the\n`admin` service on the worker API listener:"
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "The path segment after `scheduler/` is the scheduler's `name` from the config,\nnot a REAPI instance name. The worker finishes what it has and takes nothing\nnew. What loses work is a `SIGKILL` (the stop timer firing, or the OOM\nkiller): every in-flight action fails and re-queues, and in this tree a\ndisconnect **consumes a retry attempt**; enough of them in a row and the action\nfails outright with a message naming OOM-kill or eviction as the likely cause.\nNote that the admin endpoint is unauthenticated;\nSecurity hardening\nexplains why it must not be routable."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "**The CAS** is the safe one. It holds no scheduling state; a restart is a\nwindow of `UNAVAILABLE` responses and nothing more. Its `temp_path` is pruned\non the way back up, so a crash mid-write reclaims itself."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "`systemctl is-active forge-cas forge-scheduler forge-worker`\nprints `active` three times."
  }, {
    "heading": "restarts-and-upgrades",
    "content": "`journalctl -u forge-worker | grep 'Worker registered with scheduler'`\nfinds a line. If instead you find `Could not connect to endpoint` repeating\nabout twice a second, the worker cannot reach the scheduler's worker API\nport."
