export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Content-defined chunking",
  "description": "Cut remote cache transfer bytes by 80-90% for incrementally changing artifacts with the REAPI SplitBlob/SpliceBlob extension and Bazel's --experimental_remote_cache_chunking."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone re-transferring large artifacts that change a\nlittle at a time: container layers, linked binaries, archives. &#x2A;*What you'll\nhave at the end:** clients uploading and downloading only the chunks that\nchanged. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you can edit (see Configuration), and Bazel 9.1.1+ or 8.7.0+."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "When a large build output changes slightly (a relinked binary, a container\nlayer with one file modified), its digest changes, and a conventional remote\ncache re-transfers the whole blob. Content-defined chunking (CDC) splits blobs\ninto chunks at content-derived boundaries, so clients upload and download only\nthe chunks that actually changed. In our measurements a one-file change to a\n16.8 MB tar layer re-uploaded 2.1 MB (87.5% less), and a one-source-file change\nto a linked binary re-uploaded 80.8% less."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge implements the server side of the\nREAPI blob split/splice extension:\n`SpliceBlob` re-assembles chunked uploads (verifying the digest before\ncommitting anything), and `SplitBlob` serves chunk layouts for downloads,\nchunking blobs on demand with FastCDC 2020 when they were uploaded whole, which\nis what makes chunked downloads work for outputs produced by remote execution\nworkers."
  }, {
    "heading": "requirements",
    "content": "**Bazel 9.1.1+ or 8.7.0+** on the client, with\n`--experimental_remote_cache_chunking`. Avoid 9.1.0: it has a client bug that\ncorrupts outputs when the chunking flag is combined with `--disk_cache`\n(fixed in 9.1.1)."
  }, {
    "heading": "requirements",
    "content": "Chunking is **optional and off by default**. Without the configuration below,\nForge behaves exactly as before and does not advertise chunking support,\nso clients fall back to regular transfers."
  }, {
    "heading": "enabling-it",
    "content": "Add an `experimental_chunking` block to the CAS service and give it a small\nstore for chunk layouts. The index store must not verify content digests and\nmust not be the CAS store itself (naming the same store for both is rejected\nat startup):"
  }, {
    "heading": "enabling-it",
    "content": "A complete runnable example lives at\n.\nFor instances whose `cas_store` is a grpc proxy store, omit `index_store`: the\nchunking RPCs are forwarded to the backend, which owns the layouts."
  }, {
    "heading": "enabling-it",
    "content": "Then build with:"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-helps-and-when-it-doesnt",
    "content": "Chunking pays off when clients reach the cache across a real network (WAN,\nmetered links, cross-region) and artifacts change incrementally: uncompressed\narchives, linked binaries, and container layers typically save 80 to 90% of\ntransfer bytes per change. It does little on same-rack links (saved bytes only\nsave time when the wire is the bottleneck) and little for compressed\nartifacts, where everything after the first changed byte re-transfers. Small\nblobs, below the maximum chunk size (4x the average), are not worth chunking\nand clients leave them alone, so hot small-object traffic is unaffected."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-helps-and-when-it-doesnt",
    "content": "The server verifies every spliced blob's digest before committing it and\nmaterializes the full blob, so non-chunking clients and every existing read\npath see ordinary blobs."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-helps-and-when-it-doesnt",
    "content": "Storage grows by roughly the chunk bytes for\nchunk-eligible blobs; pairing the CAS with a `dedup` or `compression` store\ncomposes normally; see Compose stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add a chunk index store**: a small `filesystem` store of its own, not the\nCAS store, and not wrapped in `verify`."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `experimental_chunking`** to the CAS service pointing at it, and make\nsure the instance has a `capabilities` entry so support is advertised."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Build with `--experimental_remote_cache_chunking`**, change one source file,\nand build again."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Compare the re-upload bytes** between the two builds. That difference is the\nwhole point."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The chunk index store fills with small entries while a build runs, and stays\nfar smaller than the CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A one-file change to a large artifact re-uploads a fraction of the artifact,\nnot all of it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Outputs are byte-identical to a build without the flag."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Clients that don't pass the flag keep working against the same instance."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Bazel 9.1.0 has a client bug that corrupts outputs when the chunking flag is\ncombined with `--disk_cache`. Use 9.1.1+ or 8.7.0+."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Support is advertised through the capabilities service, so the instance needs a\n`capabilities` entry. Check the client version too: the flag exists only from\nBazel 9.1.1 / 8.7.0."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Clients do not chunk blobs below the maximum chunk size, 4x\n`avg_chunk_size_bytes`. At the 512 KiB default that's everything under 2 MiB.\nBlobs producing more chunks than `max_chunk_count` are also served whole:\n`SplitBlob` answers `NOT_FOUND` and the client downloads normally."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "It must not be the CAS store, and must not sit under `verify`, because the layouts it\nholds are not content-addressed, so digest verification rejects them. Give it\nits own plain `filesystem` store."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "No. Chunking is opt-in and advertised through the capabilities service, so\nclients that don't request it (or predate it) keep using regular transfers\nagainst the same CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "They solve different transfer problems. Chunking re-transfers only the changed\nparts of large, incrementally-changing blobs;\nwire compression shrinks compressible blobs\nwholesale. Compressed artifacts chunk poorly (everything after the first\nchanged byte re-transfers), so lean on chunking for archives, binaries, and\nlayers, and on compression for text-heavy outputs."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The complementary lever: zstd on the wire for artifacts that compress well."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The store-level `dedup` wrapper: deduplication at rest rather than on the\nwire."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "requirements",
    "content": "Requirements"
  }, {
    "id": "enabling-it",
    "content": "Enabling it"
  }, {
    "id": "when-it-helps-and-when-it-doesnt",
    "content": "When it helps, and when it doesn't"
  }, {
    "id": "steps",
    "content": "Steps"
  }, {
    "id": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "When it doesn't work"
  }]
};
