  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-tag-triggers",
    "content": "Publishing a GitHub Release"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-tag-triggers",
    "content": "Signed binaries, SBOMs, SLSA provenance"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-tag-triggers",
    "content": "`release.yaml` builds three targets (`x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`,\n`aarch64-unknown-linux-musl` and `aarch64-apple-darwin`), signs each with\nkeyless Sigstore cosign, and attaches SLSA Build Level 3 provenance."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-tag-triggers",
    "content": "Cosign v3 stopped emitting the separate `.sig` and `.pem` files. The\nOpenSSF Scorecard `Signed-Releases` check reads the GitHub Releases API and\nlooks for exactly those, so the pin is what keeps that check passing. The\ncosign signatures on the GHCR container images are invisible to it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-tag-triggers",
    "content": "Verify any asset locally:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-version-number-promises",
    "content": "Less than you would assume, and it is better to say so than to imply\notherwise."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-version-number-promises",
    "content": "**No SemVer or compatibility policy is stated anywhere in the\nrepository.** `MODULE.bazel` declares `compatibility_level = 0`, which is\nBazel's way of saying no compatibility guarantee has been asserted. The\nsecurity policy states only that the most recent tagged version is\nsupported."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-version-number-promises",
    "content": "In practice this means: read the release notes for breaking changes rather\nthan inferring from the version number; pin an exact version in production\nrather than a range; and treat a minor bump as something to test, not\nsomething to assume."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-version-number-promises",
    "content": "**Nothing is published to crates.io.** The crates exist for the workspace's\nown use. Consume Forge as a binary, a container image, or a Bazel\nmodule, not as a library dependency."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-version-number-promises",
    "content": "Backward-compatible config changes do get shims: see the `#[serde(untagged)]`\nhelpers in `forge-config`'s `backcompat.rs`, which accept the old shape\nand emit a deprecation warning. That is a convention, not a promise."
  }, {
    "heading": "regenerating-the-configuration-reference",
    "content": "The reference pages under `reference/forge-config/` are generated from\nthe Rust config crate and must never be hand-edited. The chain is:\n`cargo run --bin build-schema --features dev-schema` produces a JSON schema,\n`web/apps/docs/scripts/gen-config-reference.mjs` and `scripts/lib/schema-to-mdx.mjs`\nturn it into MDX, and the whole thing is invoked as:"
  }, {
    "heading": "regenerating-the-configuration-reference",
    "content": "Passing a tag updates `lib/config-versions.ts` (which is what the docs UI\nreads to decide the current version), rewrites the current reference from\nthat tag, and archives the previous one as a versioned page. Historical\nversions do not need regenerating."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "It is not guaranteed to. No SemVer policy is stated, and\n`compatibility_level = 0` in `MODULE.bazel` explicitly asserts none. Read\nthe release notes, which do call out breaking changes and include\nmigration instructions."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "A signed tag attests that a human made the release. Automating it would\nmove the signing key into CI, which is exactly what the signature is\nsupposed to rule out."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not from crates.io; nothing is published there. You can path- or\ngit-depend on it, but with no compatibility policy you are pinning a\ncommit, not an interface."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes. Re-run `Signed release artifacts` from the Actions tab with the tag\nas the input; it rebuilds, re-signs, and attaches the assets to the\nexisting release."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The configuration reference is generated per release and version-switched\nin the UI. Everything else describes the most recent tagged version. If a\npage's claim and your binary disagree, the binary wins, and the page is\na bug worth reporting."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The other half of a contribution: how these pages are built, what the\ncomponents are, and the rules a docs PR is reviewed against."
  }],
