    "heading": "property-types-that-constrain-nothing",
    "content": "A scheduler key configured as `priority` when it should be `exact` or `minimum`\nis the classic version of this bug, and it fails in the *opposite* direction:\nactions get scheduled onto workers that cannot run them. See\nScaling workers for the fleet-shaped version of\nthis problem and Platform properties\nfor the full matching semantics."
  }, {
    "heading": "property-types-that-constrain-nothing",
    "content": "The converse hazard is documented in the config itself: if a worker fails to\nadvertise a property the scheduler requires (`cpu_arch`, say), the scheduler\nwill never send it any work at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-string-you-will-actually-see",
    "content": "Bazel reports `FAILED_PRECONDITION`. Not `NOT_FOUND`. That translation happens\non the worker, which checks for a `NotFound` whose message contains\n`not found in either fast or slow store` and converts it before returning:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-string-you-will-actually-see",
    "content": "The error your developers report will not contain the word \"missing\" and\nwill not look like a cache problem. The underlying message is:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-string-you-will-actually-see",
    "content": "The worker matches on the substring `not found in either fast or slow store`.\nIt is a real coupling between two crates, not a cosmetic message; do not\n\"improve\" it without changing both sides."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-string-you-will-actually-see",
    "content": "The same warning is pre-announced at worker startup, which is worth grepping\nfor when you first bring a fleet up:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-causes",
    "content": "**Workers do not share CAS storage.** The fix is exactly what the message says:\nevery worker's `fast_slow` slow tier must resolve to the same CAS the clients\nupload to. In Compose, `docker-compose-multi-worker.yml` does that by pointing\neach worker's slow store at the `cas-server` over gRPC (the `cas-data` volume\nit also mounts into the workers is not what shares the data); in Kubernetes,\na shared backing store rather than per-pod local storage. See\nDeploy with Docker Compose."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-causes",
    "content": "**Cache pressure evicted the blob.** A different string, and a much more\nhelpful one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-causes",
    "content": "Take that advice literally, and read\nthe disk runbook before you pick a\nnumber; the store can reach roughly twice `max_bytes` on disk."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-near-miss-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Zero-byte outputs are not stored. Callers materialise them directly. If you see\nthis in a stack trace it is a caller bug, not a cache problem."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-timeout-message-that-tells-you-nothing",
    "content": "This means the client stopped waiting, and `client_action_timeout_s` (default\n60 s) cleaned the operation up. It is a statement about the *client*, not about\nwhy the action never ran."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-timeout-message-that-tells-you-nothing",
    "content": "It is the message operators find when chasing a hung build, and it is\nconsistently misleading: the action was almost certainly stuck in\nthe silent queue, and this\nstring is just the tombstone."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-timeout-message-that-tells-you-nothing",
    "content": "The scheduler logs an error at startup if you do, because a client that is\nstill actively waiting will be reaped between keepalives."
  }, {
