    "content": "Remote Asset protocol, push half"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`push_store`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`worker_api`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "Where workers connect and are handed actions"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`scheduler`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`experimental_bep`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "Build Event Protocol consumption"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`store`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`admin`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "REST endpoint for administrative tasks"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "nothing (path defaults to `/admin`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`health`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "Health check endpoint"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "nothing (path defaults to `/status`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "A cache-only deployment needs the first four. Adding execution adds\n`execution` and `worker_api`. The rest are opt-in."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-eleven-services",
    "content": "`capabilities` is the one that surprises people: it carries almost no config,\nbut Bazel queries it before doing anything else and fails the build if the\ncall is not served, with an error about querying capabilities rather than one\nnaming a missing service. And if `capabilities` has no `remote_execution`\nblock, the response carries no `execution_capabilities`, so the client is\ntold this endpoint caches but does not execute, which is correct for a\ncache-only Getting started deployment and a silent bug once you add Remote\nexecution."
  }, {
    "heading": "instance-names",
    "content": "Most services are declared as an **array**, and each element carries an\n`instance_name`. That field is a routing key: it selects which store (or\nscheduler) serves a request. The instance name is not part of the storage\nkey, so two instance names pointing at *different* stores are fully isolated,\nand two pointing at the *same* store share every blob."
  }, {
    "heading": "instance-names",
    "content": "That pattern (the same store registered twice, once under the empty name and\nonce under `main`) appears throughout the shipped examples, and it relies on\nthe sharing: clients differ in what they send, some omit the instance name\nentirely, and registering both means either kind of client works, against one\ncache, without a config change on their side."
  }, {
    "heading": "instance-names",
    "content": "Pick one name per logical environment. `main` for the production cache,\n`<repo-name>` for per-repo isolation, `<something>-experiments` for a sandbox\nthat shouldn't pollute the real cache, each pointed at its own store. Every\nclient targeting a given cache must use the same one."
  }, {
    "heading": "instance-names",
    "content": "A client using an instance name the server has not registered gets an\nerror on every CAS and AC call (`'instance_name' not configured for\n  '<name>'`), not a silently empty cache. Bazel surfaces that as remote cache\nwarnings and zero hits. If a new client sees 0% hit rate against a cache\nthat's demonstrably working, compare instance names before anything else."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "The `worker_api` service is different from every other service on this page,\nand the difference is a security boundary rather than a design preference."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "A client calling the CAS can upload and fetch blobs. A worker connected to the\nworker API is *handed actions to execute* and reports their results as\nauthoritative. Those are not the same permission, and the worker API has no\nauthentication of its own to distinguish them."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "The rule: &#x2A;*`worker_api` gets its own listener, on its own port, with no\nother service sharing it.** The shipped examples put the public API on\n`:50051` and the worker API on `:50061`, and the second port is the one you\nnever expose beyond the cluster:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "Note that `worker_api` takes a single object rather than an array; it has no\n`instance_name`, because it isn't part of the client-facing protocol at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "The upstream config comment is unusually direct about this: serving\n`worker_api` alongside client services \"is a security risk, as workers have\na different permission set than a client that makes the remote\nexecution/cache requests.\" Treat the worker port as an internal backend API:\nbound to a private interface, firewalled, or reachable only inside the\ncluster network."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-split",
    "content": "`admin` and `health` sit on the private port in the examples for the same\nreason. A build event protocol endpoint (`experimental_bep`), when you enable\nit, can go on its own listener so it can be scaled and secured independently\nof both."
  }, {
    "heading": "tls-when-you-need-it",
    "content": "TLS is a property of the listener:"
  }, {
    "heading": "tls-when-you-need-it",
    "content": "Adding `client_ca_file` turns that into mutual TLS (clients must present a\ncertificate signed by that CA) and `client_crl_file` lets you revoke one.\nThat's the closest thing Forge has to built-in client authentication, and\nit is a reasonable answer for a cache exposed beyond a trusted network."
  }, {
    "heading": "tls-when-you-need-it",
    "content": "`deployment-examples/docker-compose/` includes a TLS listener using files\nnamed `example-do-not-use-in-prod-rootca.crt` and\n`example-do-not-use-in-prod-key.pem`. The names are the documentation. Use\nthem to see the shape, never to serve anything."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes; the array is unbounded, and separating concerns by port is the intended\nuse. `deployment-examples/docker-compose/local-storage-cas.json5` serves the\nsame cache twice, plain on `:50051` and TLS on `:50071`. A third listener for\nbuild events, or a plain-HTTP health endpoint a load balancer can probe\nwithout TLS, follows the same pattern."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "REAPI splits them deliberately. The `cas` service handles batched reads and\nwrites of small blobs; the ByteStream service streams large ones. Bazel\nstrongly prefers the streaming interface for anything sizeable, so a cache\nthat serves `cas` but not `bytestream` will appear to work and then fail on\nyour first large artifact. Point both at the same store."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "It exposes a small REST endpoint for administrative operations at a\nconfigurable path, defaulting to `/admin`. It is not an authenticated control\nplane; it belongs on the private port with the worker API, not on anything\nreachable from outside the cluster."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "You need it *because* you have a load balancer. It serves `/status` by default\nand takes a `timeout_seconds` for the checks it runs. Without it, a probe can\nonly test whether the port accepts a TCP connection, which stays true long\nafter a process has stopped being useful."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Clients can read cached action results but cannot write new ones. It's the\nright setting for an endpoint you expose to untrusted or unverified builds\n(they benefit from the cache without being able to poison it) while a trusted\nCI endpoint on a different port writes to the same store."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The minimum config for each, and the properties contract that decides\nwhether an action ever gets picked up."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Once the ports are right, this is how you find out what's happening on them."
  }],
