export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Autonomous operation",
  "description": "What it takes for a build farm to grow and shrink with demand without anyone watching it: which properties Forge gives you, which pieces you assemble yourself, and where the seams are."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** whoever would otherwise be paged about the build farm.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** an accurate picture of how close Forge\ngets you to a hands-off farm, and exactly which parts you build. &#x2A;*Time:**\nabout fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape-of-the-problem",
    "content": "Build load is spiky in a way that punishes static capacity. It is near zero at\nnight, ragged through the morning, and vicious in the hour before a release. A\nfarm sized for the peak is idle most of the time and expensive all of the time.\nA farm sized for the average has a queue during every moment anybody cares\nabout."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape-of-the-problem",
    "content": "The way out is a farm that changes size on its own. That is not a Forge\nfeature you switch on. It's a property that emerges when the components have\nthe right characteristics, and most of this page is about which characteristics\nthose are and which ones you have to supply."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "Autoscaling only works if adding and removing a machine is uneventful. Three\nproperties of the worker make it so."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "**Workers hold no authoritative state.** A worker executes actions and writes\nresults to the CAS. Everything durable lives in the store, not on the worker, so\na worker that vanishes takes nothing with it that isn't reproducible."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "**Workers announce themselves.** A worker dials the scheduler and registers,\nrather than the scheduler being told a list of workers. Starting five more is\nenough; there is no membership to update. That's why\nrunning multiple workers is mostly a matter of\nstarting more processes."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "**Lost work is retried, not lost.** When a worker disappears mid-action, the\nscheduler notices, increments an attempt counter, and requeues the action,\nwith its worker affinity cleared, so it goes to whichever worker is free rather\nthan waiting for a machine that is gone."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "That last property is what makes preemptible and spot instances viable, and it\nis the single largest cost lever available. The retry budget is\n`max_job_retries` on the scheduler, defaulting to **3**."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "A configured `0` is read as \"unset\" and falls back to the default of 3. There\nis no way to express \"never retry\" through this field."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-workers-can-be-treated-as-disposable",
    "content": "A worker is declared dead when it stops keepaliving for `worker_timeout_s`,\nwhich defaults to **5 seconds** and is checked once a second. That is a\ndeliberately short leash: fast detection means fast requeue, and the cost of a\nfalse positive is one repeated action rather than a stall."
  }, {
    "heading": "draining-for-the-shutdown-you-know-about",
    "content": "Retries handle the machines that die without warning. Machines that give you\nnotice deserve better, and there's a specific hook for it."
  }, {
    "heading": "draining-for-the-shutdown-you-know-about",
    "content": "The admin interface exposes a drain endpoint:"
  }, {
    "heading": "draining-for-the-shutdown-you-know-about",
    "content": "A draining worker accepts no new actions and finishes the ones it is running.\nThat is exactly the right response to a spot-termination notice or a rolling\nnode upgrade: call it when the notice arrives, and the in-flight work completes\ninstead of being thrown away and redone."
  }, {
    "heading": "draining-for-the-shutdown-you-know-about",
    "content": "Nothing in the repository wires this to any cloud provider's interruption\nsignal. The handler is a short script you write, and it is the highest-value\npiece of glue on this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-scheduler-remembers-and-what-it-forgets",
    "content": "This is the constraint that shapes a hands-off design, so it's worth being\nprecise."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-scheduler-remembers-and-what-it-forgets",
    "content": "By default the scheduler keeps its queue **in process memory**. Restarting it\nloses the in-flight queue. Clients retry and the work gets done, so this is not\ndata loss in any lasting sense, but it does mean the scheduler is the one\ncomponent you cannot casually cycle under load, and that a single scheduler is a\nsingle point of interruption."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-scheduler-remembers-and-what-it-forgets",
    "content": "A Redis-backed alternative exists, and the configuration names it honestly:\nthe field is `experimental_backend`. It moves queue state out of the process so\nschedulers stop being special. It also carries the rough edges the name implies:\nthere's a `fallback_match_interval_s` in the config specifically to paper over\neventually-consistent searches."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-scheduler-remembers-and-what-it-forgets",
    "content": "`execution.active.count`, the signal an autoscaler needs, is emitted only by\nthe in-memory backend. The Redis path emits none of it, so the topology that\nmakes schedulers replaceable is also the topology where the scaling signal\ndisappears. Know this before you plan around it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "An autoscaler needs a number that says \"more work is waiting than there is\ncapacity for.\" That number is queue depth: actions sitting in the `queued`\nstage rather than `executing`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "Forge emits it as `execution.active.count`, an up-down counter attributed\nby `execution.stage`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "Three things about that metric decide your architecture, and all three surprise\npeople:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "**Telemetry is OTLP push, not Prometheus pull.** The binary has no `/metrics`\nendpoint to scrape. It pushes OpenTelemetry to a collector, and the collector is\nwhat a Prometheus-shaped world reads from.\n`deployment-examples/metrics/` is the working example of that pipeline."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "**Queue depth is a derived series, not a metric name.** What you scale on is a\nrecording rule that sums the counter filtered to the `queued` stage. The\nexample rules in `deployment-examples/metrics/prometheus-recording-rules.yml`\ndefine `forge:queue_depth`, `forge:worker_utilization` and\n`forge:actions_per_worker`. An HPA reads the recording rule through an\nexternal-metrics adapter; there is no metric the binary emits that an HPA can\ntarget directly."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "**The metrics module is Business Source Licensed.** `forge-util/src/metrics.rs`\ncarries a header requiring an enterprise license agreement, unlike the\nFunctional Source License covering most of the repository. Production\nobservability is exactly the case that header describes, so settle the licensing\nquestion before you design around it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-signal-you-scale-on",
    "content": "Every utilization number is inferred scheduler-side from what it knows about\nits workers. No worker-side telemetry exists to scrape, so host-level CPU\nand memory from your existing node monitoring is the complement to the\nscheduler's view, not a duplicate of it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt",
    "content": "Being direct, because this is the part most likely to be assumed:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt",
    "content": "**The repository contains no autoscaling manifests for Forge.** The Helm\ncharts for Kubernetes ship with Forge Enterprise,\nnot with this repository. The Kustomize building blocks under `kubernetes/`\nset fixed replica counts. No HorizontalPodAutoscaler for workers, no KEDA `ScaledObject`,\nno cluster-autoscaler or Karpenter configuration, and no node pools or\ntolerations for build workers. The one HPA in the tree scales the OpenTelemetry\ncollector, not Forge."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt",
    "content": "What the repository does give you is the harder half: a worker that is safe to\nkill, a scheduler that requeues what it loses, a drain hook for planned\nshutdown, a queue-depth signal, and a working metrics pipeline to carry it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt",
    "content": "What you assemble is the standard Kubernetes half: an HPA on the derived queue\nmetric via an external-metrics adapter, a cluster autoscaler or Karpenter for\nthe nodes underneath, spot node pools with the drain hook wired to interruption\nnotices, and node affinity for heterogeneous pools. These are not Forge\nconcepts, which is the point: nothing here needs a bespoke controller."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt",
    "content": "Kubernetes deployments, including the Helm charts, are part of\nForge Enterprise. This repository ships\nonly the Kustomize building blocks under `kubernetes/` that its own examples\nuse, and no KEDA adapter."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-no-human-in-the-loop-actually-means",
    "content": "A realistic end state, in the order the pieces earn their keep:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-no-human-in-the-loop-actually-means",
    "content": "Workers scale on queue depth and fall to a small floor overnight. Most of the\nfleet is preemptible, because retries make interruption a cost rather than an\nincident, and the drain hook converts warned shutdowns into clean ones. Node\ncapacity follows pod demand through your existing autoscaler. The CAS scales\nseparately and more conservatively, because it holds the state everything else\ndepends on. Alerts fire on the things automation cannot resolve (sustained\nqueue depth that scaling isn't clearing, error rates, storage approaching its\nlimit) rather than on capacity, which now handles itself."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-no-human-in-the-loop-actually-means",
    "content": "The queue-depth alert is the one to get right. It's the signal that your\nautoscaler has hit a ceiling it cannot pass, whether that's a quota, a node\npool's maximum, or an exhausted spot market. Everything else is noise by\ncomparison."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "More carefully. Workers are stateless and cheap to churn; the CAS holds the data\neverything else is asking for. Stateless CAS replicas in front of one shared\nbacking store scale on request rate, but a local filesystem tier is warm state\nthat a new replica does not have, so scaling in throws away cache locality.\nScale it slowly, and prefer bigger replicas over more of them.\nTuning covers the storage path."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The scheduler notices the worker stop keepaliving within `worker_timeout_s`,\nincrements the action's attempt count, clears its worker affinity, and requeues\nit. Any worker can pick it up. Once the attempt count passes `max_job_retries`\nthe action completes with an error saying it attempted to execute too many\ntimes, so a systematically failing action does not requeue forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "For most deployments, yes: the scheduler does bookkeeping, not the expensive\nwork, and it fails over by restarting. The reasons to want more are availability\nacross zones and not wanting a restart to drop the in-flight queue. Both point\nat the Redis backend, whose name says `experimental` and which stops emitting the\nqueue metric. That trade is the current state of the art here."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Less aggressively than they scale out. Scaling out has a bounded cost, a\nmachine you briefly didn't need. Scaling in during a lull that ends thirty\nseconds later costs a cold start on every action that arrives next. Generous\nscale-up, patient scale-down."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Anything that ingests OTLP works, and the collector can fan out to several\ndestinations at once. Prometheus appears throughout the examples because the\nrecording rules and the HPA external-metrics adapter are expressed in it, but the\nrequirement is OTLP ingestion plus something an autoscaler can query, not\nPrometheus specifically."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The levers underneath all of this: worker sizing, the storage path, and what\nto change when the queue is deep."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Getting the telemetry out of the binary and into something that can act on\nit, which is the prerequisite for every scaling decision here."
  }],
  "headings": [{
