    "content": "none"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "They talk to each other by service name. The worker config reaches the\nscheduler at `grpc://${SCHEDULER_ENDPOINT:-127.0.0.1}:50061` and the CAS at\n`grpc://${CAS_ENDPOINT:-127.0.0.1}:50051`; Compose sets both variables, and\nForge expands them at config-parse time; see\nCLI and environment."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "Cache and executor are **different endpoints**. That is not an accident of the\nexample: the CAS process and the scheduler process are genuinely separate\nhere, exactly as they are in the\nproduction shape."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "`cpu_count` is what the shipped README passes. The scheduler declares it as a\n`minimum` platform property and the worker advertises it from `nproc`, so an\naction is only matched to a worker with at least that many cores; ask for more\nthan the worker has and the action queues silently forever. Omitting the\nproperty is fine: an action with no platform properties matches any worker. See\nplatform properties."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Only the fields carrying a decision. Everything else in the shipped files is\nalready correct for a single host."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "File"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Why you'd change it"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`eviction_policy.max_bytes`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`local-storage-cas.json5`, `worker.json5`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Ships at 10 GB for the CAS and 500 MB for the AC, plus a separate 10 GB fast tier for the worker, all under the same host directory. Size them to your disk. The worker's tier can reach roughly **twice** its `max_bytes`, because the executable-variant directory the worker creates next to `content_path` is not counted against it. See Runbooks."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`FORGE_DIR`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "environment"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Where the CAS and worker caches land on the host. Defaults to `~/.cache/forge`."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`RUST_LOG`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "environment"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Ships at `warn`, which hides the scheduler's `INFO`-level matching diagnostics. Set `info` the first time something does not run."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`platform_properties`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`worker.json5`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "The worker's advertised capabilities. Whatever you add here must also appear in the scheduler's `supported_platform_properties`, or the scheduler rejects the worker's registration with `Unknown platform property`."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`ADDITIONAL_SETUP_WORKER_CMD`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "build arg"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "A bash string run inside the final image. This is the hook for installing whatever toolchain your actions need."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`experimental_chunking`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`local-storage-cas.json5`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "Enabled and pointed at a separate `CHUNK_INDEX_STORE`, for Bazel's `--experimental_remote_cache_chunking`. Remove both if you are not using that flag."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-change",
    "content": "`CHUNK_INDEX_STORE` holds blob-to-chunk layouts, not blobs. It must be a\nseparate store that does not verify digests; pointing `index_store` at\n`CAS_MAIN_STORE` produces verification failures on every split."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "`docker-compose-multi-worker.yml` runs one CAS, one scheduler and three\nworkers sharing a `cas-data` volume:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "The shared volume is the entire point, and the README says so more loudly than\nanything else in it: &#x2A;*all workers must share the same CAS storage path.**\nWhen they do not, actions fail with"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "which the worker reports to the scheduler as `FAILED_PRECONDITION` with that\nmessage intact, so that is the text your developers will see.\nTroubleshooting\nhas the full path."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "The first two are cosmetic if you know about them and confusing if you do not."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "The `healthcheck` on `cas-server` and `scheduler` curls `/status` on ports\n50051 and 50052. **Neither of those listeners has the `health` service\nenabled**; only the 50061 listener does. Both healthchecks therefore fail\npermanently. Nothing depends on them (`depends_on` uses\n`condition: service_started`), so the stack works anyway, but\n`docker compose ps` will show the containers as unhealthy forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "It publishes `50061:50061` on **all interfaces**, unlike\n`docker-compose.yml`, which does not publish the scheduler's 50061 at all.\nPort 50061 is the worker API. Read\nSecurity hardening\nbefore running this on anything with a routable address."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-multi-worker-variant",
    "content": "The shipped `worker-shared-cas.json5` keeps the worker's fast tier under\n`/root/.cache/forge/`, not under the `/data/cas` mount, so the three\nworkers do not actually share the volume the compose file mounts for them.\nPoint `content_path` and `temp_path` at `/data/cas/...` if you want the\nsharing the README describes."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-makes-this-a-development-setup",
    "content": "Four things, all of them deliberate in the example and all of them\ndisqualifying for production."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-executor-runs-privileged",
    "content": "`worker.json5` sets `use_namespaces: true` and `use_mount_namespace: true`,\nwhich need `CLONE_NEWUSER`/`CLONE_NEWNS`, hence the flag. Note what this buys\nand what it does not: namespaces here are a hermeticity and process-reaping\nmechanism, not a security boundary. Actions still run as the worker's own uid\nthrough an identity uid map, still see the host filesystem apart from a\nprivate `/tmp`, and still have network access to every Forge port. A\nprivileged container running\narbitrary build actions is a lateral-movement primitive.\nSecurity hardening\ncovers what the sandbox actually does."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-tls-certificates-are-expired-examples",
    "content": "The 50071 listener loads\n`example-do-not-use-in-prod-rootca.crt` and `example-do-not-use-in-prod-key.pem`\nfrom the same directory. They are committed to the repository, self-signed,\n`CN=localhost`, and **expired in October 2024**. Every client must pass\n`--insecure` or equivalent, which is what the repository's own TLS integration\ntest does. No certificate-generation script exists anywhere in the tree; you\nsupply real material yourself."
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-authentication-on-anything",
    "content": "Not a Compose limitation: open-source Forge has no inbound\nauthentication on any service, in any deployment. `docker-compose.yml` does not\npublish the scheduler's port 50061 at all; the worker reaches it over the\nCompose network by service name. That is the only thing standing between an\nattacker on your network and the ability to register as a worker. Keep it\nthat way."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-build-is-not-fast",
    "content": "The `Dockerfile` compiles Forge from the repository root with Bazel, in a\nthree-stage build, and the final image is a fresh Ubuntu with the binary copied\nin and `curl` installed. The Ubuntu base is digest-pinned and the bazelisk\ndownload is checksum-verified, so the build is reproducible, but it is a full\nfrom-source build every time the layer cache misses. For repeated use, build once and push the image to a\nregistry your hosts can pull from."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-build-is-not-fast",
    "content": "`docker compose ps` shows all three services `Up`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-build-is-not-fast",
    "content": "A `bazel build` with the two flags above completes. Run it twice; the second\nrun reports a high `remote cache hit` count in the summary line."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-build-is-not-fast",
    "content": "`docker compose logs forge_executor` contains\n`Worker registered with scheduler`. If it instead repeats\n`Could not connect to endpoint grpc://forge_scheduler:50061` roughly\ntwice a second, the worker never reached port 50061; reconnect is a hard-coded\n0.5-second retry with no backoff, so that cadence is the signature."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-build-is-not-fast",
    "content": "`ls ~/.cache/forge` shows `content_path-cas` growing as you build."
  }, {
