import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Local Remote Execution",
  "description": "What LRE actually is: a way to make your local toolchain and your remote workers the same toolchain, so both sides share one cache."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone deciding whether LRE is worth adopting, and\nanyone who has read about it and formed the wrong model of what it does.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** the actual mechanism, the guarantee it makes\nand its exact qualifiers, and what it does not cover. &#x2A;*Time:** fifteen\nminutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Local Remote Execution is a way of building your toolchain with Nix such that\n**the exact same toolchain binaries are used by your local build and by your\nremote workers**. Because the toolchain is part of every action's digest, the\ntwo sides then produce identical action digests, and a cache entry written\nby one is a cache hit for the other."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "That is the whole idea. It is worth stating what it is *not*, because the\nname invites a different reading."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "No local Forge worker exists in this design, and no container runs\non your machine during a build. Your local build is an ordinary local\nBazel build. What LRE changes is *which compiler that build invokes*. The\ntoolchain container images are, in the source's own words, \"never really\ninstantiated\"; they exist to have their environment extracted."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "The trick is absolute paths."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "A Nix-built toolchain lives at a path like\n`/nix/store/<hash>-clang-…/bin/clang`, where the hash is derived from every\ninput that produced it. LRE generates Bazel toolchain configurations that\nhardcode those absolute store paths: not `/usr/bin/cc`, not a `PATH` lookup,\nthe literal store path."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "Then it arranges for that exact path to exist on both sides:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "**On the remote side**, the worker image is built with `nix2container` and\nits environment is composed entirely of `/nix/store/…` paths. The image is a\ncarrier for that closure rather than a conventional root filesystem."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "**On the local side**, entering the Nix development shell realizes the same\nderivations into your own `/nix/store`. This is what the commented-out `PATH`\nlines in the generated `lre.bazelrc` are for: Bazel ignores them, but Nix\nneeds a reason to fetch the closure, and those lines are it. The"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "says so directly: \"all paths printed in `lre.bazelrc` will be available on\nyour local system.\""
  }, {
    "heading": "the-mechanism-precisely",
    "content": "What is shared between local and remote is **the store paths**, not the\nimage. Understanding that explains almost every question people have about\nLRE's edges."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-guarantee-with-its-qualifiers-intact",
    "content": "The source states the promise carefully, and the qualifiers are load-bearing:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-guarantee-with-its-qualifiers-intact",
    "content": "> a framework to build, distribute, and rapidly iterate on custom toolchain\n> setups that are transparent, fully hermetic, and reproducible **across\n> machines of the same system architecture** \\[…] This lets you reuse build\n> artifacts with **virtually** perfect cache hit rate."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-guarantee-with-its-qualifiers-intact",
    "content": "Both emphases are the source's meaning, not an editorial softening.\nReproducibility is claimed within an architecture, not across them; an\n`aarch64-darwin` laptop and an `x86_64-linux` worker are not going to share\nC++ artifacts, because they are not running the same binaries. And \"virtually\nperfect\" acknowledges that the toolchain is one input among several, and LRE\nonly pins the toolchain."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-guarantee-with-its-qualifiers-intact",
    "content": "The repository's own documentation opens with a warning that LRE is \"highly\nexperimental and can be challenging to set up in downstream projects\", and\na note that at the time of writing it \"only works on `x86_64-linux`\". The\nRust toolchains have since grown to four systems; the C++ toolchain has\nnot. Treat the setup cost as real."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-pinned-and-what-is-not",
    "content": "This is where careful reading pays off."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-pinned-and-what-is-not",
    "content": "**Not the image digest.** Every `container-image` value in the generated\nplatforms is `name:tag`, where the tag is a Nix derivation hash, never a\n`sha256:` content digest. The upstream config generator's digest-resolution\nstep was deliberately patched out, with the in-source justification that\n\"skipping this hash is not a generally safe practice and only works for us\nbecause our images are reproducible.\""
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-pinned-and-what-is-not",
    "content": "**Not a URL that gets pulled.** `container-image` is a *raw string matched by\nthe scheduler* against what a worker advertises. Forge does not fetch it.\nTwo different conventions coexist in-tree (`docker://lre-cc:<tag>` for C++\nand a `ghcr.io/...` reference for Rust), and a downstream user has to match\nwhatever string their own workers advertise. See\nplatform properties."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-pinned-and-what-is-not",
    "content": "**Not the image you actually deploy.** Three distinct images are in play: a\ngenerator image, the image the platform config nominally names, and the\nworker image that actually runs. The repository warns about this in its own\ndeployment manifests. The relationship is maintained by convention and by\ntag propagation, not by any mechanism that would catch a mismatch."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-pinned-and-what-is-not",
    "content": "**Not a single shared closure.** The local closure is whatever the dev shell\nrealizes; the worker image's closure is that plus extras the worker needs.\nThey are compatible by construction, not identical."
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Language"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Status"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "**Rust**"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Full toolchains across four Nix systems, stable and nightly"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "**C/C++**"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Full toolchain, **`x86_64-linux` only**"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "**Java**"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "An image and a generated runtime exist, but no Bazel toolchain is registered"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "**Python, Go**"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Not provided"
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "Windows is not supported. On macOS the C++ path has no local toolchain at\nall (the flake module only emits the C++ platform and toolchain flags on\nLinux), so an `lre-cc` action can only run on a Linux worker carrying the\nimage; you get hermeticity there, not locality."
  }, {
    "heading": "coverage",
    "content": "It is tempting to read \"local remote execution\" as \"remote execution without\nthe network\". It is not. LRE pins the toolchain; whether a given action\nruns locally or on a remote worker is a separate question decided by\nplatform matching. On the platforms where the toolchain exists locally,\nbuilds are local, but that is a consequence, not the guarantee."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-nix-specifically",
    "content": "Because the design needs every tool the build invokes to be identified by a\nhash of everything that produced it, and to live at a path that encodes that\nhash. That is precisely what a Nix store path is."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-nix-specifically",
    "content": "`/usr/bin/clang` cannot be pinned. Its identity is whatever your package\nmanager last installed, it differs across machines that both call themselves\n\"Ubuntu 22.04\", and no amount of hashing the binary recovers the headers and\nlibraries it will reach for. `/nix/store/<hash>-clang-…` has already answered\nall of those questions by existing."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-nix-specifically",
    "content": "Everything else about LRE follows from that one property. The container\nimages, the generated Bazel toolchains, the `lre.bazelrc`, all of it is\nplumbing to get those store paths onto both sides of the build."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-nix-specifically",
    "content": "For Nix itself, see Develop with Nix."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Situation"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "LRE?"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Cache hits are poor between developers and CI on the same architecture"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Yes; this is the case it was built for"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "You already build with Bazel and already use Nix"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Yes; the marginal cost is small"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "You want C++ hermeticity on `x86_64-linux`"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Yes"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Your fleet is mixed-architecture and you expected cross-arch sharing"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "No; the guarantee does not extend there"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "You need Python or Go toolchains pinned"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "Not yet"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "You are looking for sandboxing or isolation"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "No; different problem, see correctness and hermeticity"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "You want a quick win with a low setup cost"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-is-worth-it",
    "content": "No; the source calls the setup challenging, and means it"
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "The flake-side and Bazel-side wiring, the regeneration commands, and the\ndownstream-project setup are in\n.\nFor actually running it (the commands, and what is local versus remote once\nyou leave `x86_64-linux`) see\ntoolchains and hermeticity,\nwhich is in the Remote execution section."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No, and it does not try to. LRE is about toolchain identity, not about\nwhere work runs. Fan-out across hundreds of cores still wants remote\nworkers; the point is that the workers and your laptop now agree on what\na compiler is, so both populate one cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The README lists \"a functional local container setup\" alongside Nix as a\nprerequisite. Where it is actually exercised in-tree is regenerating the\ntoolchain configurations, which loads a generator image into the local\ncontainer daemon; consuming the checked-in configs is driven by Nix."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Compare the `/nix/store/...` paths in your generated `lre.bazelrc`\nagainst the ones in the toolchain configs of the `local-remote-execution`\nmodule at the commit your project pins. A mismatch there is the usual\ncause, and it usually means the flake input and the Bazel module override\nhave drifted apart. They are pinned in two separate files and nothing\nchecks that they agree."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No. `generate-toolchains` regenerates the Java configuration only; C++ and\nRust have their own separate commands. No single in-tree target runs all\nthree, so a flake update needs all of them."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not quite, and the source says so. In-tree TODOs in\n`local-remote-execution/overlays/stdenv.nix` record that the result is\nnot a pure Clang/LLVM toolchain, that an unwanted `libgcc_s` dependency\nremains, and that the stdenv used for the Cargo build and the \"mostly\nhermetic\" LLVM toolchain used by the Bazel build are still two separate\ntoolchains. LRE substantially improves hermeticity; it does not currently\ncomplete it."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The same ideas as commands you can run, in the Remote execution section."
  }],
