  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "`existence_cache`"
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "Answer existence without touching the backend; single process only."
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "AC hits point at missing blobs"
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "`completeness_checking`"
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "Re-check the CAS on every AC read."
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "Three details of composition matter for capacity specifically and aren't\napparent from the individual recipes."
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "**`fast_slow` populates the fast tier on a read miss**, streaming from the slow\nstore to the caller and into the fast store at the same time. That's what makes\nthe hot tier fill itself. It also means a single large cold blob can evict a lot\nof hot working set, which is what `bypass_dedup_threshold_bytes` is for: at or\nabove that size the read goes straight to the slow store and does not\npopulate. It defaults to `0`, which disables the bypass."
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "**`size_partitioning` splits at `size`, exclusive on the low side**: a blob of\nexactly that many bytes goes to the *upper* store. It also rejects non-digest\nkeys outright, so it must not sit anywhere string-keyed traffic flows."
  }, {
    "heading": "scale-vertically-first-by-composing",
    "content": "**A memory store silently drops blobs at or above its `max_bytes`.** The write\nis skipped with a log line rather than an error, and the blob is served from the\nbacking store instead. If a memory fast tier appears to be doing nothing, this\nis usually why."
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "Four fields, all on `EvictionPolicy`, all defaulting to `0`, and `0` means\n*disabled* in every case. A policy with no fields set is an unbounded store."
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "`max_bytes` is the ceiling. `evict_bytes` is the low watermark: when eviction\ntriggers, keep going until the store is down to `max_bytes - evict_bytes`.\n`max_seconds` expires entries by age since last access. `max_count` caps the\nnumber of entries."
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "With no low watermark configured, eviction evicts the bare minimum: it pops\nLRU entries only until the store is back under `max_bytes`, then stops. The\nstore sits pinned at its high-water mark, and the next write puts it over\nagain. Every example config in the repository leaves `evict_bytes` unset.\nSetting it to some fraction of `max_bytes` is the single cheapest tuning\nchange available on this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "Two more behaviours to plan around:"
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "**No background eviction timer exists.** Eviction runs on inserts and\nremovals (and when a worker releases a lease on an input it was using). Reads\nperform a lazy per-key expiry check on the key being read and nothing else. The consequence is that **an idle store never shrinks**; a CAS\nthat goes over quota and then stops receiving writes stays over quota, and\n`max_seconds` only takes effect on entries someone happens to ask for."
  }, {
    "heading": "eviction-is-your-capacity-control-and-its-default-thrashes",
    "content": "**Eviction is whole entries, one at a time.** It does no partial or chunked\neviction; the unit is one blob, or one file for a filesystem store."
  }, {
    "heading": "size-the-ac-and-the-cas-together",
    "content": "The Action Cache is just another store with its own `EvictionPolicy`; there is\nno separate mechanism. The shipped examples give it roughly a twentieth of the\nCAS budget, which is a reasonable starting ratio because AC entries are tiny."
  }, {
    "heading": "size-the-ac-and-the-cas-together",
    "content": "That asymmetry creates the hazard. AC entries are small and survive eviction\nreadily; the CAS blobs they *point at* are large and get evicted first. The\nresult is an AC hit that resolves to missing content. `completeness_checking`\nexists precisely for this: it re-queries the CAS on every AC read and treats an\nincomplete result as a miss. It costs a CAS round trip per AC hit, and on a\nshared production cache it is worth it."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "`shard` distributes keys across several backends by hash, with an optional\ninteger `weight` per shard. The weights are turned into a cumulative table over\nthe `u32` space at startup and the key is binary-searched into it."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "Two properties decide whether it fits."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "**Placement includes the blob's size.** The hash folds the digest *and* its\ndeclared size, so the same content at a different declared size lands in a\ndifferent shard. This is fine (it's still deterministic), but it means you\ncannot reason about placement from the hash alone."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "**Nothing rebalances.** No migration, no virtual-node consistent hashing,\nno read repair, no re-shard command. Adding, removing, or reweighting a shard\nmoves the boundaries and remaps a large fraction of keys. The data is still\nphysically where it was written; lookups just go somewhere else and never find\nit."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "Plan a shard-list change the way you would plan wiping the cache: during a\nquiet window, with the expectation that the next build is cold. Size the shard\ncount for where you expect to be in a year rather than for today."
  }, {
    "heading": "sharding-and-what-changing-it-costs",
    "content": "Sharding is the right tool when one backend is genuinely the ceiling: a single\nRedis instance's memory, one filesystem's IOPS. It is the wrong tool for\ncapacity you could have bought by pointing at object storage instead."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "Everything above scales one process. If you have exhausted that, here is the\norder to add replicas in."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Move the backing store off local disk first.** Replicas only make sense over\nstorage they can share: S3 or a compatible object store, GCS, Azure, or Redis.\nA filesystem store cannot be shared between processes at all, so this is a\nprerequisite rather than an optimisation. S3 and\ncompatible and Redis\nare the recipes."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Keep the per-replica fast tier local and private.** Each replica gets its own\n`fast_slow` hot tier on its own disk, pointed at the shared slow store. This is\nthe topology that works: shared truth underneath, independent caches above.\nAccept that the hot tiers diverge; they are caches of a cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Remove `existence_cache` from the replicated path**, or accept that it will\nserve stale positives. No cross-process invalidation exists except for a\nRedis backend with keyspace notifications enabled. If\n`FindMissingBlobs` latency is why you wanted it, a `fast_slow` hot tier gets you\nmost of the same benefit without the correctness hazard."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Make ByteStream uploads sticky.** Configure your load balancer for session\naffinity so a client's upload stream and its `QueryWriteStatus` resume reach the\nsame replica. Without it, every interrupted large upload restarts from zero.\nThis is the single most common way a horizontally scaled CAS gets slower rather\nthan faster."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Set readiness to account for the startup scan.** A filesystem store scans its\nentire `content_path` at boot and builds its index *before the process binds any\nsocket*. During that window there is nothing to connect to: not a degraded\nservice, an absent one. The window grows with the number of files on disk, so\ngive readiness and liveness probes an initial delay that reflects a full cache,\nnot an empty one."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Scale in slowly, and prefer fewer larger replicas.** Removing a replica throws\naway a warm fast tier that took hours to fill, and the actions that would have\nhit it now go to the slow store. The CAS is the opposite of the worker pool\nhere: generous, patient, and biased toward vertical growth."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "**Only then consider `shard`**, and only if a single shared backend is provably\nthe ceiling. Re-read the cache-flush warning above before you commit to a shard\ncount."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "Store size levels off below `max_bytes` and stays there, rather than sawing up\nto the limit on every write; that saw is `evict_bytes` being unset."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "A cold build populates the fast tier and the next identical build reads almost\nentirely from it."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "Interrupting a large upload and resuming it resumes from where it stopped,\nnot from zero. If it restarts, affinity isn't working."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "An AC hit never resolves to a missing blob. If it does, add\n`completeness_checking`."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "Restarting a replica does not fail client requests; it fails readiness first,\nand the load balancer takes it out before it stops answering."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-horizontal",
    "content": "Disk usage on each replica's fast tier is bounded and roughly equal."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**File descriptors, at 80% of what you configured.** `global.max_open_files`\ndefaults to 24576, and the process reserves a fifth of it for sockets and pipes,\nso the effective permit budget is about 19660."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "Every filesystem operation that goes through the store's file helpers takes a\npermit, including metadata-only ones such as `metadata`, `read_dir` and\n`rename`. (The startup scan is the exception: it holds one permit for the\ndirectory handle and runs its 200-way parallel `stat` outside the semaphore.)\nNetwork sockets are *not* counted by this semaphore but *are* counted by the\nkernel, so the kernel's limit must exceed `max_open_files` plus your expected\nconnection count."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**One connection per gRPC endpoint.** A `grpc` store's\n`connections_per_endpoint` defaults to `1`, so all traffic to a remote store\nmultiplexes over a single HTTP/2 connection. On a busy CAS-of-a-CAS topology\nthis is usually the first thing to raise."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**Redis permits and pool size.** `max_client_permits` defaults to 500 in-flight\noperations and `connection_pool_size` to 3. The permit cap exists specifically\nto stop timeouts from unbounded in-flight work, so raise it deliberately rather\nthan reflexively."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**Object-store upload concurrency.** `multipart_max_concurrent_uploads`\ndefaults to 10 per request, with a 5 MB retry buffer per request."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "**Message size, which is not a blob size limit.** The HTTP listener's\n`max_decoding_message_size` defaults to 4 MiB and bounds a single decoded gRPC\nmessage, so it caps `BatchUpdateBlobs`, not ByteStream. No\nconfiguration anywhere caps how large a blob a CAS will accept over\nByteStream. Capacity is bounded by eviction and disk, not by an upload check."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-ceilings-you-hit-before-you-expect-to",
    "content": "Tuning has the full lever table for the storage path,\nincluding `evict_page_cache` and its caveat."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "This is the finding most likely to catch you out while you're trying to measure\nany of the above."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "Forge defines a full set of cache instruments: operation duration,\noperation counts by result, bytes in and out, item size distribution, and\n(declared but never emitted)\ncurrent size and entry count."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "**No store emits any of them unless you explicitly wrap it in the\n`cache_metrics` store.** That wrapper is the only caller of the instruments, and\nthe design is deliberately opt-in so unwrapped stores carry no timers or\nattribute allocation in their hot path. **None of the shipped deployment\nconfigs wrap anything** (the only `cache_metrics` block in the tree is in the\nconfig-parser coverage fixture `forge-config/examples/stores-config.json5`),\nso out of the box an operator gets zero hit rate and zero latency data."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "Wrap the layer whose behaviour you want to see, and give each one a distinct\n`cache_type`; the recording rules group by it."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "`deployment-examples/metrics/prometheus-recording-rules.yml` defines a\n`forge_cache` group that is genuinely useful once metrics are flowing:\n`forge:cache_hit_rate`, latency at p50/p95/p99,\n`forge:cache_eviction_rate`, read and write throughput, and\n`forge:cache_error_rate`. The same group also defines\n`forge:cache_size_bytes` and `forge:cache_entry_count`, which are\nbuilt on the two instruments nothing emits and will stay empty."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "The shipped alert rules cover cache miss rate and eviction rate only. Nothing\nalerts on read latency, on throughput collapse, or on\n`forge:cache_error_rate`; write those yourself. No emitted\nsize metric exists to alert on at all, so size-approaching-`max_bytes` has to come\nfrom disk usage on the host rather than from Forge."
  }, {
    "heading": "metrics-nothing-is-on-by-default",
    "content": "Observability covers getting the telemetry out of the\nprocess at all, which is the prerequisite for any of this."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "Being explicit, because the gap between the examples and a production CAS is\nwide."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**Every shipped deployment runs exactly one CAS process.** The docker-compose\nsingle-node example, the multi-worker compose example, and the Kubernetes\nmanifest all have one. `docker-compose-multi-worker.yml` replicates *workers*\nagainst one `cas-server`; the only thing replicated anywhere in the repository\nis the worker pool."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**No multi-replica CAS example exists**, no load balancer or Service fronting\nseveral CAS pods, no HPA for the CAS, and no readiness gate accounting for the\nstartup scan. Anyone scaling the CAS horizontally is operating outside every\npath this repository exercises."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**The Kubernetes example stores the CAS in the container.**\n`kubernetes/forge/forge.yaml` is a single Deployment with\n`replicas: 1`, and its filesystem store points at a path under `/tmp` inside the\ncontainer: no PersistentVolumeClaim, no StatefulSet, not even an `emptyDir`.\nEvery pod restart wipes the CAS and the AC. It is a demo topology, and the\ncomposition inside its config is worth reading even though the storage isn't."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**Sharding has no deployment example.** The only `shard` block in the tree is a\nsingle-shard entry in a config-parser coverage fixture, which exercises no\ndistribution behaviour at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-repository-actually-ships",
    "content": "**The `cas-data` volume in the multi-worker compose file is vestigial.** It is\nmounted into the CAS server and into all three workers at the same path, but the\nworkers' config points their fast tier somewhere else entirely and reaches the\nCAS over gRPC. Don't read that mount as an endorsement of sharing a filesystem\nstore directory between processes; it isn't one."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "That's the `evict_bytes: 0` thrash. With no low watermark, every write pushes\nthe store over the limit and re-enters the eviction loop to remove the minimum.\nSet `evict_bytes` to a real fraction of `max_bytes`, enough that a burst of\nwrites fits under the headroom it creates."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Eviction is triggered by inserts and removals; there is no background timer. A\nstore that went over quota and then went idle stays over quota. Writes will\nbring it back down. If the store is idle because clients moved elsewhere, the\nspace is only reclaimed by restarting with a lower `max_bytes` or by clearing\nthe path directly."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The upload registry is per process. If a client's ByteStream write and its\n`QueryWriteStatus` resume land on different replicas, the second one has never\nheard of the upload and tells the client to start over. Enable session affinity\non the load balancer, keyed so a single client's stream stays on one replica."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "An `existence_cache` served a stale positive. It caches \"digest present\" in\nmemory, and unless the backend is Redis with keyspace notifications on there\nis no cross-process invalidation, so it will keep answering yes after the blob\nwas removed underneath it. Remove the wrapper from any replicated\ndeployment, or accept the failure mode. `completeness_checking` on the AC path\ncatches the related case where an AC hit points at an evicted CAS blob."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The filesystem store scans `content_path` and builds its eviction index before\nthe process binds a socket. The scan is O(files) (200-way parallel `stat`, then\na serialized index build), so a large cache means a long window with nothing\nlistening. Raise your probes' initial delay to cover a full cache, and expect\nthe window to grow as the cache does."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Each process builds an independent index at boot and each evicts against its\nown view, unlinking files the other still has indexed. No locking and\nno coordination exist. Give each process its own directory and share a slow tier\nunderneath instead."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Shard placement is a hash into a cumulative weight table. Changing the number of\nshards or any weight moves the boundaries, so keys resolve to different shards\nthan the ones they were written to. The data is still on disk; nothing looks for\nit there. Treat the change as a cache flush."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The OpenTelemetry cache instruments only fire from inside the `cache_metrics`\nwrapper store, and no shipped config uses it. Wrap the store you want to observe\nand give it a `cache_type`. The recording rules will start evaluating against\nreal series immediately afterwards."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "A memory store skips any write at or above its own `max_bytes`, logging rather\nthan erroring, and the blob is served from the backing store instead. If your\nblobs are larger than the tier, raise `max_bytes` or put a `size_partitioning`\nstore in front so only small objects are routed there."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
