import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Codebase internals",
  "description": "The traits, the error model, the async rules and the two metric systems: the conventions a reviewer will assume you already know."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone about to write Forge code rather than read\nit. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** the four things that are enforced\nrather than suggested: the store trait split, the error model, the\nspawning rules, and which metric system to use. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Most of Forge's review feedback is about conventions, not logic. The\nconventions are strong, mostly mechanically enforced, and each one exists\nfor a reason you can point at in the source. This page is the set of them\nthat a first contribution reliably trips over."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The repository targets Rust edition 2024 with `rust-version = \"1.97.1\"`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "Three of them exist, and they are not interchangeable."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "**`StoreDriver`**\n()\nis the one you implement. Its supertraits are\n`Sync + Send + Unpin + MetricsComponent + HealthStatusIndicator + 'static`,\nso a new store is observable and health-checkable by construction, not by\nremembering to add it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "**`Store`** is a `#[repr(transparent)]` newtype over `Arc<dyn StoreDriver>`.\nIt is what everything else holds."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "**`StoreLike`** is the caller-facing surface: two required methods, every\nconvenience built on top of them as a default, and blanket impls so that\nboth `Store` and `impl StoreDriver` satisfy it. You call `StoreLike`; you\nimplement `StoreDriver`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "The `StoreDriver` methods you must supply are `post_init`,\n`has_with_results`, `update`, `get_part`, `inner_store`, `as_any`,\n`as_any_arc` and `register_remove_callback`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "It is how a caller resolves an optimization (\"can I hardlink this?\")\nthrough a stack of wrappers. A routing wrapper forwards it; a wrapper that\nchanges the bytes must return `self`, because what is underneath is not the\nblob. Getting this wrong is a **runtime** error, not a compile error: the\ntrait checks with `addr_eq` and fires an `error_if!` if a store returns\n`self` while advertising an optimization it cannot honour. The store\nmodel covers the semantics from the operator's\nside."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "`StoreKey` is a two-variant enum, `Str` or `Digest`, with a variant-salted\n`Hash` so that a string key and a digest key cannot collide."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "Register health with the `default_health_status_indicator!(YourType);`\nmacro as the last line of the file, unless the store has something real to\nreport. Health checks across all components run **concurrently**\n(`buffer_unordered(usize::MAX)`), with a comment in"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-store-traits",
    "content": "explaining that a serial sweep would blow the kubelet's probe timeout."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-scheduler-traits-are-split-by-caller",
    "content": "No `ActionScheduler` trait exists. The state layer is split into\n`ClientStateManager`, `WorkerStateManager` and `MatchingEngineStateManager`\nin\n,\nplus `WorkerScheduler`, `KnownPlatformPropertyProvider` and\n`AwaitedActionDb`. The split by *caller* is what allows the in-memory and\nRedis-backed implementations to be swapped wholesale. See\nscheduler internals."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "`Code` **is** `tonic::Code`, re-exported from\n.\nNo separate internal code enum and no mapping layer exist, which is why\nan error raised deep in a store arrives at a client with a sensible gRPC\nstatus without anyone writing a conversion."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "An `Error&#x60; carries a code, a **`Vec<String>` of messages**, and a context.\nThe messages are a stack, joined with `\" : \"` when displayed. This is the\nwhole point of the model: each layer appends what it was trying to do, so\nthe final message reads outside-in."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "Three macros do most of the work: `make_err!`, `make_input_err!` and\n`error_if!`. `ResultExt`, and therefore `err_tip`, is implemented for\nboth `Result` and `Option`, so an `Option` can become a well-described\nerror without an intermediate `ok_or_else`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "Around twenty-five `From<…> for Error` impls exist for the common foreign\nerror types, so `?` usually just works."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "`err_tip` takes a closure so the string is only built on the error path.\nPassing an already-formatted `String` compiles and costs an allocation on\nevery success."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-error-model",
    "content": "`clippy::todo` is denied. `clippy::unwrap_used` is currently `allow`, with a\n`TODO` in the manifest recording the intent to flip it; write new code as\nthough it were already denied."
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "The Tokio runtime is built by hand in `main()`, not with `#[tokio::main]`:\nthe builder installs an `on_thread_start` hook (macOS QoS), and the process\nsignal handlers and the shutdown broadcast are spawned onto it before\n`inner_main` runs. Each of those calls carries an\n`#[expect(clippy::disallowed_methods, reason = …)]`, because the same lint\nthat bans `tokio::spawn` everywhere else bans the runtime builder too."
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "Fourteen entries in"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "make the standard spawning API a lint error. Use the three macros in"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "instead:"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "Macro"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "Returns"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "Use when"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "`spawn!`"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "`JoinHandleDropGuard`, which **aborts on drop**"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "The task's life is tied to the caller's"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "`background_spawn!`"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "A bare `JoinHandle`"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "The task should outlive the caller"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "`spawn_blocking!`"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "`JoinHandleDropGuard` around a blocking-pool task"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "The work blocks a thread"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "The first argument to every one of them is a task name, which becomes a\n`tracing::error_span!`. That name is how a task is identified in logs and\ntraces, so make it specific. OpenTelemetry context propagates into spawned\ntasks automatically."
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "This is a feature (it makes cancellation the default) and a trap if you\nspawn work and then let the handle fall out of scope. If the task must\nsurvive, use `background_spawn!` and say why in a comment."
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "File I/O goes through `forge_util::fs`, which serialises open file\ndescriptors behind a global semaphore (`DEFAULT_OPEN_FILE_LIMIT` is 24576).\nOpening a file with `std::fs` or `tokio::fs` directly bypasses the limit and\nwill eventually exhaust the process's descriptors under load."
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "Shutdown is coordinated by"
  }, {
    "heading": "async-tokio-spawn-is-banned",
    "content": "with three priorities: `P0`, `P1`, `LeastImportant`. Awaiting `wait_for` at\na given priority blocks until everything less important has drained. `SIGTERM`\ndrains and then exits `143`; `SIGINT` is a hard exit `130`."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "They are not redundant, and picking the wrong one is a common review\ncomment."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "**System A: `forge-metric`.** A `#[derive(MetricsComponent)]` plus\n`#[metric(help = …, kind = …, group = …, handler = …)]` on fields, with\n`RootMetricsComponent` marking a tree root. Roughly 190 `#[metric]`\nattributes exist. Publishing a tree walks the live object graph and emits\none `tracing` span per component and one event per field, on the target\n`forge_metric`, so what it describes is the *structure* of a running\nprocess. Nothing in the v1.6.5 binary serves that tree over HTTP; the old\nmetrics collector went away with the OpenTelemetry migration, so today the\nderive is what keeps every store and scheduler describable, and the trait\nbound on `StoreDriver` is what forces new ones to be."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "**System B: `forge-util/src/metrics.rs`.** OpenTelemetry instruments\nin eight `LazyLock` bundles (`CACHE_METRICS`, `EXECUTION_METRICS`,\n`WORKER_METRICS`, `RPC_METRICS`, `SCHEDULER_METRICS`, `STORE_TIER_METRICS`,\n`HEALTH_METRICS`, `CONNECTION_METRICS`), wired up in\n`telemetry.rs::init_tracing` and exported over OTLP (`NL_OTEL_ENDPOINT`\noverrides the endpoint). Attribute sets for the hot paths are\n**pre-computed** into `CacheMetricAttrs` and `ExecutionMetricAttrs` structs\nspecifically to bound cardinality."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "Choose A when you are exposing the *structure* of a component: how many\nstores, which config, what state. Choose B when you want a number someone\nwill graph or alert on."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "`forge-util/src/metrics.rs` carries the **Business Source License**\nheader, not the FSL header the rest of the crate uses; do not paste the\nwrong one in. And never attach a per-action or per-worker identifier to a\nhot instrument; the pre-computed attribute structs exist precisely to stop\nthat, and adding a high-cardinality dimension will take down a Prometheus\nbefore it takes down Forge."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-metric-systems",
    "content": "Instrument names are dotted OpenTelemetry style, with `.with_description`\nand a `.with_unit` that is either a UCUM unit (`\"By\"`, `\"s\"`, `\"ms\"`) or a\nbraced count such as `\"{entry}\"`, `\"{action}\"` or `\"{worker}\"`."
  }, {
    "heading": "lints-formatting-and-the-small-stuff",
    "content": "`clippy::all`, `clippy::nursery` and `clippy::pedantic` are all denied at\nthe workspace level. `std_instead_of_core` is denied too, which is why you\nwill see `use core::…` throughout for anything that does not need `std`."
  }, {
    "heading": "lints-formatting-and-the-small-stuff",
    "content": "Suppress a lint with `#[expect(lint, reason = \"…\")]` rather than a bare\n`#[allow]`; `expect` fails the build once the suppression is no longer\nneeded, which is how they get cleaned up. `#[allow]` still appears where the\nlint only fires on some targets or feature sets, and it should carry a\n`reason` too. Nothing enforces this mechanically; reviewers do."
  }, {
    "heading": "lints-formatting-and-the-small-stuff",
    "content": "`.rustfmt.toml` uses `group_imports = \"StdExternalCrate\"` and\n`imports_granularity = \"Module\"`, both nightly-only options. Do not run\nstable `cargo fmt` and expect it to match; run the Bazel target instead:"
  }, {
    "heading": "lints-formatting-and-the-small-stuff",
    "content": "`unsafe` is rare: a few dozen blocks in the whole tree, mostly FFI, a few\nmeasured optimizations, and environment-variable writes in tests. Most carry\na `// SAFETY:` comment, and a new one without a justification will be asked\nfor one."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Because a bare spawn has no name, no tracing span, no OpenTelemetry\ncontext and no cancellation story. The three macros in\n`forge-util::task` supply all four, and the clippy config makes the\nalternative a build failure rather than a review comment."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "If it describes what a component *is* (its config, its children, its\ninternal counters as part of the object tree), derive; the\n`MetricsComponent` bound on `StoreDriver` requires the impl anyway. If a\ndashboard would graph it or an alert would fire on it, OTel. A few things\nlegitimately want both; most want exactly one."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "You returned something other than `self` from `inner_store` while\nadvertising an optimization your store cannot honour, or the reverse.\nA wrapper that changes the bytes on the way through must return `self`;\na wrapper that only routes should forward to its inner store."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Copy it from the file next to yours. FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0 is the default;\n`forge-util/src/metrics.rs` and everything under\n`forge-worker/src/persistent_worker/` are Business Source License,\nand a handful of files are Apache-2.0."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The formatting config uses nightly-only options. Stable `cargo fmt`\nsilently ignores them and produces different output. Run\n`bazel run --config=rustfmt @rules_rust//:rustfmt`, which is what CI runs."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The four recipes (a store, a config field, a metric, a gRPC service),\neach with the complete list of files a working change touches."
  }],
