export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone about to open their first pull request.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** the git configuration the project\nrequires, the commit conventions, and an accurate picture of how review\nworks. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes, most of it one-time setup."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge accepts contributions from anyone. The process is more specific\nthan most projects' (signed commits, a fork, one commit per pull request,\nreview in Reviewable rather than in GitHub's own UI), and the specificity is\ndeliberate: it keeps `main` a linear history of signed, reviewable commits."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "is the authoritative version; this page is the orientation around it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Welcome"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "With a caveat"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Bug fixes, with a regression test"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Performance improvements"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Bring reproducible numbers"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "New stores, schedulers, worker types"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Open an issue and agree the approach first"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Documentation fixes, including this page"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Smaller is better"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Tooling that makes development or operations easier"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "Three things reliably do not land: breaking changes to the Remote Execution\nAPI surface, which is an upstream spec the project tracks rather than owns;\nvendor-specific code paths only one deployment can use; and refactoring\nproposed on its own merits rather than as part of a change that needed it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-gets-accepted",
    "content": "If you want to work on an existing issue, &#x2A;*claim it by commenting first.**\nThat is the only mechanism preventing two people from writing the same patch."
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "**Create distinct authentication and signing keys** in your\nGitHub key settings. Two keys, not\none reused."
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "**Fork the repository** and clone your fork, not upstream:"
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "`git remote -v` should show `origin` pointing at your fork and\n`upstream` at `toxicwind/forge`."
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "**Configure git to sign every commit and tag.** In `~/.gitconfig`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "Forge requires cryptographically **signed** commits, SSH or GPG, via\n`commit.gpgsign`. It does not use a Developer Certificate of Origin\nsign-off, so `git commit -s` alone will not satisfy the requirement."
  }, {
    "heading": "git-setup",
    "content": "Direct commits and human-created branches on `toxicwind/forge` are\nnot allowed, including for members of the organization. Everything arrives\nas a pull request from a fork."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "**Sync your fork**, then branch:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "**Make one commit.** The title rules are enforced by review, not by a\nhook, and they are short: start with a capital letter, use the\nimperative, no trailing period, keep it as short as it can be, and put\ndetail in the body wrapped at 72 characters."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "The word \"and\" in a title is the signal to split the commit."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "**Push and open the PR** from your fork, then click the purple\n**Reviewable** button on the GitHub page to add reviewers with\n`+@somereviewer`. Review conversation happens there."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "**Revise with `--amend`, not a follow-up commit.**"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-pull-request-loop",
    "content": "Reviewable keeps the diff between commit versions visible, so nothing is\nlost by rewriting, and the branch stays one commit, which is what lands."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-push",
    "content": "`.rustfmt.toml` uses `group_imports` and `imports_granularity`, both\nnightly-only. Stable `cargo fmt` ignores them silently and produces output\nCI rejects. Use the Bazel target."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-push",
    "content": "`cargo test --all --profile=smol` is the fast inner loop, but a green Cargo\nrun is not a green CI: `bazel test` attaches rustfmt and clippy aspects that\nCargo does not. See the testing guide for what\nthe suites contain and which local failures are environmental."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-reviewers-look-for",
    "content": "**One change per pull request.** Small ones land; large ones get split."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-reviewers-look-for",
    "content": "**A body that explains why.** The diff already shows what changed. The body\nis where the constraint, the incident or the measurement goes."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-reviewers-look-for",
    "content": "**Tests that match the change.** A bug fix wants a regression test; new\nbehaviour wants both unit and integration coverage. No coverage\npercentage gate; the question is whether the new path is exercised, not\nwhether a number moved."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-reviewers-look-for",
    "content": "**Conventions followed rather than argued.** Most review feedback on a first\ncontribution is about the house rules: the task macros instead of\n`tokio::spawn`, `#[expect(..., reason = \"…\")]` rather than a bare `#[allow]`,\nthe `BUILD.bazel` `srcs` entry. Codebase internals is the page that\ngets you past those in one read."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "Your contribution is licensed under the license that applies to the file or\nmodule you change. Most of the repository is `FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0`; a handful\nof files are Apache-2.0; and two areas,\n`forge-util/src/metrics.rs` and everything under\n`forge-worker/src/persistent_worker/`, are **Business Source\nLicense**. Copy the header from the file next to the one you are creating\nrather than from memory."
  }, {
    "heading": "licensing",
    "content": "Meaningful contributors may be eligible for license waivers on the Business\nSource License modules. Raise an issue or contact the maintainers before\ndepending on one."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Cargo for small localized changes, the fastest way\nin. Bazel when you want exactly what CI runs.\nNix for the pinned shell, required for LRE work and\nfor the generated `bazelrc` fragments."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Two usual causes. A new source file missing from its crate's\n`BUILD.bazel` `srcs` list, since Bazel enumerates where Cargo globs; or\nformatting, because `bazel test` attaches a rustfmt aspect and stable\n`cargo fmt` produces different output."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No. That adds a DCO trailer, which this project does not use. Configure\n`commit.gpgsign = true` with an SSH or GPG signing key so commits are\ncryptographically signed."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Amend and force-push. `git commit --amend && git push -f`. Reviewable\ntracks the diff between commit versions, so reviewers can see exactly\nwhat you changed without a follow-up commit polluting the history."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Every PR is reviewed by a core contributor, including PRs from core\ncontributors. No turnaround is published; if a PR goes quiet,\ncomment on it or ping in the Reviewable thread."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Where your change actually goes: twelve crates, a strict layering, and the\nsix places the first guess is wrong."
  }],
