import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Compose stores",
  "description": "The wrapper vocabulary (fast_slow, verify, dedup, existence_cache, size_partitioning, shard and the rest) and the order the layers have to go in."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who has picked a backend and now needs it to be\nfast, cheap and trustworthy. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a layered store\nyou can explain line by line, and the vocabulary to read anyone else's config.\n&#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file with at least one backend in it. See Storage backends."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Most store types in Forge are not places to put bytes. They are wrappers:\neach one takes another store and changes something about how it behaves. A real\nCAS store is four or five of these nested inside each other, and the nesting\norder is the design."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The things at the bottom of the nesting are the backends:\nfilesystem,\nS3 and compatible,\nGCS, Azure Blob,\nOCI, Redis,\nMongo, each with a page of its own under\nStorage backends. Everything on this page goes above them."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-ways-stores-refer-to-each-other",
    "content": "A wrapper can hold its inner store inline:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-ways-stores-refer-to-each-other",
    "content": "Or it can name a store defined elsewhere in the `stores` array, with\n`ref_store`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-two-ways-stores-refer-to-each-other",
    "content": "Both configs describe the same thing. Order in the array does not matter, because\n`ref_store` is resolved after every entry is read, so a store may reference one\ndefined below it. Reach for `ref_store` when two stores genuinely share one\nbackend, or when nesting has got deep enough to be unreadable."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow--a-tier-in-front-of-a-tier",
    "content": "The single most important wrapper. Reads try `fast` first and fall through to\n`slow` on a miss, populating `fast` as they go. Writes go to both."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow--a-tier-in-front-of-a-tier",
    "content": "Two knobs are worth knowing. `fast_direction` and `slow_direction` restrict\nwhat each half participates in: `update` means writes land here but reads\nnever consult it, which only makes sense on the fast half; `get`, `read_only`\nand the default `both` do what they sound like."
  }, {
    "heading": "fast_slow--a-tier-in-front-of-a-tier",
    "content": "`bypass_dedup_threshold_bytes` makes reads at or above a given size skip the\nfast tier entirely and stream straight from `slow`. It defaults to `0`, which\ndisables the bypass; 256 MiB is a reasonable starting point if huge artifacts\nare evicting everything useful out of your fast tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify--refuse-to-serve-corruption",
    "content": "Checks the size and hash of an upload against the digest it is filed under,\nand rejects the write on a mismatch. Reads pass straight through to the\nbackend unchecked, so this protects what enters the store, not what a backend\nlater hands back."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify--refuse-to-serve-corruption",
    "content": "A CAS entry is addressed by the hash of its content, so both checks are\nmeaningful. An Action Cache entry is not: its key is the action digest, and\nits value is a result message, so `verify_size` and `verify_hash` should both\nbe `false` for an AC store. Setting them true there rejects every valid\nupdate."
  }, {
    "heading": "completeness_checking--the-action-cache-wrapper",
    "content": "The AC's counterpart. Before returning an action result as a hit, it confirms\nevery output digest that result references still exists in the CAS. Without it,\na client gets a cache hit pointing at blobs that have since been evicted."
  }, {
    "heading": "completeness_checking--the-action-cache-wrapper",
    "content": "This is why the AC and CAS eviction policies are coupled in practice: evicting\naggressively from the CAS quietly invalidates AC entries, and this wrapper is\nwhat turns that from a confusing build failure into a clean cache miss."
  }, {
    "heading": "existence_cache--stop-asking-the-same-question",
    "content": "Caches the answer to \"does this digest exist\" so `FindMissingBlobs` doesn't hit\nthe backend for every blob of every build."
  }, {
    "heading": "existence_cache--stop-asking-the-same-question",
    "content": "The `eviction_policy` here governs the existence *index*, not the data. It is\na small map of digests, so it can hold far more entries than the store itself.\nThis is the highest-leverage wrapper in front of object storage, where every\nexistence check is a billed request."
  }, {
    "heading": "compression--smaller-on-the-backend",
    "content": "Compresses blobs with LZ4 before they reach the wrapped store."
  }, {
    "heading": "compression--smaller-on-the-backend",
    "content": "LZ4 is the only algorithm. `block_size` defaults to 64 KiB, and\n`max_decode_block_size` defaults to match it. The latter bounds how much a\nmalformed block can ask the decoder to allocate, so raise it only alongside\n`block_size`."
  }, {
    "heading": "compression--smaller-on-the-backend",
    "content": "This is store-level compression, on the way to the backend. It is a different\nthing from remote cache compression, which\ncompresses on the wire between the client and Forge. The two are\nindependent and can both be on."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup--store-the-changed-parts-only",
    "content": "Splits blobs into content-defined chunks, stores each chunk once, and keeps a\nper-blob index of which chunks it is made of."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup--store-the-changed-parts-only",
    "content": "`index_store` should be small and fast; `content_store` is the large slow one.\nThe three size fields bound the chunker: `normal_size` is the target average,\n`min_size` and `max_size` are hard limits, and they default to 64 KiB, 256 KiB\nand 512 KiB. Every blob gets an index entry, even one small enough to be a\nsingle chunk; there is no size below which the store skips the index."
  }, {
    "heading": "dedup--store-the-changed-parts-only",
    "content": "`max_concurrent_fetch_per_get` (default 10) bounds parallel chunk fetches.\nWorst-case memory per `get()` is about\n`max_concurrent_fetch_per_get × max_size`, so the defaults cost roughly 5 MB\nper in-flight read."
  }, {
    "heading": "size_partitioning--route-by-blob-size",
    "content": "Sends everything under `size` to one store and everything at or above it to\nanother."
  }, {
    "heading": "size_partitioning--route-by-blob-size",
    "content": "The usual use is to keep small objects out of a `dedup` store, where the\nper-blob index costs more than the deduplication saves. The other use is a hard\nceiling: point `upper_store` at `noop` and blobs above the threshold are\ndiscarded rather than stored."
  }, {
    "heading": "shard--spread-across-several-backends",
    "content": "Distributes blobs across stores by digest, weighted."
  }, {
    "heading": "shard--spread-across-several-backends",
    "content": "A store's share is its `weight` divided by the sum of all weights, so `SHARD_C`\nabove takes half. `weight` defaults to `1`, so an unweighted list is an even\nsplit."
  }, {
    "heading": "shard--spread-across-several-backends",
    "content": "Blobs are placed by hashing against the current set of stores and weights.\nAdd, remove or reweight a shard and existing entries stop being looked for\nwhere they were put. Treat a shard change as a cache flush."
  }, {
    "heading": "cache_metrics--measure-one-layer",
    "content": "Wraps a store to record hit rates and timings under a label:"
  }, {
    "heading": "cache_metrics--measure-one-layer",
    "content": "`cache_type` should be low-cardinality: `cas` or `ac`, not something per-user.\nThe wrapper is opt-in: stores not wrapped by it are built without it and pay\nnone of its hot-path timing cost. Wrap the layer whose behaviour you actually\nwant to see, not every layer."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-with-nothing-to-configure",
    "content": "`memory` takes an `eviction_policy` and nothing else. It is the right fast half\nfor a short-lived process and the wrong one for anything that must survive a\nrestart."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-with-nothing-to-configure",
    "content": "`grpc` points at another REAPI cache upstream, and can attach `headers` and\nforward selected headers from the incoming request. Use it to front an existing\ncache."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-with-nothing-to-configure",
    "content": "Development builds also support `grpc.store_type: \"event_sink\"` for forwarding\nBEP and origin events to a dedicated receiver. This mode accepts string keys\nwith exact payload sizes and sends uncompressed `ByteStream.Write` requests.\nThe resource hash identifies the event key, while the size describes the event\nbytes. Configure it only as an event publisher's store: reads, existence checks,\nand digest-key writes are rejected. A normal CAS endpoint cannot receive these\nwrites. The receiver must acknowledge the full payload size only after its\ndurable append completes; Forge rejects a different committed size, and\nits BEP response waits for that acknowledgement. A lost acknowledgement can\ncause a retry after the receiver has already persisted an event. The receiver\nmust use the stable event key to detect and ignore duplicate events, including\nretries that start a new upload; the upload UUID alone is not an event identity.\nOrigin publishing retains its existing bounded retry policy. This transport\nprovides neither exactly-once processing nor a durable publisher recovery ledger."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-with-nothing-to-configure",
    "content": "`noop` takes nothing at all and discards everything written to it, reporting\nevery read as a miss. It is how you deliberately turn a half of something off."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "Reading a real config outside-in, each layer answers a different question:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "Four placements are load-bearing rather than stylistic."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "**`verify` goes outermost.** It checks an upload against the digest the client\nfiled it under. Underneath compression or dedup, the bytes it sees are no\nlonger the bytes the digest describes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "**`existence_cache` goes above the expensive store, not below it.** Its whole\npurpose is to avoid a round trip, so anything between it and the backend is\nwork it was meant to skip."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "**`dedup` goes above `compression`, never below it.** Compressed bytes of\nsimilar inputs are not similar, so a `dedup` store fed by a `compression`\nwrapper finds nothing to share. Put `compression` on the dedup store's\n`content_store`, as the full example below does."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-order-the-layers-go-in",
    "content": "**`fast_slow` goes innermost, just above the backends.** Layers above it apply\nto both halves once, rather than being paid twice."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-full-example",
    "content": "The shipped GCS and Azure examples both use this shape, and it is a good\ndefault for a cloud-backed CAS:"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-full-example",
    "content": "Read outside-in: verify what we serve; dedup so we store each chunk once; keep\nthe index on fast local disk backed by the bucket; compress the content;\ntier that over the bucket too."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Start with the backend alone** and confirm it works. Every layer you add\nmakes a failure harder to attribute."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `fast_slow`** with a local filesystem fast half, and confirm the second\nbuild is a hit with the fast tier emptied."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `verify`** to the CAS, and *not* to the Action Cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `completeness_checking`** to the Action Cache, pointing `cas_store` at\nthe CAS store by name."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `existence_cache`** if the slow tier is object storage, and watch the\nrequest count rather than the byte count to see whether it helped."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `dedup` and `compression`** last, and only if artifact size is the\nproblem you actually have."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The config loads. An unknown field anywhere in the nesting is rejected at\nstartup, so a clean start means every layer name is right."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A build is a cache hit on the second run with the fast tier emptied, which\nproves the layers pass through to the slow tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Backend request counts are far below blob counts once `existence_cache` is\nin."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Uploading a blob under a digest that does not match its content (for\nexample with a hand-built ByteStream write) is rejected rather than stored.\nThat's `verify` earning its place."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Almost every store spec is strict about unknown fields, so this is usually a\nfield placed at the wrong nesting level: `eviction_policy` on the wrapper\nrather than on the store, or a backend option one layer too high. Check what\nthe innermost brace you're inside actually is."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`verify` with `verify_hash` or `verify_size` true on an AC store. An AC entry's\nkey is the action digest and its value is a result message, so both checks\nfail by construction and every AC update is rejected, leaving nothing to hit.\nSet both false there, and use `completeness_checking` instead."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The Action Cache has no `completeness_checking` wrapper, so it hands out\nresults referencing outputs the CAS has since evicted. Add it with `cas_store`\npointing at the CAS store."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The name is a store name from the `stores` array, matched exactly. Order does\nnot matter, so a forward reference is fine. A typo or a renamed store is the\nusual cause."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Small blobs. A blob that fits in one chunk gains nothing and still costs an\nindex write plus an extra existence check on every read. Put a\n`size_partitioning` above the dedup store and route small objects straight to\nthe backend."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Placement is derived from the set of stores and weights. Changing it moves\nwhere blobs are looked for. Nothing rebalances, so treat it as a flush."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The other kind of compression: on the wire, between the client and\nForge."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "What these compositions look like once the three processes are separate."
  }],
