  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "GCS, Azure Blob and\nOCI Object Storage use the same store type\nwith their own `provider` values and have their own pages."
  }, {
    "heading": "amazon-s3",
    "content": "`region` and `bucket` are the only fields you have to set. Credentials come\nfrom the standard AWS chain (environment variables, the shared credentials\nfile, the EC2/ECS/EKS instance or task role), so nothing secret goes in the\nconfig."
  }, {
    "heading": "amazon-s3",
    "content": "`key_prefix` lets one bucket hold several logical stores. Give the CAS `cas/`\nand the Action Cache `ac/` rather than provisioning two buckets; lifecycle\nrules can then treat them differently."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "The `aws` provider reaches any S3-compatible service, not only AWS. Four\noptional fields cover the difference:"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "Set it when"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "`endpoint`"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "The service is not AWS. Give it the service URL, such as `https://s3.example.com`"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "`force_path_style`"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "The service addresses objects as `{endpoint}/{bucket}/{key}` rather than `{bucket}.{endpoint}/{key}`. Most self-hosted implementations do"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "`access_key_id`"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "The credentials are not ones the AWS chain would find"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "`secret_access_key`"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "As above. Set both or neither, and prefer `${VAR}` expansion over a secret in the file"
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "Set `region` even when the endpoint is not AWS: it is the SigV4 signing region,\nand the service has to agree with the value you pick."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-services-that-speak-s3",
    "content": "A plain `http://` endpoint needs `insecure_allow_http` as well, from\nthe shared options. It turns off transport\nencryption for the whole store, so keep it to local testing."
  }, {
    "heading": "cloudflare-r2",
    "content": "R2 has no regions from the client's point of view: the endpoint is derived\nentirely from your account ID."
  }, {
    "heading": "cloudflare-r2",
    "content": "`access_key_id` and `secret_access_key` are optional in the schema (omitting\nboth falls back to the AWS credential chain), but R2 tokens don't live in that\nchain, so in practice you set them, from environment variables. The\n`${VAR}` syntax is expanded at config-load time; see\nThe config file."
  }, {
    "heading": "cloudflare-r2",
    "content": "R2 charges no egress, which changes the arithmetic on how small a fast tier\nyou can get away with."
  }, {
    "heading": "netapp-ontap-s3",
    "content": "On-premises S3 needs an explicit endpoint, the storage VM serving it, and\nusually a private CA bundle:"
  }, {
    "heading": "netapp-ontap-s3",
    "content": "`root_certificates` is optional and points at a PEM bundle for the CA that\nsigned the endpoint's certificate. Without it, the system roots are used and\nan internally-signed endpoint fails TLS verification. Credentials come from\nthe same AWS chain as `aws` (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`\nin practice), and `vserver_name` doubles as the signing region."
  }, {
    "heading": "netapp-ontap-s3",
    "content": "The ONTAP implementation differs from `aws` in three ways: it always uses\npath-style addressing, its default `max_retry_buffer_per_request` is 20 MB\nrather than 5 MB, and it is HTTPS-only with HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 both enabled,\nso `insecure_allow_http` and `disable_http2` have no effect on it."
  }, {
    "heading": "netapp-ontap-s3",
    "content": "ONTAP has one extra store type of its own, `ontap_s3_existence_cache`, which\nkeeps a persisted index of what the bucket contains and refreshes it on an\ninterval:"
  }, {
    "heading": "netapp-ontap-s3",
    "content": "It exists because `FindMissingBlobs` against an on-premises S3 endpoint is\noften the slowest thing in the build, and the answer is usually stale-tolerant."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Every provider flattens the same block of common options. These are the ones\nworth setting deliberately:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Default"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "What it decides"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`key_prefix`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "none"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Namespacing inside one bucket"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`retry`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "see below"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "How hard to try before surfacing an error"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`consider_expired_after_s`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`0` (never)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "How old an object may be and still count as present"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`max_retry_buffer_per_request`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "5 MB (20 MB for `ontap`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "How much of a single-request upload is buffered so it can be retried"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`multipart_max_concurrent_uploads`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "10"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Concurrent `UploadPart` requests within one multipart upload"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`insecure_allow_http`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`false`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Permit plain HTTP; local testing only (`aws`, `r2`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`disable_http2`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`false`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Fall back to HTTP/1.1 for proxies that mishandle h2 (`aws`, `r2`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`retry` is exponential with jitter: up to `max_retries` retries after the\nfirst attempt, a base `delay` in seconds that is doubled before the first\nretry and again for each retry after it, and `jitter` as a fraction of the\ncomputed delay (`0.5` means each wait is randomised within plus or minus 25%).\nThe shipped examples all use `{ max_retries: 6, delay: 0.3, jitter: 0.5 }`:\nwaits of about 0.6, 1.2, 2.4, 4.8, 9.6 and 19.2 seconds, roughly forty seconds\nof retrying across seven attempts."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "Objects smaller than 5 MB with a known size are uploaded in one `PutObject`\nrequest; anything larger, or of unknown size, goes through a multipart upload\nin parts of at least 5 MB, each part held in memory and retried on its own."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-options-all-three-share",
    "content": "`consider_expired_after_s` is the one people are surprised by. When set, an\nobject whose `last_modified` is older than this many seconds is reported as\n*not existing*, even though it is still in the bucket. Pair it with a bucket\nlifecycle rule whose window is longer by a comfortable margin (a few days):\nthe rule deletes the object, and this setting stops Forge handing out a\ndigest that is about to disappear. Clients that get a `NotFound`\nre-upload."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Create the bucket** and a least-privilege credential that can head, get and\nput objects and create, upload to, complete and abort multipart uploads on it,\nnothing else. The store never lists or deletes objects (only the\n`ontap_s3_existence_cache` wrapper lists the bucket, to build its index)."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add a lifecycle rule** that aborts incomplete multipart uploads after a few\ndays. A process killed mid-upload leaves orphaned parts you are billed for."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Export the credentials** into the process environment, and reference them\nwith `${VAR}` rather than pasting them into the config."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the store** as the `slow` half of a `fast_slow` pair whose `fast` half\nis a local filesystem store."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Run a build twice**: once cold, once after clearing the local fast tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Objects appear in the bucket under `key_prefix` during the first build."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The second build is a cache hit even after the local `content_path` is\nemptied, which proves reads are falling through to the bucket."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Bucket request counts are far lower than blob counts, which proves the fast\ntier is absorbing repeat reads."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "No incomplete multipart uploads accumulate between builds."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "For `aws`, the credential chain found nothing. Confirm the process can see\n`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, or that the instance/task\nrole is attached and reachable. For `r2`, set `access_key_id` and\n`secret_access_key` explicitly, because R2 tokens are not in the AWS chain."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Set `root_certificates` to the PEM bundle for the CA that signed the\nendpoint's certificate. Do not reach for `insecure_allow_http`, which\ndisables transport security entirely and is meant only for a local test\ndouble."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "You're going to the bucket on every read. Check that the store is the `slow`\nhalf of a `fast_slow` pair and that the `fast` half's `eviction_policy` isn't\nso small it evicts everything before it's re-read."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Same cause. A build cache issues enormous numbers of small existence checks\nand reads; object storage prices those individually. A local fast tier, and\nan `existence_cache` in front of the slow\nstore, are the two levers."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Objects of 5 MB and above go through multipart upload, so check that the\ncredential can `CreateMultipartUpload`, `UploadPart`, `CompleteMultipartUpload`\nand `AbortMultipartUpload`; a policy that only allows `PutObject` breaks\nexactly at that size. Also check `max_retry_buffer_per_request` if you have\nlowered it: a single-request upload larger than the buffer cannot be replayed\nafter a transient failure, and setting it to zero disables upload buffering\nentirely, which trades retry safety for memory."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check `consider_expired_after_s`. A non-zero value makes objects older than\nthat window invisible on purpose. If you didn't intend that, set it back\nto `0`."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Deduplication, compression and existence caching in front of the bucket,\nthe layers that decide what it actually costs."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "What this looks like once several processes share the same bucket."
  }],
