import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Stores",
  "description": "Declaring stores, naming them, and the idea that unlocks the rest of the config: stores compose, and one store refers to another by name."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who has seen a Forge config with a store\nnested five levels deep and wanted to know why. &#x2A;*What you'll know at the\nend:** how to declare a store, how to name one, how composition works, and\nwhich layers are worth reaching for. &#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The config model from The config file."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-store-is-one-entry-in-an-array",
    "content": "The `stores` array holds every storage backend the process can use. Each entry\nis an object with a `name` and exactly one type key:"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-store-is-one-entry-in-an-array",
    "content": "That's a complete, working cache backend pair: a content-addressable store for\nblobs and an action cache for results. Everything lives in RAM, so a restart\nwipes it, which is exactly right for a demo or a short-lived CI runner, and\nexactly wrong for anything else."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-store-is-one-entry-in-an-array",
    "content": "The name is arbitrary. `CAS_MAIN_STORE` and `AC_MAIN_STORE` are conventions\nthe shipped examples use, not keywords; the services in your `servers` block\nwill refer to whatever names you pick."
  }, {
    "heading": "backing-it-with-disk",
    "content": "Swap the type key and the same two stores survive a restart:"
  }, {
    "heading": "backing-it-with-disk",
    "content": "`content_path` is where the bulk of the data lives. `temp_path` is a staging\narea for in-flight writes and deletes, and it must be **on the same block\ndevice** as `content_path` so a completed write can be moved into place\natomically rather than copied. Everything in `temp_path` is deleted on every\nstartup."
  }, {
    "heading": "backing-it-with-disk",
    "content": "On boot, `content_path` is scanned and every file found is added back to the\ncache; that scan is what makes the store durable across restarts."
  }, {
    "heading": "backing-it-with-disk",
    "content": "`eviction_policy` is optional in the schema and unbounded when omitted. A\nstore without one never removes anything, which for a `memory` store means\nthe process grows until the kernel kills it, and for a `filesystem` store\nmeans the disk fills. Set `max_bytes` on every store you declare."
  }, {
    "heading": "backing-it-with-disk",
    "content": "CAS blobs are typically small: function-level outputs, header files, link\narguments. Default ext4 works fine; a filesystem tuned for many small files\ndegrades more gracefully under load."
  }, {
    "heading": "stores-compose",
    "content": "Here is the idea the rest of this page is about. &#x2A;*Most store types don't\nstore anything.** They wrap another store and change its behaviour, and the\nstore they wrap is written inline, as a full store spec:"
  }, {
    "heading": "stores-compose",
    "content": "`compression` has no storage of its own. It compresses on write, decompresses\non read, and hands the bytes to whatever is in `backend`. The client sees raw\nbytes either way. Build artifacts are usually highly compressible, so this\nsaves disk in exchange for CPU; the only algorithm offered is `lz4`, which is\nfast and gives up early on data that won't compress."
  }, {
    "heading": "stores-compose",
    "content": "Because the wrapped store is a full spec rather than a reference to a type,\nwrappers nest arbitrarily. A `verify` around a `size_partitioning` whose upper\nhalf is a `dedup` whose index is a `fast_slow` of memory-over-disk is not an\nexotic config; it's `forge-config/examples/filesystem_cas.json5`, and\neach layer is doing one comprehensible job."
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "Nesting alone can't express *sharing*. If two stores should write to the same\nplace, inlining the backend twice gives you two independent stores that\nhappen to have identical settings."
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "`ref_store` is the escape hatch. It's a store type whose entire body is the\nname of another top-level store:"
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "Both halves now land in the same physical store. Swap either `ref_store` for\nan inline spec and they diverge."
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "Two properties of `ref_store` are worth knowing before you use it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "**Order doesn't matter.** A store can reference a name declared later in the\narray. `forge-config/examples/redis.json5` has `AC_MAIN_STORE` referring\nto `CAS_MAIN_STORE`, which is declared below it. References are resolved in a\npost-init pass after every store has been constructed."
  }, {
    "heading": "ref_store-naming-instead-of-nesting",
    "content": "**A typo is not a parse error.** `deny_unknown_fields` catches a misspelled\n*field*, but `ref_store: { name: \"CAS_MAIN_STROE\" }` is structurally valid;\nthe failure surfaces in that post-init pass (`Failed to find store\n'CAS_MAIN_STROE' in StoreManager in RefStore`), still at startup and still\nbefore any socket is bound, but after the parse itself has succeeded."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "You don't need all eighteen store types to write a good config. These are the\nones that appear in nearly every non-trivial one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "What it does"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Why you'd add it"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`filesystem`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Stores blobs on disk"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Durability across restarts"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`memory`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Stores blobs in RAM"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Speed, for small hot data"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`compression`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Compresses around a backend"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Disk savings on build artifacts"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`dedup`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Splits blobs into chunks, stores each once"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Large, similar artifacts"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`fast_slow`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Reads from `fast`, falls back to `slow`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "A RAM tier over a disk tier"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`size_partitioning`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Routes by blob size to two stores"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Keep small objects out of an index"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`verify`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Checks hash and size on write"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Catch corruption at the boundary"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`completeness_checking`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Rejects AC entries whose blobs are gone"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Correct action-cache results"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`existence_cache`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Remembers what the backend already has"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Skip redundant existence checks"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`ref_store`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Names another top-level store"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Sharing, instead of duplicating"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`shard`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Spreads across weighted backends"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Horizontal capacity"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "`noop`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Discards everything"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "A required slot you don't want to fill"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Two of these are load-bearing for correctness rather than performance.\n&#x2A;*`verify`** with `verify_hash: true&#x60; catches a blob whose content doesn't\nmatch the digest it was uploaded under. &#x2A;*`completeness_checking`** wraps an\naction cache and checks the CAS still holds every blob a cached result refers\nto; without it, an eviction in the CAS turns cached results into builds that\nfail on a missing output."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "That's why most of the shipped backend examples (`filesystem_cas.json5`,\n`s3_backend_with_local_fast_cas.json5`, and the other object-storage examples)\nwrap the CAS in `verify`, why `redis.json5` wraps the AC in\n`completeness_checking`, and why you should do both."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "The full list, with every field and default, is in the\nstore reference. Recipes for\nspecific backends (object storage, Redis, OCI registries) live in\nHow-to guides."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-layers-worth-knowing",
    "content": "Cloud object storage is one store type, `experimental_cloud_object_store`,\nwith a `provider` field selecting between `aws`, `gcs`, `azure`, `ontap`,\n`r2`, and `oci`. Configs and older docs showing a top-level `s3` key predate\nthat consolidation and will not parse."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-keys-the-store",
    "content": "A store is content-addressed, so what goes in a key is the digest, and every\nserver, worker, and build client sharing a cache must compute that digest the\nsame way. SHA-256 and BLAKE3 produce different keys for identical bytes, so\nuploads made under one function are cache *misses* for clients using the\nother."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-keys-the-store",
    "content": "Forge defaults to SHA-256. Declare it explicitly anyway, in the `global`\nblock, so the file says what it depends on:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-keys-the-store",
    "content": "Then match it on the client. For Bazel, in `.bazelrc`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-keys-the-store",
    "content": "Forge accepts uploads whose client-declared digest function differs from\nthe server default, but logs a warning the first time it sees each mismatch:\nmixed digest functions divide the cache and reduce shared hits."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-keys-the-store",
    "content": "`max_open_files` is in that block for a related reason: a `filesystem` store\nholds file descriptors. At startup Forge tries to raise its own soft\n`nofile` limit to this value (default 24576) and sizes an internal semaphore\nfrom whatever the kernel actually granted, so if the hard limit is lower the\neffective value is lower too, and a warning is logged."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "We compared both algorithms on an Apple M2 Max with 12 CPU cores and 32 GB of\nmemory, running macOS 15.6 and Bazel 9.1.1. Each of two alternating runs built\n`//:forge` against a fresh local Forge filesystem cache with hash and\nsize verification enabled. Uploads were synchronous, and the Bazel output and\nForge cache directories were isolated by algorithm."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "Phase"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "SHA-256 runs"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "SHA-256 mean"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "BLAKE3 runs"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "BLAKE3 mean"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "Compile and populate an empty cache"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "179.90 s, 180.18 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "180.04 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "181.56 s, 185.57 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "183.57 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "Rebuild from the populated cache"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "6.39 s, 5.93 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "6.16 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "6.74 s, 6.41 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "6.58 s"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "Both population builds performed 2,620 actions. Each cached rebuild reported\n1,458 remote cache hits. SHA-256 was 1.9% faster while populating the cache and\n6.3% faster on the cached rebuild in this test, so SHA-256 remains the default."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-function-to-pick",
    "content": "These are whole-build measurements from one macOS machine, not a universal\nhashing benchmark; compilation, storage, scheduling, and operating-system\ncaching dominate much of the elapsed time. Measure your own representative\nworkload before changing algorithms. Keeping every participant aligned matters\nfar more than a small isolated timing difference."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "`eviction_policy.max_bytes` caps it, and once full the least-recently-used\nentries are evicted to make room. An evicted CAS entry is just a future cache\nmiss; the client rebuilds and re-uploads. An evicted entry that a cached\naction result still points at is the problem `completeness_checking` exists to\nsolve."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and the config loader enforces it: if a `cas` and an `ac` entry with the\nsame `instance_name` name the same store, startup fails with `CAS and AC use\nthe same store '...' in the config`. Beyond that check, they have very\ndifferent shapes. The CAS holds many large-ish blobs and wants compression and\ndeduplication; the AC holds small metadata records, wants none of that, and\nwants `completeness_checking`."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A worker materialises action inputs onto disk by hard-linking out of its\nstore, which only works if the store is filesystem-backed. `fast_slow` with a\n`filesystem` fast tier gives it that, and lets the slow tier be a shared\nremote CAS, or a `noop`, when the fast tier *is* the storage. `basic_cas.json5`\ntakes the second option."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. A `filesystem` store owns its directories: it scans them at boot and\nmanages eviction against its own view of what's there. Two processes pointed\nat the same paths will evict each other's data. Share by putting a real shared\nbackend behind them (object storage, Redis) or by having one process serve the\nCAS over gRPC to the others."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Listeners, the eleven services, and the port split that keeps the worker API\noff your public interface."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "All eighteen store types, every field, every default."
  }],
