import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Platform properties",
  "description": "How the scheduler decides which worker gets an action, why a mismatch queues forever instead of failing, and how to read the matching diagnostics it already emits."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone whose actions are queueing, and anyone about to\nrun more than one kind of worker. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** an\naccurate model of the matching rules, plus the log lines that tell you which\nrule you tripped. &#x2A;*Time:** fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Platform properties are the only vocabulary a build tool and a worker fleet\nshare. The action says *I need this*; the worker says *I have this*; the\nscheduler decides whether those two statements are compatible. No\ntype checking happens across that boundary beyond what you configure, and no\nnegotiation, which is why a typo produces a build that waits rather than a\nbuild that fails."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-places-a-property-appears",
    "content": "A property key has to be declared in all three, and they have to agree."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-places-a-property-appears",
    "content": "The scheduler's `supported_platform_properties` is the schema. It maps each\nkey to a **type**, and that type decides how the value on the action is\ncompared against the value on the worker. A key that isn't in this map is\nnot a valid key anywhere, for either side."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "What the action's value means"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "What it takes to match"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "`minimum`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "A numeric floor. Parsed as `u64`."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "The worker's value must be &#x2A;*≥** the action's."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "`exact`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "A literal string."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "The worker's value must be **byte-for-byte identical**."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "`priority`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Informational; passed through to the worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Any value matches, but the worker must still **have the key**."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "`ignore`"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Requestable but unenforced."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Always matches, and the worker need not have the key at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-four-property-types",
    "content": "Most documentation you'll find elsewhere describes three of these. `ignore`\nexists and is the only one that exempts a worker from carrying the key."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-priority-is-not-the-escape-hatch-people-assume",
    "content": "The matching loop checks key presence *before* it checks value\ncompatibility. For every property the action requests, the scheduler looks\nthe key up on the worker; if it isn't there, the worker is rejected,\nregardless of type. `priority` only tells the scheduler to stop caring about\nthe *value*."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-priority-is-not-the-escape-hatch-people-assume",
    "content": "A worker that omits `OSFamily` entirely will never receive an action that\nrequests `OSFamily`, even though the scheduler declares `OSFamily` as\n`priority`. This is why the example configs advertise `OSFamily: { values: [\"\"] }`: an empty string is still a value, and presence is what's being\ntested. Only `ignore` skips the presence check."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "This is the most consequential and least visible behaviour in the system."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "When the scheduler assigns an action to a worker, it **subtracts** every\n`minimum` value the action requested from the worker's advertised value. When\nthe action completes, it adds them back."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "A worker advertising `cpu_count: 16` that receives four actions each asking\nfor `cpu_count=4` is, at that moment, advertising `cpu_count: 0`, and will\nmatch nothing further until one of them finishes. `minimum` properties are\ntherefore your real concurrency control, and `cpu_count` in particular is the\nknob that governs how many actions a worker runs at once."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "Two consequences worth internalising:"
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "An action requesting `cpu_count=32` against workers that advertise `16`\nwill never be scheduled. Not slowly; never. No bin-packing across\nworkers happens, and no error is raised."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "Setting `cpu_count=1` on every action, as the getting-started examples do,\nmeans a 16-core worker runs 16 concurrent actions. That's usually what you\nwant; it is a decision you're making either way."
  }, {
    "heading": "minimum-is-a-consumable-resource-not-a-filter",
    "content": "`max_inflight_tasks` on the worker is the separate, blunter cap: it counts\ntasks rather than resources, and `0` means unlimited."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-when-it-doesnt-match",
    "content": "The two failure modes are asymmetric, and knowing which one you're in tells\nyou where to look."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-when-it-doesnt-match",
    "content": "**An unknown key on a worker is a hard failure at registration.** Worker\nproperties go through the same type map as action properties, so a worker\nadvertising a key that isn't in `supported_platform_properties` is rejected\nwith `Bad Property during connect_worker()` and never joins the pool. You'll\nsee this in the worker's log at startup, and the effect is a fleet that looks\nsmaller than you provisioned."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-when-it-doesnt-match",
    "content": "**An unknown key on an action queues forever.** The scheduler types an\naction's properties at match time, not at submission time. When that typing\nfails, the scheduler logs `Error while running do_try_match` (wrapping\n`Unknown platform property 'x'`), moves on to the other queued actions, and\nleaves this one where it is; the action is not errored out and the client\nis not told anything. Because the pass ended in an error, the scheduler\nre-runs it after 100 ms, failing identically each time. From the build's\nside this is indistinguishable from a busy queue."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-when-it-doesnt-match",
    "content": "If a build sits at `0s remote` for more than a few seconds on an idle\ncluster, do not wait it out. The scheduler is not going to time out and\ntell you. Read the match logging described below; it is on by default."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "The scheduler has a built-in periodic dump of exactly this. It's on by\ndefault at a ten-second interval:"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "On each interval it logs, for every queued action, why no worker took it,\nplus the oldest five actions in each state (`Oldest actions in state`).\nMatching happens in two passes, and each pass has its own lines. First a\ncapability index narrows the fleet by key presence and by `exact` value:"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "Then the surviving candidates are checked for free capacity, which is where\n`minimum` values are compared:"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "Read those lines literally, because they mean different things:"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "**`lack of key`**: no worker advertises the key at all (for a `priority`\nor `minimum` property). Add it to the worker's `platform_properties` (an\nempty string is fine for `priority`), or change the scheduler's\ndeclaration to `ignore`."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "**`lack of matching`**: for an `exact` property, no worker carries that\nexact value; the line lists the values workers do have, so check for\ntrailing whitespace and case. A worker that omits the key entirely shows\nup here too, with an empty list."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "**`Property mismatch`**: the worker has the key, but its remaining\n`minimum` capacity (on the left) is below what the action asks for."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "**`All workers are fully allocated`*&#x2A; or &#x2A;*`cannot accept work`**: the\nproperties are fine; every worker is paused, draining, or at its\n`max_inflight_tasks` cap."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "None of these appearing at all, while actions queue, points at typing rather\nthan matching; look for `Unknown platform property` in the same log, which\nis the unknown-key-on-an-action case above."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "The example config"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-schedulers-own-diagnostics",
    "content": "shows the opposite setting for clusters where this output is too noisy in\nsteady state."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "Say you have two worker pools and want tests tagged for GPU to land only on\nthe GPU machines."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "**Scheduler**: declare the key and its type once:"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "**CPU workers**: they can omit the key entirely; an `exact` key a worker\ndoesn't carry never matches an action that requests it, and actions\nthat don't request `gpu` are unaffected either way. Advertising a value no\naction asks for behaves the same and keeps the intent visible in the config:"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "**GPU workers:**"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "**The build**: request it per target rather than globally:"
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "Targets that say nothing about `gpu` still match every worker, because the\nscheduler only checks properties the *action* requested. The asymmetry is\ndeliberate: adding a capability to your fleet doesn't invalidate the actions\nthat don't need it."
  }, {
    "heading": "a-worked-example-routing-to-a-gpu-pool",
    "content": "Note `query_cmd` above: a worker property can be computed at startup by\nrunning a command (not a shell; the string is split into words shell-style\nwith `shlex` and executed directly, with a cleared environment) and\nsplitting its output on newlines, one value per line. `nproc` is the\ncanonical use."
  }, {
    "heading": "about-container-image",
    "content": "It appears in nearly every example config and it is worth being precise about\nwhat it does, because the name suggests something that isn't happening:\n&#x2A;*Forge does not pull or launch a container per action.** No\ncode sits behind this key. It is an ordinary string property like any other."
  }, {
    "heading": "about-container-image",
    "content": "What it actually means is *this worker process is running inside this image*,\nwhich the worker asserts, and the scheduler matches on. The value has to be\nbyte-for-byte what your build requests, or actions queue forever in the usual\nsilent way. Containers and images\ncovers using it correctly."
