import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Scheduler internals",
  "description": "Action merging, the awaited-action database, how a worker is chosen, and what happens when an action fails."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone tuning a scheduler, debugging an action that\nqueued forever, or wondering why two identical builds did not share work.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** the merge rule, the ordering rule, the\nmatching rule, and the retry rule, each of which has at least one\ncounter-intuitive edge. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty-five minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The scheduler's job sounds small: take actions from clients, give them to\nworkers. Almost all of its complexity comes from three questions it has to\nanswer that the protocol does not specify: when are two actions the same\none, which of the queued actions goes next, and what does \"this failed\" mean."
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-actionscheduler-trait",
    "content": "If you go looking for one (and older material about Forge will send you\nlooking) you will not find it. The abstraction is split by *who is asking*,\ninto three traits in\n:"
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-actionscheduler-trait",
    "content": "**`ClientStateManager`*&#x2A; is the client's view: submit an action, subscribe to\nits progress. &#x2A;*`WorkerStateManager`*&#x2A; is the worker's view: report that an\noperation changed state. &#x2A;*`MatchingEngineStateManager`** is the matcher's\nview: list actions that could be dispatched, assign one to a worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-actionscheduler-trait",
    "content": "Two more sit beside them: `WorkerScheduler`, which owns worker lifecycle, and\n`KnownPlatformPropertyProvider`, which is how the capabilities service learns\nwhich platform property keys an instance's scheduler knows about."
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-actionscheduler-trait",
    "content": "The split is what allows the whole state layer to be swapped:\n`SimpleSchedulerStateManager` implements all three traits and is generic over\nan `AwaitedActionDb`, which is either `MemoryAwaitedActionDb` or the\nstore-backed `StoreAwaitedActionDb` used for Redis. It is also why \"the\nscheduler\" in configuration can be a stack (the `cache_lookup` decorator is\noptional, and none of the shipped example configs use it):"
  }, {
    "heading": "there-is-no-actionscheduler-trait",
    "content": "The decorator in front is why a cache hit never appears in queue depth, and\nwhy `ActionStage::CacheCheck` is never emitted by the scheduler crate."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "Two clients asking for the same work should wait on one execution. The key\nthat decides this is `ActionUniqueKey`, and it has exactly three fields:\ninstance name, digest function, and the action digest."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "That key is wrapped in `ActionUniqueQualifier`, which is either `Cacheable`\nor `Uncacheable`. Only the `Cacheable` variant merges."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "Three things people expect to be in that key and are not:"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "**No salt exists.** A comment in the source refers to one; the struct has no\nsuch field. If you need two textually identical actions to be distinct, the\ndifference has to be in the action itself."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "**`do_not_cache` is never read.** The REAPI flag exists on the message and\nForge parses it, but no code in the repository branches on it. Setting\nit does not stop a result being written."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "**The flag that actually matters is `skip_cache_lookup`,** and its effect is\nasymmetric in a way worth stating plainly: it disables the cache **read**,\nand it disables **merging**, but it does **not** disable the cache\n**write**. An action submitted with `skip_cache_lookup` runs on its own,\nignoring any existing result, and then overwrites that result."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-are-two-actions-the-same-action",
    "content": "It is exactly what you want for \"re-run this and fix the cache\": force a\nreal execution, and let everyone else benefit from the answer. If it also\nsuppressed the write, the poisoned entry you were trying to replace would\nsurvive."
  }, {
    "heading": "ordering-is-one-64-bit-integer",
    "content": "`AwaitedActionSortKey` packs the entire queue ordering into a single `u64`:\nthe upper 32 bits are the priority (shifted from `i32` into unsigned range so\nbyte order matches numeric order), and the lower 32 bits are the insertion\ntimestamp in whole seconds XORed with `u32::MAX`."
  }, {
    "heading": "ordering-is-one-64-bit-integer",
    "content": "The XOR is what makes an *earlier* timestamp sort *higher*, so the rule falls\nout of the encoding: **priority dominates absolutely, and ties break in\nfavour of the action that arrived first**, at one-second resolution; actions\ninserted within the same second are ordered by operation ID, which is\neffectively arbitrary. It has no aging, no starvation\nguard, and no fairness across clients. A client that submits everything at\npriority 1000 will starve a client at priority 0 indefinitely, and nothing in\nthe scheduler will intervene."
  }, {
    "heading": "ordering-is-one-64-bit-integer",
    "content": "Priority is a contract between the people sharing a cluster, enforced\nsocially. If you need it enforced mechanically, that has to happen in front\nof the scheduler."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-state-enums-and-one-that-never-appears",
    "content": "`ActionStage` is the wire-facing state a client sees.\n`SortedAwaitedActionState` is the database index state the scheduler sorts\non. They overlap but are not the same, and conflating them while reading the\nsource is a reliable way to get lost."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-state-enums-and-one-that-never-appears",
    "content": "Notably: &#x2A;*`ActionStage::CacheCheck` is never produced by the scheduler\ncrate.** It exists in the protocol, but by the time an action reaches the\nscheduler the cache lookup has already happened in the decorator in front of\nit; see where an action's path forks.\nA client waiting to observe `CacheCheck` will wait forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "The matching engine walks the queue in sort order, and for each action looks\nfor the first worker that satisfies the action's platform properties. The\nsource still carries an `O(n*m)` TODO in\n,\nbut the walk is narrowed first: a `WorkerCapabilityIndex` (an inverted index\nover `Exact` and `Priority` properties) produces the candidate set, and only\nthat set is scanned in LRU order, checking availability and `Minimum`\nvalues per worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "For clusters of tens or low hundreds of workers this is not the bottleneck.\nIt is worth knowing about before you plan for thousands."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "`allocation_strategy` is a smaller knob than its name suggests. The two\noriginal values choose **which end of the LRU the scan starts from**, and\nnothing else. Starting from the least-recently-used end spreads load;\nstarting from the most-recently-used end packs work onto hot workers and\nleaves the rest idle long enough to be scaled down. `least_loaded` takes the\neligible worker with the fewest running actions, least recently used among\nequals, so a worker that just joined takes the next action instead of\nwaiting for every older worker to be used once more, and a burst that lands\nwhile most of the fleet is still starting does not fill the first worker to\nits concurrency cap. None of them is a bin-packing algorithm."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "The admission ledger only subtracts what an action declares, so an action\nthat declared too little is invisible to it. &#x2A;*`live_memory_veto`** names a\n`minimum` property, usually `memory_kb`, that the scheduler checks against the\nfree memory each worker reports with its keepalive (the cgroup limit less\nits working set, or `MemAvailable` without a limit). The working set is\n`memory.current` less the `inactive_file` page cache in `memory.stat`, as\nkubelet counts it for eviction, except that `file_dirty` and\n`file_writeback` pages still count as used until they reach the disk, so\ninactive, clean cache a worker's own I/O built up does not count against\nit. A worker reporting less than the action asks for is skipped for that\naction; a worker that reports\nnothing, such as one older than v1.7.3, is never vetoed. Two metrics go with\nthis: `worker.keepalive.gap` is the seconds between consecutive messages from\na worker, which is what `worker_timeout_s` should be judged against, and\n`worker.disconnections` carries a `worker.disconnect.reason` of\n`disconnected`, `drained`, `timeout`, `kill_unacknowledged`, `error`,\n`removed` or `shutdown`."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "Within a priority the queue is ordered by insert time to the nanosecond, on\nboth backends, so a burst submitted together dispatches in arrival order."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "The pass keeps that order when a worker frees up while it is running. A pass\nreads the queue one action at a time, a store round trip each on a\nstore-backed queue (the listing serves the record it already loaded, and\nonly the client keepalive is read), so on a long queue a worker that\nfinishes mid-pass would otherwise hand its room to whatever action the pass\nhappened to be reading, newer than everything it had already passed over. Instead, actions that found\nno room are parked in the order the queue listed them, highest priority first\nand then oldest, and when the fleet's capacity changes the pass offers the\nroom to the parked actions in that order before the one in hand, asking the\nfleet once per property shape. `scheduler.matching.parked_dispatched` counts\nthe parked actions a worker was actually told to run that way; a non-zero\nrate is normal on a busy fleet and means the head of the queue is being\nserved."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "When a worker reports an action killed for memory, by its own reservation\nenforcement or by the kernel near the limit, &#x2A;*`memory_escalation`** requeues\nthe action with the reservation multiplied by `percent` (default 200) instead\nof failing it. With `ladder_kb` set to the memory values of the fleet's size\nclasses, a kill steps the reservation to the next class up instead of\nscaling it. The next dispatch carries the raised value, so the worker holds\nthe action to that. The reservation grows to the largest memory any connected\nworker advertises (or `max_kb`); the last step reserves that worker whole,\nits memory and, through `cpu_property`, its CPU, so the action runs alone.\nOnly a kill there fails the action, with a message saying no worker in the\nfleet can run it. Escalations have their own budget (`max_steps`, default 8)\nand do not spend `max_job_retries`. The execution histograms carry an\n`execution.outcome` attribute so the killed samples can be told from the\ncompleted ones."
  }, {
    "heading": "matching-is-first-fit-over-an-lru",
    "content": "The `historical_resource` scheduler that fills reservations reads hints by a\nkey ladder, each step probed only when the action carries that key:\n`(target_id, action_mnemonic)`, then `target_id`, then the\naction's `action_digest`, then its `command_digest` (each `hash-size` or\n`hash/size`), then the mnemonic alone. The digest keys reach every client,\nwhether or not it sends Bazel request metadata; the action digest is what\norigin events carry, so it is what a producer fed by them writes. A hint may name a `class&#x60; instead of numbers, and\n&#x2A;*`classes`** on the same scheduler defines the fleet's size ladder: named\npoints with `cpu_count`, `memory_kb` and `disk_kb`, listed ascending. An\naction no hint matches takes the class picked by `class_by_mnemonic`, then\n`class_by_timeout` (Bazel test sizes arrive as timeouts), then\n`default_class`; without classes the raw `cold_start` numbers apply. With\n`memory_escalation.ladder_kb` set to the classes' memory values, a memory\nkill steps the reservation to the next class instead of scaling it, and a\nkill at the top class fails the action. Older hint files still load: every\nnew field is optional."
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "Every property key a worker advertises has a declared type:"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "Type"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "Meaning"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "`Exact`"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "The action's value must equal the worker's"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "`Minimum`"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "The worker's value must be at least the action's, **and is consumed**"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "`Priority`"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "The worker must have the key; its value is not compared (the source marks value-based preference as a TODO)"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "`Ignore`"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "Accepted and disregarded"
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "A *worker* announcing a property key the scheduler does not know is rejected\nat connect time. An *action* naming an unknown key is not rejected at\nsubmission: the key is only translated during the matching pass, where it\nproduces an `Unknown platform property` error that is logged, and the action\nstays queued. That is the harder of the two to diagnose, so if an action\nsits in the queue with workers idle, check the scheduler log for that\nmessage before anything else."
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "`Minimum` is the one that changes how you think about a worker. Its value is\n**decremented when an action is dispatched and restored when the action\ncompletes**; see\n. A\nworker advertising `cores: 16` is not saying \"I am a 16-core machine, send me\nanything\"; it is a pool of 16 units that actions draw down and return. That\nis the mechanism behind running several actions concurrently on one worker,\nand it is why getting the advertised number wrong oversubscribes the machine\nrather than queueing."
  }, {
    "heading": "platform-properties-are-typed-and-minimum-is-a-resource-pool",
    "content": "Platform properties covers this from\nthe configuration side."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "The attempt counter is incremented by one event, and two other events that\nused to spend it now have budgets of their own."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "**`UpdateWithError` increments the attempt count, unless the error is\n`ResourceExhausted`.** Backpressure is free. A worker saying \"I am too busy\"\ndoes not spend one of the action's lives, which is what makes it safe for a\nworker to shed load."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "**`UpdateWithDisconnect` counts against `max_worker_loss_retries`** (default\n10\\), not `max_job_retries`. A worker that vanishes mid-action is usually not\nthe action's doing, so the action goes back to the queue without spending one\nof its lives; the separate budget is what stops an action that reliably\nOOM-kills its worker from taking one down forever. Past it the client sees a\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION` saying the worker was lost that many times."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "**`UpdateWithEscalation` counts against `memory_escalation.max_steps`**\n(default 8), not `max_job_retries` either: the scheduler chose to run the\naction again with more memory, and the fleet's ceiling bounds how far that\ngoes."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "The limit is checked as `attempts > max_job_retries`, strictly, so the\ndefault of `3` permits **four** total executions. That off-by-one is in the\nname: three *retries* after one initial attempt."
  }, {
    "heading": "retries-and-what-failed-means",
    "content": "When an action exhausts its retries, the client receives\n`ActionStage::Completed` with `ActionResult.error` set: a *successful* RPC\ndescribing a failed action. A client that only inspects the gRPC status will\nread this as a clean completion. This is what the REAPI specifies, and it\nis the single most common source of \"the build passed but nothing was\nbuilt\" reports."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-timeouts-with-very-different-defaults",
    "content": "`max_action_executing_timeout_s` &#x2A;*defaults to `0`, which means disabled.**\nIt is not the only guard against a hung action: the worker kills the process\nwhen the action's own timeout (or the worker's `max_action_timeout_s`, 20\nminutes by default) elapses, and the scheduler re-queues an executing action\nwhose worker stops sending updates for `worker_timeout_s`. What this knob\nadds is a ceiling for the case where the worker is alive and heartbeating\nbut sends no update for a specific action; with it disabled, that case has no\nceiling. On a shared cluster it is worth setting."
  }, {
    "heading": "two-timeouts-with-very-different-defaults",
    "content": "`client_action_timeout_s` defaults to 60. If no client is listening for that\nlong, the action is killed with `DeadlineExceeded`. This is a deliberate\nanti-orphan measure (a client that pressed Ctrl-C should not leave work\nrunning), but it also means a client that disconnects and reconnects slowly\ncan lose work it expected to still be running."
  }, {
    "heading": "who-writes-the-action-cache-entry",
    "content": "Not the scheduler. &#x2A;*The worker writes the action cache entry, and it does so\nbefore telling the scheduler that the action finished.** The ordering is what\nguarantees that a client which observes completion and then queries the cache\nfinds the result there."
  }, {
    "heading": "who-writes-the-action-cache-entry",
    "content": "What gets written depends on the exit code, via `should_cache_result` in\n.\nWith the defaults, a **successful** action's result goes to the action cache\n(`SuccessOnly`), and a **failed** action's result goes to the historical\nresults store (`FailuresOnly`) instead. A non-zero exit code is not an error\nin the REAPI sense (it is a legitimate, cacheable outcome), but by default\nForge keeps it out of the action cache so that a failing compile is\nre-attempted rather than replayed."
  }, {
    "heading": "where-the-exact-truth-lives",
    "content": "Every scheduler field and default is in the generated\nscheduler configuration reference.\nFor the operational levers, see tuning."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Check the three fields in the merge key first: instance name, digest\nfunction, action digest. A different instance name is the usual answer,\nand the second most common is one client sending `skip_cache_lookup`,\nwhich opts that action out of merging entirely."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Cache hits never enter the queue; on a Bazel client they are answered by\n`GetActionResult` before `Execute` is ever sent, and with a `cache_lookup`\nscheduler they are answered by the decorator in front of the queue. If\nclients are genuinely waiting with an empty queue, look at whether the\n`Execute` calls are being rejected up front (missing inputs return\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION`) rather than queued."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "It swaps the state layer for one backed by RediSearch indexes with\noptimistic concurrency. Two consequences: `Code::Aborted` from an internal\noperation means \"reload and retry\", not \"failed\"; it is the normal\noutcome of a version conflict. And action merging becomes best-effort,\nbecause there is a small window where a just-written action is not yet\nvisible to the index; a short retry narrows it but does not close it. Two\nidentical actions submitted within milliseconds of each other may both\nrun."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not with the Redis backend. Matching there is driven by pub-sub with this\ninterval as a poll fallback, and pub-sub delivery is not guaranteed, so\nthe interval is load-bearing, and raising it raises worst-case dispatch\nlatency directly. It defaults to five seconds."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not inside the scheduler. Ordering is priority-dominant with no aging and\nno per-client fairness, so a client submitting everything at high priority\nwill starve everyone below it. Enforcement has to sit in front of the\nscheduler: separate instance names with separate schedulers, or an\nadmission layer that normalises priority."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "What the worker does with an action once the scheduler hands it over,\nincluding exactly how much isolation you are and are not getting."
  }],
