export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "CMake with recc",
  "description": "Accelerate CMake builds with Forge as a remote cache, using recc as the bridge."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running Forge instance. If you don't have one, start with the Quickstart."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "This tutorial follows the approach Reid Kleckner laid out in\nDistributed builds of LLVM with CMake, recc, and Forge.\nRead his post for the LLVM-scale walkthrough and the reasoning behind\neach piece. This page is the short, hands-on version for your own\nprojects."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "*Read time: \\~5 minutes. Hands-on time: another \\~5 if you have Docker and a\npackage manager handy; longer on Linux if you build `recc` from source.*"
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "This tutorial gets you from zero to a CMake build hitting a Forge\nremote cache. Works on Linux and macOS (tested on macOS Apple Silicon;\nLinux x86\\_64 follows the exact same commands)."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "We use `recc`, a small Remote Execution\nv2 client originally from Bloomberg, as a CMake compiler launcher. We run\nit in **cache-only mode**: compiles still happen on your machine, only the\n*outputs* travel through Forge. That keeps the setup tiny while still\ngiving you cross-machine and cross-branch caching."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "Docker"
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "CMake 3.16+"
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "A C/C++ compiler (clang or gcc)"
  }, {
    "heading": "1-start-forge",
    "content": "The image is multi-arch (x86\\_64 and ARM64) as of v1.6.0, so it runs\nnatively on Linux and Apple Silicon alike."
  }, {
    "heading": "1-start-forge",
    "content": "Confirm it came up:"
  }, {
    "heading": "2-install-recc",
    "content": "Works on Linux too if you have Homebrew on Linux."
  }, {
    "heading": "2-install-recc",
    "content": "`recc` now lives in the BuildBox monorepo (the old `recc` and\n`buildbox-common` repositories redirect there). On Ubuntu / Debian,\ninstall the build deps listed in the BuildBox README:"
  }, {
    "heading": "2-install-recc",
    "content": "Then build BuildBox, which builds and installs `recc` with it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "2-install-recc",
    "content": "`recc` is now on your `PATH`. If you hit issues, see the\nBuildBox install docs\nand the packaged recc page."
  }, {
    "heading": "3-create-the-example",
    "content": "Drop these two files into a fresh project directory and `cd` into it.\nYou can also copy them from"
  }, {
    "heading": "3-create-the-example",
    "content": "in the repo."
  }, {
    "heading": "4-point-recc-at-forge",
    "content": "What each one does:"
  }, {
    "heading": "4-point-recc-at-forge",
    "content": "`RECC_SERVER`: gRPC endpoint for the CAS / Action Cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "4-point-recc-at-forge",
    "content": "`RECC_INSTANCE=main`: must match an `instance_name` in your Forge\nconfig. `basic_cas.json5` exposes both `\"\"` and `\"main\"`; `recc`\ndefaults to `\"\"` unless it was built with a different\n`DEFAULT_RECC_INSTANCE`, so setting it explicitly removes the guesswork."
  }, {
    "heading": "4-point-recc-at-forge",
    "content": "`RECC_CACHE_ONLY=1`: if there's no cached result, build locally\ninstead of waiting on a remote worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "4-point-recc-at-forge",
    "content": "`RECC_CACHE_UPLOAD_LOCAL_BUILD=1`: push the result of a local compile\nback up so the next build (or the next teammate) gets a hit. It only\nhas an effect together with `RECC_CACHE_ONLY`."
  }, {
    "heading": "5-build-it",
    "content": "You should see `Hello, Forge!`."
  }, {
    "heading": "6-confirm-the-cache-is-live",
    "content": "Wipe the build directory and rebuild. This time the compile output\nshould come straight out of Forge:"
  }, {
    "heading": "6-confirm-the-cache-is-live",
    "content": "You should see an `Action Cache hit` line for the compile, like the\ncaptured output below:"
  }, {
    "heading": "6-confirm-the-cache-is-live",
    "content": "That's Forge serving the compile result instead of the compiler\nrunning again. Linking still happens locally. `recc` only ships compile\nactions, not link actions, by default."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Not in this setup: `RECC_CACHE_ONLY=1` means misses compile locally\nand only the outputs travel through Forge. Drop that variable\nonce you have remote workers configured and recc will dispatch\ncompiles to them."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "recc ships compile actions only by default; links always run locally.\nFor remote link steps you want a full RBE client; see\nExamples and templates."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Point `RECC_SERVER` at a shared Forge deployment and keep\n`RECC_INSTANCE` identical across machines. Forge rejects\nrequests for an instance name it does not serve, so a mismatch shows\nup as recc errors on stderr and every compile running locally."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-further",
    "content": "Point `RECC_SERVER` at a shared Forge deployment to share the\ncache across your team."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-further",
    "content": "Drop `RECC_CACHE_ONLY` once you have remote workers configured to\nactually offload compile work. See\nRemote execution."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-further",
    "content": "For PCH, LTO, and other gnarly C++ build features at scale, read\nReid Kleckner's writeup."
  }, {
    "heading": "going-further",
    "content": "recc is caching compiles against one server. Next is making that server shared, which is where a compiler cache stops being a local optimisation."
  }],
  "headings": [{
