  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache working on your own machine. The Quickstart gets you there."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The source-available release of Forge is designed to run on your\nown infrastructure. This page covers what changes between the\n10-minute quickstart and a deployment that\nserves your team without 3 AM pages."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-to-self-host",
    "content": "Pick on-prem when one of these is true:"
  }, {
    "heading": "when-to-self-host",
    "content": "**Data residency.** Build artifacts can carry source code,\ntoolchains, even credentials. Self-hosting keeps them in whatever\nregion or boundary you need (EU GDPR, US FedRAMP boundaries,\nair-gapped corp networks)."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-to-self-host",
    "content": "**Specialised hardware.** GPU workers, ARM cross-compile fleets,\nin-house silicon. On-prem lets you use anything that can run a\nLinux binary."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-to-self-host",
    "content": "**You already operate stateful services.** If your team owns\nKubernetes, Postgres, S3-compatible storage, adding Forge is\nmarginal work."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-to-self-host",
    "content": "If you'd rather not run it yourself, Enterprise offers a managed\ndeployment; see Open source and Enterprise."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "A Forge deployment is composed of four roles. The same binary\nserves all of them; the JSON5 config decides which subset to run."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Role"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "What it does"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Statefulness"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "**CAS server**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Stores and serves content-addressed blobs."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Stateful"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "**AC server**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Maps `Action` digests to `ActionResult`s."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Stateful"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "**Scheduler**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Receives `Execute` calls, dispatches to workers."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Queue in memory by default, or in Redis"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "**Worker**"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Runs the action in a per-action work directory, uploads outputs to CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "Stateless"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-ships-in-the-box",
    "content": "A single binary can run all four. For anything beyond a single\ndeveloper, run them on separate processes so you can scale and restart\neach independently."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Pick the storage backend.** The quickstart's `basic_cas.json5` keeps\neverything under `/tmp` on one machine, which is fine for a 10-minute\ndemo and useless for a team. Pick one before rolling out:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Filesystem**: single-node clusters, sub-100GB caches."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Cloud object storage**: anything multi-node. The\n`experimental_cloud_object_store` has providers for S3, R2, GCS,\nAzure Blob, ONTAP S3 and OCI; see\nS3 and compatible."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Redis**: hot-key acceleration in front of the durable store.\nOptional but cheap latency wins."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Pick the deployment substrate.** Most teams land on one of:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Kubernetes**: Helm charts for Kubernetes ship with\nForge Enterprise; the `kubernetes/`\ndirectory in this repository holds only Kustomize building blocks used\nby its own examples."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Bare VMs**: `systemd` units, one binary per role, with a load\nbalancer in front. See Deploy on bare metal."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Docker Compose**: a reasonable starting point for a handful of\ndevelopers. See Deploy with Docker Compose."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Plan capacity.** Heuristics from production clusters:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "CAS storage: 5 to 20 GB per active developer, depending on\nlanguage. C++ skews high; Go skews low."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "Worker CPU: 1 vCPU per concurrent action. Headroom matters more\nthan peak."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "Network: cache reads are the hot path. Provision at least 1 Gbps\nbetween workers and CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Set up TLS.** TLS on every listener, and client certificates where\nthe network is not trusted. The\nProduction configuration page has the\ncertificate layout and TLS and auth the\nper-hop settings."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-rollout-checklist",
    "content": "**Wire up metrics.** Forge pushes OTLP metrics; there is no\nPrometheus scrape endpoint. Send them to an OpenTelemetry Collector\nand let Prometheus and Grafana read from there. See\nObservability."
  }, {
    "heading": "container-registry",
    "content": "Official images are published to GitHub Container Registry. Pull a\nspecific version tag; there is no `latest` tag to fall back on, and\npinning is what you want in production anyway."
  }, {
    "heading": "container-registry",
    "content": "The `/pkgs/container/forge`\npage lists every published tag."
  }, {
    "heading": "backups--recovery",
    "content": "The CAS is the only stateful piece you can't trivially rebuild from\nclients. Snapshot strategy depends on the backend:"
  }, {
    "heading": "backups--recovery",
    "content": "**Filesystem**: `rsync` or your filesystem's snapshot facility\n(ZFS, Btrfs). Restore by stopping the CAS, swapping the directory,\nstarting again."
  }, {
    "heading": "backups--recovery",
    "content": "**S3-compatible**: versioning + lifecycle policies handle the\nprimary copy. For DR, cross-region replication."
  }, {
    "heading": "backups--recovery",
    "content": "**Redis**: treat as ephemeral. Loss is a cache miss, not data loss."
  }, {
    "heading": "backups--recovery",
    "content": "The Action Cache can be wiped without data loss; you'll re-execute\neverything until it warms back up."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, for permitted use. Most of the monorepo is\n`FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0`: internal use, modification, and redistribution\nfor non-competing purposes are allowed. Metrics and remote persistent\nworkers are Business Source License modules: fine for an individual\ndeveloper's cache, but shared, production, or commercial use of those\nmodules needs Forge Enterprise or an intentionally\ninexpensive commercial license. Meaningful contributors may be\neligible for waivers; contact the maintainers before relying on one.\nOpen source and Enterprise works through which files\ncarry which licence and why nothing in the binary tells you; the operative\ntext is the license page."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Compute for the control plane (a few small VMs), CAS storage (the\ndominant cost in a healthy cluster), worker compute, and the engineer\nhours to run it all. Which of those dominates depends on your fleet and\nyour build, and the honest answer is that you will not know the shape of\nthe bill until you have run it for a quarter.\nOpen source and Enterprise sets out what the\nmanaged Enterprise tier does instead of you."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. Nothing artificially limits cache size, action count,\nworker count, or team size: the self-hosted build is the same\ncodebase run in production, subject to each module's license, minus\nthe operational tooling and support the paid tiers add."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A cache your whole team hits is the prerequisite for the next section: having\nthe actions themselves run somewhere other than a laptop."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The JSON5 shape for a real cluster, and the settings that only start to\nmatter under load."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "One binary per role under systemd, if that is where this is going to live."
  }],
  "headings": [{
