  "title": "Run multiple workers",
  "description": "Add workers to a running Forge: how they share the CAS, how the scheduler matches them, and the two things about the shipped compose example that will mislead you."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone with remote execution working against one worker\nwho now wants several. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** three workers pulling\nfrom one queue, and a clear idea of how to make it thirty. &#x2A;*Time:** forty\nminutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A working scheduler and at least one worker. See Remote execution."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "One worker is a demonstration. The reason to add more is that the scheduler\nalready assumes them: it holds a queue, and every worker that connects to the\nworker API announces what it can run and starts taking work off that queue.\nNothing about adding the second worker is different from adding the tenth."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "What does need thought is the part that goes wrong most often: workers do not\nshare a disk. They share the CAS, and they share it over gRPC."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-topology",
    "content": "Three roles, three kinds of process, on one network:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-topology",
    "content": "The CAS server owns the bytes. The scheduler owns the queue. Workers own\nnothing durable. They are the disposable tier, which is exactly why they are\nthe tier you scale."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-topology",
    "content": "Each worker keeps a local filesystem cache in front of the shared CAS so that\nan input it has fetched once does not cross the network again. That is a\n`fast_slow` store whose `slow` half is a `grpc` store pointing at the CAS\nserver. Compose stores covers the wrapper in\ngeneral; this is its most load-bearing use."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-worker-config-looks-like",
    "content": "`servers: []` is not an oversight. A worker listens on nothing; it dials out to\nthe scheduler's worker API and to the CAS. That is why the worker tier needs no\ningress, no certificates and no load balancer, and why it is safe to run many\nof them on machines that are otherwise unreachable."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-worker-config-looks-like",
    "content": "Every worker can run this identical file. The only things that must differ per\nworker are the paths, and only because two processes on the same host would\notherwise collide."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-worker-config-looks-like",
    "content": "A worker removes its `work_directory` and recreates it every time it starts.\nPoint it somewhere that exists only for this purpose, never at a directory\nthat holds anything you want to keep, and never at a path two workers on the\nsame host both use."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-scheduler-picks-a-worker",
    "content": "The scheduler will only send an action to a worker whose advertised\n`platform_properties` satisfy the action's platform requirements, and it will\nonly consider property keys listed in its own\n`supported_platform_properties`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-scheduler-picks-a-worker",
    "content": "The value is the matching rule. `exact` means the strings must be equal.\n`minimum` means the worker's number must be at least what the action asks for,\nwhich is what makes `cpu_count` usable as a sizing hint. `priority` does not\nrestrict matching at all: the worker only has to advertise the key, and the\nvalue is passed to the worker as information. Despite the name, the scheduler\ndoes not currently use it to prefer one worker over another. `ignore` lets an\naction request a key without requiring the worker to have it."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-scheduler-picks-a-worker",
    "content": "A key an action requests that the scheduler does not list is a key the\nscheduler cannot match on: matching that action fails with `Unknown platform\nproperty`, and the usual symptom is an action that queues forever while idle\nworkers sit next to it. A worker that advertises a key the scheduler does not\nlist is rejected when it connects."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-scheduler-picks-a-worker",
    "content": "Platform properties is the page for\nthat contract; this page only needs you to know that every worker in a pool\nshould advertise the same keys."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-the-pool",
    "content": "Workers are stateless, so growing the pool is adding processes. Three things\nscale with them and are worth watching before you double the count."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-the-pool",
    "content": "The CAS server is now serving every input to every worker. It is the first\nthing to saturate, and Tuning has the storage levers for it.\nGive each worker a large enough local `fast` tier and most reads never reach\nit."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-the-pool",
    "content": "The scheduler holds one queue and matches against every connected worker. It is\nCPU-cheap but not free, and it is a single point of failure until you give it a\nshared store."
  }, {
    "heading": "scaling-the-pool",
    "content": "Disk on each worker host is consumed by the `fast` tier plus the work\ndirectory of every action in flight. `max_bytes` bounds the first and nothing\nbounds the second, so leave headroom."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Split the processes** if you have not already: one CAS server, one\nscheduler, workers separately. A single all-in-one config cannot become a\nmulti-worker deployment without this."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Put the scheduler's worker API on its own listener**, conventionally\n`:50061`, separate from the client-facing `:50052`. It is a backend API with a\ndifferent trust level."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Write one worker config** with a `fast_slow` store over a `grpc` store, and\nconfirm it works with a single worker before adding any others."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Start two more workers** from the same config, giving each its own\n`content_path`, `temp_path` and `work_directory` if they share a host."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Run a build with high parallelism** (`--jobs=50` or so) and confirm the\nactions land across all three."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Each worker logs `Worker registered with scheduler` at startup, and the\nscheduler's `worker.connected.count` metric reads three rather than one."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A build with high `--jobs` shows actions executing on more than one worker."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Stopping one worker mid-build does not fail the build. Its actions are\nretried elsewhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Each worker's local `content_path` grows and then plateaus at its\n`max_bytes`, rather than growing without bound."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "`deployment-examples/docker-compose/` contains a working three-worker\ndeployment: `docker-compose-multi-worker.yml` with\n`cas-server-multi-worker.json5`, `scheduler-multi-worker.json5` and\n`worker-shared-cas.json5`. Start there. Two things in and around it will send\nyou the wrong way."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "**The `MULTI_WORKER.md` advice to share a CAS volume is not what the shipped\nconfig does.** That document tells you all workers must mount the same\n`cas-data` volume and share a `content_path`. The worker config next to it does\nnot do that. It uses a worker-local filesystem tier over a `grpc` store, which\nis the correct design, and it writes to a path the shared volume does not even\ncover. Workers share the CAS by talking to the CAS server. A shared filesystem\nbetween workers is neither required nor sufficient, and on anything other than\none host it is not available."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "You will also see this line in every worker's log at startup (the quoted name\nis the worker's `name`, which the shipped config leaves empty):"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "It is unconditional (every worker prints it, including a single worker) and\nit is describing the same stale advice. If your workers reach one CAS server\nover gRPC, you have satisfied the requirement it is gesturing at."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "**The healthchecks in the compose file probe a path that isn't served.** Both\nservices healthcheck `http://localhost:PORT/status`, but `/status` only exists\nwhen that server's `services` block includes `health`. Neither the CAS config\nnor the scheduler's client-facing listener has one. Add `health: {}` to the\nservices on the port you're probing, or drop the healthcheck."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads",
    "content": "`docker compose up -d --scale worker=5` only works against a service literally\nnamed `worker`. The shipped file declares `worker-1`, `worker-2` and\n`worker-3` as three separate services, so scaling it means collapsing them\ninto one service without per-worker volume mounts, at which point each\nreplica needs its paths derived from something unique rather than hardcoded."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Almost always the platform properties. Compare the keys the worker advertises\nagainst `supported_platform_properties` on the scheduler and against what the\nclient requests: a key the scheduler doesn't list can't be matched on, and a\nkey the worker doesn't advertise excludes it from anything requiring that key.\nCheck the scheduler log for the queue depth: actions queuing while workers\nidle is the signature."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The worker cannot reach the blob through its `slow` store. Check that the\n`grpc` store's address resolves from inside the worker (a container name that\nworks on the host network usually doesn't) and that the CAS server is the\nsame one the client uploaded to. This error also appears when the scheduler and\nthe workers point at different CAS servers."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "They are sharing `work_directory`, `content_path` or `temp_path`. The work\ndirectory is the worst of the three, because it is deleted and recreated at\nstartup: the second worker to start wipes the first one's in-flight actions.\nGive each process its own paths."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "You have moved the bottleneck to the CAS server, which is now serving inputs to\nevery worker. Confirm by watching its network and disk while a build runs.\nRaising each worker's `fast` tier `max_bytes` cuts the read traffic; past that\nit is a CAS scaling problem."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check `worker_api_endpoint.timeout` on the worker against `worker_timeout_s`\non the scheduler, and any proxy or load balancer between the two. The worker\nsends a `KeepAlive` RPC every `timeout / 2` (default timeout 5 seconds), and\nthe scheduler evicts a worker it has not heard from within `worker_timeout_s`\n(default 10 seconds), so a hop that delays or drops those RPCs looks like a\nflapping worker. HTTP/2 keepalive pings on this listener come from\n`advanced_http.http2_keep_alive_interval` on the scheduler's worker API\n`servers` entry. The worker API is a long-lived streaming connection, not a\nseries of requests."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Yes, and this is what `ISA` and `OSFamily` are for. Advertise the real values\non each worker and set `ISA` to `exact` on the scheduler, so an action built\nfor `x86-64` never lands on an `aarch64` worker. A pool of mixed architectures\nwith everything advertising the same values is a way to get silent, confusing\nbuild failures."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The worker and storage levers that matter once the pool is large enough to\npush something to its limit."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The matching contract in full, and the single most common reason an action never\ngets scheduled."
  }],
