export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Verify your cache",
  "description": "Prove the cache is actually serving hits rather than quietly falling back to local work, using your build tool's own accounting, not a status page."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who has pointed a build at Forge and wants\nevidence it is working, rather than the absence of an error. &#x2A;*What you'll\nhave at the end:** a measurement you can repeat, a number you can put in a\nmessage to your team, and the ability to tell a cold cache from a broken one.\n&#x2A;*Time:** about ten minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running Forge and a build pointed at it. See the Quickstart and Connect your build."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A misconfigured remote cache almost never fails loudly. Bazel's default is to\ntreat cache errors as soft: if the endpoint is unreachable, the digest function\ndoes not match, or the instance name is wrong, it logs a line you will not\nnotice and builds everything locally. The build succeeds. It is just not\nusing the cache."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "\"No errors\" proves nothing. What proves something is the client's own\naccounting of where each action's result came from."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge exports telemetry over OTLP and nothing else: there is no HTTP\nmetrics route in the binary, and the `/status` path a `health` service\nmounts only reports whether the process is up and its stores pass a\nself-check. It will happily return OK for a server no client is\nsuccessfully talking to. Use it as a liveness check and nothing more; see\nObservability for the real telemetry\npath."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-measurement",
    "content": "The test is a build, a clean, and the same build again. The first populates\nthe cache; the second must not do the work."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-once-cold",
    "content": "Read the last line of the output. It looks like this:"
  }, {
    "heading": "build-once-cold",
    "content": "Every process is local, which is what you expect: nothing was in the cache\nyet. If this run reports remote cache hits, something built these outputs\nbefore. That is fine, but you are no longer measuring a cold start, so pick\na target nobody has built or change a source file."
  }, {
    "heading": "discard-the-local-results",
    "content": "Not `bazel clean --expunge`, which also throws away the external repositories\nand turns the next step into a ten-minute fetch that tells you nothing about\nthe cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "Now the same line should read differently:"
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "That number, `remote cache hit`, is the whole measurement. It is the count\nof actions Bazel did not run because Forge handed back a result."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "The second build's `processes:` line reports a non-zero `remote cache hit`\ncount."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "The second build is substantially faster in wall time than the first."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "The store's `content_path` on the server has grown; for the shipped\n`basic_cas.json5` that is\n`/tmp/forge/data-worker-test/content_path-cas` (inside the container\non the Docker path), and the action cache is beside it under\n`content_path-ac`."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-again-warm",
    "content": "Stopping Forge and re-running the build makes the hits disappear. This\none matters more than it looks: it is the only check that proves the hits\nwere coming from *this* server rather than from a `--disk_cache` on the\nclient."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-number-honestly",
    "content": "A hit rate below 100% on an unchanged tree is normal. Bazel counts several\nkinds of work in that line, and only some of it is cacheable:"
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-number-honestly",
    "content": "`internal` actions (symlink trees, file writes, workspace status) never go\nto a remote cache. They are cheap and they will always appear."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-number-honestly",
    "content": "Actions whose inputs genuinely changed are supposed to miss. If you edited a\nheader between the two runs, everything downstream of it will rebuild, and\nthat is the cache working correctly rather than failing."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-number-honestly",
    "content": "Actions that are not deterministic will miss forever. A rule that embeds a\ntimestamp, an absolute path, or a hostname produces a different action digest\non every machine and every run, so it can never hit. If your rate is stuck low\non an unchanged tree, this is usually why, and\nCorrectness and hermeticity is the\npage that explains how to find them."
  }, {
    "heading": "reading-the-number-honestly",
    "content": "What should worry you is *zero* hits on a second run of an unchanged tree.\nThat is not a tuning problem; that is the cache not being used at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "getting-more-detail-than-the-summary-line",
    "content": "When the summary is not enough, ask Bazel for the per-action record:"
  }, {
    "heading": "getting-more-detail-than-the-summary-line",
    "content": "The execution log records, for each action, whether it was a cache hit and\nwhat its digest was. It is the tool for answering \"why did *this specific*\naction miss\": compare the digest for the same action across two runs, and if\nit changed, the action's inputs or command line are not stable."
  }, {
    "heading": "getting-more-detail-than-the-summary-line",
    "content": "For wire-level questions (is the client even reaching the server, is it\ncalling `FindMissingBlobs`) turn on gRPC logging instead:"
  }, {
    "heading": "getting-more-detail-than-the-summary-line",
    "content": "An empty log means Bazel never opened a connection, which points at the flag\nrather than at the server."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "The measurement is the same everywhere: build, clean, rebuild, and read the\ntool's own accounting. Only the vocabulary changes. Buck2 shows where each\naction ran in `buck2 log what-ran`; Pants counts remote cache requests in\nits end-of-run stats when `[stats].log` is on; recc logs an\n`Action Cache hit` line per compile with `RECC_LOG_LEVEL=info`. Look for\nwhatever your tool calls a cache hit rather than for a Forge-side signal;\nthe server cannot tell you whether the client asked it the right question."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "The overwhelmingly common cause is `--remote_instance_name`. Forge\nregisters services under the instance names its config lists, and the shipped\n`basic_cas.json5` registers each service twice, once under `\"\"` and once\nunder `\"main\"`, precisely so both work. A config that registers only `main`\nanswers a client that sends the empty instance name with an error,\n`'instance_name' not configured for ''`; Bazel treats a cache error as a\nwarning, prints it once, and builds locally, which reads as zero hits."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "Second most common: the two runs did not use the same digest function. Bazel\nsends SHA-256 by default; if you set `--digest_function=blake3` on one run and\nnot the other, every digest differs and nothing can hit."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "Two builds share cache entries only if they produce identical action digests,\nand the digest covers the full command line, the input file contents, *and*\nthe platform properties. Different compiler versions, different absolute paths\nto the workspace, or a different `--platforms` value all produce different\ndigests."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "This is the problem remote execution and toolchain pinning exist to solve, and\nToolchains and hermeticity\ncovers it properly. For a cache-only setup, the practical first step is making\nsure everyone builds with the same toolchain rather than the one on their\n`PATH`."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "`bazel clean` removes the output tree but not the repository cache or a\n`--disk_cache` if you have one configured, and disk cache hits are counted\nseparately from remote ones. A run that is fast with zero remote hits is\nbeing served locally. Stop Forge and run the build again: if it is\nstill fast, you have been measuring Bazel's local caches the whole time."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "Eviction. The shipped example caps the CAS at 10 GB and the action cache at\n1 GB, and a filesystem store evicts least-recently-used content when it\ncrosses the limit. On a real workload those limits are small."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "The failure mode worth knowing is that the two stores evict independently: a\nCAS eviction can leave an action cache entry pointing at blobs that no longer\nexist, so the client gets a hit and then fails to download it. That is what a\ncompleteness-checking store prevents, and\nScaling the CAS covers sizing."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "The flags for Bazel, Buck2, Pants, Siso, and CMake with recc, now that you\nknow how to tell whether they worked."
  }, {
    "heading": "other-build-tools",
    "content": "A cache serving hits is the prerequisite for running the actions themselves\nsomewhere else."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "the-measurement",
    "content": "The measurement"
  }, {
    "id": "build-once-cold",
    "content": "Build once, cold"
  }, {
