  "title": "Local testing",
  "description": "Keep a Forge cluster on your own machine to test config changes, reproduce scheduler behaviour, and prove a client works, in the two shapes the repo itself uses."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone changing a config, debugging a client, or\nreproducing something a shared cluster did. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:**\na local cluster in whichever of the two shapes matches what you're testing,\nand a reliable way to tell what actually ran where. &#x2A;*Time:** ten minutes\nfor the single-process shape."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Your first remote action gets you\none remote build. This page is what you do afterwards: keep a cluster around\nas a test fixture, and know which shape to run for what you're testing."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Nothing here has a toolchain opinion; it uses whatever is on your `PATH`,\nand needs no Nix. That's deliberate: it isolates *protocol and config*\nproblems from *toolchain* problems.\nToolchains and hermeticity\nis the other axis."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "You're testing"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Run"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Why"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "A client's flags, a store config, scheduler matching"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Single process"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Fastest loop; one log to read"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Cross-process wiring, `grpc` stores, separate CAS and scheduler"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "The compose split"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Same topology as production, and what CI exercises"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Toolchain hermeticity"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Neither; see Toolchains"
  }, {
    "heading": "which-shape-to-run",
    "content": "Different problem"
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-1-one-process",
    "content": "`--remote_cache` and `--remote_executor` both point at `127.0.0.1:50051`.\n`50061` exists only so the worker can register with the scheduler; Bazel\nnever talks to it."
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-1-one-process",
    "content": "The interesting parts of that config, for when you start editing it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-1-one-process",
    "content": "Then:"
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-1-one-process",
    "content": "It's tempting to assume `--remote_executor` alone is enough, since the\nexecutor reads and writes the CAS anyway. It isn't. Without an explicit\n`--remote_cache`, Bazel calls `Execute` with an action digest it never\nuploaded, and the server correctly rejects it:\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION: Action ... is missing from CAS`."
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-2-the-compose-split",
    "content": "When you need separate processes (a CAS process and a scheduler process\ntalking over `grpc` stores, which is what a real deployment looks like),\nthe repo ships that too, and its own integration tests run against it."
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-2-the-compose-split",
    "content": "This publishes the CAS on `50051` (plus a TLS listener for the same services on\n`50071`) and the &#x2A;*scheduler, with the execution and AC services, on\n`50052`**. The scheduler's worker API on `50061` is not published from the\nscheduler container at all, so it is only reachable inside the compose\nnetwork; the worker reaches it by service name."
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-2-the-compose-split",
    "content": "That port split is why the repo's `.bazelrc` reads the way it does:"
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-2-the-compose-split",
    "content": "`self_execute` points at `50052`. If you're running\n`local_rbe_self_test.json5`, which puts everything on `50051`, these two\n`--config` flags will not work; use the explicit flags from shape 1\ninstead. This mistake is common because both live in the same\nrepo."
  }, {
    "heading": "shape-2-the-compose-split",
    "content": "Production configuration explains the split\ntopology this is a miniature of."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "Wall-clock time is a bad signal and Bazel's summary line is only a rough\none. Ask the build event log directly:"
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "That is exactly what CI asserts, twice: once cold, then again after\n`bazel clean` to confirm the second run is served remotely rather than from\nBazel's local disk cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "For per-action detail rather than per-test, `--execution_log_json_file`\nrecords the runner for every spawn."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "First run: Bazel's `INFO: N processes: ...` summary line counts the\ngenrule and the test as `remote`, with no `remote cache hit`."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "After `bazel clean` and a re-run: the genrule that produces\n`dummy_test.sh` shows up as `remote cache hit` while the test itself\nre-executes remotely, because `--nocache_test_results` is set."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-what-actually-ran",
    "content": "`jq` reports `remote` both times."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "At least one worker's `platform_properties` must satisfy what your actions\nrequest, and every requested key must be declared in the scheduler's\n`supported_platform_properties`. Starting with just `cpu_count: \"minimum\"`\non the scheduler and `cpu_count: { values: [\"1\"] }` on the worker unblocks\nmost local setups.\nPlatform properties has the full\nmatching rules and the scheduler's own diagnostic logging."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Every service in the example config omits `instance_name`, so it defaults to\nthe empty string, the same default Bazel uses when you don't pass\n`--remote_instance_name`. If you add an `instance_name` server-side, pass\nthe matching flag client-side, or every request fails this way."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "`use_namespaces` and `use_mount_namespace` are Linux-only and default to\n`false`. Setting either to `true` on an unsupported platform makes the\nworker exit at startup. Leave them unset for local testing."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Everything the example config writes lives under\n`/tmp/forge-local-rbe-test/`. Stop the server and delete that tree for\na genuinely cold start; `bazel clean` only clears Bazel's side."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Usually a previous run still holding `50051` or `50061`, or the compose\nstack still up. `docker compose rm --stop -f` in\n`deployment-examples/docker-compose` clears the second case."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes: the config exposes standard Remote Execution API services, so Buck2,\nPants, Siso, and recc point at `127.0.0.1:50051` the same way. The Bazel\nflags are just the worked example;\nConnect your build has the others."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "For sandboxed, cached single-machine builds, yes. What it can't give you is\nparallelism beyond one worker's `cpu_count`, or sharing with anyone else.\nThe moment you want either, you want a real deployment:\nProduction configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and it's the main reason to keep a local cluster around. Store\ncomposition, scheduler property maps, and service wiring all fail the same\nway locally as they do in a cluster, and the feedback loop is seconds\ninstead of a deploy."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Configs and project scaffolds to copy, for the shape you actually want."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "When you want to write these config files yourself rather than\nedit an example."
  }],
