import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "TLS and authentication",
  "description": "Put TLS on the listeners clients reach, use mutual TLS as the access control, and understand exactly what Forge does and does not authenticate."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone about to expose Forge beyond a single trusted\nnetwork. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** encrypted listeners, client\ncertificates as the gate, and an accurate picture of what is still unguarded.\n&#x2A;*Time:** an hour, most of it spent on certificates."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running deployment you can restart. See Production configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Start with the honest version, because it changes what you build."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Forge has no user accounts, no password, no token check and no\nauthorization rules. Nothing in the binary decides whether a\nparticular caller is allowed to read a particular blob. The access control you\nget is **mutual TLS** (the listener refuses connections from clients that\ncannot present a certificate signed by a CA you name) plus whatever your\nnetwork already does. Everything past the handshake is trusted equally."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "That is a coherent security model, and it is the one to build. It is not the\nmodel you would get from a system with an architect table, so it pays to be explicit\nabout it before you plan around a feature that does not exist."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "A production deployment exposes three surfaces, and they do not want the same\ntreatment."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "The **CAS and AC** listener, conventionally `:50051`, is what every developer's\nBazel talks to. It is the widest surface and the one that needs TLS."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "The **execution** listener, conventionally `:50052`, carries the same client\ntraffic for remote execution. Treat it identically to the CAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "The **worker API**, conventionally `:50061`, is not a client API. Workers dial\nit, and a caller who reaches it can register as a worker and be handed your\nbuilds to execute. It belongs on a private interface or inside a private\nnetwork, and no amount of TLS makes it safe to expose publicly. Bind it to an\ninternal address, and keep it on a separate `servers` entry from anything a\nclient touches so that the two cannot be confused.\nRun multiple workers covers why that listener\nexists and who dials it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "Servers and services covers how those\nlisteners are declared; this page is about what to put on them."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-tls",
    "content": "Every entry in `servers` may carry a `tls` block on its `listener.http`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-tls",
    "content": "Both paths are PEM files read at startup. The certificate file may hold a full\nchain. The key may be PKCS8, SEC1 or PKCS1 (all three are accepted), but it\nmust contain **exactly one** key, and a file with two will fail startup rather\nthan pick one."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-tls",
    "content": "Once TLS is on, the listener negotiates ALPN `h2`, and clients must address it\nas `grpcs://` rather than `grpc://`. That scheme change is the single most\ncommon reason a first TLS rollout fails: a `grpc://` client sends plaintext\ninto the handshake, the server logs `Failed to accept tls stream` and drops the\nconnection, and the client sees a connection error that says nothing about\nTLS."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-tls",
    "content": "`deployment-examples/docker-compose/` ships\n`example-do-not-use-in-prod-key.pem` and\n`example-do-not-use-in-prod-rootca.crt`. The names are the documentation.\nTheir private key is in the public repository, so anything they protect is\nprotected from nobody."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-the-client-prove-who-it-is",
    "content": "Adding `client_ca_file` changes the listener from encrypted to gated:"
  }, {
    "heading": "make-the-client-prove-who-it-is",
    "content": "Now every connecting client must present a certificate signed by that CA. This\nis the access control. No optional or opportunistic mode exists: set\n`client_ca_file` and client certificates are required, leave it unset and no\nclient certificate is ever examined."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-the-client-prove-who-it-is",
    "content": "`client_crl_file` is a PEM certificate revocation list, and it is how a leaked\nclient key stops working before its expiry. It is worth setting up on day one\neven if the list is empty, because the moment you need it is the moment you do\nnot want to be learning how it is wired."
  }, {
    "heading": "make-the-client-prove-who-it-is",
    "content": "What you get from this is coarse: a client either holds a valid certificate or\nit does not. Forge does not read the subject, does not map it to an\nidentity, and does not vary permissions by it. Issuing per-team or per-developer\ncertificates is still worth doing, because it lets you revoke one without\nreissuing all of them, but the difference is visible to your CA, not to\nForge."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-clients-at-the-new-scheme",
    "content": "Bazel needs the CA that signed the server certificate, and its own certificate\nif you turned on mutual TLS:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-clients-at-the-new-scheme",
    "content": "Forge is also a client of itself: a worker dials the scheduler, and a\n`grpc` store dials an upstream CAS. Those connections carry their own\n`tls_config`, with a different shape from the listener's:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-clients-at-the-new-scheme",
    "content": "`ca_file` validates the remote. `cert_file` and `key_file` are what the remote\nsees when it demands a client certificate, so they are only needed against a\nlistener with `client_ca_file` set (setting one without the other is a startup\nerror). Setting `use_native_roots: true` instead of `ca_file` uses the\noperating system's trust store, which is what you want against a public CA and\nnot what you want against your own; when it is set, `ca_file` is ignored with\na warning. The scheme and the block go together: a `grpcs://` address with no\n`tls_config` is rejected at startup, and so is a `tls_config` on a `grpc://`\naddress."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-clients-at-the-new-scheme",
    "content": "The worker's `worker_api_endpoint` takes the same block:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-experimental_identity_header-actually-does",
    "content": "You will find this on `ServerConfig`, and its name invites a wrong conclusion:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-experimental_identity_header-actually-does",
    "content": "Two things are true about it. The first is that it is not authentication: the\nvalue it gates on is caller-supplied and never verified against anything, so any\nclient can claim any identity. The second is that it does not read the header\nyou name. `required` is passed to the telemetry layer, which looks for an\nOpenTelemetry **Baggage** entry named `enduser.id`, and rejects the request with\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION` when that entry is absent:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-experimental_identity_header-actually-does",
    "content": "`header_name` is not read anywhere in the codebase."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-experimental_identity_header-actually-does",
    "content": "`required: true` is useful for exactly one thing: making sure your traces and\nlogs can attribute a request to a person or a CI job, by refusing traffic that\nwould be unattributable. That is a real operational need. Just do not put it in\nthe column marked access control."
  }, {
    "heading": "credentials-to-an-upstream-service",
    "content": "When Forge proxies to something that does want a token (a hosted REAPI\ncache, a gateway in front of one), a `grpc` store can attach headers:"
  }, {
    "heading": "credentials-to-an-upstream-service",
    "content": "`headers` are static and sent on every request. Note that, unlike most string\nfields in the config, header values are not run through `${VAR}` expansion:\nthe value in the file is sent verbatim, so a secret placed here lives in the\nfile and the file needs to be protected accordingly. `forward_headers` takes a\nlist of header names to copy from the incoming client request instead, which is\nhow a client's own credential reaches the upstream without Forge holding\none. Forwarding is incompatible with `experimental_read_batching`, and a store\nthat configures both is rejected at startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Decide the boundary.** Which listeners are reachable from where. If the\nworker API is reachable from anywhere a developer is, stop and fix that first.\nIt is a bigger hole than plaintext."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Issue certificates** from a CA you control: one server certificate per\nlistener hostname, one client certificate per team or per CI system. Keep the\nCA key somewhere the build system cannot read."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `tls` to the client-facing listeners** with `cert_file` and `key_file`\nonly. Restart, and confirm a `grpcs://` client works before adding anything\nelse."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add `client_ca_file` and `client_crl_file`** to those same listeners, and\ndistribute client certificates. Every client breaks at this step until it has\none, so do it deliberately rather than at the end of the day."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Update the internal hops** (worker to scheduler, `grpc` stores to upstream)\nto `grpcs://` with a matching `tls_config`."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A build against `grpcs://` succeeds and populates the cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The same build against `grpc://` on the same port fails rather than silently\nfalling back."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "With `client_ca_file` set, a client with no certificate is rejected at the\nhandshake, not after."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Revoking a client certificate and adding it to the CRL locks that client out\nafter a restart."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "`openssl s_client -connect host:50051 -alpn h2` shows your chain and\nnegotiates `h2`."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The key file holds more than one key (a file with none fails earlier, with\n`Could not extract key(s) from file`). A concatenated bundle is the usual cause.\nSplit it, and leave the chain in `cert_file` where multiple PEM blocks are fine."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Almost always `grpc://` against a TLS listener. The client sends plaintext HTTP/2\ninto a socket waiting for a handshake, the handshake fails, and the server drops\nthe connection. Check the scheme everywhere: Bazel flags, `grpc` store\naddresses, `worker_api_endpoint.uri`, and any healthcheck."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The client does not trust the CA that signed the server certificate. Point it at\nthe right `ca_file`, or `--tls_certificate` for Bazel. When the client is\nForge itself (a `grpc` store or a worker) the error reads `invalid peer\ncertificate: UnknownIssuer`. If the server certificate was issued by an\nintermediate, `cert_file` must contain the full chain, leaf first, because\nclients will not fetch a missing intermediate for you."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The listener now requires client certificates and this client has none. The\nserver logs it as `Failed to accept tls stream` with that reason, and the client\nsees the handshake aborted. That includes clients you may have forgotten are\nclients: workers dialing the scheduler, `grpc` stores, and anything probing a\nhealth endpoint."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Server certificates and a `grpc` store's client certificates are read once at\nstartup, and a worker re-reads its client files only when it reconnects to the\nscheduler. Renewal means restarting the process. Plan for it, because a\ncert-manager rotation that nothing restarts will quietly expire."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "You can't, inside Forge. It has no per-caller permissions. The\napproaches that work are running separate instances with separate stores for\nseparate trust domains, or putting an authorizing proxy in front and letting\nForge trust everything the proxy passes through. If you take the second\nroute, the worker API must not be behind the same proxy."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The rest of what changes between the example configs and something you run for\nother people."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "How listeners and service blocks fit together, and why splitting them by trust\nlevel is the shape to start from."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "the-three-listeners-have-different-trust-levels",
    "content": "The three listeners have different trust levels"
  }, {
    "id": "turn-on-tls",
    "content": "Turn on TLS"
  }, {
    "id": "make-the-client-prove-who-it-is",
    "content": "Make the client prove who it is"
  }, {
    "id": "point-clients-at-the-new-scheme",
    "content": "Point clients at the new scheme"
  }, {
    "id": "what-experimental_identity_header-actually-does",
    "content": "What `experimental_identity_header` actually does"
  }, {
    "id": "credentials-to-an-upstream-service",
    "content": "Credentials to an upstream service"
  }, {
    "id": "steps",
    "content": "Steps"
  }, {
    "id": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "When it doesn't work"
