  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** you are running Forge for other people and need to\nsee queue times, cache hit rates and worker behaviour on a dashboard.\n&#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a collector receiving OTLP from every\nForge process, Prometheus holding the series under the names the shipped\nrecording rules query, and a Grafana dashboard drawing them. &#x2A;*Time:** 30\nminutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running deployment you can set environment variables on."
  }, {
    "heading": "forge-pushes-it-is-never-scraped",
    "content": "The binary has no `/metrics` endpoint and no configuration that creates\none. Forge builds three OTLP exporters at startup (logs, traces and\nmetrics) and pushes to whatever endpoint the environment names. Every\nPrometheus-shaped thing in this pipeline happens downstream of the process."
  }, {
    "heading": "forge-pushes-it-is-never-scraped",
    "content": "That `namespace: forge` on the collector's Prometheus exporter is\nload-bearing, and it is the single most misunderstood part of this pipeline.\nThe instruments Forge declares are named `cache.operations`,\n`execution.queue.time` and so on, with **no `forge` prefix anywhere in the\nbinary**. The prefix is added by the collector. Bypass the collector and you\nlose it, along with every shipped recording rule and dashboard panel. See\nwhat happens if you skip the collector."
  }, {
    "heading": "forge-pushes-it-is-never-scraped",
    "content": "Individual developer cache use does not need a commercial licence. Teams\nusing metrics in shared, production or commercial settings can use Forge\nEnterprise or a separate licence. Note that instrumentation cannot be\nswitched off (see\nOpen source and Enterprise), and\nthe operative text is the licence page."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "Point every Forge process at a collector. Two variables carry the whole\ndecision:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "The value is parsed as a URL and the host and port are pulled out\nindividually, and each step `unwrap`s. A value with no scheme, or with no\nexplicit port, yields `None` for the port and **panics the process during\nstartup**, before it binds a socket."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "`http://otel-collector:4317` is correct. `otel-collector:4317` and\n`http://otel-collector` both crash the binary."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-configuration",
    "content": "`NL_OTEL_ENDPOINT` builds one client-side load-balanced gRPC channel shared by\nall three exporters. Leave it unset and each exporter falls back to the\nOpenTelemetry SDK's own resolution: `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` (or the\nper-signal `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_ENDPOINT` and friends), defaulting\nto `http://localhost:4317`. Both work; the explicit channel is what you want in\na cluster, because it re-resolves the collector's DNS as pods move. Either way\nthe transport is OTLP over gRPC: the binary is built with only the gRPC\nexporter, so `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL` cannot switch it to HTTP."
  }, {
