    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "Port 9090 belongs to the collector's Prometheus *exporter*, the thing\nPrometheus scrapes. Opening `http://localhost:9090` gets you a page of raw\nOpenMetrics text, not a query UI. The UI is `http://localhost:9091`."
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "The shipped collector config also runs a second metrics pipeline that\npushes the same stream into Prometheus's own OTLP receiver over HTTP\n(`otlphttp/prometheus`). Those series arrive without the `forge_`\nprefix, so do not be surprised to find both spellings in the same\nPrometheus; the rules and dashboard use the prefixed one."
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "Both manifests live in a `forge` namespace; `prometheus.yaml` is the\none that creates it, so apply it first. The collector's `ConfigMap`\ncarries the same metrics pipelines as the Compose file (no traces\npipeline), with a 1 GiB memory limiter instead of 512 MiB."
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "Then add the endpoint to every Forge container, CAS, scheduler and\nworker alike:"
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "On the scrape path, the collector's Prometheus exporter has\n`resource_to_telemetry_conversion` enabled, so every resource attribute,\nincluding anything you set in `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`, becomes a label\n(`deployment_environment`, `k8s_cluster_name`, `service_instance_id`).\nThe `promote_resource_attributes` list in the shipped Prometheus config\n(`k8s.pod.name`, `k8s.namespace.name`, `k8s.deployment.name` and a dozen\nmore) only governs the collector's second pipeline into Prometheus's own\nOTLP receiver; on that path attributes not on the list do not become\nlabels on the series (they survive only on `target_info`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "Prometheus can receive OTLP itself (`--web.enable-otlp-receiver`), but its\nreceiver is OTLP over HTTP at `/api/v1/otlp/v1/metrics`, and Forge's\nexporters are built with the gRPC transport only. Setting\n`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf` does nothing, so there is no\nworking configuration that sends the binary's metrics straight into\nPrometheus. Something that speaks OTLP/gRPC has to sit in between; the\nshipped collector is that something, and its `otlphttp/prometheus`\nexporter is exactly this hop."
  }, {
    "heading": "bring-up-the-pipeline",
    "content": "Read what happens if you skip the\ncollector before pointing the\nbinary at any OTLP/gRPC receiver other than the shipped collector: it\nrenames every metric."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "The `namespace: forge` setting on the collector's Prometheus exporter is\nwhat produces every metric name you have ever seen in a Forge dashboard.\nSend the OTLP stream anywhere else (another collector without that setting,\nan OTLP-native backend, or the shipped collector's own `otlphttp/prometheus`\npipeline into Prometheus's receiver) and the names change:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "Path"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "Series name for the `cache.operations` counter"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "Forge → collector → Prometheus scrapes the `prometheus` exporter"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "`forge_cache_operations_total`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "Forge → OTLP receiver that adds no namespace"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "`cache_operations_total` (or `cache.operations_total`, depending on the receiver's name translation)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "Everything shipped under `deployment-examples/metrics/` (the recording rules,\nthe Grafana dashboard, the alert expressions) is written against the first\nspelling. Against the second they all evaluate to an empty vector, which on a\ndashboard renders as a flat healthy zero rather than as a missing signal."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "If you take the un-prefixed path anyway, either rewrite the rules without the\nprefix or add a `metric_relabel_config` that puts it back. The collector path\nis the one the repository supports."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector",
    "content": "The exporter also rewrites OTLP names into Prometheus form: dots become\nunderscores, monotonic counters gain a `_total` suffix, and each histogram\nbecomes `_bucket`, `_sum` and `_count` series. The\nmetrics reference\nrecords the exact result for every instrument."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-cache-metrics",
    "content": "Every family except cache metrics is emitted with no configuration. Cache\nmetrics exist only for stores you explicitly wrap:"
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-cache-metrics",
    "content": "With `cache_metrics` absent, Forge constructs the same store graph without\na wrapper, a timer, an attribute allocation or an OpenTelemetry call on the hot\npath. The feature costs nothing when it is off, which is why it is off."
  }, {
    "heading": "turn-on-cache-metrics",
    "content": "Wrap the stores you actually want to measure, usually `CAS_MAIN_STORE` and the\nAC. Wrapping every layer of a `fast_slow` composition gives you one series per\nlayer and a hit-rate query that no longer means what you think it means."
  }, {
    "heading": "which-metrics-exist",
    "content": "The authority is the metrics reference, which is\ngenerated from `forge-util/src/metrics.rs` and its call sites. It records\nevery declared instrument, its type and unit, its histogram buckets, and (the\npart no hand-written table stays honest about) **whether anything in the\nbinary ever emits it**."
  }, {
