export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Your first remote action",
  "description": "Run a scheduler and a worker on your own machine, point Bazel at them, and watch a build action execute somewhere other than where you typed the command."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone with a working cache who wants to see remote\nexecution actually run. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a build whose\nactions Bazel reports as `remote`, running against a scheduler and worker\nyou started yourself. &#x2A;*Time:** about fifteen minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The goal here is deliberately small: prove the *protocol* works before you\ntouch anything else. Everything runs on your machine, in one process, using\nthe toolchain already on your `PATH`. No containers to build, no fleet to\nprovision, no hermeticity questions yet; those come next, and they're much\neasier to debug once you know the wiring is sound."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A Bazel workspace you can build (any one will do; the Forge repo\nitself works), and either Docker or Nix. Up to about 4 GB of free disk\nunder `/tmp` for the caches this creates (the three filesystem stores in\nthe config cap at 2 GB, 200 MB, and 2 GB)."
  }, {
    "heading": "get-the-config",
    "content": "The repo ships a config that is exactly this tutorial: CAS, action cache,\nscheduler, and one worker, all in a single process on localhost."
  }, {
    "heading": "get-the-config",
    "content": "It is worth reading before you run it: it is short, and every section of it\nmaps onto something you already know or are about to learn."
  }, {
    "heading": "start-the-cluster",
    "content": "Or, if you'd rather use the container image, mount the config in and publish\nboth ports:"
  }, {
    "heading": "start-the-cluster",
    "content": "Leave it running. You'll want its output visible; the scheduler logs here\nare the primary diagnostic for the rest of this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-the-worker-registered",
    "content": "The worker connects *outward* to the scheduler on `50061` a moment after\nstartup. In the server's log you should see it announce itself before you\nrun anything, at the default `INFO` level:"
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-the-worker-registered",
    "content": "If instead the log repeats `Error` with a connection failure every half\nsecond, the worker cannot reach the `worker_api` listener; check that the\n`servers` block in the config you downloaded still has the `50061` listener.\nForge does not serve gRPC reflection, so `grpcurl ... list` is not a\nway to check which services are up; the config file is."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "From any Bazel workspace:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "Both flags point at the same port here, because this config puts the cache\nand the execution service on the same listener. In a real deployment they're\nusually different processes and different ports; the flags don't change."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "`--remote_default_exec_properties=cpu_count=1` is worth passing even though\nthis config would accept an action without it. The scheduler here declares\n`cpu_count` as a `minimum` property and the worker advertises exactly `1`;\nan action that asks for nothing matches any worker and consumes none of\nthat capacity, which happens to work here, but declaring it explicitly is\nthe habit that will save you on the next page."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "Ask Bazel where each action ran rather than trusting the wall-clock number:"
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "That single word is the whole tutorial. `--nocache_test_results` forces the\naction to actually execute instead of being served from the action cache,\nwhich is what you want the first time; otherwise a cache hit will look\nlike a success and tell you nothing about whether execution works."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "The server's log shows `Worker registered with scheduler` before you run\nthe build."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "The build event log reports `strategy` as `remote`, not `local` or\n`worker`."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "The server's log shows `Executing command` from the in-process worker, so\nthe action left the queue rather than sitting in it."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "Running the same build again *without* `--nocache_test_results` completes\nnear-instantly: the worker's output went into your action cache, so the\nsecond run is a cache hit."
  }, {
    "heading": "read-the-result",
    "content": "That last one is the point of the whole reading path in miniature: remote execution\ndoesn't replace your cache, it *fills* it. Every action a worker completes\nbecomes a hit for whoever asks next."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "Four processes' worth of work happened inside one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "Bazel uploaded the action's inputs to the **CAS** and asked the\n**execution service** to run it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "The execution service handed the action to the **scheduler**, which\ncompared what the action asked for (`cpu_count=1`) against what the\nregistered worker advertised (`cpu_count: 1`) and found a match."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "The **worker** fetched the inputs from the CAS by digest, hardlinked them\ninto a sandbox under its `work_directory`, and ran the command."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "The worker uploaded the outputs back to the CAS and the result to the\n**action cache**, and the scheduler streamed completion back to Bazel."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-just-happened",
    "content": "Everything after this is that same loop with more machines in it."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Your action's platform properties don't match any registered worker, so the\nscheduler is holding it in the queue. This is the single most common\nfirst-run failure and it is silent by design: the scheduler keeps waiting\nin case a suitable worker shows up later."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check the server log for the scheduler's periodic match logging: `Property\nmismatch`, `No candidate workers due to a lack of key`, or `No workers\navailable to match!` (that last one means the worker never registered).\nEither way,\nPlatform properties walks through\nreading the scheduler's own matching diagnostics."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The execution service looked up the action digest in its `cas_store` and\ndid not find it, so the store Bazel uploaded to is not the store the\n`execution` service reads. The usual causes are `--remote_cache` and\n`--remote_executor` pointing at different deployments, or an `execution`\nentry whose `cas_store` is not the same store (or a `ref_store` to it) as\nthe `cas` and `bytestream` services on the client listener. Forge\nreturns the error with a `PreconditionFailure` detail so Bazel can re-upload\nthe missing blobs and retry; if it keeps repeating, the two stores are not\nthe same store."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The worker executed your action in a sandbox that doesn't have something\nyour local environment provides implicitly. This is remote execution doing\nits job: it converted an unstated dependency into a visible failure. It's\nalso out of scope for this page:\nToolchains and hermeticity\nis the fix."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Note that on this config the worker runs as *you*, on *your* machine, with\nyour `PATH`. If a command isn't found even here, the action is depending on\nsomething Bazel isn't declaring at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Some action types are excluded from remote execution by the workspace's own\nconfiguration; check for `--strategy` or `--spawn_strategy` settings in the\nworkspace's `.bazelrc`, and for `no-remote` tags on the target. Try a\ndifferent target before concluding the cluster is at fault."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "This config leaves `use_namespaces` and `use_mount_namespace` at their\n`false` defaults precisely so it runs unprivileged and on macOS. If you\nenabled them, you now need Linux and the corresponding capabilities. Turn\nthem back off for this tutorial."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Because it's the fewest moving parts that still exercises the real code\npaths: the worker here uses the same gRPC worker API, the same scheduler,\nand the same store implementations a production cluster does. Splitting into\nseparate processes changes the deployment, not the protocol. See\nProduction configuration for the split\nversion."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "You can, and some people do; a single-process cluster is a reasonable way\nto get sandboxed, cached builds on one machine. But it gives you no\nparallelism beyond one worker's `cpu_count`, and the filesystem stores are\npointed at `/tmp`. Local testing covers\nthe longer-lived version of this setup."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The cluster is build-tool agnostic: it speaks the Remote Execution API, and\nBuck2, Siso, Pants, and recc all connect to the same endpoints.\nConnect your build has the flag\nequivalents. The verification step differs per tool; look for whatever your\ntool calls its execution strategy in its own logs."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Everything this config writes lives under\n`/tmp/forge-local-rbe-test/`: CAS content, action cache, the worker's\nfast store, and its work directory. Stop the server and delete that tree to\nstart clean."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "How the scheduler decides which worker gets an action, and why a\nmismatch queues forever instead of erroring."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "If your action ran but failed on a missing compiler, this is the page you\nwant."
  }],
  "headings": [{
