import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "The config file",
  "description": "JSON5 mechanics, environment substitution, how the binary is pointed at a config, and how to find out a config is wrong before it takes a cluster down with it."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone about to edit a Forge config for the first\ntime. &#x2A;*What you'll know at the end:** what dialect the file is written in,\nhow to keep secrets and addresses out of it, how the binary finds it, and how\nto check one is valid without waiting for a production rollout to tell you.\n&#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you got from an example, for instance from Examples and templates."
  }, {
    "heading": "its-json5-not-json",
    "content": "Forge parses its config with `serde_json5`, not `serde_json`. The\ndifference matters every time you edit a file:"
  }, {
    "heading": "its-json5-not-json",
    "content": "Three things are legal here that plain JSON rejects: `//` and `/* */`\ncomments, unquoted object keys, and trailing commas. Nearly every shipped\nexample in `forge-config/examples/` uses all three, so a strict-JSON tool\npointed at one of them will report syntax errors that aren't real."
  }, {
    "heading": "its-json5-not-json",
    "content": "Most editors infer the dialect from the extension. `.json5` is the extension\nthe repo uses, and it's the one that gets you comment support instead of a\nwall of red squiggles. If you rename a config to `.json` to make a tool\nhappy, the tool will be happy and wrong."
  }, {
    "heading": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "Any string field that is deserialized through the config crate's shellexpand\nhelpers accepts shell-style variable references, including the\ndefault-if-unset form:"
  }, {
    "heading": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "`${SCHEDULER_ENDPOINT:-127.0.0.1}` means \"the value of `SCHEDULER_ENDPOINT`,\nor `127.0.0.1` if it isn't set\". That single feature is what lets one config\nfile serve a laptop and a cluster: the file names a default that works on your\nmachine, and the deployment overrides it with an environment variable."
  }, {
    "heading": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "The docker-compose deployment in this repo is built entirely on that pattern.\nIts worker and scheduler configs point at\n`grpc://${CAS_ENDPOINT:-127.0.0.1}:50051` and\n`grpc://${SCHEDULER_ENDPOINT:-127.0.0.1}:50061`, so the same files run\nunmodified whether the pieces are on one host or several."
  }, {
    "heading": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "Substitution also works for numeric-looking fields that take a size, because\nthose are parsed from strings:"
  }, {
    "heading": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "Substitution is opt-in per field, applied by the deserializer the field\ndeclares. The endpoint, path, and size fields you're most likely to want to\nparameterise do expand; an arbitrary field may not. If a `${VAR}` shows up\nverbatim in an error message or a log line, that field isn't one of them."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-binary-finds-the-config",
    "content": "One way only, and it is positional:"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-binary-finds-the-config",
    "content": "No `--config` flag, no search path, no default location, no environment\nvariable naming the file. The single positional argument is the whole\ninterface."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-the-binary-finds-the-config",
    "content": "That has one practical consequence worth internalising: **a container image\ndoes not carry its config**. Every deployment recipe in this repo mounts a\nconfig file in and passes its path as the argument. If you're writing a\nKubernetes manifest, the config is a ConfigMap you mount, not something baked\ninto the image."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "Here is the honest answer, because the alternative wastes an afternoon:\n&#x2A;*there is no `--validate` flag and no dry-run mode.** The binary takes a\nconfig path and starts."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "What saves you is the order in which it starts. `main()` reads and parses the\nentire config, and only afterwards raises the open-file limit, constructs the\nstores, and binds any socket, so a config that fails to parse is rejected\nbefore the process has touched a port, which makes \"run it and see\" a\ngenuinely safe validation step even on a machine where the real ports are\nalready in use by something else."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "Combined with `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`, which is on nearly every\nstruct in the config crate, that gives you a fast, precise loop:"
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "**Start the binary against the config.**"
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "**Read the first error, not the last.** A parse failure names the offending\nkey and its position. A misspelled `evicton_policy` produces an\n`unknown field` error naming `evicton_policy` and listing the fields that\nwere expected, not a silent default and a cache that never evicts."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "**Stop it once it's listening.** Once the process reports its listeners are\nup, the config parsed. Everything after that point is runtime behaviour, not\nconfig validity."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "A deliberately misspelled key makes the process exit before it prints\nanything about listeners."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "Fixing the spelling makes it start."
  }, {
    "heading": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "The failing run did not need a free port, because it never reached the\nbind."
  }, {
    "heading": "editor-validation-if-you-want-it",
    "content": "The config crate can emit a JSON Schema for the whole `CasConfig` type, which\neditors will use for completion and inline errors:"
  }, {
    "heading": "editor-validation-if-you-want-it",
    "content": "That writes `forge_config.schema.json` into the working directory. The\nfile is **not committed to the repo**; it's generated on demand, and it's\ngenerated from the same Rust types the binary parses with, so it can't drift\nfrom them. Point your editor's JSON5 schema mapping at the file you generated\nand misspellings show up as you type rather than at startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "editor-validation-if-you-want-it",
    "content": "Because the schema comes from `schema_for!(CasConfig)`, regenerating it after\nyou change Forge versions is the whole upgrade procedure. There's no\nhand-written schema to fall out of date."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Not with an include directive; the parser reads exactly one file. What you do\ninstead is split by *role*: one config for the CAS process, one for the\nscheduler, one for the workers, each a complete file naming the others by\nnetwork address. That's the same split described in\nthe section overview, and it's how the docker-compose\ndeployment is laid out."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Use environment substitution for the fields that carry them and set the\nvariables from your platform's secret mechanism: a Kubernetes Secret mounted\nas environment variables, a systemd `EnvironmentFile`, or your process\nsupervisor's equivalent. The config file itself then contains a variable name,\nwhich is safe to commit."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Every valid JSON document is also a valid JSON5 document, so that direction\nnever breaks. The other direction does: a config using comments or trailing\ncommas will be rejected by any strict-JSON tooling you point at it. If a\nlinter, formatter, or CI check complains about a file that Forge accepts\nhappily, the tool is the thing that's misconfigured."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and that's the boundary worth knowing. Parsing checks structure and\nspelling. It does not check that a store name referenced by a service exists\nin a useful state, that a worker can reach the scheduler address you gave it,\nor that a path is writable. Those are runtime failures, and they show up in\nlogs rather than at startup. Observability is where\nyou go to read them."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Declaring stores, naming them, and the idea that unlocks the rest of the\nconfig: stores compose, and one store can refer to another by name."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Every field and default, generated from the same Rust types described here."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "its-json5-not-json",
    "content": "It's JSON5, not JSON"
  }, {
    "id": "environment-substitution",
    "content": "Environment substitution"
  }, {
    "id": "how-the-binary-finds-the-config",
    "content": "How the binary finds the config"
  }, {
    "id": "checking-a-config-before-you-trust-it",
    "content": "Checking a config before you trust it"
  }, {
    "id": "editor-validation-if-you-want-it",
    "content": "Editor validation, if you want it"
  }, {
    "id": "faq",
    "content": "FAQ"
