    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "A Bazel `cc_binary` project pre-wired for Local Remote Execution: Nix-pinned toolchain, the LRE Bazel module, and a `user.bazelrc` you point at a cache and executor"
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "The template does **not** bundle a Forge server; you point it at one you\nalready have. It is also Linux-only, because the `lre-cc` toolchain it wires\nin currently supports `x86_64-linux` only.\nToolchains and hermeticity\nwalks the whole flow with real output, including the `user.bazelrc` values."
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "Older material referenced them. `nix flake init -t\n  github:toxicwind/forge#lre` (or `#cargo`, `#kubernetes`) fails with\n`does not provide attribute 'templates.<name>'`. If you came looking for one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "**A local cluster with no Nix**:\nLocal testing."
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "**Cargo / sccache**: not packaged as a template; the `bazel` template\nis the maintained path."
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "The repo also has a `templates/cmake/` directory (CMake with `recc`,\ncache-only), but `flake.nix` does not expose it under `templates`, so\n`nix flake init -t github:toxicwind/forge#cmake` fails the same way;\ncopy the directory by hand if you want it."
  }, {
    "heading": "project-templates",
    "content": "To customise: it's a flake, so fork and edit. The usual changes are pointing\n`user.bazelrc` at a real cluster, adding platform properties in\n`platforms/BUILD.bazel`, and pinning `inputs.forge.url` in `flake.nix`\nto a specific commit, kept in sync with the `local-remote-execution`\noverride in `MODULE.bazel`."
  }, {
    "heading": "three-client-side-patterns",
    "content": "Independently of which config you start from, there are three ways teams\nwire their builds. Most adopt them in this order."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-a--cache-only",
    "content": "Every action runs locally; results land in the shared cache. No worker fleet\nneeded, which makes it the cheapest thing that helps."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-a--cache-only",
    "content": "Right when you have one CI environment building similar things repeatedly,\nwhen you want shared caching across laptops without operating workers, or\nwhen you're still evaluating. This is\nGetting started."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-b--cache-plus-execution",
    "content": "Actions ship to a worker fleet; the cache underneath stores everything."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-b--cache-plus-execution",
    "content": "`--jobs=200` is the change people forget. Without it Bazel dispatches only\nas many actions as your laptop has cores, and the fleet sits idle; the\nlimiting factor is supposed to be pool size, not local CPU count."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-b--cache-plus-execution",
    "content": "Right when full builds take hours, when you're trying to shrink a CI fleet,\nor when you need actions to run on hardware developers don't have."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-c--hybrid",
    "content": "Small actions local, everything else remote, decided by policy."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-c--hybrid",
    "content": "This forces `Javac` local (for very small compilations the round-trip\ndominates) while everything else goes remote."
  }, {
    "heading": "pattern-c--hybrid",
    "content": "Right when your workload mixes very fast and very slow actions, or when\nlatency to the fleet is high enough that small actions lose. Reach for it\nwhen you have telemetry showing where time actually goes, not before;\nTuning covers getting that telemetry."
