export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Oracle Cloud (OCI) Object Storage",
  "description": "Back the cache with OCI Object Storage through its S3 Compatibility API: Customer Secret Keys, the two adjustments the store makes for you, and what has actually been verified."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running Forge on Oracle Cloud who wants the\ndurable tier to be an OCI bucket. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a\nbucket-backed CAS and Action Cache authenticated with a Customer Secret Key.\n&#x2A;*Time:** thirty minutes, most of it namespace and policy setup."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A config file you can edit (see Configuration), and an OCI tenancy you can create a bucket in."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "OCI is one `provider` value of the same cloud object store used by\nS3 and compatible,\nGCS and Azure Blob: six\nproviders over one store type, sharing the same multipart, retry and streaming\ncode paths."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-it-works",
    "content": "OCI Object Storage\nexposes an S3 Compatibility\nAPI,\nand the `oci` provider is a thin adapter pointing the S3 store at it. Two\nOCI-specific adjustments are applied automatically, and you do not configure\neither:"
  }, {
    "heading": "how-it-works",
    "content": "**Path-style addressing.** OCI requires `endpoint/bucket/key` rather than\nvirtual-hosted `bucket.endpoint/key` URLs. The endpoint is derived as\n`https://{namespace}.compat.objectstorage.{region}.oci.customer-oci.com`."
  }, {
    "heading": "how-it-works",
    "content": "**Checksums downgraded to \"when required\".** The AWS SDK otherwise adds a\ndefault trailing checksum that forces `Content-Encoding: aws-chunked`, which\nOCI rejects with `501 NotImplemented: AWS chunked encoding not supported`.\nWithout the downgrade, small single-`PUT` objects (notably Action Cache\nentries) fail."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-start",
    "content": "You need three things."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-start",
    "content": "**A bucket.** Create one in the Console under *Storage → Buckets*, or with\n`oci os bucket create`. Note its region, for example `ap-mumbai-1`."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-start",
    "content": "**Your Object Storage namespace.** A tenancy-wide identifier shown under\n*Tenancy details*, or from `oci os ns get`. It goes into the derived endpoint."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-start",
    "content": "**A Customer Secret Key.** See below."
  }, {
    "heading": "authentication",
    "content": "The S3 Compatibility API authenticates with Customer Secret Keys, a static\naccess-key and secret-key pair used as `access_key_id` and\n`secret_access_key`, signed with AWS SigV4."
  }, {
    "heading": "authentication",
    "content": "Generate one in the Console under *Profile → User settings → Customer secret\nkeys → Generate secret key*. The secret is shown **only once** at generation time, so copy it\nimmediately; the access key stays listed in the table and can be copied\nwhenever you like. OCI has no way to retrieve a secret after generation, so if\nyou lose it you rotate rather than recover."
  }, {
    "heading": "authentication",
    "content": "Grant the associated user read and write on the bucket with a least-privilege\npolicy:"
  }, {
    "heading": "authentication",
    "content": "Supply both keys through environment variables and reference them with `${VAR}`\nrather than pasting them into the config; see\nThe config file."
  }, {
    "heading": "authentication",
    "content": "The S3 Compatibility API supports no other credential. OCI-native\nauthentication (API signing keys, instance principals, resource principals)\nis not available through this store. If you run Forge on OCI compute and\nwant keyless workload identity, that is not currently supported: you use\nstatic Customer Secret Keys and rotate them yourself."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "A minimal CAS and Action Cache sharing one bucket, segmented by `key_prefix`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-recipe",
    "content": "That is the smallest thing that works, not the thing to run. In production,\nwrap the CAS in `verify` for integrity and front it with a `fast_slow`\nfilesystem tier to cut round trips, exactly as for AWS.\nCompose stores has the shapes."
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Field"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Required"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "What it is"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`namespace`"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "yes"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Object Storage namespace; used to derive the endpoint"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`region`"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "yes"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "OCI region id, for example `ap-mumbai-1`; also the SigV4 signing region"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`bucket`"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "yes"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Target bucket"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`access_key_id`"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "no"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Customer Secret Key access key; if either key is unset the store falls back to the AWS default credential chain, which reads `AWS_*` names, not OCI ones"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`secret_access_key`"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "no"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "Customer Secret Key secret"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "`key_prefix`, `retry`, …"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "no"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "The shared cloud object options"
  }, {
    "heading": "fields",
    "content": "OCI also accepts `us-east-1` as a `region` value to target the tenancy home\nregion, which is occasionally useful when a tool insists on an AWS region name."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Create the bucket** and note its region and your tenancy's Object Storage\nnamespace."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Generate a Customer Secret Key** and copy the secret immediately, because it is\nshown once."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Apply a least-privilege policy** scoped to that bucket, and export both keys\ninto the process environment."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add a lifecycle rule** aborting incomplete multipart uploads after a few days\nas a backstop."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the stores** with distinct `key_prefix` values, then run a build twice."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Objects appear under both `cas/` and `ac/` in the bucket during the first\nbuild."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The second build is a cache hit, which proves the Action Cache round trip\nworks. That is the path the checksum adjustment exists for."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A large artifact uploads and downloads intact, which exercises multipart."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "No incomplete multipart uploads accumulate between builds."
  }, {
    "heading": "operational-notes",
    "content": "**Reap incomplete multipart uploads.** When a multipart upload fails mid-flight\nthe store issues `AbortMultipartUpload` to clean up, and OCI honors both\n`ListMultipartUploads` and `AbortMultipartUpload`. A process killed *before* it\ncan abort still leaves orphaned parts, which is what the lifecycle rule is for."
  }, {
    "heading": "operational-notes",
    "content": "**Status.** This backend lives under `experimental_cloud_object_store`. When it\nwas added, its core paths (read, write, existence checks, ranged reads,\nmultipart and integrity) were verified against live OCI, including an\nend-to-end Bazel remote cache round trip, a 2 GiB multipart upload of roughly 400 parts with full byte\nverification, multipart abort and cleanup, and 200-way concurrent load. It has\nnot been benchmarked at sustained production scale or duration."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Something is re-adding the default trailing checksum the store removes. This is\nthe failure the checksum downgrade exists to prevent, and it shows up first on\nAction Cache entries because they are small single-`PUT` objects. Confirm you\nare on a Forge version that carries the `oci` provider rather than\npointing the `aws` provider at an OCI endpoint by hand."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The S3 Compatibility API takes Customer Secret Keys only. An API signing key,\nan instance principal or a resource principal will not work here. Check also\nthat `region` matches the bucket's region. It is the SigV4 signing region as\nwell as part of the endpoint."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`namespace` is wrong. It is the tenancy's Object Storage namespace from\n`oci os ns get`, not the tenancy name and not the compartment."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "It cannot be retrieved; OCI shows it once. Generate a new Customer Secret Key\nand delete the old one."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Yes, and they should. Segment them with `key_prefix` (`cas/` and `ac/` in the\nexample above) rather than provisioning two buckets. Lifecycle rules can still\ntreat the prefixes differently."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The `verify` wrapper and the `fast_slow` tier that turn this into something\nyou'd run in production."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The shared cloud object options (retry, expiry, buffering) documented in\nfull."
  }],
