export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Develop with Nix",
  "description": "The dev shell that generates the bazelrc files the rest of the build assumes, and what is actually inside it."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who wants the toolchain CI uses, and anyone\nworking on LRE, which needs it. &#x2A;*What you'll have at\nthe end:** the shell, the five generated `bazelrc` files it produces, and an\naccurate list of what it does and does not contain. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty\nminutes, mostly download."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The flake in the repository root is where almost all of Forge's tooling\nlives. Entering the shell is not just a convenience: the shell hook is what\nwrites the `bazelrc` fragments that `.bazelrc` pulls in with `try-import`,\nand `try-import` means their absence is silent. A build outside the shell is\nnot a broken build; it is a *differently configured* one."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix",
    "content": "The\nnext-gen Nix installer\nis the easiest path:"
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix",
    "content": "The `--enable-flakes` flag turns on the `nix-command` and `flakes`\nexperimental features, which the repository's flake needs."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix",
    "content": "**`nix` not found after installing.** Restart your terminal, or source\nthe profile script:"
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix",
    "content": "**`experimental Nix feature 'nix-command' is disabled`.** You used the\nstandard installer. Add this to `~/.config/nix/nix.conf`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "install-nix",
    "content": "**Disk space.** Toolchains, compiler binaries and build artifacts are\nlarge. Have **15 to 20 GB free** before the first `nix develop`."
  }, {
    "heading": "enter-the-dev-shell",
    "content": "First run downloads the toolchain, which takes a few minutes. After that,\nentering is close to instant; everything is content-addressed in\n`/nix/store`."
  }, {
    "heading": "enter-the-dev-shell",
    "content": "`CONTRIBUTING.md` recommends it strongly, and the reason is that the shell\nhas to be re-entered after every branch switch or change to a `.nix` file.\n`direnv` does that for you:"
  }, {
    "heading": "enter-the-dev-shell",
    "content": "To check the environment is active: `env | grep NIX` should print several\n`*NIX_*` variables."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "This is the part that matters and the part that is invisible. The shell hook\nwrites five things into your working tree:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Generated file"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "What it does"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`.pre-commit-config.yaml`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Symlink enabling the repo's pre-commit hooks"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`lre.bazelrc`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Points Bazel at the LRE toolchains"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`forge.bazelrc`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Gives your builds access to Forge's read-only cache"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`nixos.bazelrc`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Adds the required NixOS binary paths (NixOS only)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`darwin.bazelrc`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "Configures Darwin libs and frameworks (macOS only)"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-the-shell-generates",
    "content": "`.bazelrc` picks all four `bazelrc` fragments up with `try-import`, so\noutside the shell Bazel silently proceeds without them. That is why a build\ncan succeed outside Nix and still not match CI."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "The package list is in\n.\nBroadly:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "**Rust and build**: a `bazel` wrapper around Bazelisk (it unsets `TMPDIR`\nfirst), the pinned stable Rust toolchain from the LRE overlay,\n`rust-analyzer`, `buck2`, `python3`, `git`, `pre-commit`, `git-cliff`."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "**Web**: `bun`, `nodejs_22`, `lychee`, the Playwright driver. This is what\nlets you work on the docs without installing anything."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "**Infrastructure**: `kubectl`, `kubernetes-helm`, `kustomize`, `kubectx`,\n`kind`, `cilium-cli`, `fluxcd`, `pulumi`, `skopeo`, `dive`, `cosign`,\n`trivy`, `awscli2`, `google-cloud-sdk`, `docker-client`, `tektoncd-cli`,\n`go`."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "**Docs and prose**: `vale`, which is the style linter the docs are checked\nagainst."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "**Repo-specific helpers**: the toolchain generators, the local image\nbuilders, `local-image-test`, and the LRE clang."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-is-in-the-shell",
    "content": "No `protoc` is provided; the generated Rust is checked into\n`forge-proto/genproto/` and nothing invokes `protoc` at build time.\nNo prebuilt `forge` binary is in the shell; build it with Bazel\nor Cargo, or run `nix run .` which builds and runs the flake's default\npackage. And `CONTRIBUTING.md` still advises installing Clang separately;\nthe shell does now ship `lre.clang`, so try without first, and fall back to\n`nix profile install nixpkgs#clang` if a C++ toolchain error says\notherwise."
  }, {
    "heading": "running-things-inside-the-shell",
    "content": "Everything from the Bazel and\nCargo workflows works unchanged. The shell guarantees\nthe versions, and supplies the `bazelrc` fragments those workflows assume."
  }, {
    "heading": "running-things-inside-the-shell",
    "content": "The two commands worth knowing that only exist here:"
  }, {
    "heading": "lre-and-worker-images",
    "content": "The flake builds the LRE worker images rather than exposing an LRE shell.\nThe ones you are likely to want:"
  }, {
    "heading": "lre-and-worker-images",
    "content": "`lre.bazelrc`, generated on entering the shell, is what points a local Bazel\nbuild at the matching toolchains. See LRE for what the\nscheme is doing and why the `/nix/store` paths have to match on both sides."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-nix-isnt-worth-it",
    "content": "For a typo fix, a doc tweak or a single unit test, the\nCargo workflow is faster to get into and nothing will\ngo wrong. Reach for Nix when you are touching the build graph, working on\nLRE, chasing a difference between your machine and CI, or working on the\ndocs, since `vale` and `bun` both come from here."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "A package manager that builds every package in isolation and addresses\nthe result by a hash of all its inputs, which is why two machines\nrunning `nix develop` on this repo get bit-identical toolchains."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Not for building or testing; Cargo and Bazel both stand on their own.\nIt is required for LRE work, and it is the only way to get the generated\n`bazelrc` fragments, so a build outside it is configured differently from\nCI even when it is green."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Almost certainly the `try-import`ed fragments. `lre.bazelrc`,\n`forge.bazelrc`, `nixos.bazelrc` and `darwin.bazelrc` are generated\nby the shell hook and silently skipped when absent. Enter the shell, or\naccept that you are building without the LRE toolchains and without the\nread-only cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Yes, whenever the `.nix` files change, which a branch switch can do\nwithout you noticing. This is the argument for `direnv`: it re-enters\nautomatically on `cd` and on file changes."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "With a working shell, the next question is where your change goes. Twelve\ncrates, a strict layering, and six places the first guess is wrong."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "install-nix",
    "content": "Install Nix"
  }, {
    "id": "enter-the-dev-shell",
