import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Migrate from another REAPI cache",
  "description": "Put Forge in front of your existing cache as a read-only diode, let real builds warm it, and cut over when the hit rate says you can."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone running Buildbarn, Buildfarm, bazel-remote or a\nhosted REAPI cache who wants to move to Forge without a flag day. &#x2A;*What\nyou'll have at the end:** Forge serving your builds, backed by the old\ncache, with a cutover you choose rather than one you're forced into. &#x2A;*Time:**\nan hour to set up; days to weeks of running both, at your pace."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A working Forge CAS. See Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The instinct is to copy the data across. Don't. A remote cache is not a\ndatabase: every entry in it is derived, reproducible, and has a key that is a\nhash of its own contents. Copying terabytes to preserve something your build can\nregenerate is work you can skip."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The move that works instead is to put Forge in front of the old cache and\nlet it read through. Builds hit Forge; anything it doesn't have, it fetches\nfrom the old cache and keeps. Your hit rate never drops, the new cache warms\nitself on real traffic, and the old one is never written to again, so if you\nneed to go back, it is exactly as you left it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-diode",
    "content": "A `fast_slow` store whose `slow` half points at the old cache, marked read-only:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-diode",
    "content": "`slow_direction: \"read_only\"` is the whole trick, and it is a documented use of\nthe field rather than a clever misuse: the source calls this shape a diode.\nReads fall through to the old cache when Forge misses. Writes stop at\nForge. The old cache is a source and never a destination."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-diode",
    "content": "The `tls_config` block is required on any `grpcs://` address; the\n`use_native_roots` form is right for a hosted cache with a public certificate,\nand `ca_file` is the alternative for a private CA\n(TLS and authentication has both)."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-diode",
    "content": "Do the same for the action cache with a second `grpc` store at\n`store_type: \"ac\"`. Action results are the entries that actually decide your hit\nrate; the CAS blobs they reference come along behind them."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-diode",
    "content": "Leave `rpc_timeout_s` at `0`. It disables the per-RPC deadline, which matters\nbecause pulling a multi-gigabyte blob through the diode on a cold cache can take\nlonger than any timeout you would have guessed. Dead connections are still\ncaught by the HTTP/2 and TCP keepalives."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Digests must match.** If your old cache holds SHA-256 entries and your\nbuilds now ask for BLAKE3, nothing is found, not because of a bug but because\nthe key of a blob is its hash, and you are asking a different question. The\ndigest function is chosen by the client (Bazel's `--digest_function`, SHA-256\nunless you changed it); Forge's `global.default_digest_hash_function`,\ndescribed in Stores, only fills in when a client does\nnot say. Keep the client, Forge and the old cache on the same function.\nThis is the one incompatibility that cannot be worked around."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Instance names may need rewriting.** `instance_name` on the `grpc` store\nreplaces the instance name on every proxied call, so an old cache that expects\n`main` while your clients send an empty instance is a one-field fix rather than\na client migration."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Older backends want older resource names.** Modern REAPI resource names look\nlike `{instance}/blobs/{digest_function}/{hash}/{size}`. Buildbarn before v0.3\nand other older servers expect the original `{instance}/blobs/{hash}/{size}`, and\nsending them the modern form produces `InvalidArgument: Unsupported digest\nfunction`. Set `use_legacy_resource_names: true` when you see that."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Credentials come with you.** A hosted cache that wants a token takes it via\n`headers`, and one that wants each developer's own token takes `forward_headers`\nto pass the client's credential upstream:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "Header values are sent verbatim: they are not `${VAR}`-expanded like most\nstring fields, so the token lives in the config file and the file must be\nprotected accordingly."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Remote execution does not transfer.** Workers, platform properties and queue\nstate are per-system. A read-through migration moves your cache; the execution\nside is a separate build-out, and\nRemote execution is where it happens."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "**Anything vendor-specific doesn't transfer either.** If you depend on a\nfeature of your current system outside the REAPI surface (a custom HTTP\ninterface, a proprietary authorization model, a bespoke admin API), that\ndependency is what you should be examining before the cache bytes."
  }, {
    "heading": "run-both-while-you-cut-over",
    "content": "Nothing forces a single switch date. Give the two caches separate config groups\nin `.bazelrc`, so a build can pick:"
  }, {
    "heading": "run-both-while-you-cut-over",
    "content": "Move CI first, because it is the highest-volume traffic and warms the new cache\nfastest, and because its failures are visible immediately rather than reported\nby a person mid-afternoon. Developers follow once CI has been steady for a week."
  }, {
    "heading": "run-both-while-you-cut-over",
    "content": "Once Forge's own hit rate matches what the old cache was giving you, the\n`slow` tier is doing nothing but adding a network hop on misses. Remove it,\nand the `fast_slow` wrapper with it, leaving the filesystem store on its own.\nDeciding to remove it is the actual end of the migration."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Confirm the digest function** your builds use, and set Forge's\n`default_digest_hash_function` to match. Everything downstream depends on this\nbeing right."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Stand up Forge normally**, with its own filesystem or object store, and\ncheck that a build against it works before the old cache is involved at all."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Add the diode**: `grpc` stores for CAS and AC pointing at the old cache,\nwrapped as the `slow` half of `fast_slow` with `slow_direction: \"read_only\"`."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Point CI at Forge** and watch the hit rate. It should start near what the\nold cache gave, because misses are being served through the diode, and it should\nstay there."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Move developers**, then wait. Give it long enough that a full build's worth of\nactions has passed through, typically a week or two of normal work."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "**Remove the diode** once misses are no longer being answered by the old cache,\nthen decommission it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A build that got hits from the old cache gets hits from Forge on the\nfirst run against it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Forge's `content_path` grows during that run, because it is keeping what it\nreads."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "A second identical build is served entirely by Forge, with no traffic to\nthe old cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "The old cache's storage stops growing, confirming nothing is writing to it."
  }, {
    "heading": "steps",
    "content": "Turning the old cache off entirely, at the end, changes nothing."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Check the digest function first; a mismatch produces exactly this and no error.\nThen check `instance_name`, since an old cache scoped to a named instance\nreturns nothing for a differently-named one. Then confirm the address is\nreachable from the Forge process rather than from your laptop."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The upstream is an older server that does not understand modern resource names.\nSet `use_legacy_resource_names: true` on the `grpc` store."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "`slow_direction` is not set to `read_only`, or it is set on the wrong store. It\nbelongs on the `fast_slow` wrapper next to `slow`, not inside the `grpc` spec.\nIts default is `both`, which is a normal read-write tier."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "A per-RPC timeout. Set `rpc_timeout_s: 0` on the `grpc` store (the default),\nor raise it well past the worst-case transfer time. Multi-gigabyte blobs\npulled cross-region are slower than any guess."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Every miss now costs a round trip to the old cache on top of the local lookup.\nThat is the diode working as designed and it is temporary. If it is painful,\nraise the local store's `max_bytes` so fewer reads fall through, and check\nwhether the old cache is in another region."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "Rarely, and only when the old cache holds artifacts that are expensive or\nimpossible to reproduce: a toolchain nobody can rebuild, or outputs from a\nsource revision that no longer exists. That is a one-off copy of specific blobs,\nnot a migration strategy. For everything a build can regenerate, the diode is\nboth faster and safer."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "The wrapper vocabulary the diode is built from, and what else it can do."
  }, {
    "heading": "when-it-doesnt-work",
    "content": "What to change once Forge is the cache rather than a proxy in front of\none."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "the-diode",
    "content": "The diode"
  }, {
    "id": "what-crosses-the-boundary-and-what-doesnt",
    "content": "What crosses the boundary and what doesn't"
  }, {
    "id": "run-both-while-you-cut-over",
    "content": "Run both while you cut over"
  }, {
    "id": "steps",
    "content": "Steps"
  }, {
