    "content": "Deliberately discarding writes"
  }, {
    "heading": "pick-a-backend",
    "content": "`memory`, `grpc` and `noop` have no page of their own because there is nothing\nto decide: `memory` takes an eviction policy, `grpc` takes an upstream address,\nand `noop` takes nothing at all. All three are covered in\nCompose stores."
  }, {
    "heading": "pick-a-backend",
    "content": "Six providers (`aws`, `r2`, `ontap`, `gcs`, `azure`, `oci`) are declared\nunder `experimental_cloud_object_store`, and Mongo under `experimental_mongo`. The prefix signals that the\nconfiguration key may be renamed in a future release. It is not a claim\nthat the store is unfinished. Pin your Forge version and read the\nrelease notes before upgrading, the same as for any config surface."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape-almost-everyone-lands-on",
    "content": "Very few production deployments name a cloud bucket as their CAS store\ndirectly. The read pattern of a build cache (many small objects, requested\nrepeatedly, in bursts) is exactly what object storage prices and latency are\nworst at. The shape that works is a local fast tier in front of a durable slow\ntier:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape-almost-everyone-lands-on",
    "content": "Reads hit the filesystem first and only fall through to the bucket on a miss.\nWrites go to both. Every shipped cloud example in\n`forge-config/examples/` is built this way (some with a `memory` fast\ntier instead of `filesystem`), and every backend page below shows its own\nversion of it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-shape-almost-everyone-lands-on",
    "content": "Compose stores covers the rest of the\ncomposition vocabulary (deduplication, size partitioning, integrity\nverification, sharding) and the order the layers have to go in."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Two pages in this group are not backends at all. They change how many bytes\ntravel between the client and Forge, whichever backend is underneath:"
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Remote cache compression: zstd on the wire,\nfor compressible artifacts on a real network."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Content-defined chunking: re-transfer\nonly the changed parts of large, incrementally-changing blobs."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "They compose with everything above, and with each other."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Your slow tier survives the loss of any single machine, or you've decided\nout loud that losing the cache is acceptable."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Your fast tier is on the same host as the process reading it, and has an\n`eviction_policy` with a `max_bytes` you can afford."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "If you run workers, the fast half of their `cas_fast_slow_store` is a\n`filesystem` store, because nothing else supports the hard-linking workers need."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "The one backend nearly every deployment uses, whether or not it's the\ndurable one."
  }, {
    "heading": "shrinking-what-you-move",
    "content": "Wrapping, layering and splitting stores, and the order the layers have to\ngo in."
  }],
