import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from "react/jsx-runtime";
export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "From cache to execution",
  "description": "You have a cache serving hits. This is what changes when the misses stop running on your laptop: in the deployment, in the build invocation, and in what breaks first."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who finished Getting started and is deciding whether to\ntake the next step. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** an accurate picture of\nwhat remote execution adds, what it costs, and which wall you'll hit first.\n&#x2A;*Time:** ten minutes of reading, no commands."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache answers exactly one question: &#x2A;has this exact action already been\nrun somewhere?* When the answer is yes, you get the result back in\nmilliseconds. When the answer is no (and on a first build, or after a\ntoolchain bump, the answer is no for almost everything) something still has\nto run the action. With caching alone, that something is the machine you're\nsitting at."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Remote execution changes the answer to that second question. The misses go\nto a fleet."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-execution-buys-you",
    "content": "Two things, and it's worth being clear that they're separate."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-execution-buys-you",
    "content": "**The misses stop being your problem.** A cache makes repeated work free; it\ndoes nothing for work nobody has done yet. A clean build, a dependency bump,\na merge that invalidates half the graph: these are all cache misses by\ndefinition, and they are exactly the builds that hurt. Remote execution is\nwhat turns those into a fan-out across as many cores as you're willing to\npay for, instead of a fan-out across the eight your laptop has."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-execution-buys-you",
    "content": "**Actions get to run on hardware that fits them.** Once a scheduler is\nrouting work, actions stop being homogeneous. A link step that needs 64 GB\nof RAM, a test that needs a GPU, a cross-compile that needs a specific libc:\neach can be routed to a worker that has it, without every developer\nneeding that machine under their desk. This is the part that has no local\nequivalent at all, and for some teams it's the whole reason to adopt\nForge."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-execution-buys-you",
    "content": "A third thing people expect and don't get: remote execution is not\nautomatically *faster* per action. A single action that takes 200 ms locally\nwill take 200 ms plus a network round-trip plus input materialization\nremotely. The win is parallelism and placement, not per-action latency,\nwhich is why Classic patterns\ncovers keeping small actions local."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "Three components, and one of them is a security boundary."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "**A scheduler.** It owns the queue and the matching engine. It holds no\nblobs of its own; it reads the CAS through a `grpc` store pointed at the\nCAS process, and its whole job is deciding which worker gets which action."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "**At least one worker.** It connects *outward* to the scheduler, announces\nwhat it can do, and then pulls actions. Workers do not listen for client\ntraffic; a worker config has an empty `servers` array."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "**The `execution` service on a server.** This is the client-facing half:\nthe `Execute` and `WaitExecution` RPCs your build tool calls. It sits\nalongside the `cas` and `ac` services you already have, and it needs a\n`scheduler` reference."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "And one port that is genuinely new in a way that matters: the **worker API**\nlistener, `50061` in every example config in the repo that has one. Workers register\nthere. It is a backend port with a completely different permission set from\nthe client port, and it must not be reachable from where your clients are.\nProduction configuration\nis blunt about why."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "Anything that can reach the worker API can register as a worker and\nreceive actions to execute, which means arbitrary commands, from your\nbuild, running on its machine, with your CAS credentials. Bind it to a\nprivate network. The repo's own example config says so in a comment."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-build-invocation",
    "content": "Less than you'd think. Two flags and a declaration."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-build-invocation",
    "content": "`--remote_executor` is the new one. `--remote_cache` you already had. If\nyou leave it out, Bazel reuses the `--remote_executor` endpoint as the\ncache, which is fine when one listener serves both; keep it explicit when\nthe cache and the executor are different endpoints, which is the usual\nproduction shape. Whichever store the `execution` service reads must be\nthe store the client uploads to, or `Execute` fails with\n`FAILED_PRECONDITION: Action ... is missing from CAS`."
  }, {
    "heading": "whats-new-in-the-build-invocation",
    "content": "`--remote_default_exec_properties` is the declaration. It's how your build\ntells the scheduler what kind of machine the action needs. Getting this\nwrong is the single most common way a first remote build fails, which is why\nPlatform properties is its own\npage rather than a paragraph here."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-stays-exactly-the-same",
    "content": "Your CAS and your action cache. The same stores, the same digests, the same\nhits you were already getting."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-stays-exactly-the-same",
    "content": "This is not a coincidence; it's why the sections are in this order. Remote\nexecution is a *consumer* of the cache you built in Getting started, not a\nreplacement for it. The worker fetches an action's inputs from your CAS by\ndigest and uploads its outputs back to the same place, so every result a\nworker produces is immediately a cache hit for the next person who asks for\nit. If your cache was serving hits before you added a scheduler, it is\nserving the same hits afterwards, plus new ones."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-stays-exactly-the-same",
    "content": "The practical consequence: &#x2A;*if something breaks after you turn on remote\nexecution, the cache is almost never the thing that broke.** You can drop\n`--remote_executor`, confirm your build still works cache-only, and know the\nproblem is in the two components you just added."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-expect-the-first-time",
    "content": "The first remote action rarely works on the first attempt, and it is nearly\nalways one of two walls. Both are ordinary; both have a page."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-expect-the-first-time",
    "content": "**Wall one: platform properties.** The scheduler will only hand an action to\na worker whose advertised properties satisfy what the action asked for. When\nthey don't line up, nothing errors: the action sits in the queue\nindefinitely, which reads like a hang rather than a misconfiguration. Your\nbuild appears to stall at \"0s remote\" and stays there.\nPlatform properties covers the\nmatching rules and how to read the scheduler's own diagnostic logging."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-expect-the-first-time",
    "content": "**Wall two: toolchain hermeticity.** An action that succeeds on your machine\nbecause your machine happens to have the right compiler will fail on a\nworker that doesn't. Remote execution converts every unstated dependency on\nyour local environment into a hard failure. This is uncomfortable the first\ntime and extremely valuable afterwards; it's the same property that makes\nbuilds reproducible.\nToolchains and hermeticity\nis the fix."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-to-expect-the-first-time",
    "content": "A reliable way to keep these two walls from arriving together: run\nthe whole cluster on your own machine first, with whatever toolchain is\nalready on your `PATH`. That isolates the protocol wiring from the toolchain\nquestion, and it's what Your first remote action\ndoes next."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. One `forge` process can host the CAS, the action cache, a\nscheduler, and a worker at once. That's exactly what"
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "does, and it's the right shape for learning. The split into separate\nprocesses is an operational decision you make when you want to scale or\nrestart the pieces independently, not a protocol requirement. See\nProduction configuration for the shape a\nreal cluster uses."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes, and it's additive: the scheduler and workers read and write the CAS\nyou already have. The change on the server side is a `schedulers` entry,\nan `execution` service (plus a `capabilities` service with\n`remote_execution` set, so clients learn execution is available) on a\nclient-facing listener, and a `worker_api`\nlistener on a private port. Nothing about the existing `cas`, `ac`, or\n`bytestream` services changes, so clients that only pass\n`--remote_cache` keep working exactly as before."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The action: input file digests, the command line, the environment, and\nthe platform properties. Not the input files themselves: the worker\nfetches those from the CAS by hash, and skips anything it already has.\nOutputs go back the same way. This is why a fat input tree is cheap to\nre-run and a cold worker is expensive to start."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "One. A single worker proves the whole path end to end, and the number of\nactions it will run concurrently is governed by its `cpu_count` property\n(and how much of it each action requests) rather than by worker count. Add workers when the queue has depth, not\nbefore. Tuning has the signal to watch."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A scheduler and one worker on your own machine, and a build you can watch\nexecute on them."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "If you already have workers and your actions are queueing forever, skip\nahead: this is almost certainly why."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "what-execution-buys-you",
    "content": "What execution buys you"
  }, {
    "id": "whats-new-in-the-deployment",
    "content": "What's new in the deployment"
  }, {
    "id": "whats-new-in-the-build-invocation",
    "content": "What's new in the build invocation"
  }, {
    "id": "what-stays-exactly-the-same",
    "content": "What stays exactly the same"
  }, {
    "id": "what-to-expect-the-first-time",
    "content": "What to expect the first time"
  }, {
    "id": "faq",
    "content": "FAQ"
  }]
};
