  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Nothing beyond a build system that speaks the Remote Execution API."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "This guide takes you from zero to your first cached build. Pick the\ninstallation method that matches your platform, then wire your build\nsystem to the running cluster."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "You need a build tool that speaks the Remote Execution API: Bazel,\nBuck2, Siso, Pants, Goma, or CMake via recc. For the Docker path,\nDocker. For the Nix path, a recent Nix install with flakes enabled."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "The fastest way is the prebuilt container image. It runs anywhere Docker\nruns; the configuration it needs is a single JSON5 file from the\nrepository."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "The image is multi-arch: x86\\_64 and ARM64 both run natively (use\nv1.6.0 or later; earlier tags are x86\\_64-only). The server is now\nlistening on `localhost:50051`."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "Slower than the prebuilt image because it builds from source, but works on\nmacOS (Apple Silicon and Intel) and any Linux with Nix. Use it when you\nwant a native binary instead of a container; the Docker path above also\nruns natively on Apple Silicon."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "**Prerequisites**: Make sure your Nix installation has experimental features enabled (add `experimental-features = nix-command flakes` to your `~/.config/nix/nix.conf`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "**Disk Space**: The installation and build process requires downloading and compiling toolchains and compilers. Ensure you have at least **15 to 20 GB of free space** on your drive."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "**Portability**: Executables built for macOS link dynamically against libraries from the\nNix store. They will not run on systems without those libraries\navailable. Stick to the Docker path if you need to copy the binary to\nanother machine."
  }, {
    "heading": "install-forge",
    "content": "The image is a Linux image published for x86\\_64 and ARM64, so Docker\nDesktop runs it on either kind of Windows machine. WSL2 with the Linux\ninstructions above works too."
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-its-running",
    "content": "In another terminal:"
  }, {
    "heading": "verify-its-running",
    "content": "You should see the connection open and an HTTP response come back. The\nstatus will be an error, since `/` is not a gRPC route; the exact body\ndoesn't matter, the connection succeeding does."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Add to your `.bazelrc`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Or pass them per invocation:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Run any build target. Subsequent rebuilds of unchanged targets should\nreport a `remote cache hit` count in the final `processes:` summary line.\nLeave `--remote_executor` off for now: the container image holds only the\nForge binary, no shell or compilers, so actions sent to its worker\ncannot run. Remote execution sets up workers with a\ntoolchain."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "In your project root, add `.buckconfig`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Then build as usual:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Buck2 also needs an execution platform with `remote_enabled = True`; the\nBuck2 page shows the one the\nchecked-in integration test uses."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Set the endpoint variables Siso reads:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "The Chromium build is the canonical Siso consumer; see\nChromium for a full example."
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "In `pants.toml`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "point-your-build-system-at-it",
    "content": "Then run any goal as usual; Pants will route through the cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-cache-hits",
    "content": "Re-run your build a second time without changing any source files. Every\naction should land in the cache and report a hit. With Bazel:"
  }, {
    "heading": "confirm-cache-hits",
    "content": "If you see hits, you're done. If not, jump to the troubleshooting\nsection below."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "The Forge server isn't listening on the port your client is\npointing at. Confirm with `lsof -i :50051` (macOS / Linux) or\n`netstat -ano | findstr 50051` (Windows). Check the Docker container is\nstill running with `docker ps`."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Most often this means two clients are using different `instance_name`\nvalues or different toolchain configurations. Compare the\n`--remote_instance_name` flag (Bazel) or the equivalent in your build\nsystem across the two invocations. Toolchain mismatches surface as\naction hashes that never collide."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "SELinux-enforcing distributions (Fedora, RHEL) require an explicit\n`:Z` label on the mounted volume:\n`-v $(pwd)/basic_cas.json5:/config:Z`."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Every action's inputs (source digests, the compiler binary, the\nexact command line, declared headers) are hashed together, and the\naction's outputs are stored under that hash. Anyone whose action\nhashes identically gets the stored outputs back in milliseconds\ninstead of redoing the work."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The hash covers every declared input, so any difference (a header,\na compiler patch version, a leaked environment variable) produces a\nnew hash. That's by design: a hit is a guarantee the result is\nidentical. If you see misses where you expect hits, start with the\ntroubleshooting section above and\ncorrectness and hermeticity."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. When `--remote_cache` is empty Bazel reuses the `--remote_executor`\nendpoint for the cache, so one flag is enough when the same Forge\nserves both. Set `--remote_cache` explicitly only when the cache lives\nat a different address from the scheduler."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A server that starts and a build that succeeds are not evidence that\nanything was cached. This is the line to read, and what it means when it\nsays zero."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "What is in that JSON5 file, and how to grow it from a single node to a\nfleet."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "What each piece of the cluster is responsible for, and why the split is\ndrawn where it is."
  }],
