export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Containers and images",
  "description": "What the container-image property actually does (and doesn't), plus how to build worker images and keep what your build requests in sync with what your fleet advertises."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone whose actions need a specific environment, and\nanyone running more than one kind of worker image. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the\nend:** worker images that carry your toolchain, and a routing scheme that\nsends each action to the right one. &#x2A;*Time:** twenty minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A cache serving hits to your build. If you don't have one yet, start with Getting started."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-container-image-is-not",
    "content": "Start here, because the name is misleading and the misunderstanding is\nexpensive."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-container-image-is-not",
    "content": "**Forge does not pull an image and launch a container for each action.**\nNo code sits behind the `container-image` key. Search the source for it\nand you will find it in example configs, in the LRE platform definitions under\n`local-remote-execution/`, and in CI wiring; nothing in the scheduler or worker\nsource reads it. It is an ordinary string platform property with an evocative\nname."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-container-image-is-not",
    "content": "What it actually expresses is: *this worker process is running inside this\nimage*. The worker asserts a value, the build requests a value, and the\nscheduler matches them like any other `exact` or `priority` property. The\nenvironment is real; it's just established by how you *started the worker*,\nnot by anything the scheduler does per action."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-container-image-is-not",
    "content": "The repo's own CI action puts it plainly: the value you request \"must\nbyte-for-byte match the `container-image` the claim's worker pool\nadvertises, or actions queue forever.\" No normalisation, no tag\nresolution, no digest comparison. `debian:bookworm-slim` and\n`docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim` are different strings and\ntherefore different pools."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-container-image-is-not",
    "content": "That failure is the silent-queue failure from\nPlatform properties, and it is by\nfar the most common way a container-image scheme goes wrong."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-actual-model",
    "content": "One image per worker *pool*. Every action a given worker runs shares that\nworker's environment. Heterogeneity comes from running several pools and\nrouting between them, not from varying the image per action."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-actual-model",
    "content": "This has a consequence worth planning around: &#x2A;*image churn is a fleet\noperation.** Changing a toolchain means building a new image, rolling a pool\nonto it, and changing what builds request, in an order that doesn't strand\nactions in the queue. There's a safe sequence below."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-a-worker-image",
    "content": "A Forge worker image is any image that contains your toolchain and can\nrun the `forge` binary with a worker config. The repo's Nix helper,\n`createWorker`, turns a toolchain image into a `forge-worker-*` image:"
  }, {
    "heading": "build-a-worker-image",
    "content": "What it adds on top of the base image is small: a `forge` user (uid\n1000\\) and home directory, a world-writable `/tmp`, `/usr/bin/env`, and\n`coreutils`, `bash` and `gnused` on `/bin`. It keeps the base image's tag so\nthe worker image and the toolchain it wraps stay visibly paired. It does\n*not* bake the `forge` binary in, whatever its header comment says: the\nKubernetes examples inject the binary at pod start with the\n`forge-worker-init` init container, which copies it into a shared\nvolume, and the worker container then runs `/shared/forge /worker.json5`.\nThat keeps the toolchain image and the Forge version independently\nupgradable."
  }, {
    "heading": "build-a-worker-image",
    "content": "Push one to a registry the way the repo's own tooling does:"
  }, {
    "heading": "build-a-worker-image",
    "content": "You do not have to use Nix for this. Any image that contains your toolchain\nand runs `forge` with a config is a valid worker image: a Dockerfile\nthat starts `FROM your-toolchain-base`, copies in the binary, and sets the\nentrypoint works identically. Nix is how this repo gets *reproducible* images;\nit isn't a requirement of the worker protocol."
  }, {
    "heading": "declare-the-key-on-the-scheduler",
    "content": "Use `exact` once you have more than one image. The examples ship `priority`,\nwhich matches any value: fine for a single-pool tutorial, useless as a\nrouting mechanism."
  }, {
    "heading": "advertise-it-on-each-worker",
    "content": "Pin a tag you can change deliberately. `:latest` makes the advertised string\nstable while the actual contents drift, which defeats the entire point: the\nscheduler will keep matching, and your builds will silently change behaviour."
  }, {
    "heading": "advertise-it-on-each-worker",
    "content": "Better still, template it from the deployment so the value can't disagree\nwith the image that's actually running:"
  }, {
    "heading": "request-it-from-the-build",
    "content": "Globally, if your whole build wants one environment:"
  }, {
    "heading": "request-it-from-the-build",
    "content": "Or per target, when only some actions need it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "request-it-from-the-build",
    "content": "The build reports actions as `remote` rather than sitting at `0s remote`."
  }, {
    "heading": "request-it-from-the-build",
    "content": "Stopping the pool that advertises the requested image makes builds queue,\nand starting it drains them. That's the proof the routing is real rather\nthan everything falling through to one pool."
  }, {
    "heading": "request-it-from-the-build",
    "content": "With `exact`, requesting a *wrong* image string queues indefinitely. If it\nsucceeds instead, the key is still declared `priority` somewhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-an-image-without-stranding-actions",
    "content": "Order matters, because the two sides are matched on a literal string."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-an-image-without-stranding-actions",
    "content": "**Bring up the new pool first**, advertising the new image string, while\nthe old pool keeps running and advertising the old one. Both are now\nsatisfiable."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-an-image-without-stranding-actions",
    "content": "**Switch what builds request.** New builds land on the new pool;\nin-flight ones finish on the old."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-an-image-without-stranding-actions",
    "content": "**Drain and remove the old pool** once nothing requests it."
  }, {
    "heading": "rolling-an-image-without-stranding-actions",
    "content": "Doing this in the other order (changing the request before the pool exists,\nor removing the pool before builds stop asking for it) produces a queue that\nnever clears and no error anywhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Symptom"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Cause"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Fix"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Everything queues after an image bump"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Requested string doesn't match any advertised string"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Compare them character by character; registry prefixes and tags both count"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "`Property missing on worker property container-image`"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "A pool doesn't advertise the key at all"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Add it to that pool's `platform_properties`, even with an empty value"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Routing \"works\" but actions land on the wrong pool"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Key is declared `priority`, which matches any value"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Change the scheduler's declaration to `exact`"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Builds change behaviour with no config change"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Advertised tag is mutable (`:latest`)"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Pin an immutable tag or digest"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Worker never joins the pool"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Key not declared in `supported_platform_properties`"
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "Look for `Bad Property during connect_worker()` in the worker log"
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Not meaningfully. A worker process runs in one environment; the property is\nan assertion about that environment. You can advertise several values for a\n`priority` key, but that's a routing label, not a second toolchain. Run one\npool per image."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "The worker's `entrypoint` option lets you interpose a wrapper: the configured\nprogram is prepended to every action's command line as its first argument\n(the config comment says \"command + arguments\", but the code prepends the\nstring as a single program path, so point it at one executable and let that\nexecutable take the action's command as its arguments), which makes a script\nthat launches a container and forwards the command possible. If that wrapper\nenforces its own timeouts, set\n`timeout_handled_externally` so Forge doesn't also time the action out.\nThis is a real deployment shape, but it is something you build, not something\n`container-image` gives you."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. Workers run fine as plain processes on bare metal or a VM; the property\nis only a matching string, so a fleet that doesn't use containers\ndoesn't declare the key.\nDeploy on bare metal covers that shape."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "They're the two answers to the same question. Images make the *fleet*\nreproducible: every action in a pool sees the same environment.\nLRE makes the *action*\nreproducible by pinning the toolchain into the action itself, which\nadditionally lets a developer's laptop share cache entries with the fleet.\nThey compose: the `lre-cc` and `lre-java` workers are container images whose\ncontents are Nix-pinned."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A digest is stricter and safer; an immutable dated tag is easier to read in\nbuild logs and error messages. Either is fine. What matters is that the string\nchanges when the contents change, so that a stale pool stops matching instead\nof silently serving the old environment."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Keeping a warm process between actions, for toolchains where startup cost\ndominates."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Running several worker pools, each on its own image, as a real fleet."
  }],
