export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Why Forge",
  "description": "What Forge is, what a remote cache and remote execution actually do, and a 30-second check for whether your build is one that benefits."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone deciding whether to put a remote cache and\nexecution layer in front of their build, including people meeting the idea for\nthe first time. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a working mental model of what\nForge does, and a clear yes or no for your own situation. &#x2A;*Time:** about\nten minutes."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-forge-is",
    "content": "Forge is a **remote build cache** and a **remote execution** service in one\nprogram. Your build tool asks it \"have you already built this?\" and, when the\nanswer is no, \"can you build it for me over there?\" Nothing about your source\ncode changes. You point your build tool at a URL, and work that used to happen\non your machine stops happening on your machine."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-forge-is",
    "content": "It's written in Rust, ships as a single binary that plays every role, and\nspeaks the Remote Execution API,\nan open protocol, not a Forge invention, which is why the build tool you\nalready use can talk to it without a plugin."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem-it-exists-to-solve",
    "content": "Every build system computes the same thing twice. Once on your laptop, once in\nCI. Once for you, once for the colleague who pulled the same commit. Once\nbefore lunch, once after you reverted the change that broke it. The work is\ndeterministic, the inputs are identical, and the machine does it again anyway."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem-it-exists-to-solve",
    "content": "That waste has two different shapes, and they need two different fixes."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem-it-exists-to-solve",
    "content": "Some work is *redundant*: somebody, somewhere, already produced exactly this\noutput from exactly these inputs. The fix is a cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem-it-exists-to-solve",
    "content": "Some work is *genuinely new* but badly placed: it has to run, but it doesn't\nhave to run on the laptop you're typing on, one action at a time. The fix is to\nrun it somewhere else, in parallel, on hardware that suits it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-problem-it-exists-to-solve",
    "content": "Forge is both fixes."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-remote-cache-is",
    "content": "A build is a graph of actions. Each action has inputs (source files, compiler\nflags, the compiler itself) and produces outputs. A **remote cache** stores\nthose outputs on a server keyed by a hash of all of the inputs."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-remote-cache-is",
    "content": "Before running an action, your build tool hashes the inputs and asks the server\nwhether it has a result for that hash. A hit means the output is downloaded\ninstead of computed. A miss means the action runs and the result is uploaded so\nthat nobody else has to run it."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-remote-cache-is",
    "content": "The key property is that the key *is* the content. This is called\n**content-addressed storage**, and it has a consequence worth pausing on:\nentries can never be stale. A cache keyed on file paths and timestamps needs\ninvalidation logic, and invalidation logic is where build caches go wrong. A\ncache keyed on the hash of the inputs cannot return the wrong answer for a\nquestion, because a different question has a different key."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-remote-cache-is",
    "content": "Forge stores these separately. The **CAS** (content-addressable storage)\nholds the bytes of files. The **action cache** maps \"this action, with these\ninputs\" to \"these output files.\" Most sentences about \"the cache\" mean both\nat once."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-a-remote-cache-is",
    "content": "The direct consequence: the more people and machines share one cache, the more\noften any given action has already been run by somebody. A cache used by one\nlaptop is a modest optimization. A cache shared by a team and its CI is a\ndifferent kind of thing."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-remote-execution-is",
    "content": "Caching only helps with work that has already been done once. Remote execution\nis what happens on the miss."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-remote-execution-is",
    "content": "Instead of running the action locally, your build tool sends the action's\ndescription and its inputs to a **scheduler**, which hands it to a **worker**,\na machine in a pool that does the work and returns the outputs. Because the\npool has many machines, hundreds of actions can be in flight at once, which is\nthe part your laptop cannot do no matter how new it is."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-remote-execution-is",
    "content": "Four roles, CAS, action cache, scheduler and worker, and in Forge all four\nare the same binary started with different configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-remote-execution-is",
    "content": "Remote execution asks something of you that caching does not: the action has to\nbe **hermetic**. Everything it needs must be declared as an input, because a\nworker in another datacenter does not have your `/usr/local`, your environment\nvariables, or your home directory. Builds that quietly depend on the machine\nthey run on work locally and fail remotely, and that is the honest cost of the\nfeature. Toolchains and hermeticity\nis the page about earning it."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-forge-specifically",
    "content": "The protocol is open and there are other implementations. What's particular\nhere:"
  }, {
    "heading": "why-forge-specifically",
    "content": "**It's one binary.** CAS, action cache, scheduler and worker are the same\nexecutable with different config. No separate control plane needs operating,\nand the thing you run locally is the thing you run in production."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-forge-specifically",
    "content": "**It's Rust, with no garbage collector.** Build caches are latency-sensitive at\nthe tail: a lookup that usually takes a millisecond and occasionally takes two\nhundred is felt by every action in the graph. Predictable memory behaviour is\nthe reason for the language choice."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-forge-specifically",
    "content": "**Storage is composable rather than fixed.** A store is declared, named, and\nwrapped by other stores: a fast local tier in front of a durable object store, a\ncompression layer, a shard across several backends. You describe the shape you\nwant instead of choosing from a menu.\nStorage backends is that vocabulary."
  }, {
    "heading": "why-forge-specifically",
    "content": "**You can read all of it.** The source is public, and the code that runs your\nbuilds is code you can audit. On licensing, use the precise words rather than\nthe loose ones: most of the repository is under the Functional Source License\n(`FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0`), which is source-available and converts to Apache 2.0\nover time, and two modules, metrics and persistent workers, are under the\nBusiness Source License and require an enterprise agreement for production\nuse."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "You will get something out of Forge if any of these are true:"
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "Your build takes long enough that people context-switch while waiting."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "CI rebuilds artifacts that a developer already built minutes earlier."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "Your team shares a codebase, so you share cache hits by construction."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "Some of your actions want hardware your laptop is not: many cores, much\nmemory, a GPU, a specific OS."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "You run coding agents against the repo, and their edit-build-test loop is\nbounded by build time rather than by model latency."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "You will get less out of it if your build is already seconds long, if every\naction is genuinely unique (some ML training and fuzzing workloads are), or if\nyour build tool doesn't speak the Remote Execution API and you aren't willing to\nchange that."
  }, {
    "heading": "is-this-for-you",
    "content": "Build tools with a real setup path in these docs are Bazel, Buck2, Siso, Pants,\nBuildStream and CMake via recc.\nConnect your build has the specifics,\nincluding which of them are documented for caching only."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-cases-worth-their-own-page",
    "content": "Most people arrive with one of three problems. Each of these is the long-form\nversion of a bullet above; read the one that's yours and skip the others."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-cases-worth-their-own-page",
    "content": "**AI coding agents**: an agent's loop is\nedit, build, test, repeat, and it runs that loop far more often than a person\ndoes. Build time is the loop's wall clock and a large share of its token cost."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-cases-worth-their-own-page",
    "content": "**Autonomous operation**: the case for a\nbuild farm that grows and shrinks with demand instead of being sized for the\nworst Tuesday of the quarter, and an honest account of how much of that\nForge hands you versus how much you assemble."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-three-cases-worth-their-own-page",
    "content": "**Silicon and large builds**: EDA\nflows, big C++ and LLVM trees, and ML graphs: thousands of actions, hardware\nthat differs from action to action, and a long tail that decides the wall\nclock. This is the workload remote execution was invented for."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Same instinct, different scope. `ccache` caches compiler invocations on one\nmachine, and `sccache` extends that to a shared bucket. Forge caches every\naction in the build graph rather than just compiles, and it can also *run* the\nactions that miss. If a shared compile cache is all you need, you may not need\nthis."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No, and you shouldn't start there. Caching is the whole of Getting started and it works\nwithout hermetic toolchains, without a worker pool, and without changing how\nyour actions are written. Remote execution is the next section and it is a bigger\ncommitment. Most of the win, for most teams, is in the cache."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "No. Anything speaking the Remote Execution API can use Forge, and the\ndocs cover Buck2, Siso, Pants, BuildStream and CMake via recc alongside Bazel.\nWhat matters is whether your build tool can describe an action's inputs\nprecisely enough to hash them."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The software is free to run and the bill is infrastructure: a machine for the\ncache, storage for the blobs, and worker machines if you adopt remote\nexecution. The trade to weigh is that storage and machines are cheaper than\nengineer-hours spent waiting, which is a real argument but not an automatic\none: it depends on how much waiting there actually is."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "\"Source-available\" is the accurate phrase. Most of the repository is under the\nFunctional Source License, which lets you read, modify and use the code with a\nrestriction on offering it as a competing service, and converts to Apache 2.0\non a schedule. Two modules, metrics and persistent workers, are\nBusiness Source Licensed and need an enterprise agreement for production use.\nEverything in these docs is runnable without one unless the page says otherwise."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "The fastest way to find out is to run one. Getting started gets a cache serving hits\nto your build, and it's the foundation everything else here stands on."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-questions",
    "content": "Prefer to understand the machine before you run it? The architecture page\ncovers the four roles and how a request moves through them."
  }],
