export let frontmatter = {
  "title": "Chromium",
  "description": "Building Chromium with Forge as the Siso backend, a worked example for the largest public consumer."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "A running Forge instance. If you don't have one, start with the Quickstart."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Chromium's build system shells out to\nSiso,\nwhich speaks the Remote Execution API. Pointing it at a Forge cluster is a\nconfiguration change, with no patches to the Chromium tree."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The same pattern works for upstream Chromium and any Chromium-based\nbrowser."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "A Forge cluster reachable from your build machines. Cache-only\nis fine to start; remote execution drops build times further."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "A Chromium checkout with `depot_tools` on `$PATH`."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "The Chromium source checkout (`fetch chromium`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-siso",
    "content": "Siso reads its server settings from environment variables. Add\nto your build shell:"
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-siso",
    "content": "`SISO_REAPI_INSTANCE` must match an `instance_name` your Forge\nserver exposes. The repository's own Chromium example uses the default\nempty instance name (`SISO_REAPI_INSTANCE=''`) and also sets\n`SISO_REAPI_CAS_ADDRESS` to the same endpoint:\n."
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-siso",
    "content": "For local development against a non-TLS Forge:"
  }, {
    "heading": "generate-chromium-build-files",
    "content": "In your Chromium checkout:"
  }, {
    "heading": "generate-chromium-build-files",
    "content": "`autoninja` reads `args.gn`; with `use_siso=true`, it invokes\n`siso ninja` for the build."
  }, {
    "heading": "build",
    "content": "Watch the build go. On a fresh cluster the first build will be\nmostly misses; subsequent builds hit the cache and run in a fraction\nof the time."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-good-looks-like",
    "content": "**Cache hit rate**: on a warm cache the hit rate for an incremental\nbuild should be high and climb from build to build; watch it in the\nForge cache metrics."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-good-looks-like",
    "content": "**Local fallback rate**: close to zero. Anything higher means workers\nare rejecting actions (platform mismatch, capacity)."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-good-looks-like",
    "content": "**Network errors**: zero. Anything else is a problem."
  }, {
    "heading": "worker-requirements",
    "content": "Chromium's Siso config takes its remote platform properties from\n`build/config/siso/backend_config/backend.star` (copy `template.star`\nand edit it, or point the `reapi_backend_config_path` gclient custom\nvar at your own file). Each platform entry names an `OSFamily` and a\n`container-image`, and every Forge worker that should run Chromium\nactions must advertise the same values, with the property key spelled\n`container-image` (hyphen)."
  }, {
    "heading": "worker-requirements",
    "content": "The repository ships a Chromium worker image built from Chromium's\npublic `siso-chromium/linux` RBE image, and a kustomization that deploys\nit alongside a Forge cluster:"
  }, {
    "heading": "worker-requirements",
    "content": "for the deployment,"
  }, {
    "heading": "worker-requirements",
    "content": "for the worker, which uses the shared worker config in"
  }, {
    "heading": "worker-requirements",
    "content": "(`OSFamily: Linux`, `container-image` from the worker image, `cpu_count`\nfrom `nproc`)."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "**All actions falling back to local.** Likely a platform-property\nmismatch. Compare what Chromium is requesting (in the siso\nlog) against what your workers advertise."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "**Siso is not being selected.** If the output directory was already\ngenerated for Ninja, run `gn clean out/Default`, regenerate with\n`use_siso=true`, and build again."
  }, {
    "heading": "troubleshooting",
    "content": "**Cache writes succeed, reads always miss.** Almost always a\ntoolchain version mismatch between the action that wrote the\ncache and the one trying to read."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. Pointing Siso at Forge is configuration only: GN args, the\nSiso environment variables, and a local `backend.star` for the\nplatform properties. The same applies to Chromium forks."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Yes. A cache-only cluster already cuts incremental build times; add\nremote execution later for the full win on link-heavy full builds."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "A Chromium build is where the difference between one machine's cache and\nthe team's is largest. This is how to make it the team's."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "For the hot toolchain paths, where process startup is a measurable share of\nthe action."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Chromium builds produce a lot of telemetry; it is worth having the\ndashboards before you need them."
  }],
  "headings": [{
