  "title": "Developing with Cargo",
  "description": "Pure Cargo workflow for Forge contributors who'd rather not run Bazel."
};
export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Every Forge crate compiles cleanly with plain Cargo. Use this\nworkflow if you want a smaller dev shell, faster incremental builds\nduring iteration, or just don't have Bazel installed."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "A recent stable Rust toolchain. No `rust-toolchain.toml` is checked in; the\nminimum is `rust-version` in the root `Cargo.toml`, currently 1.97.1,\non edition 2024."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "On Linux: `mold` is optional and has to be configured by hand; see\nfaster incremental builds."
  }, {
    "heading": "prerequisites",
    "content": "You do **not** need `protoc`. The generated Rust for every proto is\nchecked into `forge-proto/genproto/`, and `forge-proto`'s\n`[lib] path` points straight at it; there is no build script."
  }, {
    "heading": "first-build",
    "content": "First build is a few minutes (dependency compilation); subsequent\nincremental builds are typically a few seconds."
  }, {
    "heading": "common-commands",
    "content": "`.rustfmt.toml` uses `group_imports` and `imports_granularity`, both\nnightly-only. Stable `cargo fmt` silently ignores them and produces\noutput CI rejects. Run `bazel run --config=rustfmt @rules_rust//:rustfmt`\ninstead."
  }, {
    "heading": "faster-incremental-builds",
    "content": "A few flags that help:"
  }, {
    "heading": "faster-incremental-builds",
    "content": "`mold` as linker (Linux):"
  }, {
    "heading": "faster-incremental-builds",
    "content": "`CARGO_TARGET_DIR` set to a shared directory across checkouts to\nreuse incremental artifacts."
  }, {
    "heading": "caveats",
    "content": "Three directories are excluded from the workspace and build on their\nown: `forge-config/generate-stores-config`, `forge-test/fuzz`,\nand `tools/generate-bazel-rc`. The LRE flake outputs are Nix, not Cargo."
  }, {
    "heading": "caveats",
    "content": "Regenerating protos goes through Bazel\n(`bazel run forge-proto:update_protos`), not Cargo."
  }, {
    "heading": "caveats",
    "content": "CI runs Bazel, and `bazel test` attaches rustfmt and clippy aspects that\na Cargo run does not. A green `cargo test` does not guarantee a green CI.\nRun `bazel test //...` before submitting."
  }, {
    "heading": "caveats",
    "content": "For the Bazel-based workflow, see\nDevelop with Bazel. For what the test suites contain,\nsee the testing guide."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "Not to build or test; the whole workspace builds with plain Cargo.\nYou need Bazel for a pre-submit `bazel test //...`, for regenerating\nprotos, and for formatting, since that's what CI runs."
  }, {
    "heading": "faq",
    "content": "No. The generated Rust is committed under `forge-proto/genproto/`\nand there is no build script invoking `protoc`. If you change a `.proto`,\nregenerate with `bazel run forge-proto:update_protos` and commit the\nresult. `//forge-proto:update_protos_test` is the CI gate for that."
  }],
  "headings": [{
    "id": "prerequisites",
    "content": "Prerequisites"
  }, {
    "id": "first-build",
    "content": "First build"
  }, {
    "id": "common-commands",
