export let structuredData = {
  "contents": [{
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "**Who this is for:** anyone who has read the rest of this section and wants to\nprove it by writing a config from nothing. &#x2A;*What you'll have at the end:** a\nsingle-process Forge serving a cache and executing actions, from a file\nyou wrote yourself and can explain line by line. &#x2A;*Time:** about an hour."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "The four previous pages in Configuration: the config file, stores, servers and services, and the scheduler and workers."
  }, {
    "heading": undefined,
    "content": "Everything here runs in one process on one machine. That's deliberate: it\nkeeps every name resolvable in one file, so when something is wrong the\nfeedback is immediate. The same blocks split across machines unchanged; see\nProduction configuration once this works."
  }, {
    "heading": "before-you-start",
    "content": "You need the `forge` binary and a writable directory. This walkthrough\nuses `/tmp/forge`; if you use somewhere else, keep the **content path and\nthe work directory on the same filesystem**, because the worker hard-links\nbetween them."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "Start a file called `my-config.json5`. The smallest useful Forge is a\ncache: two stores and one server."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "Run it:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "The process starts and logs `Ready, listening on 0.0.0.0:50051`."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "`/tmp/forge/data/` now contains a content and a temp directory for\neach store (plus a `content_path-*.exec` sibling on Unix), all empty apart\nfrom their `s/` and `d/` subdirectories."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "Deliberately misspelling `eviction_policy` and re-running makes it exit\nimmediately, naming the key it didn't recognise."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-1--two-stores-and-a-listener",
    "content": "That last check is worth actually doing once. It's the fastest feedback loop\nyou have while editing configs, and knowing what the failure looks like means\nyou'll recognise it later."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "Nothing about the config changes here, but proving the cache works before\nadding execution keeps the next failure unambiguous."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "For Bazel, in `.bazelrc`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "Build something twice. The second build should report remote cache hits."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "The first build uploads: `content_path-cas` grows."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "The second build reports remote cache hits and finishes faster."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "`--remote_instance_name` matches the `instance_name` in the config. If it\ndoesn't, the server rejects every cache call with `'instance_name' not\nconfigured for '<name>'`, which Bazel reports as remote cache warnings and\nzero hits."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-2--point-a-build-at-it",
    "content": "Connect your build covers the\nequivalent flags for Buck2, CMake, Goma-family clients, and others. The\nconfig is the same either way."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-3--add-a-scheduler",
    "content": "Execution needs a queue. Add a `schedulers` array beside `stores`:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-3--add-a-scheduler",
    "content": "Three keys is enough to start. `cpu_count` lets an action ask for a machine of\na certain size, `OSFamily` stops a Linux action landing on a macOS worker, and\n`container-image` is carried through to the worker without being matched on."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-3--add-a-scheduler",
    "content": "Restart. Nothing observable changes yet: the scheduler exists but nothing\nreaches it, and no worker has joined."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-4--expose-execution-on-two-ports",
    "content": "Now wire the scheduler in. Add `execution` to the public server, give\n`capabilities` a `remote_execution` block, and add a **second server** for the\nworker API:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-4--expose-execution-on-two-ports",
    "content": "The `remote_execution` block on `capabilities` is the part that's often\nforget. Without it, clients are told this endpoint caches but does not\nexecute, and they'll never send an `Execute` call to find out otherwise."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-4--expose-execution-on-two-ports",
    "content": "Two listeners come up: `:50051` and `:50061`."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-4--expose-execution-on-two-ports",
    "content": "`curl http://localhost:50061/status` answers."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-4--expose-execution-on-two-ports",
    "content": "Nothing client-facing is served on `:50061`. That port is the security\nboundary from\nServers and services."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "A worker needs a `fast_slow` store with a filesystem fast tier. Replace\n`CAS_MAIN_STORE` with one:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "The `noop` slow tier looks strange and is correct here: workers *require* a\n`fast_slow` store, and in a single-process deployment the fast tier already is\nthe storage. In a fleet, `slow` becomes a `grpc` store pointing at the shared\nCAS."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "Then add the `workers` array:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "Every key the scheduler declared as `minimum` or `exact` is here. That is the\ncontract, and it is the single most common thing to get wrong."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "Restart."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "The logs show `Worker registered with scheduler` with the worker id it was\nassigned."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "`content_path-cas` and `/tmp/forge/work` are on the same filesystem:\n`df /tmp/forge/data /tmp/forge/work` reports the same device."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-5--add-a-worker",
    "content": "`/tmp/forge/work` is empty; the worker purges it on startup."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-6--execute-something",
    "content": "Point the build at execution rather than just the cache:"
  }, {
    "heading": "step-6--execute-something",
    "content": "The build reports actions running remotely, not `0 remote`."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-6--execute-something",
    "content": "The worker logs actions starting and finishing."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-6--execute-something",
    "content": "A second build of the same targets hits the action cache instead of\nexecuting, proving `upload_action_result` is wired up."
  }, {
    "heading": "step-6--execute-something",
    "content": "If actions queue instead of running, the properties don't line up.\nPlatform properties is the page for\nthat, and it is far more likely than anything being wrong with what you built\nhere."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-finished-file",
    "content": "Compare it with `forge-config/examples/basic_cas.json5` in the source\ntree. The differences are instructive: the shipped example registers every\nservice twice, under `instance_name: \"\"` and `\"main\"`, so clients that omit\nthe instance name also work, and it declares nineteen platform properties\nrather than three."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "This config is correct and it is not production. Four things are missing, and\neach has a page:"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "**Durability.** A `noop` slow tier means the cache is exactly as durable as\n`/tmp`. Production configuration."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "**Separation.** One process is one failure domain, and one machine's worth of\nexecution capacity. Deploy on bare metal."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "**Security.** `0.0.0.0:50061` is bound to every interface with no\nauthentication. Servers and services\nexplains why that port matters most."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "**Visibility.** Nothing here tells you hit rate, queue depth, or worker\nhealth. Observability."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "You can now read and write a config. These are the specific blocks that do\nspecific things: object storage, Redis, compression, chunking."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-this-is-not-yet",
    "content": "The shape this evolves into once one process on one machine stops being\nenough."
  }],
