  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "`split_blob_support`, `splice_blob_support`, `fast_cdc_2020_params`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "set when the instance's `cas` entry configures `experimental_chunking`; otherwise `false`/unset"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "`execution_capabilities`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "present only when the instance's `capabilities` entry has `remote_execution`; then `exec_enabled` is `true` and the priority range is 0 to `i32::MAX`"
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "Two of these need a caveat."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "**`exec_enabled` is hardcoded to `true`**, with a TODO in the source\nacknowledging it. Whenever a `capabilities` entry names a `remote_execution`\nscheduler, execution is advertised as enabled; there is no way to advertise\n`exec_enabled: false`. A cache-only `capabilities` entry (no\n`remote_execution`) omits `execution_capabilities` altogether, which is the\ncorrect signal for a cache-only deployment."
  }, {
    "heading": "what-capabilities-advertises",
    "content": "**The 64 KiB batch limit is advisory and small** relative to what some\nclients assume. The CAS handlers do not enforce it themselves; the hard stop\nis the listener's `max_decoding_message_size` (4 MiB unless configured), and\na batch request above that is rejected at the gRPC layer. Clients that honour\nthe advertised limit send anything larger through ByteStream."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**ByteStream resource names are parsed right to left.** A resource name is\n`{instance_name}/blobs/{hash}/{size}`, and the instance name may itself\ncontain slashes, so left-to-right parsing cannot find the boundary, while\nright-to-left can, because the trailing components are fixed. See\n."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**`GetTree` page tokens are `\"{hash}-{size}\"`**, constructed and parsed by\nsplitting on `-`. They are neither opaque nor signed. The protocol asks\nclients to treat them as opaque, and clients that do will keep working if the\nformat changes."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**The `Execute` pre-flight input check is shallow and racy by design.** It\ndoes not walk the full input tree, and a blob can be evicted between the\ncheck and the worker's fetch. It exists to turn the common \"inputs were never\nuploaded\" mistake into a fast error, not to provide a guarantee. See\n."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**`ActionStage::CacheCheck` is never emitted.** The cache lookup happens in a\ndecorator in front of the scheduler, so the state is skipped entirely. A\nclient waiting to observe it will wait forever."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**Final action failure arrives as success.** An action that exhausts its\nretries returns `ActionStage::Completed` with `ActionResult.error` set: a\nsuccessful RPC describing a failed action. This is what the REAPI specifies.\nClients that only inspect the gRPC status will misread it."
  }, {
    "heading": "wire-level-details-worth-knowing",
    "content": "**`server_logs` is never populated.** The field exists; the worker does not\nfill it."
  }, {
