    "heading": "the-port-inventory",
    "content": "The configuration crate says this itself, in the doc comment on the field:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-port-inventory",
    "content": "> This is the service used for workers to connect and communicate through.\n> NOTE: This service should be served on a different, non-public port. In other\n> words, `worker_api` configuration should not have any other services that are\n> served on the same port. Doing so is a security risk, as workers have a\n> different permission set than a client that makes the remote execution/cache\n> requests."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "Port 50061 is the one that turns a cache into a compromise. A process that can\nopen a gRPC connection to it can call `ConnectWorker`, be added to the\nscheduler's pool, and start receiving actions, including the compiler\ninvocations, the secrets in your action environments, and the outputs that get\nwritten back into the CAS under digests your developers will later trust."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "No handshake exists beyond the protocol's own. The scheduler does not check\nwho is connecting, because there is nothing to check with."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "Three consequences, in order of how often they bite:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "An attacker who can reach 50061 can **register a malicious worker** and be\nhanded real actions. Whatever those actions can see, they can see."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "An attacker who can reach 50061 can **poison the cache**, because a worker\nuploads action results and the CAS accepts them. A poisoned result for a\nwidely-cached action propagates to every developer who gets a cache hit on it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "An attacker who can reach 50061 can **denial-of-service the scheduler** by\nregistering and disconnecting, since each disconnect requeues that worker's\nin-flight actions and, in this tree, consumes one of their retries."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "What to do:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "Bind it to an address only workers can reach. The single-host Compose file gets\nthis right (the scheduler's 50061 is not published to the host at all; workers\nreach it over the Compose network) and\nthe multi-worker variant\ndoes not; it publishes `50061:50061` on all interfaces. Fix that before running\nit anywhere routable."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "On Kubernetes, keep 50061 off any `GRPCRoute` or `Ingress`, and add a\n`NetworkPolicy` that admits only the worker pods, since nothing else stops a\npod in another namespace from dialling the Service directly."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-worker-api-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "On bare metal, bind it to the private interface or\nfirewall it. It should never appear in a load balancer."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-admin-endpoint-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "The admin REST API registers under `/admin` by default. Every shipped example\nthat enables it puts it on the worker API listener; one\n(`forge-config/examples/worker_with_redis_scheduler.json5`) also enables it\non the public listener, which is the thing not to copy. Its only route is:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-admin-endpoint-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "where the first segment is the scheduler's `name` from the config (the route\nparameter is called `instance_name` in the source, but it is looked up against\nscheduler names). Anyone who can issue that request can drain every worker in\nyour fleet, one `worker_id` at a time, and your builds stop. No credential protects it. It\nis also the endpoint you will legitimately want during an incident (see\nRunbooks), so the answer is to make\nit reachable from your operator network and nowhere else, not to disable it."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-admin-endpoint-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "The `health` service is on the same footing: path `/status` by default, five\nsecond timeout, no authentication. It leaks less, but it is another reason the\n50061 listener belongs on a private network rather than behind an\nauthentication proxy you have not written."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-admin-endpoint-has-no-authentication",
    "content": "It is tempting to put `/status` on 50051 so a load balancer can probe it. That\nmoves the admin API there too if they share a `services` block, and it means\nthe health path is the same listener your developers reach. Configure a\nseparate listener if a probe needs a reachable endpoint, and put nothing else\non it."
  }, {
