    "heading": "configure-tls",
    "content": "Revocation list checked against presented client certificates."
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-tls",
    "content": "All four paths go through shellexpand, so `${VAR}` and `${VAR:-default}` work,\nwhich is how one configuration file serves several environments. Expansion\nhappens once, at parse time; rotating a certificate on disk needs a restart."
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-tls",
    "content": "mTLS proves that a client holds a certificate your CA issued. It does not map\nthat identity to a permission; every authenticated client can do everything.\nNo read-only role, no per-instance-name restriction, and no way exists to\nlet CI write to the cache while developers only read."
  }, {
    "heading": "configure-tls",
    "content": "If you need that distinction, it lives in front of Forge: separate\nlisteners, separate CAs, and a proxy or network policy that decides which\nclients reach which listener."
  }, {
    "heading": "outbound-tls",
    "content": "Outbound gRPC connections (a `grpc` store's `endpoints`, and the worker's\n`worker_api_endpoint`) configure their client side separately with a\n`tls_config` block on the endpoint. Object-store backends do not use it; they\ncarry their own settings (for example `root_certificates` on the `ontap`\nprovider)."
  }, {
    "heading": "outbound-tls",
    "content": "`use_native_roots: true` trusts the host's system trust store instead of a\nnamed CA. Convenient against a public endpoint, wrong against an internal one:\nit means any publicly-trusted certificate for that hostname is accepted."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "For isolation from state left by an earlier action, development builds offer\nthe opt-in `single_use` lifecycle.\nIt requires a launcher that replaces the container and its writable volumes\nafter every action. This prevents reuse of local files, background processes,\nand worker caches. It does not create a new kernel boundary or make untrusted\nactions safe on a privileged container host. Continue applying the network\nand process restrictions described on this page."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "The development-only\n`experimental_buck2_file_capture`\noption additionally runs an operator-supplied collector inside that dedicated\nLinux container. Configure it to exclude platform credentials and internal\ncaches, and use narrowly scoped credentials for its ingest service. The hook\ndoes not isolate a token from action processes with the same user privileges.\nThe collector and\nits ingest service must enforce authorization and filesystem boundaries.\nInvocation metadata identifies the client request; it does not authenticate its\nowner. Separate action mount namespaces and entrypoint wrappers are rejected so\nthe collector cannot accidentally archive the outer worker's filesystem."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Workers can be configured with `use_namespaces: true` and\n`use_mount_namespace: true`. Both are real, and neither makes an action safe to\nrun on your behalf."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "What the worker actually does before executing an action:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Read the uid map carefully. It is an **identity** map: uid *N* inside the\nnamespace is uid *N* outside it. The action runs as the worker's own user, with\nthe worker's own privileges on the host filesystem. This is not a\nuid-remapping sandbox; it is a process-reaping and hermeticity mechanism. PID\nnamespacing means orphaned children die with the action instead of leaking;\nIPC and UTS namespacing means actions cannot see each other's semaphores or\nhostname (the hostname is set to `forge`). With `use_mount_namespace`\nthe flag list also gains `CLONE_NEWNS`, the worker's own root action\ndirectory is masked so one action cannot see another's tree, and `/tmp` is\nreplaced with the action's own temporary directory, on the worker's disk and\nremoved with the action, so actions cannot collide on, or leak state\nthrough, predictable paths there (`isolate_tmp: false` keeps the host's\n`/tmp` visible; the bound `/tmp` carries the work volume's mount options,\n`noexec` included, not the host `/tmp`'s); the rest of the host filesystem\nstays visible."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Note what is absent from that flag list: &#x2A;*`CLONE_NEWNET`**. No network\nnamespace is created. An action has the worker's full network access, including to port\n50061 on your scheduler, and to anything else on the worker's network."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "An action can, by design:"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Read and write every file the worker's user can, outside its work directory"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Open a connection to the worker API and register as a worker"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Reach any internal service the worker host can reach"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Consume the whole host's memory and CPU, since there is no per-action limit\nof any kind in the configuration; the only bound is\n`max_inflight_tasks` and the container's own limits"
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "The practical stance: &#x2A;*treat the right to submit an action as equivalent to\nshell access on a worker host.** Then the controls follow: workers on their own\nnetwork segment with egress rules, no ambient cloud credentials on worker\ninstance profiles, no shared secrets in the worker's environment, and a worker\nhost you are willing to rebuild."
  }, {
    "heading": "the-sandbox-is-not-a-security-boundary",
    "content": "Containerising the worker helps only as far as your container runtime is a\nboundary. The shipped Compose executor runs `privileged: true` precisely so\n`unshare` works, which removes most of what the container was giving you."
  }, {
